window.VAULT_NOTES = {"20th-century American presidents have expanded presidential powers beyond their original constitutional intentions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The presidents of the 20th century have expanded the powers of the presidency beyond their previously utilized limits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The presidents have expanded the power of the presidency in the following manners:</p><ul><li><strong>Expanding war powers:</strong> Truman bypassed Congress to fight the Korean War; LBJ and Nixon waged the Vietnam War without a formal congressional declaration of war.</li><li><strong>Growth of the administrative state:</strong> Known as “congressional delegation,” which essentially transfers legislative power to the executive. Roosevelt’s New Deal essentially gave agencies the powers to enact regulations, bypassing the legislative process entirely.</li><li><strong>Executive orders:</strong> Theodore Roosevelt pioneered the “Stewardship Theory,” whereby the president could do anything that was not explicitly forbidden by the Constitution. FDR and Trump are examples of presidents who expanded executive powers greatly.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy":"<h4>Introduction</h4><p>In contemporary discussions of meritocracy, two perspectives are common. Either meritocracy is the champion of individual abilities and effort, or it is a sham, a societal misconception which only entrenches existing power and wealth differentials. I argue that this is a false binary: meritocracy can be both, <em>at difference times.</em> This argument is developed within the context of liberal societies—the only context in which meritocracy is seriously advanced as a fairness claim, since it presupposes commitments to individual liberty and family autonomy that liberalism independently affirms.[^1] Through a diachronic analysis, we will find that meritocracy is either illiberal, or fundamentally an incoherent, self-defeating concept. In this paper, I specifically attack desert-based meritocracy, the principle that the allocation of social goods is intrinsically justified because individuals deserve outcomes proportional to their merits. [@mulliganMeritocracy2023, sec. 2.2] I concede that instrumental merit, i.e., it is clear that allowing the most qualified to hold a position is a sound principle, regardless of how one is able to become the most qualified, is valid to a certain degree.[^2] <strong>In short, meritocracy, by necessity, it promotes an unacceptable level of structural inequality, which gives us good normative reasons to reject it as a viable principle of societal organization.</strong></p><h4>Justifying Meritocracy</h4><p>Desert-based meritocracy (DBM) claims to be a <em>just</em> system of allocation where positions and rewards go to those who earn them through relevant talent and effort, disregarding irrelevant characteristics.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p. 22] But what does it mean to be just? We can distinguish between two interpretations.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p. 22; @rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, sec. 47] (1) <em>Procedural justice</em> states that if the procedure involved in the distribution of social goods is justice, then the outcome of that distribution is just. John Rawls’ terms this “pure procedural justice,” giving the analogy of a fair gambling game, where as long as the procedure is carried out according to fair rules, the outcome is just—though it may result in a differential distribution of goods. [@rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, p. 74-76] (2) <em>Background justice</em> regards the circumstances which must hold for procedural justice to be meaningful, that is, that preexisting institutions must be fair before the procedure executes, otherwise the procedure just launders whatever inequalities preceded it. It seems that any strong meaning of “justice” must satisfy the criteria from both interpretations of the term. Disregard the former, and there is no guarantee that the the people who are the hardest working are compensated for their efforts. Disregard the latter, and the society merely entrenches extant inequities over time. Therefore, to be a just system of distribution, meritocracy must satisfy justice in this strong sense. Let’s consider (2) in more specificity. Following the convention first explicated in Rawls’s <em>A Theory of Justice</em>, we can distinguish between two why in which background justice may be violated: natural contingencies and social contingencies.[@rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, sec. 12] Natural contingencies represent differences in ability which are innate, broadly encompassing traits such as strength, intelligence, and creativity. Social contingencies represent differences in ability which are due to social circumstances outside one’s control, such as the one’s family wealth, class background, and level/quality of schooling. Background justice for DBM therefore requires equalization of social contingencies specifically; call this Equality of Life Chances (ELC), following Fishkin.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p. 32-34] Social contingencies have no bearing on one’s merits, and simply launder pre-existing inequality while talent and effort are themselves natural endowments, and to require their equalization would dissolve M entirely, since there would be nothing left to reward. In fact, this is not merely a stipulation: it reflects the meritocrat's own preferred account, shared by Mulligan and Miller alike, that natural talent is the legitimate desert base and social circumstances are the distorting factor.[@millerTwoCheersMeritocracy1996; @mulliganJusticeMeritocraticState2017, sec. 4.3] In short, we can define what I term the <em>necessary condition</em>; that is, M is normatively justified if and only if ELC holds. On this note, however, the meritocrat may argue that the argument proves too much. Alan Zaitchik argues that, since it seems like a pre-theoretical certainty that some people deserve things, any argument whose conclusion is “nobody deserves anything” must have a false premise.[@zaitchikDeservingDeserve1977] But this objection misidentifies the target. The biconditional is an institutional claim, not a claim about personal desert. It claims that any desert-based justification for meritocracy fails at the societal and institutional level, because the system is self-defeating. In fact, this distinction is well-established in the literature: Feinberg distinguishes between institutional entitlements and genuine personal desert,[@feinbergDoingDeservingEssays1970, p. 55-94] and McLeod separates “p-desert,” or a pre-institutional moral claim grounded in a person's character, effort, or conduct, from “i-desert,” or what a person deserves relative to the rules of an existing institution.[@pojmanWhatWeDeserve1999, p. 186-95] We can granting Zaitchik that individuals have genuine personal desert claims while leaving the institutional biconditional entirely untouched. So, the necessary condition stands.</p><h4>The Necessary Condition</h4><p>Liberalism generally upholds the principle of Family Autonomy (FA): that parents the right to invest time, attention, culture, values, and parenting in one’s children.[@fishkinJusticeEqualOpportunity, p.39-40] Some have objected to the entire concept of FA. Harry Brighouse and Adam Swift have argued that FA is not a unitary principle but decomposes into (a) “familial relationship goods,” realized through intimate partialities like bedtime stories, conversation, moral modeling, and (b) transferable positional advantages like elite schooling and bequest, which are not constitutive of family value. Only (a) generates genuine liberal duties of non-interference; (b) can be regulated, taxed, or abolished without damaging the family as such.[@brighouseFamilyValuesEthics2014] In fact, I accept this decomposition by defining FA rather narrowly, with factors such as the wealth and connections of a family left out. Factors such as a family’s wealth and power instead falls under the purview of ELC. Still, there is research which suggests that even these immaterial things as described in (a) has real effect on the distribution of wealth; in other words, that ELC and FA are difficult to separate. Specifically, the type of family a child is born into has wide-ranging impacts on the future success of the child. For instance, studies have shown how family types,[^3] class, racial backgrounds, and even parenting styles significantly affect student outcomes.[@fisherComparativeStudyUnderachieving;  @potterPsychosocialWellbeingChildren2017; @vangelderenQualityLifeAdolescents2012; @lareauUnequalChildhoodsClass2003; @segallIfYoureLuck2011; @gheausWhatAbolishingFamily2018] So, it seems that Brighouse-Swift's decomposition fails empirically. If this is the case, then, given the the necessary condition and FA, we can deduce that M is incompatible with liberal societies. The meritocrat might raise two objections here:</p><ol><li>The meritocrat accepts the empirical evidence, but argues that it is evidence of a fixable policy, not a structural incompatibility.</li><li>The ELC does not require perfect equalization, only that life chances are not predictable from morally arbitrary facts beyond some acceptable threshold.[@lopezEsTrilemaFishkin1996] </li></ol><h4>The Diachronic Argument</h4><p>To address these concerns, I will develop an argument that demonstrates the conceptual incompatibility of FA and ELC, which I term the <em>diachronic argument</em>. Assuming a liberal society and granting that all persons are equalized at some initial time ($ELC_{t_{0}}$) and that at $t_{0}$ FA is irrelevant ($\\lnot FA_{t_{0}}$; e.g., all families have the exact same structure and parent in the exact same manner), we can imagine the following to occur:</p><ol><li>$M$ operates from $t_{0} \\rightarrow t_{1}$ under perfectly fair conditions.</li><li>$M$ produces winners (due to people’s natural contingencies), who are then free to exercise $FA$ at $t_{1}$.</li><li>$FA$ means that parents are free to investment differentially in children, which means $\\lnot ELC_{t_{2}}$.</li><li>Operating $M$ once again, and since the winners of $M$ continue to differentially invest in their progeny, furthering $\\lnot ELC$, $\\neg\\,{ELC}<em>{t_2} \\rightarrow \\neg\\,{ELC}</em>{t_3} \\rightarrow \\cdots \\rightarrow {ELC}_{t_n}$.</li><li>Therefore, even granting ${ELC}<em>{t_0}$, ${FA}</em>{t_1} \\rightarrow \\neg\\,{ELC}_{t_n}$ for all $n \\geq 2$.</li><li>Then, since it fails to satisfy the necessary condition, liberal societies must reject $M$.</li></ol><p>Importantly, this argument is different from Rawls’ luck objection. The luck objection states that, <em>at a single moment,</em> your talent is based on arbitrary and thus morally irrelevant factors.[@logarRawlssRejectionPreinstitutional2013] Thus, you cannot be said to deserve the rewards that flow from those talents, and the basic structure should not allow them to determine distributive shares unchecked. The diachronic argument, however, grants that the initial allocation of rewards was fully deserved, and shows instead that M is necessarily self-refuting. The injustice is therefore not located in the arbitrariness of talent at a single moment, but in the very structure of the intersection of M and liberal societies. This means that one can reject the luck objection entirely and yet accept the diachronic critique; in fact, this argument is stronger than the luck objection since it offers more concessions to the meritocrat.</p><h4>Some Considerations</h4><p>Returning to the aforementioned concerns, as long as one accepts the presence of natural contingencies, the diachronic argument ensures that M eventually undermines itself. The meritocracy might then argue for the equalization of natural contingencies in order to resolve the argument, but this faces several problems. First, it is unclear whether this would be liberal in nature; its hard to see how a society could equalize something like IQ or creativity without massive intrusions into the rights of families to their own children. Second, there are some natural contingencies which are extremely difficult, if not impossible, to mitigate. For instance, the location or time at which someone is born. It would be extremely challenging for a society to be able to coordinate the location and time of birth of every individual—yet, one’s birth location is clearly relevant for one’s future prospects, and thus must be mitigated given the overarching goal of mitigating natural contingencies.[@shacharBirthrightLotteryCitizenship2009; @rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999, sec. 12] Having examined the specific objections towards the incompatibility between FA and ELC, let us examine some other, a general defenses of meritocracy. In <em>Justice and the Meritocratic State</em>, Thomas Mulligan presents a rather unique defense of meritocracy, arguing from a metaphysical front. Drawing from Kripke’s concept of the essentiality of origin,[@kripkeNamingNecessity1980, p. 111-116] Mulligan argues that since one’s innate traits are metaphysically necessary to your identity, those traits are therefore not “arbitrary” in the morally relevant sense. One could not wish to be born with different talents, since the person with different talents would not be you. Napoletano (2022), however, demonstrates that this argument conflates metaphysical necessity with moral non-arbitrariness: that a trait could not have been otherwise does not entail that it is a fair basis for distributing social rewards.[@napoletanoCanEssentialityOrigin2022] But this refutation is in fact unnecessary for the basic function of the diachronic argument, since it operates through social contingencies. Natural contingencies are only relevant at $t_{0}$, though it then begins to produce social contingencies which become the main problem for the meritocrat. To address one final objection, a meritocrat might argue for the re-imposition of ELC after the application of FA in each time period. But this concedes that M requires permanent external correction to sustain its own justificatory conditions, meaning $M$ is not a self-standing principle of justice but a temporary corrective mechanism dependent on possibly illiberal interventions of state to restore a favorable condition. It is hard to see any meritocrat accepting this as a satisfactory interpretation of meritocracy.</p><h4>Conclusion</h4><p>Framing meritocracy as a binary is a false dichotomy. It fundamentally creates the situation for its own demise, even when it’s given the charitable concessions at to begin with. So, if we want to preserve meritocracy, the right question to ask is is: are we willing to give up the very foundations of the liberalism in order to do so?</p><p>[^1]: For the classic case in favor of liberalism, see [@millLiberty1864]. Furthermore, liberalism has proponents across the political spectrum, ranging from left-egalitarians, e.g. Ronald Dworkin in his [@dworkinTakingRightsSeriously1977] to liberal egalitarians, e.g. Rawl’s [@rawlsTheoryJusticeRevised1999] to right-libertarians, e.g. Nozick’s [@nozickAnarchyStateUtopia1974]. [^2]: Literature such as [@mulliganMeritocracy2023] and [@danielsMeritMeritocracy1978, p. 207-208] distinguish these two forms of meritocracy and how belief in instrumental meritocracy does not necessitate a corresponding belief in desert-based meritocracy. [^3]: Types of family arrangements, i.e., two parent nuclear family, one parent family, same-sex parent families, etc.</p>","A Dutch Auction is one whereby the price is slowly lowered":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In an English auction, the price of an item is slowly bid up until only one bidder remains. In an Dutch auction, the opposite is true. The price of the item is slowly reduced until someone makes a bid.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a different type of auction that differs from our typical perceptions of a auction.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Revenue Equivalence Theorem states that any auction where the highest bidder wins will generate the same expected revenue for the seller\">The Revenue Equivalence Theorem states that any auction where the highest bidder wins will generate the same expected revenue for the seller</a><ul><li>Both these types of auctions abide by the Revenue Equivalence Theorem.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Vickrey Auctions prevents bid shading</span><ul><li>Another type of auction, and an associated charactersitic.</li></ul></li></ul>","A Short History of Chinese Philosophy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Traces Chinese thought from its ancient origins through the twentieth century, focusing on how different schools addressed human nature, morality, social order, politics, and the relationship between humanity and the natural world. It surveys the “Hundred Schools” of the classical period—including Confucianism, Daoism, Mohism, the School of Names, Yin-Yang theory, and Legalism—before examining the development of Confucian metaphysics, the influence of Buddhism and Chan (Zen), the emergence of Neo-Confucianism, and the eventual encounter with Western philosophy. Throughout, Fung presents Confucianism and Daoism as the central currents of Chinese philosophy while emphasizing that Chinese thinkers generally valued practical wisdom, ethical cultivation, harmony, and the art of living over abstract speculation alone.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a good work to understand roughly the nature of Chinese philosophy through history.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>Meta &amp; Misc.</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation\">Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China\">Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy is inseparable from political thought\">Chinese philosophy is inseparable from political thought</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Civilizational continuity, for the Chinese, requires cultural continuance, not political continuance\">Civilizational continuity, for the Chinese, requires cultural continuance, not political continuance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art\">Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act\">A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China’s defeat in the Sino-Japanese war undermined Chinese belief in their cultural superiority\">China’s defeat in the Sino-Japanese war undermined Chinese belief in their cultural superiority</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The paramount contribution of Western philosophy to Chinese philosophy is that of positive logical analysis\">The paramount contribution of Western philosophy to Chinese philosophy is that of positive logical analysis</a></li></ul><p>There are three types of concepts by intuition:</p><ol><li>Differentiated aesthetic continuum,</li><li>Undifferentiated aesthetic continuum, and</li><li>Concepts of the differentiations.</li></ol><p>It is argued that Confucianism is characterized by a study of the concrete concepts of the differentiations, whereas Taoism is characterized by a study of the undifferentiated aesthetic continuum.</p><h5>Confucianism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucius reinterpreted existing moral teachings\">Confucius reinterpreted existing moral teachings</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucius emphasized righteousness (义) and human-heartedness (仁)\">Confucius emphasized righteousness (义) and human-heartedness (仁)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives\">Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader\">The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader</a></li><li>Mencius: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a></li><li>Xunzi: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction\">Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rituals help us manage scarcity, process emotions, form group bonds\">Rituals help us manage scarcity, process emotions, form group bonds</a></li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality\">To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality</a></li></ul><p><strong>Neo-Confucianism</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things are one and the same\">All things are one and the same</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For the sage, emotions are external\">For the sage, emotions are external</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should act according to one’s natural abilities\">One should act according to one’s natural abilities</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a></li><li>The School of Principle: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One receives external moral truths\">One receives external moral truths</a><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There exist eternal and perfect metaphysical frameworks of physical things\">There exist eternal and perfect metaphysical frameworks of physical things</a></li><li>Zhu Xi has an easier time explaining objective standards independent of individual judgment, but a harder time explaining how we gain reliable access to them.</li></ul></li><li>The School of Mind: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral truths arise internally and naturally\">Moral truths arise internally and naturally</a><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality\">To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality</a></li><li>Wang makes moral knowledge immediately accessible in principle, but then has a harder time explaining how we can independently verify that what feels like <em>liangzhi</em> really is <em>liangzhi</em> rather than prejudice, desire, or self-deception.</li></ul></li></ul><h5>Mohism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society\">Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’\">Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’</a></li></ul><h5>Taoism</h5><p><strong>Yang Zhu</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has no other obligations except to oneself\">One has no other obligations except to oneself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to be useless\">One ought to be useless</a></li></ul><p><strong>Laozi</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite\">To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should desire less\">We should desire less</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should do as little as possible\">The state should do as little as possible</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should strive to overcome emotion with reason\">One should strive to overcome emotion with reason</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao\">By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a></li></ul><p><strong>Neo-Taoism: Rationalism &amp; Sentimentalism</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political institutions must constantly change in order to meet the needs of the times\">Political institutions must constantly change in order to meet the needs of the times</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should act according to one’s natural abilities\">One should act according to one’s natural abilities</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Morality is relative\">Morality is relative</a></li></ul><h5>School of Names</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things are one and the same\">All things are one and the same</a></li></ul><h5>Legalism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Legalist school taught the theory and method of organization and leadership, not jurisprudence\">The Legalist school taught the theory and method of organization and leadership, not jurisprudence</a></li><li>Han Feizi: <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ruler must be able to choose competent subordinates\">A ruler must be able to choose competent subordinates</a></li></ul><h5>Buddhism</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things in the universe are manifestations of the mind and thus illusory and impermanent\">All things in the universe are manifestations of the mind and thus illusory and impermanent</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment\">Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment</a></li></ul><p><strong>Zen</strong></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Once enlightenment is achieved, the sage returns to normal life\">Once enlightenment is achieved, the sage returns to normal life</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism (book)\">Utilitarianism (book)</a><ul><li>Some of the arguments, specifically ones made by the Mohists, bare a resemblance to utilitarian arguments.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>Confucian ethics is similar in some way to Aristotelian ethics as both have a focus on virtue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>This work likewise touches on issues of what is just and what is not.</li></ul></li></ul>","A Theory of Justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the foundational works in politics philosophy in contemporary philosophy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding the view of justice that is developed/reference by many contemporary academics.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>The Original Position &amp; Methodology</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While most people share a common set of fundamental beliefs regarding justice, more broadly, conceptions differ\">While most people share a common set of fundamental beliefs regarding justice, more broadly, conceptions differ</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness is specifically designed for a closed, well-ordered society\">Justice as fairness is specifically designed for a closed, well-ordered society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons\">Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The theory of justice is in large part the theory of rational choice\">The theory of justice is in large part the theory of rational choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics\">A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics</a></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Rawls' Reflective Equilibrium</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The priority problem has several solutions\">The priority problem has several solutions</a></li></ul><h5>The Two Principles of Justice</h5><p></p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only realized institutions can be judged to be just or unjust\">Only realized institutions can be judged to be just or unjust</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A principle must satisfy generality, universality, publicity, ordering, and finality in order to be a candidate principle in the original position\">A principle must satisfy generality, universality, publicity, ordering, and finality in order to be a candidate principle in the original position</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice and injustice can manifest in-whole, discretely, or through sum-threshold criteria\">Justice and injustice can manifest in-whole, discretely, or through sum-threshold criteria</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions\">Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected\">The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with\">Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them\">The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There are two considerations relevant to social positions, which are the existence of equal citizenship, and where someone is in the distribution of wealth\">There are two considerations relevant to social positions, which are the existence of equal citizenship, and where someone is in the distribution of wealth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily\">The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses fraternity\">The difference principle expresses fraternity</a></li></ul><h5>Arguments for Justice as Fairness</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position\">Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A society where the worst off are extremely sensitive to their circumstances at the expense of the best off is impossible\">A society where the worst off are extremely sensitive to their circumstances at the expense of the best off is impossible</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of insufficient reason is false\">The principle of insufficient reason is false</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony\">Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a></li></ul><h5>Liberty &amp; Political Application</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The intolerant must mostly be tolerated\">The intolerant must mostly be tolerated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberty can only be restricted in order to protect or strengthen the overall system of equal basic liberties\">Liberty can only be restricted in order to protect or strengthen the overall system of equal basic liberties</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Paternalistic decisions are to be guided by an individual’s settled preferences\">Paternalistic decisions are to be guided by an individual’s settled preferences</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are not apportioned a greater share of liberties beyond a certain limit of their capacity for justice\">People are not apportioned a greater share of liberties beyond a certain limit of their capacity for justice</a></li></ul><h5>Theory of the Good &amp; Stability</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral principles originate from rational choice\">Moral principles originate from rational choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Thin Theory of the Good serves as a standard framework for agents to consider in the original position\">The Thin Theory of the Good serves as a standard framework for agents to consider in the original position</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Self-respect is the most important primary good\">Self-respect is the most important primary good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Excellencies are positive externalities\">Excellencies are positive externalities</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Shame can be natural or moral\">Shame can be natural or moral</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The right is not the same as the good\">The right is not the same as the good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Having a moral personality is sufficient for the full protections of justice\">Having a moral personality is sufficient for the full protections of justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a Rawlsian society, people would be educated for autonomy\">In a Rawlsian society, people would be educated for autonomy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human social relations are non-trivial\">Human social relations are non-trivial</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Dominant-end moral theories fail\">Dominant-end moral theories fail</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones\">The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved\">A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>These two books overlap substantially on ethics and justice. Rawls' thin theory of the good is an explicit evolution of Aristotle's account, and several atomic notes (e.g., <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a>) draw the connection directly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>Contains a dedicated section on Rawls' four critiques of meritocracy (no desert, impracticability of tracking merit, incompatibility with public reason, insufficiently democratic). Connects directly to <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a> and <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice and the Meritocratic State\">Justice and the Meritocratic State</a><ul><li>Mulligan's defense of desert directly targets Rawls' no-desert argument. His response — that genetics are metaphysically essential to a person and thus a valid desert base — is the most developed reply to <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice, Equal Opportunity, and the Family\">Justice, Equal Opportunity, and the Family</a><ul><li>Fishkin explicitly draws on Rawls' pure procedural justice. His trilemma (Merit + Equality of Life Chances + Family Autonomy) exposes structural limits of <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a> when family autonomy is taken seriously.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy</a><ul><li>Builds directly on Rawls' distinction between natural and social contingencies, and on pure procedural justice, to argue that desert-based meritocracy is self-defeating in liberal societies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism and Freedom\">Capitalism and Freedom</a><ul><li>Friedman's thesis that economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom stands in direct tension with Rawls' lexical priority of equal basic liberties over economic gains (see <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle</a>).</li></ul></li><li><em>Defending the One Percent</em><ul><li>Mankiw explicitly attacks the Rawlsian veil of ignorance, arguing it generates unintuitive conclusions (e.g., mandatory organ donation). Also contests the empirical premise that income inequality is primarily rent-seeking rather than talent-driven.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free Will\">Free Will</a><ul><li>Rawls' claim that natural talents are \"morally arbitrary\" implicitly presupposes that agents bear no moral responsibility for unchosen traits — a claim that sits at the intersection of moral luck and compatibilism debates covered here.</li></ul></li></ul>","A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A bad system is one where only the incumbents are able to benefit. In such a system, innovation is reduced, and the total surplus is usually reduced. Systems with entrenched power hierarchies are usually ones that benefit only the incumbents.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for both political and economic systems, as in such system, the consequences of only incumbents benefiting is large.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hungary&#x27;s economy is suffering from a lack of demand, deficit spending, and corruption\">Hungary's economy is suffering from a lack of demand, deficit spending, and corruption</a><ul><li>This is an example of a system that is currently rather corrupt and benefits only incumbents.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a><ul><li>This highlights how without certain economic freedoms (e.g., economic mobility), political freedom is likewise impossible.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a><ul><li>Highlights a trait of a good institution, the exact opposite of what is described herein.</li></ul></li></ul>","A belief is a habit of action":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A belief is a habit of thought which guides our actions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fundamental axiom which underlies the philosophy of pragmatism.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Belief is not merely a habit of thought, but also the accompanying actions. To Pierce, merely a change in thought doesn’t mean anything. It is an accompanying change in action that actually matters. The goal of inquiry is that we may be able to form beliefs.</p><h4>References</h4>","A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Income Effect and the Real Wealth Effect are two different economic concepts that, although somewhat related, describe different phenomena. The former is produced by a change in the price level, and the latter is produced via an increase in an individual’s net worth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These two concepts are very similar, but there are some crucial differences between the two that should be clarified.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The real-world applicability of the Pigou effect is contested\">The real-world applicability of the Pigou effect is contested</a><ul><li>The Pigou Effect is a particular type of real wealth effect whereby a general deflation of prices corresponds with a general increase in spending.</li></ul></li></ul>","A change in some exogenous variable may affect both demand and supply":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A change in some exogenous variable may affect both the demand and supply in some market, and thus, affect the quantity demanded and quantity supplied, and thereby the price.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core concepts in comparative statics, and it underlies how exogenous factors shape endogenous variables in a market.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Factors that are outside of the control of some model/market may affect the supply and demand. For example:</p><ul><li>A change in consumer tastes &amp; preferences</li><li>A change in input costs</li><li>A change in consumer income</li><li>A change in exchange rates</li><li>A change in government policy/new subsidies/taxes</li><li>Etc.</li></ul><p>all may shift the demand and supply curves, thus resulting in changes in price and quantity demanded/supplied at the equilibrium.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs\">Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The effects of capital deepening are uncertain\">The effects of capital deepening are uncertain</a></li></ul>","A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A rational action not identified with desire or temper that is clearly voluntary. It involves the process of deliberation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Choice is important in Aristotle’s philosophy since virtue and vice are things that an individual chooses to do, not merely feel.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Choice is making a decision after a decision, directing our aims in accordance with the decision.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a><ul><li>Deliberation is a process that is integral to the way that choice is structured.</li></ul></li></ul>","A city is by nature a plurality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A city is, by its nature, a conglomeration of reciprocal, free, and equal citizens who operate under the rule of law with a common goal in mind. However, this “common goal” should not be taken too strongly, since merely having an aggregate of similar people destroys the self-sufficiency of the city and reduces it to the level of household or an individual.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s views on the nature of plurality in cities, and it seems to be an application of its doctrine of the Golden Mean.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This seems to me to be a very good heuristic for understanding a nation-state, as something that is ideologically and culturally diversified (e.g., left vs. right, religious differences), but where the ultimate aim of citizens are aligned (e.g., to create a democracy, ensure individual rights/freedoms).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The city is the fundamental political unit\">The city is the fundamental political unit</a><ul><li>This note is important because of the city’s role as the fundamental political unit.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>In fact, it seems to me that justice can only be applied between people who at least have some level of difference between them.</li></ul></li></ul>","A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A conductor, due to the presence of free electrons, allow for electric charge/current to flow through the material with ease. An insulator does not allow electric charge/current to easily flow through the material, as the electrons in such a material are tightly bound to the atoms.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Induction</h4><p>Since electrons are able to easily move in a conductive material, it can generate strong inductive effects. On the other hand, insulators can only generate local polar effects.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object\">In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object</a><ul><li>The equilibrium state that free-flowing charge settles into.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Conductivity is determined by the resistance of a material\">Conductivity is determined by the resistance of a material</a><ul><li>Quantifies “freely” — conduction is a matter of degree, not a binary.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor\">Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor</a><ul><li>Induction is what causes charge to flow through a neutral conductor.</li></ul></li></ul>","A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One objection that Rawls addresses is that of the idea that contract theories are fundamentally unacceptable because they represent mere agreement, and it seems that mere agreement cannot constitute a moral theory.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common critique of contract theories.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls argues that since he began with commonly accepted premises, a logical deduction to some final conclusion is more than merely a proof of that final conclusion, it is in fact a justification for that final conclusion. It “proves” something more than mere agreement, and establishes a sort of metaphysical weight behind the result of that final conclusions, in his case, justice as fairness.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>This defense against the arguments seems rather weak in my opinion. It seems like a weak Moorean shift.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of the city is to foster virtue\">The goal of the city is to foster virtue</a><ul><li>Aristotle would disagree with Contractarianism, and this note would be one of his critiques.</li></ul></li></ul>","A desert claim requires both aboutness and fitness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For a desert claim to hold, the basis of the claim must be about the person who makes it, which is usually cashed out as responsibility, and there must be a natural fit between that basis and the thing deserved.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These two conditions are what allow desert claims to be evaluated rather than merely asserted, and most objections to desert attack one or the other.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aboutness fails when the basis is a circumstance rather than an exercise of agency, which is the form the Rawlsian objection takes. Fitness fails when the reward has no natural relation to the basis, as when a prize for one excellence is paid in a currency belonging to another.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some people must deserve something\">Some people must deserve something</a><ul><li>The pre-theoretical claim these conditions are meant to regiment.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The attack on aboutness.</li></ul></li></ul>","A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Beliefs are rules of action, in other words, they dictate a specific set of actions that must be taken in accordance with the belief.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This defines what it means to have a belief, and allows for the construction of the pragmatic maxim.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A belief that “it is raining,” for instance, leads us to some change in our behavior, such as prompting us to shelter in place or bring an umbrella. However, there is no fundamental difference between the claim that “it is raining,” and “it is raining acid, except the acid is impossible to be detected in anyway and for all intents and purposes, is identical to rain.” Since the latter would not necessitate a difference in conduct, there is no difference between the two claims.</p><h4>Chinese Philosophy</h4><p>The schools of philosophy that originated in China likewise also display a view like this towards epistemology. In fact, they (with the exception of Buddhism, introduced from India), had no formal study of epistemology, so a pragmatic stance towards knowledge was assumed.</p><h4>References</h4>","A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A drop in productivity growth is often hugely detrimental to overall economic growth, due to the immense impact that productivity plays in economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Productivity growth is a huge component in overall economic growth, so a weakening of productivity growth may indicates weak economic growth, even in the long term.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Suppose rGDP (index = 100) and trend productivity growth is 3%/yr. If productivity slows to 1.5%/yr for 4 years, then recovers to 3%:</p><ul><li>Old rGDP path after 4 years: $100×1.034≈112.55100\\times1.03^4 \\approx 112.55100×1.034≈112.55$.</li><li>With slowdown: $100×1.0154≈106.14100\\times1.015^4 \\approx 106.14100×1.0154≈106.14$.</li></ul><p>RGDP is ~6% below the pre-slowdown path and stays there unless productivity growth runs above 3% later to catch up.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a><ul><li>While productivity growth is important, it is not the only cause of economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>This is a point of contrast between productivity growth and uncertainty for economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a><ul><li>This would mean that growth is more and more being driven my labor sectors, though it may be restricted by the Baumol effect.</li></ul></li></ul>","A firm produces at minimum efficient scale when its long run average cost is minimized":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A firm produces at minimum efficient scale (MES) when its long run average cost is at its minimum, meaning that if it produced less, it would not be utilizing its economy of scale effectively, and any more might lead to diseconomies of scale. This is also where the average cost is equal to the marginal cost.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Firms always want to be producing at their minimum efficient scale, since it is the most economically efficient for them.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> The minimum efficient scale is found where the marginal cost ($MC$) is equal to the average cost ($AC$). When the average cost is above the marginal cost, the firm has not reached the full potential of its economy of scale.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition\">Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition</a><ul><li>A feature that MES allows us to note about markets.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a><ul><li>Describes the formation of a monopoly, which can occur when there are relatively large MES.</li></ul></li></ul>","A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a friendship occurs between two people of different status (father/son, elder/younger, ruler/subject, etc.), the affection, respect, and service exchanged between the two ought to be proportional to the status differential between the two.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important to understand friendships between two people with different statuses.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>In such a friendship, the one who benefitted financially or morally should give the other person honor in return, since that is the only way for the lesser to repay the greater.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality\">A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality</a><ul><li>The other case.</li></ul></li></ul>","A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A friendship between two people who are of equal status ought to be based on equality. This means that each persons renders the same amount of love, respect, and service to the other.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle distinguishes between friendships between equals and unequals and this addresses the former case.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality\">A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality</a><ul><li>The content of this note is contrasted with this other note.</li></ul></li></ul>","A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A good conception of justice is one which accommodates our intuitions (our \"firmest convictions\") about morality and also derives a moral structure above that which can be used to arrive at conclusions about more controversial moral topics where intuition fails.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is criteria by which Rawls judges his own conception of justice (justice as fairness).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A good conception of justice should be able to allow for the moral convictions that we intuitively hold to be true, but at the same time, provide a structure that is able to ensure that other moral issues which are not easily addressed through intuition are likewise covered under the moral system.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons\">Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is the reason why Justice as fairness would so easily be accepted? Because it fits our intuitions, but also offers a robust structure for dealing with more controversial moral topics.</li></ul></li></ul>","A good person is someone who has an above average moral character":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that someone who is “good” goes above and beyond the standard moral conventions that agents would rationally agree to in the original position.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the definition that Rawls gives, and the definition which he uses in his book.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A good person engages in good acts. A good act is an non-compulsed act which is intended to advance the rational long-term plan of another person.</p><h4>References</h4>","A good work of literature is morally complex":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This describes the value/nature of literature, in that the best literature is good because it is able to explore the moral complexity of its characters.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important when it comes to evaluating the merit of works of literature.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is the case because the real world cannot be abstracted into a simple black/white or good/bad dichotomy. Rather, by representing different perspectives and complex moral situations that forbid easy categorization, a work of literature is representing a more realistic view of society.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>This, for example, explores such themes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All the King&#x27;s Men\">All the King's Men</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moby-Dick (book)\">Moby-Dick (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Brothers Karamazov\">The Brothers Karamazov</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Stranger\">The Stranger</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","A government's fiscal health and the health of banks are interlinked, and if one fails, the other likely will as well":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The health of the government economy is related to the health of the banking industry, and vice versa.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why government fiscal failures and bank failures are often correlated, and how the failure of one my instigate a failure of the other due to how tight the two entities are bound together.</p><h4>The Doom Loop</h4><p>There are two possible formulations of the doom loop:</p><ol><li>Due to rising government default risk, the value of their bonds plummets, causing the banks to lose massive value and stop lending. The government has to then bail-out the banks, meaning issuing more debt and devaluing their debt even more. At the same time, the economy enters recession due to the end of lending by banks, lowering tax revenue, making the government have to issue even more debt to spend.</li><li>The central bank raises interests rates, meaning that old government bonds become relatively more worthless. Banks retain these bonds, which are unrealized losses. This creates a delicate situations whereby when depositors rush to withdraw their funds, the bank is forced to sell their old bonds at a much lower price than initially expected, which may lead to the government having to bail them out, costing the government money, meaning that they have to issue more debt and thus devaluing more debt.</li></ol><p></p><h4>Empirical Data</h4><p>It has been shown that banks with higher sovereign exposure with a risky sovereign leads to a large fall in their stock value and higher Credit Default Swap spreads (meaning that they have to pay more to insurance their risk).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>An aspect of government spending that contributes to the federal debt, which could increase the chance for default.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation\">The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation</a><ul><li>This could be used to calculate the hypothetical debt limit of a country, something that is delated to this idea, since once the limit is surpassed, the country would almost certainly default.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>This may be another related effect when government debt rises.</li></ul></li></ul>","A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the emergence of the European Union as a major trade power that could rival the US, the intense religiosity of Americans, and radical political differences between the US and Europe, a wide gap has formed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant as the split between the US and Europe is immense geopolitical implications, as the two were long-time allies.</p><h4>References</h4>","A kinase can be activated if it is phosphorylated, or bound to a secondary messenger, cyclin, or calcium ion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A kinase, in addition to being controlled by being phosphorylated, can also be controlled via the use of a secondary messenger, cyclin, or calcium ion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for the function of kinases, since by having multiple means of activation, it ensures that the kinases are only active in the right environments.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers\">In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers</a><ul><li>This shows how only one type of signal receptors need kinases.</li></ul></li></ul>","A limited war is best fought on the strategic defensive and the operational offensive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As a part of his belief in limited warfare, Julian Corbett argued for militaries to adopt a strategically defensive, but operationally offensive approach towards engaging the enemy as to shift the cost-benefit calculus of the enemy until it is more cost efficient for them to surrender.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core concepts of Corbettian strategy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This does not mean that a military should adopt such a stance, <strong>always</strong>. During the opening stages of the war, it may be beneficial to go in the strategic offensive and engage the enemy while they are still unprepared. After establishing control in enemy, the military may then switch to a strategically defensive posture to slowly weaken the enemy.</p><h4>References</h4>","A minor incident can encapsulate a larger movement in a plot":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A small and apparently incidental event can be constructed so as to contain the shape of the larger conflict, which lets a work signal its direction without announcing it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is how a reader is given a deeper understanding of a theme directly rather than through exposition, and it is a reliable marker of authorial control.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Hardy uses the device throughout. When the two wagoners collide in <em>The Mayor of Casterbridge</em>, one hired by Henchard and one by Farfrae, the collision is the whole struggle between the old and the new in miniature.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work\">An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work</a><ul><li>The same compression operating across scenes rather than within one.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a><ul><li>The claim about life that this technique depends on being true.</li></ul></li></ul>","A mixed-member proportional system (MMP) places greater emphasis on politicians solving local issues that standard PR":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because, under a MMP system, half of the seats in government are filled by people directly elected by their local constituency, the government of MMP is able to have a moral local outlook than strict PR, which typically has a more national perspective.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the advantage that MMP has over PR.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Advantages of both over SMP:<ul><li>Cannot be gerrymandered.</li><li>Prevents minority rule.</li><li>Allows for political diversity.</li></ul></li><li>Under MMP, a voter casts two votes: a vote for a representative, and a vote for a party. <ul><li>For the vote for the representative, the representative who receives the most vote wins, and becomes the MP for that area. All of the MPs here comprise half of the parliament.</li><li>For the vote for the party, the other half of parliament is made up of MPs from the parties, with the seats divided based on the portions of votes that they received.<ul><li>The use of the “Party List,” a list of preferred candidates to receive the seats allocated for a particular party increases party loyalty.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect\">Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect</a><ul><li>Both the systems proposed in this note solve many of the problems that are found in instant-runoff and SMP.</li></ul></li></ul>","A nation cannot permanently hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a fixed exchanged rate system, a country cannot hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity, since if a country were to hold more than their relative productivity, the money would simply leave and move to other countries through increased imports.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why although nations may seek to use the expansion of credit as a means of increasing consumption, this does not work.</p><h4>References</h4>","A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A natural monopoly arises when the fixed costs are high in an industry, but the variable/marginal costs are low. This means that once production, which may be initially expensive, starts, the firm can quickly achieve an economy of scale.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Natural monopolies are often a key justification for the existence of some monopolies.</p><h4>Graphical Depiction</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4>","A post-Christian culture is not sustainable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Today's world is a post-Christian one, where the ideas originally from Christianity are taken out of their original religion context and adopted without such a basis.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means (perhaps anticipates) the return to prominence of religion.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Romanticism was the first form of this, as it was when the human desire to communion with the infinite and real became detached from Christianity, instead attaching itself onto nature.<ul><li>However, due to the fact that the structure of nature is not metaphysically large enough to contain the whole of the idea of the sublime and the infinite, Romantic works of art thus showcase a melancholic and yearning side.</li></ul></li><li>Liberalism inherits the idea of universal human dignity and the primacy of conscience, maintaining both while not adopting the metaphysical foundations that underly such a belief. <ul><li>This is why cracks in liberal beliefs began to form in the 20th century and continues today.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Quote</p><p>Romanticism is not a temperamental preference shared by a group of hypersensitive poets, though it was indeed vulgarized into that. It is a Christian longing for the infinite surviving the collapse of the ground that gave it its proper object and its proper discipline, and finding nothing adequate to attach itself to.</p></blockquote><ul><li>Even attempts at overcoming are not successful, because ultimately, there is no overcome, but merely the performance of an overcoming. <ul><li>Nietzsche’s <em>Ubermensch</em> is, for instance, just a return to the prelapsarian innocence of man, a “Christian eschatological figure with the theology removed.”</li></ul></li><li>Since the end of WWII, the world has been running based on a “negative,” fueled by the narrative of “never again.” However, this structure is not able to remain stable forever, and has already fallen apart due to the weight of the metaphysical meaning attached to it.</li><li>We are now living through the recoil, when Christianity is coming back into fashion as a means of replenishing the depleting metaphysical reserves.<ul><li>New Atheism was the final attempt to halt this.</li></ul></li><li>The post-Christian world is associated with kitsch, where things become meaningless as become mere aesthetic objects, and this aestheticization is fetishized and itself aestheticized. <ul><li>This means that rather than actually being aware of the lack of meaning in art, with the aestheticization of aesthetics, a genuine reckoning with what is lacking disappears.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Philosophy of History</h4><p>When it comes to the philosophy of history, our modern conception of secular progress is also something that is unsustainable or at least metaphysically questionable without Christianity. As Lowith argues, when the Christian put their faith in God, it is not as much a debate as the relative reasonableness of such a belief, but that whether such faith can be put into the “God-Man” and not God himself.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 206</p><p><strong>Hope is justified only by faith which justifies itself.</strong> Perhaps both grow only on the ruins of all-too-human beliefs and expectations, on the fruitful soil of despair of what is subject to illusions and deceptions.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>Examples of the cracks in liberal belief today.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>Perhaps a post-Christian world would end this too?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a><ul><li>Similar claim to this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","A principle must satisfy generality, universality, publicity, ordering, and finality in order to be a candidate principle in the original position":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In order for a principle to be considered in the original position, there are several conditions that it must meet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines some of the requirements that principles must abide by in the original position.</p><h4>Conditions</h4><p>There are five conditions that Rawls identifies in order for a principle to be seriously considered in the original position:</p><ol><li>Generality condition: principles must be general and does not refer to specific people or require knowledge of contingent particulars.</li><li>Universality condition: principles must be universally applicable, can’t be self-defeating, and should be judged given ensured compliance to the principles.</li><li>Publicity condition: principles have to be public, since secret principles which people don’t know about can’t be relied upon in society.</li><li>Ordering condition: principles must produce a deterministic ordering of conflicting claims based on morally relevant factors.</li><li>Finality condition: there are no higher principles, other than the principles defined in the original position, which people appeal to.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>Perhaps at least partly because of the conditions that it adheres to?</li></ul></li></ul>","A proton and electron carry equal but opposite elementary charges":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A proton and an electron carry the same magnitude of charge, except in opposite directions. This magnitude of charge is $e$, or an elementary charge, which is roughly $1.602 \\times 10^{-19} C$.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important because despite their mass differences, a proton and an electron carry the same amount of charge.</p><h4>Graphical Demonstration</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Charges refer to different particles depending on the context\">Charges refer to different particles depending on the context</a><ul><li>Disambiguates which particles count as “charges” in a given problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","A ranked-choice voting system cannot satisfy all four conditions of fairness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Arrow’s Impossibility Theorem states that it is impossible for a ranked-choice voting system to satisfy all four of the fairness conditions that he identifies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This exposes a crucial flaw with ranked-choice voting.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The four fairness criteria that he identifies are:</p><ol><li><strong>Unrestricted Domain</strong>: voters are free to rank their candidates however they choose.</li><li><strong>Non-Dictatorship</strong>: no one person is able to decide the outcome of the election; e.g., if every other individual ranks $A$ above $B$, then the dictator’s ranking of $B$ above $A$ should not change the result of the election.</li><li><strong>Pareto Efficiency</strong>: consensus rule; if every individual prefers $A$ to $B$, then $A$ would be elected.</li><li><strong>Independence of Irrelevant Alternatives (IIA)</strong>: given rankings, the introduction of a new candidate into the election should not change the relative ranking of $A$ and $B$.</li></ol><p>A ranked choice voting system cannot, Arrow concludes, satisfy all four of these conditions at once.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect\">Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect</a><ul><li>Instant-runoff voting would not be able to satisfy all four conditions of fairness as outlined here.</li></ul></li></ul>","A regime should prevent its defining style of organization from being pushed too far":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A regime should not intensify its defining principle, since that would only worsen the regime and eventually prevent it from functioning at all.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a guideline that Aristotle provides when it comes to reforming regimes.</p><h4>Example</h4><p>For instance, a democracy should not try to reform itself to be even more democratic when it is already democratic, since doing so would cause the regime to lose its moderateness, and thus highlight the negative aspects of any type of regime, and eventually bring about its downfall.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>Its always best to strive for the mean, and not one of the extremes.</li></ul></li></ul>","A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A right is a claim to something which the society has an obligation to defend me in having that thing.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Mill’s definition of a right.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When a right a violated, the following occurs:</p><ul><li>Some individual is hurt.</li><li>There is a demand for the punishment of the offender.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Given that basic rights are protected, inequality ought to be reduced as much as possible\">Given that basic rights are protected, inequality ought to be reduced as much as possible</a><ul><li>This could be the definition of a right that Rawls would accept, and would thus clarify the principle stated here.</li></ul></li></ul>","A ruler must be able to choose competent subordinates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the most important traits of a good leader is that they are able to choose good subordinates who are competent and loyal.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Very often, the most successful rulers are the ones who were able to find the best subordinates.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Fundamentally, this principles seems to arise out of the fact that both the time, energy, and scope of a leader’s personal abilities are limited, and therefore, much of the administration of a state must be left up to subordinates, who thus ought to be as competent as possible. A leader who is able to delegate tasks, especially ones that they themselves are not well-versed in, to proficient subordinates expands his administrative capabilities.</p><h5>Legalist School</h5><p>The Legalist School argued that, in fact, the only job of the ruler is to pick competent officials, who then run the state for him. The ruler mere sets the goals, and the officials carry out the directive to the best of their ability. Those who succeed are rewarded, while those who fail are punished.</p><ul><li>The ruler must delegate since what the ruler contributes is the <em>unity of decision</em>, not personal ability. Therefore, the ability to delegate must never by delegated to another individual, and neither should the force to enforce punishment upon wayward officials.</li></ul><p>To the ruler, the methods with which his goals are accomplished is irrelevant—such matter are the purview of the officials that he delegates.</p><h4>References</h4>","A society where the worst off are extremely sensitive to their circumstances at the expense of the best off is impossible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is a possible society where the prospects of the worst off can be improved marginally, but the prospects of all those better are extremely negatively affected. This seems hardly acceptable, and yet, would be supported by Rawls’ views. Rawls argues that this is impossible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common and powerful objection against Rawls’ views.</p><h4>Counterargument</h4><p>Rawls argues that this is simply an inconceivable situation, since with realistic institutional assumptions, this situation could never occur. Specifically, his close-knit and chain-connected assumptions (see <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected\">The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected</a>) entail that improving the position of the least advantaged will in practice tend to improve or leave unchanged the positions of the better-off — making the extreme sensitivity scenario empirically implausible.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses fraternity\">The difference principle expresses fraternity</a><ul><li>If this objection works, then this is no longer true.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>But under this edge case, it seems wrong to demand the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","A sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein generating membrane potential in animal cells":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase or sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein that generates membrane potential in animal cells, as it moves 3Na⁺ out, while moving 2K⁺ in, generating an overall negative charge in the cell.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for the movement of molecules across the cell membrane.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cells are typically negatively charged\">Cells are typically negatively charged</a><ul><li>The pump mentioned herein is what keeps the cell negatively charged.</li></ul></li></ul>","A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that strictly following some moral code has downsides.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in many works of literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Examples include Oedipus, Hamlet, and Frankenstein.</li><li>A negative example of this would be Odysseus: during his journey back to his homeland after the Trojan war, he repeatedly lies, and as a result, he is able to make it back home safe and sound.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Frankenstein (book)\">Frankenstein (book)</a></li></ul>","A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes ensuring the will of the majority, and not ensuring the that outcome is optimal in the utilitarian sense.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Because electing a Condorcet Winner is only concerned with electing the leader who beats all other candidates individually, it does not take into consideration the level of support that the candidate has relative to other candidates.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Where as Condorcet Winner are merely the candidates who are supported by the most people, a winner elected via Score Voting ensures that the winner selected maximizes social welfare.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Score Voting ensures that the outcome of an election maximizes social utility</span><ul><li>The alternative voting method that contrasts with a system that seeks a Condorcet Winner.</li></ul></li></ul>","A tax on immutable and observable traits would raise revenue without distorting incentives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because a tax changes behaviour only where the taxed thing can be changed, a levy on fixed and visible characteristics such as height would raise revenue with no deadweight loss, which is what a consistent optimal-tax theory recommends.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The proposal is a reductio rather than a policy: nobody supports it, and the reason nobody supports it is evidence that our tax intuitions are not in fact grounded in welfare maximisation.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mankiw uses the argument this way, as an internal critique. The force of it depends on the traits genuinely correlating with earning capacity, which the height literature does report, and on there being no independent objection to taxing people for what they are rather than for what they do.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utility cannot be compared across individuals\">Utility cannot be compared across individuals</a><ul><li>The other half of the internal critique of utilitarian taxation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same\">Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same</a><ul><li>The incidence result this argument relies on.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation\">The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation</a><ul><li>The same non-distortion logic applied to an immobile factor rather than a person.</li></ul></li></ul>","A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The tragedy of the commons, which occurs when there is a public good that is not administered by anyone, cannot be solved through moral suasion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Moral suasion is a common tactic used to attempt to prevent the tragedy of the commons, though it does not work.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>First, when moral suasion is attempted, in the mind of the intended target, they can choose to either</p><ol><li>Ignore the moral suasion, and live with shame, or</li><li>Follow along, but watch as others continue to exploit the commons.</li></ol><p>This results in cognitive dissonance that can confuse or disorientate people. In addition, conscience is self-eliminating. That is, those that do not exploit a commons may be less successful than those that do, eventually leading those who do not exploit the commons to be out-competed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a><ul><li>If this is the case on a somewhat large scale, then would problems of the commons become much less problematic?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Selective incentives are excludable benefits or sanctions that groups use to overcome the free-rider problem\">Selective incentives are excludable benefits or sanctions that groups use to overcome the free-rider problem</a><ul><li>Incentives solve the problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A tragic protagonist can be constructed so as to embody the political character of the society that produced the work, so that the traits that drive the plot are also the traits that the audience recognised as their own.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Where this holds, the play is evidence about the self-understanding of its audience and not only about its author, which is what makes tragedy usable as a historical source.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Sophocles builds Oedipus this way. He espouses democratic commitments, acts with speed and rationality, adapts, and reaches for a Promethean scale of action, all of which are the qualities fifth-century Athens claimed for itself.</p><ul><li><strong>Support for democracy</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I thought it wrong, my children, to hear the truth from others, messengers. Here I am myself— you all know me, the world knows my fame: I am Oedipus.</span><ul><li>Shows how Oedipus is coming down from his position of power to engage with the people on a more personal basis. He doesn’t even want there to be a messenger that brings him news. He wants to directly engage with the people.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Your pain strikes each of you alone, each in the confines of himself, no other. But my spirit grieves for the city, for myself and all of you</span><ul><li>Oedipus describes how he pains for the entire city, something that is clearly “democratic” as he thinks for the wellbeing of the entire city.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Speak out, speak to us all. I grieve for these, my people, far more than I fear for my own life.</span><ul><li>Oedipus decides to have Creon reveal the news about Apollo’s  prophecy in the public, clearly showing his democratic nature.</li></ul></li><li></li></ul></li><li><strong>Quickness of action</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Your king was murdered—royal blood! What stopped you from tracking down the killer then and there?</span><ul><li>Oedipus’ dissatisfaction with the inaction of the people around him, by contrast, illustrates his quickness of nature.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I've not been slow with that. On Creon's cue I sent the escorts, twice, within the hour. I'm surprised he isn't here.</span><ul><li>Oedipus had already predicted the correct next course of option before the leader of the chorus even said it.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">When my enemy moves against me quickly, plots in secret, I move quickly too, I must,</span><ul><li>While the leader of the chorus instructs Oedipus to act with caution and deliberation, Oedipus says that he must move quickly, since that is the speed with which his enemies move. This is possibly a critique of the impulsiveness of the Athenians, rather than a compliment, since this impulsiveness is what keeps Oedipus from learning the truth.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><strong>Pragmatic</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">You pray to the gods? Let me grant your prayers. Come, listen to me—do what the plague demands: you'll find relief and lift your head from the depths.</span><ul><li>Oedipus does not believe in prayer to the gods as the solution to the crisis that Thebes faces. This is something that Oedipus comes to reject by the end of the play.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li><strong>Mathematical thinking</strong><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">You said thieves— he told you a whole band of them murdered Laius. So, if he still holds to the same number, I cannot be the killer. One can't equal many. But if he refers to one man, one alone, clearly the scales come down on me: I am guilty.</span><ul><li>“One cannot equal many”; clearly shows the mathematical thinking here and the mathematical language being used.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a><ul><li>Part of the reason for writing the play it sees, is for Oedipus to repudiate the diminishing belief in the gods. He uses the idea of determinism to illustrate the dangers of such an idea to the contemporary Athenian.</li></ul></li></ul>","A tyranny makes its population uneducated, mistrust one another, and incapable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The tyrant, in order to maintain his power, tries to make the population</p><ul><li>uneducated, unambitious, and parochial, </li><li>lose trust in others, and </li><li>incapable of action against the tyrant, i.e., keeping them impoverished or overworked or under crushing taxation.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>These are the three things which Aristotle identifies that tyrants do in order to maintain power, and they remain true today.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, the second point is something that is true by the very nature of common knowledges, since what is common knowledge is something that people don’t always know, and if the punishments are strict enough and people mistrust each other enough, they will not break free of chains of the state for the fear that others might not feel the same. Aristotle also identifies some other characteristics of tyrants:</p><ul><li>That they are warmongers, so that a strong leader always seems needed,</li><li>That they set the rich against the poor and highlight other divisions,</li><li>That they enforce heavy surveillance, and</li><li>That they prevent people some associating freely.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a><ul><li>It seems that through education, the state is able to assert some of the control highlighted here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>It could be shaped wrongly in a tyrannical regime.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check\">Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check</a><ul><li>Maybe taxes are not always effective as a means of suppression?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rentier states typically have high inequality and low political participation\">Rentier states typically have high inequality and low political participation</a><ul><li>Rentier states are often tyrannical too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division\">Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division</a><ul><li>This is related to the point of tyrants being warmongering.</li></ul></li></ul>","A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A multitude of factors may prevent us from being able to act in a continent manner.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why incontinence exists.</p><h4>Incontinence</h4><p>Incontinence is the lack of self-control, and it regards pleasure and pain (specifically, an excess of one or the other).</p><ul><li>It is acting wrongly beyond one’s own control (where as licentiousness is willed wrongness of action).</li><li>It is something to avoid and to condemn.</li></ul><p>Additionally, one can be incontinent contingently, i.e., incontinent regarding a specific thing. If this is the case, such a person is not generally “incontinent,” but simply specifically incontinent about something. In fact, we can judge different particular incontinences based on how bad they are. For example:</p><ul><li>Incontinence of temper is not as bad as incontinence of desire, since temper is rational (though exaggerated), and it is not cultivated or indulgent, and it is “less deliberate.”</li></ul><p>There are also two types of incontinence:</p><ol><li>Impetuosity: failure to deliberate –&gt; acting in accordance with one’s desires.</li><li>Weakness: successful deliberation –&gt; but failure to abide by the decision of the deliberation.</li></ol><p>It is easier to cure the former (if they are not also weak), but the latter is difficult to change. Additionally, it easier to change habits than natures.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some factors that may cause incontinence include:</p><ul><li>Having knowledge but not using it.</li><li>Having a mistake in applying the Practical Syllogism.<ul><li>This may be because there was an error during the deliberation of the syllogism, which causes the wrong action to be performed, or</li><li>Desire pushed us to abide by the incorrect syllogism.</li></ul></li><li>By mentally incapacitated or just in a circumstance where continence or use of knowledge is impossible.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","A viable system must be internally consistent":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Practically, any system that could be considered viable must be internally consistent (as much as possible), since if that is not the case, the system is self-defeating, and thus, no longer a coherent system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This framework can be used to analyze many different systems and judge system based on their consistency.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A system is internally consistent if both $\\phi$ and $\\lnot \\phi$ cannot both be proven from some $\\xi$ . This is specifically referring to semantic consistency (since syntactic consistency is assumed in any second-order system above a purely deductive/logical one). If it is the case that a system is actually inconsistent, then by <em>ex contradictione quodlibet</em>, anything can be proven to be true.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>This note is also related to systems in general.</li></ul></li></ul>","A world dominated by China will be radically different from a world dominated by the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A world that is dominated by China would be completely different from the current world which is dominated by the US. This is due to cultural and economic differences between the two powers.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This note predicts how a world dominated by China would look, compared to a world dominated by the US.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>First, it is unlikely that a lack of demand from the US is going to cause a huge problem for China, since contrary to popular belief, China is not a export-based economy.  <a href=\"https://data.worldbank.org/indicator/NE.EXP.GNFS.ZS?locations=CN-FR-DE-KR-US\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bank</a></li><li>China surpasses the US in state capacity, but the state is not always the bets at being able to use the resources that it has in the most efficient manner.<ul><li>This means that while nationally, China might dominate, they won't necessarily live better than the citizens of the US have.</li></ul></li><li>China is more selfish than the US, and would not provide the same public goods that the US as provided, though they are perfectly capable of doing so if needed.<ul><li>China's had a generally inward looking foreign policy in its history.</li></ul></li><li>Chinese culture won't be as influential as the US's was to the isolation of China was the rest of the world digitally.</li><li>Due to the weakness of IP protection and \"other institutional factors,\" innovation in China will likely be a lot of incremental improvements rather than big technological change.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China will dominate in East Asia\">China will dominate in East Asia</a><ul><li>Although China might dominate, its dominance could be quite different from the dominance of the US.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>See above note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>This could be a reason why China has a different approach when it comes to being the dominant global power.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies\">Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies</a><ul><li>This could potentially make it more likely that China will come to dominate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a><ul><li>This note echoes this sentiment.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon\">States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon</a><ul><li>With the rise of China, states around it will have to make this choice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a><ul><li>This could have been part of the reason for the rise of China.</li></ul></li></ul>","A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A “torn country” is one where there are competing dominant culture groups in a nation. There are several steps that may be taken in order to redefine a nation’s culture.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>There are many examples of torn countries in the world, and this note gives a way that they may be able to redefine themselves</p><h4>Torn Countries</h4><p>Torn countries are able to redefine their culture, given that they meet the following three criteria:</p><ol><li>Support from the political and economic elite of the country.</li><li>Public acquiescence (not necessarily support) towards the shift.</li><li>The dominant elements in the host civilizations must be receptive to the new civilization joining.</li></ol><h4>Examples of Torn Countries</h4><p><strong>Russia</strong></p><ul><li>During the Soviet Era, they were united by their ideology and the fact that they were different from the West because they were more advanced.</li><li>Today, there are divisions in the nation between the “nationalists” and the “Atlantists,” the former arguing that Russia should turn its focus to the East/South, and the latter arguing that Russia should turn to the West.</li></ul><p><strong>Turkey</strong></p><ul><li>Ataturk was able to Westernize and modernize the nation.</li><li>Increasingly, there is a tide of shifting towards Islamization.<ul><li>This is partly because when Turkey tried to join Western civilization (the EU), they were rejected since the West was unable to accept the cultural different between them and the Muslim Turks.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Mexico</strong></p><ul><li>It was economically liberalized by Salinas in the 1980s.</li><li>It represented a struggle between North American and Latin American cultures.<ul><li>It has not been able to completely North Americanize itself due to resistance from the general public.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Australia</strong></p><ul><li>It was historically Western.</li><li>Today, it has expressed a desire to join Asia. This is impossible, since:<ul><li>Both its elite and general population are either apathetic about the idea or oppose it.</li><li>Other Asian countries oppose it on the grounds of a cultural difference.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture\">Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture</a><ul><li>Australia has tried to shift its culture to that of Asia, but this has not been possible despite increasing trade ties between the two civilizations.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the accessibility that AI provides, it will allow consumers to gain more information about the products that they purchase. This can help reduce the information asymmetry that currently exist in markets, allowing consumers to make more optimal choices in their purchase and avoid being taken advantage of.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The article estimates that around a quarter of all consumer purchases are of products that suffer from extreme informational asymmetry, meaning that the ability to reduce or eliminate those asymmetries may be incredibly useful.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>How AI could make markets more efficient depends on two factors:</p><ol><li>People have to know how to correctly use AI, since that is the only way for its benefits to manifest.</li><li>How AI is used by retailers or suppliers, which could affect how much of an advantage the consumer gets.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capital&#x27;s share of income will rise in the future due to AI\">Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI</a><ul><li>This is a potential for increased inequality, though the thing described in this note should lower inequality.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI represents a new epistemological category":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>AI represents something that is entirely new compared to past innovations (in terms of epistemology) of humans. This manifests both in its potential upside and downside.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents AI as something that is entirely new in terms of the epistemology that it represents.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The \"magics\" referenced here are to broad, informational improvements that humanity has experienced, not specific technological advancements. Humanity's first \"magic\" is history, a means of storing information which meant that information could be passed down to future generations and thus preserved with detail. Humanity's second \"magic\" is science, a means of making observations of nature rigorous and testable, creating generalizable laws of nature in the process. Humanity's third \"magic\" is AI, which is able to take in massive amounts of data and come to some sort of conclusion, despite there being no discernable generalizable laws that are formulated in the process.</p><ul><li>While this gives us a huge amount of power in that it allows us to find regularities in complex systems that we would not otherwise be able to find, it also means that internal mechanisms of AI are not able to be comprehended by humans.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a><ul><li>Perhaps another examples/case study of humans engagement with AI.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI will reduce wage inequality through 'simplification'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While many people have talked about how AI could potentially increase inequality, evidence suggests that AI could likewise provide something to the opposite effect.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This disputes the narrative that AI can only increase inequality and argues for why it will benefit the average worker.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>21% average wage growth, with gains concentrated at the bottom of the income distribution.</li><li>26-34% welfare impact (equivalent variation, or permanent proportional wage increase) across all occupations and skill levels.<ul><li>The average is estimated at 21%, and this is lower because AI disproportionately affects lower-skill workers.</li></ul></li><li>AI helps through:<ul><li>Augmentation: it saves 20.2% worker time on average.</li><li>Automation: 22% of tasks can be fully automated.</li><li>Simplification (most important): it lowers the required skill level by 18.3%.<ul><li>It lowers the barrier to entry for complex tasks, leading to wage compression and thus the scarcity premium for experts decreases.</li><li>This is also the key aspect that causes inequality to decrease in the presence of AI. While augmentation drives the average wage boost, it is simplification that drives the distribution.</li><li>Simplification means that the value shifts from “knowing how to do something” to “knowing what to do.”</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>It especially helps bridge the gap between experts and novices in verbal-heavy domains. This means that math and social skills become the new “defensible moats.”</li><li>Generative AI reduces inequality due to simplification, through generative AI + physical AI means that inequality would increase, since that would lead to mass automation.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that although this is the case, it could potentially make labor markets more competitive, which would generally just lower wages. Like if everybody becomes AI enhanced, even if the service they provide has a “human premium” (e.g., a therapist), there remain ways to augment the service that the human provides such that it is easier for others to enter the market and compete.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets\">AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets</a><ul><li>This describes another potential way that AI could help individuals.</li></ul></li></ul>","AI's Effect on Employment (Unfinished)":"<h3>Suppose AI Reduces the Number of People Needed to Perform a Large Fraction of Jobs, Threatening to Cause High Unemployment. What Measures Should Be Put in place to Limit Inequality in Pay (And, iDeally, also in hOurs wOrked)?</h3><ul><li>This is a tenuous counterfactual. But lets say that it is true at some point in the future.</li><li>The inequality of pay referred to seems to be the difference between the people with jobs and those without.</li><li>The real wages of the remaining human workers would increase as a result of AI automation, since that lowers the price of goods generally in the market, meaning that, even though the human workers’ productivity has not increased, they are paid more.</li><li>It seems to me that complete replacement of work by AI (that is, AI serving as a substitute and not a complement to human labor) is unlikely.</li><li>Perhaps with the exception of the lowest-level tasks, AI will merely lead to a simplification of tasks alongside humans, actually improving wages (through improving people’s productivity).</li></ul><h3>Meta</h3><h4>Relevant Notes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most profits from AI have gone to producers of hardware\">Most profits from AI have gone to producers of hardware</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capital&#x27;s share of income will rise in the future due to AI\">Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI will reduce wage inequality through &#x27;simplification&#x27;\">AI will reduce wage inequality through 'simplification'</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Minimum wage drives capital substitution\">Minimum wage drives capital substitution</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a></li></ul><h4>Per Claude Fable:</h4><p>This looks like the s  Economics Society competition, where this exact prompt appears as one of the topic options, with a September 1, 2026 deadline. Notably, the topic list features Christopher Pissarides (the search result references his book, Equilibrium Unemployment Theory, described as a canonical reference in the economics of unemployment), and he is well known for advocating shorter work weeks in response to automation. That tells you something about what the question-setter cares about. Now, your core worry rests on a mistaken premise, so let me push back on it directly: originality in an essay competition is not a novel thesis backed by new data. Nobody expects that from you, and judges know every entrant is drawing on the same literature. Originality here means three things: an original diagnosis, an original combination and prioritization of policies, and original stress-testing of your own position. You can adopt building blocks from Pissarides, Autor, Korinek, whoever, and still write something no one else will write, because the argument architecture is yours.</p><p>Concretely, here is how I would approach it:</p><ol><li>Diagnose before you prescribe. Most entrants will jump straight to a policy shopping list: UBI, retraining, robot taxes. Weak essays list policies; strong essays derive them. Start by asking why inequality rises in this scenario. The mechanism is roughly that AI shifts income from labor to capital: if output is $Y = F(K, L)$ and AI substitutes for labor, the labor share $\\frac{wL}{Y}$ falls, and since capital ownership is far more concentrated than labor endowments, inequality rises even if total output booms. Once you frame it this way, policies sort themselves: wage subsidies treat symptoms, while broadening capital ownership (sovereign wealth funds, employee ownership) or taxing capital income treats the mechanism. That derivation is where you look smart.</li><li>Exploit the clause everyone will ignore. The prompt explicitly asks about inequality in hours worked, and most entrants will treat that as decoration. It is actually the interesting part. A useful reframing: economist Alex Tabarrok recently argued that 40% unemployment and a 3-day work week are, to a first approximation, the same thing, since 60% of people employed full-time equals 100% of people employed at 60% of the hours, and the difference between catastrophe and wonderland comes down to how society distributes the gains. An essay built around the claim that the unemployment problem is an hours-distribution problem, and that policy should convert involuntary unemployment for some into voluntary leisure for all (work-sharing, hours regulation, overtime penalties, portable benefits decoupled from full-time status), would be distinctive and directly responsive to the prompt. There is even empirical grounding for caring about this: research finds that higher economic inequality itself increases working time and worsens work-life balance, operating through status anxiety and competitiveness, which gives you a nice feedback loop to discuss (inequality distorts hours, and maldistributed hours worsen inequality).</li><li>Commit to a thesis and attack it yourself. Do not survey five policies neutrally. Pick a position, e.g. “work-sharing plus capital diffusion beats UBI,” then spend a third of the essay on the strongest objections: lump-of-labor fallacy accusations against work-sharing, incentive effects of capital taxation, political feasibility. An essay that anticipates and answers the best counterargument reads as original even when every component is borrowed.</li><li>Interrogate the prompt’s premises, briefly. The prompt assumes AI displacement leads to unemployment rather than reallocation (history suggests reallocation has usually won so far), and assumes hours inequality is bad (someone voluntarily working 20 hours is different from someone rationed to 20 hours). One sharp paragraph noting these assumptions, then proceeding conditionally, signals rigor without derailing the essay.</li></ol><p>One honest warning: do not fake a formal model you cannot defend. A single well-deployed equation like the labor share point above is fine; a page of half-understood algebra will hurt you more than plain prose. Your comparative advantage at this level is clarity and argumentative discipline, not technique.</p><h3>Outline (1500 Words)</h3><h4>Introduction (~150)</h4><h4>How Inequality Happens (~400)</h4><p>There are multiple theories that seek a causal basis for technological unemployment</p><ul><li>Traditional theories (factor-augmenting models): these theories are essentially based on the assumption that productivity increases uniformly vis-a-vis factors of production (e.g., skilled vs. Unskilled labor).</li><li>Task-based approach: argues that the aforementioned theories are over-simplifications which ignore the actual nature of most innovations, which improve productivity for specific tasks and not for factors.</li></ul><p>For the second idea, the main point is just that tasks are automated faster than they are reinstated. There is also a potential explanation for persistent unemployment, which is the idea of efficiency wages, which is the idea that employers actually typically pay employees above the market-clearing wage in order to maintain morale and keep workers from reducing their productivity during work (shirking). However, combined with AI automation, this could lead to a situation where there is constantly an over-supply of labor given the high wages, despite firms hiring to their maximum.</p><p>It seems that “so-so” productivity increases have been far more common than brilliant technologies that completely reshape economies. It is mostly technologies that produce “so-so” productivity increases that have been causing unemployment.</p><ul><li>But it seems to be that it could be the case that these “so-so” improvements are riding on the coat-tails of new GPTs, and since AI is one such new GPT, across the board, it could have massive effects on productivity. </li><li>I’m not sure how these productivity improvements have been measured (specifically the 3.5 statistic). Look more into this.</li></ul><h4>Some Policy Proposals (~300)</h4><ul><li>Reduce/lower the work week?<ul><li>There is evidence to suggest that more free-time for the worker could potentially improve productivity or something?</li><li>There probably is diminishing marginal returns for continuous working hours. So maybe a better idea would be to retain the 5 day work week, but have shorter hours?<ul><li>What is the empirical research on this?</li></ul></li><li></li></ul></li></ul><h4>Why X is the Best Option (~500)</h4><h4>Conclusion (~150)</h4><h4>Bibliography</h4>","Absurdity can be revealed either by estranging the mundane or by inventing the impossible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are two opposite routes to the same effect: one can show that ordinary life is already strange, or one can construct a world whose strangeness is manifest, and both leave the reader unable to take the familiar for granted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two routes are not equivalent in what they cost. The second requires machinery that the first does without, which bears on how much of the effect is attributable to the insight and how much to the construction.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Camus takes the first route, writing about the tragedy of human existence in the absence of transcendence and finding the absurd in mundane things.</li><li>Kafka takes the second, revealing worlds full of what cannot be understood, which represent transcendence that is impossible rather than merely absent.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>On this criterion Camus is the greater writer, since he does not rely on constructed scenarios to exhibit the absurdity of existence. The obvious objection is that the constructed scenario is doing a different job, namely isolating the structure from the noise of ordinary life, in which case the comparison is unfair.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome\">Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome</a><ul><li>The account of the absurd that both routes are trying to exhibit.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a><ul><li>The response that follows from the first route.</li></ul></li></ul>","Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Absurd most directly manifests through cleavages between two diametrically opposed concepts, which both must, to the human mind, exist in some capacity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the nature of the Absurd.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some examples of such cleavages include:</p><ul><li>The mind &amp; nature vs. Unity.</li><li>The eternal vs. The finite.</li><li>“Concern” vs. The ultimate vanity of one’s actions.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Acting virtuously is not painful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle says that acting virtuously should not be painful (so it should at least be painless), and in many cases, it would actually be pleasurable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an interesting part of Aristotle philosophy because it seems contradictory, as he names some virtuous that are painful.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that the concept of “pain” is somewhat complicated in the case of Aristotle’s ethics. Although not formally stated, there is a rough bifurcation possible when it comes to examining what he means by “pain.”</p><ol><li>Physical pain: as the name suggests.</li><li>Psychological pain: negative mental states, such as distress, sorrow, grief, fear, shame, etc. </li></ol><p>When Aristotle states that virtues are not painful, he is approximately referring to the psychological definition of pain.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>By extension, it means that acting in the mean is not painful.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal\">The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal</a><ul><li>Inferentially, the process of attaining a goal of acting moral would not be painful.</li></ul></li></ul>","Activist organizations often shift priorities in order to self-perpetuate":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Often times, activist organizations will switch focus after their initial stated goals have been competed in order to continue functioning. This often leads such groups to be detrimental overall to society, rather than helping society.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that often times, these organizations can stray away from their original goals and support causes that may actually be harmful.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is known as the “March of Dimes” syndrome. Many activist groups in history, once they have successfully achieved their goals, shifted to oftentimes even counterproductive goals in order to continue operating.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><strong>The March of Dimes itself</strong> — Founded to combat polio, it achieved its goal through vaccines in the 1950s but kept going by shifting its mission to preventing birth defects.</li><li><strong>Antiwar activists turning antinuclear</strong> — Veterans of the Vietnam War protest movement pivoted to leading the anti-nuclear power movement after the Three Mile Island accident in 1979, with figures like Jane Fonda and Tom Hayden at the forefront.</li><li><strong>The LGBTQ+ rights movement</strong> — After achieving major wins (decriminalization of homosexuality, same-sex marriage legalization in 2015, civil rights protections in 2020), activists declared a \"national state of emergency\" in 2023 and pushed for a range of new and more controversial causes.</li><li><strong>The civil rights movement / NAACP</strong> — After landmark legislative victories (Civil Rights Act of 1964, Voting Rights Act of 1965), the movement shifted from demanding equality to demanding affirmative action, reparations, and other causes that contradicted its original integration-focused mission.</li><li><strong>The Southern Poverty Law Center (SPLC)</strong> — Launched to represent individual discrimination victims, it pivoted to suing the KKK, then to publishing expanding lists of \"hate groups\" that critics say include mainstream conservative organizations, all as a fund-raising strategy.</li><li><strong>Black Lives Matter</strong> — Emerged and grew into a massive movement despite the rarity of the specific type of deaths it ostensibly addressed, and critics argue its antipolice posture contributed to more Black deaths via the \"Ferguson Effect.\"</li><li><strong>The #MeToo movement</strong> — Rose to prominence during a period when rates of sexual violence had actually fallen nearly 60% (from 1995 to 2010), and came after corporate America had already instituted strict harassment protections.</li><li><strong>Antismoking activists opposing vaping</strong> — After smoking rates dropped sharply thanks to nicotine vaping, antismoking groups like the Campaign for Tobacco-Free Kids manufactured scares about vaping to maintain relevance and funding, despite vaping being widely recognized as far less harmful than tobacco.</li><li><strong>Environmental/climate activists</strong> — After previous doomsday predictions (overpopulation, energy scarcity, a synthetic-chemical \"cancer epidemic\") proved false, green activists escalated to claiming humanity faces an \"existential threat\" from climate change.</li><li><strong>Jane Fonda specifically</strong> — Highlighted as a special case (\"the March of Jane syndrome\"): she helped kill the nuclear power industry through activism, which caused a switch to coal and increased carbon emissions — and then became a leading climate activist fighting the very problem her earlier activism helped create.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In scientific research, the use of agentic AI has the potential to radically speed up the rate of scientific research, leading to more breakthroughs in far less time. AI does not only need to be used as a tool for merely analyzing data.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This use of AI could potentially lead to radical development of a huge amount of scientific innovations that could have huge upside for humanity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>AI would be especially useful for fields with high marginal returns to intelligence, since this is the area in which AI excels. What’s more, once the supply of researchers increases, the marginal cost of research decreases, meaning that agents will be able to pursue research into research with ostensibly low expected value, which could have high potential upsides.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a><ul><li>Perhaps AI research could solve this problem? They could potentially be able to coordinate research better.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)\">The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)</a><ul><li>AI researchers could also potentially mitigate this?</li></ul></li></ul>","Aggregating many imperfect judgments can outperform the judgment of experts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If each member of a group is more likely than not to judge correctly and judges independently, the probability that the majority is right approaches certainty as the group grows, which is the formal core of the case for democratic over expert decision.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the strongest epistemic argument against rule by the competent, since it grants the premise that competence matters and still favours the crowd.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Three replies are available to the defender of expert rule. One may deny that the model applies, since real electorates are neither independent nor reliably better than chance. One may concede the theorem and argue that a competence-weighted electorate captures the same aggregation benefit without the noise. Or one may argue that weighting the more informed makes the aggregate converge faster, so the theorem supports rule by the competent rather than undermining it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism\">A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>A different result from the same tradition, concerning which outcome is selected rather than how reliable it is.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people\">Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people</a><ul><li>The claim that this argument is meant to answer.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Plural voting admits no unique assignment of votes\">Plural voting admits no unique assignment of votes</a><ul><li>A defect in the competence-weighting reply.</li></ul></li></ul>","All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All ethical theories, despite their justification, are ultimately justified by their consequence (their teleology).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is what Mill contends, and one of his supporting arguments for utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Even though theories might claim that their ethical principles are derived out of some first principles unrelated to the outcome of those principles, ultimately, it is the outcome of those principles that lead the first principles to be established in the first place.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>This is a potential counterargument to this idea.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Fundamentally, the purpose of this politcal/ethical system is to maximize some desired end.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","All humans are innately evil":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Humans are innately evil, and the often accompanying claim that this evil must then rectified in some way.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a philosophy thesis for many different philosophies across the world and through time, most prominently, Hobbes.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This fact is often cited when justifying a powerful state or otherwise some kind of cultural force which would be able to rectify this issue inherent in humans. For instance, this is the fundamental axiom Hobbes employs in order to justify his Leviathan. Between the Confucianist schools and Legalist schools, there is a persistent tension between a sort of idealism regarding human nature and a stark realism. Confucianism argues for the moral uplifting of all, such that all would be able to harmoniously live among each other, while the Legalist school argues for the lowering of the moral standard and ruling people merely through totalitarian laws and regulations.</p><h5>Xunzi</h5><p>Argued that people are born evil, but that they are use their intellects in order to bring themselves out of their evil and into goodness. Specifically, through <em>li</em>, people are instructed to do the right things. Then, they are able to steer themselves towards what is right using their intellect. Eventually, through repeated practice, one becomes virtuous.</p><h5>Han Feizi</h5><p>Believed that while people are inherently evil, unlike Xunzi, that there is no mechanism for “saving” people. The only method, then, of running society, is to imbue a monarch with absolute authority and have them rule fairly and competently the rest of the population.</p><ul><li>The Taoists object to this, arguing that Legalism does not tell you how to become this ruler who is perfectly unselfish and impartial.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a><ul><li>This is the opposite thesis.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This is analogous to what Xunzi says.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>In both cases, it is existing morality, the formal guidelines (but especially in the case of the state) that does the teaching, analogous to the idea of <em>li</em>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All the King&#x27;s Men\">All the King's Men</a><ul><li>Referenced here.</li></ul></li></ul>","All humans are innately good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that when we are first born, we are naturally good, or at least that there are good elements within us. According to Rousseau, it is rather society which corrupts us and makes us capable of doing evil things.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for many different philosophies, for example, Rousseau’s ideas. This also relates to the thought of some Eastern philosophers, for instance, Mencius.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mill claims that humans innately want to act in ways which are beneficial to others (in the absence of external reasons).</p><h5>Confucianism &amp; Neo-Confucianism</h5><p>Mencius also argued for this, saying that people are born naturally with good elements and neutral elements.</p><ul><li>He developed the idea of the four cardinal/constant virtues, which originate as the “four beginnings”:<ul><li>The feeling of commiseration is the beginning of benevolence or human-heartedness.</li><li>The feeling of shame and dislike is the beginning of righteousness.</li><li>The feeling of modesty and yielding is the beginning of propriety.</li><li>The feeling of right ant and wrong is the beginning of wisdom. </li></ul></li></ul><p>Neo-Confucianist theory, especially from the <strong>School of Principle</strong>, argues that one is born with the Supreme Ultimate, and it is through a careful “investigation of things to extend knowledge” (格物致知) that we are able to find the Supreme Ultimate within ourselves and thus become a sage.</p><ul><li>This process involves investigating the things (<em>qi</em>) first, and then, though that process, one is then able to understand that which exists in the eternal world, the <em>li</em>.</li><li>Additionally, this enlightenment requires “attentiveness of mind” (用敬), in order to accomplish, since otherwise this process becomes merely intellectual.</li></ul><p>In the <strong>School of Mind</strong>, it is said that the individual possess <em>liangzhi</em> (良知), which is a universally shared, innate sense of morality. The goal of the individual then, is to act uninterfered by selfish desire, which my obscure the intentions of the <em>liangzhi</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a><ul><li>This involves the belief that humans were at least originally born good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are born as blank slates, gaining knowledge a posteriori\">Humans are born as blank slates, gaining knowledge a posteriori</a><ul><li>It seems that if humans are born as blank slates, they must be either naturally predisposed to be neutral or good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral truths arise internally and naturally\">Moral truths arise internally and naturally</a><ul><li>This is the view of the School of Mind.</li></ul></li></ul>","All philosophy reduces to metaphysics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that any philosophical debate is fundamentally a metaphysical debate.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this were the case, then we could essentially simplify any debate down to the level of metaphysics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For instance, debates such as the nature of morality can often be regarded merely as debates about what is counts as a “thing” or the relatively ontological statuses of things which make them ethically different. Even claims in metaphysics such as “what is art” seems to be fundamentally a question of ontology, rather than a question of what “aesthetically” (whatever that means) counts as art. In logic, different logical systems (classical, modal), seem all to have different metaphysics assumptions which is what fundamentally makes them different. A weaker version of this claim, which seems to be better generally supported is the idea that all philosophy makes metaphysical assumptions, and therefore, it is the fundamental branch of philosophy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a><ul><li>This is another example?</li></ul></li></ul>","All the King's Men":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Jack Burden, a cynical journalist who becomes the aide and confidant of Willie Stark, an ambitious politician who rises from a small-town reformer to the powerful governor of a Southern state. As Stark gains influence, he becomes increasingly corrupt, using manipulation, intimidation, and political deals to achieve his goals, while Jack investigates the secrets and scandals of those around him. Jack’s search into the past of Judge Irwin, a respected public official, reveals hidden connections and forces him to confront his own family history and moral responsibility. After Stark’s actions lead to tragedy and betrayal, both he and Jack face the consequences of their choices. The novel explores power, corruption, guilt, ambition, and the idea that everyone is connected to the past.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A book the explores politics in an extremely in-depth way and which provides a close look into the philosophical underpinnings of populism.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Power corrupts those who possess it\">Power corrupts those who possess it</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos\">People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People sin so that they may be loved\">People sin so that they may be loved</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are a product of their times and circumstance\">People are a product of their times and circumstance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a></li></ul><h4>Questions</h4><ul><li>Is Adam Stanton a good person? The book presents us with a dilemma: is someone’s moral stance improved more by the nature of their actions, or the final result? Do the ends justify the means?</li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Willie Stark</strong></p><ul><li>One seems to be able to divide his life into multiple stages:<ol><li><em>Ex ante</em>: he was innocent and his worldview was limited. In a stroke of luck, through family connections, he becomes the town treasurer. He, however, loses the school house fight and his political career sinks.</li><li>Enlightenment: after he is vindicated on the school house issue and receiving Tiny Duffy’s offer, Stark gained ambition. He was genuinely invested with a divine calling and tried to spread his message to the voters, though the masses did not care. They were unable to see the light which shone so brightly in Stark’s mind. It is ultimately due to Burden and Burke that Stark regains his footing.</li><li>Post-enlightenment: his fantastical worldview is shattered after he learns that the reason for his divine calling was merely to split the vote of another political candidate. He gains a sense of stubbornness and a will that turns him into a Machiavellian character who is willing to do anything in order to, somewhat ironically, serve the public good.<ul><li>This is a worldview that he advocates to his disheartened and apathetic consituency.</li></ul></li></ol></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 133</p><p>This is the truth: you are a hick and nobody ever helped a hick but the hick himself. Up there in town they won’t help you. <strong>It is up to you and God, and God helps those who help themselves.</strong></p></blockquote><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 175</p><p>“He's interested in Willie. Quite simply and directly. <strong>And when anybody is interested in himself quite simply and directly the way Willie is interested in Willie you call it genius.</strong> It’s only the half-baked people like Mr. Patton who are interested in money Even the big boys who make a real lot of money aren’t interested in money. Henry Ford isn't interested in money. He's interested in Henry Ford and therefore he is a genius.”</p></blockquote><p><strong>Jack Burden</strong></p><ul><li>Is able to see the situation in a more objective light than Stark. He begins to see that Stark’s quest is fundamentally rudderless and empty in the middle.<ul><li>In his “first excursion,” he looked naively for some sense in the world, some justice, but he found none. The facts, he realized, are much more important than the (moral) truth.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 217</p><p>It was to be my second excursion into the past, more interesting and sensational than the first, and much more successful. In fact, this second excursion into the past was to be perfectly successful. But the first one had not been successful. It had not been successful because <strong>in the midst of the process I tried to discover the truth and not the facts.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>It seems like at the end of college, Burden went through a period of his life characterized by the same listlessness that characterized period’s of Stark’s life, and he eventually found a purpose (his own divine calling) in working for Stark.<ul><li>It seems like maybe he is fed up with “thinking”? He often makes rhetorical, equivocational arguments which seem merely to look like arguments. Often, he does not even think out loud. Burden seems to be the embodiment of a disillusioned academic.</li><li>He finds Stark’s searching for the truth in his “heart” to be a welcome change to his laborious academic methods.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 139</p><p>For God and Nothing have a lot in common. <strong>You look either one of Them straight in the eye for a second and the immediate effect on the human constitution is the same.</strong></p></blockquote><p><strong>Adam Stanton</strong></p><ul><li>Wants to be someone who is morally in the right every time. He does not care about the outcome of something as much as that the process is done in a way that is morally acceptable.<ul><li>This is the reason he has so much reluctance when Stark asked him to head his hospital.</li><li>But I think the fact that Adam eventually joined the hospital I think in a way undermines his character: he joins the hospital once he finds out about his father taking a bribe, since at that point, there is no more purpose to maintaining his moral purity in the same way.</li><li>But if this is the case, I feel like his original moral principles were not worth holding on to in the first place. It seems like he did not believe them <em>for themselves</em>, but rather, as a heuristic for outward action. Why should it matter to him that his father took a bribe? It’s something that’s already been done, so there is no reason for him to let that disturb his moral principles.</li></ul></li><li>Adam values keeping himself in a morally pure world. This is the circumstance under which he refuses to do things for a utilitarian benefit.</li><li>Jack Burden explicates Adam’s flaws most succintly here:</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, p. 598</p><p>I tried to tell her how if you could not accept the past and its burden there was no future, for without one there cannot be the other, and how if you could accept the past you might hope for the future, for only out of the past can you make the future.</p></blockquote><h4>Note</h4><ul><li>“Love” seems to be entirely absent from the book. There is no relationship between any of the characters which can, genuinely, be categorized as a relationship built on love. All relationships are <em>quid pro quo</em> or merely out of habit.<ul><li>This changes later in the book, with the two true “loves” between Jack and Anne and Jack’s mother and Judge Irwin it seems.</li><li>In this, even, I think the book is making a commentary on the nature of love. Jack often states the importance of truth, and how truth underlies everything, but it seems that love is the one that that can muddle truth.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 50</p><p>All he wants is to have that child sit in a chair for a couple of hours and then go off to bed under the same roof. It’s not love. I am not saying that there is not such a thing as love. I am merely pointing to something which is different from love but which sometimes goes by the name of love. It may well be that without this thing which I am talking about there would not be any love. <strong>But this thing in itself is not love. It is just something in the blood. It is a kind of blood greed, and it is the fate of a man.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Willie Stark and Adam Stanton represents the two opposite ways of dealing with the existence of evil in the world. Stark channels it in order to do good, and Stanton shys away from it.<ul><li>When the two inevitably butt heads, the result is destruction.</li><li>And it seems that both methods have their good sides and bad sides. <ul><li>Willie: although he is able to do good in his state, to do so, he often had to resort to very coercive or ignoble actions. By doing things in this reckless manner, Stark lost his son, and eventually his own life.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Message of the Book?</p><p>It seems that what the book is trying to say is that both types of men are needed in the world. With only Willie Starks, the world would combust under the multitudinous, bull-headed pressure, exerted from all sides and with no regards to collateral damage. With only Adam Stantons, nothing impressive will ever get done.</p></blockquote><ul><li>All the characters in the book are single minded in what they want. Once they find a goal, they stop questioning whether its the right thing the do, instead, solely focusing on achieving the desired outcome.</li><li>At its core, this book seems to be some form of a critique of post-modernism, showing the chaos and psychological instability that it engenders.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All The King’s Men</em>, p. 541</p><p>More money for graft, the opposition always screamed. \"Sure,' the Boss had said, lounging easy, “<strong>sure, there's some graft, but there's just enough to make the wheels turn without squeaking. And remember this. There never was a machine rigged up by man didn't represent some loss of energy.</strong>”</p></blockquote><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, p. 485</p><p>For either killing or creating may be a crime punishable by death, and the death always comes by the criminal's own hand and every man is a suicide.</p></blockquote><ul><li>This is how everybody in the book died: Stark, Irwin, Adam Stanton, Mortimer, etc.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>Touches on similar themes regarding fate and epistemology.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"East of Eden\">East of Eden</a><ul><li>This book almost seems like the opposite of East of Eden, not in terms of messages (for it seems that both books are fundamentally getting at the same thing), but one is told with the Yang and the other the Ying and the their message is the one and the same, and that constitutes the Ying and the Yang.</li></ul></li></ul>","All things are one and the same":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core principle of Hui Shi’s (惠施) branch of the School of Names, the idea that since all differences between things are merely relative, we cannot objective state there to be any differences between things, and thus, we must love all things equally. A similar concept of <em>qi</em> appears in Neo-Confucianist thought.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was one the main schools of the School of Names and the Neo-Confucianist school.</p><h4>School of Names</h4><p>Hui Shi gives the following example that what is big must be big in relation to something else, and what is small must be small in relation to something else. Therefore, we can say that a mountain and a grain of sand are the same size, since relative to the massiveness of the cosmos, they are both insignificant.</p><h4>Neo-Confucianism</h4><p>Their understanding, explicates by people such as Zhang Zai (张载) of the <strong>School of Principle</strong>, is that the universe is made of <em>qi</em>. Therefore, since all things are made of one underlying substance, this metaphysical unity must constitute a commensurate moral unity. Since everything is ultimately “part of one body,” harming any part of the body is equivalent to harming the self. The concept of 生无所得 is likewise related to this. He argues that there is two types of <em>qi</em>:</p><ul><li><strong>Dispersed</strong>: this manifests as the formless Great Void.</li><li><strong>Condensed</strong>: these are the particular things in the world, including humans.</li></ul><p>When we die, we merely transform from the latter to the former. In life, we follow and serve as per the Confucian doctrine, and then we simply die.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">《西铭》（亦作《正蒙·乾称篇》）</p><p>存，吾顺事；没，吾宁也。 In life I follow and serve; in death I am at peace.</p></blockquote><p>The School of Mind claims unity in the world rather through the concept that the <em>li</em> is something that is universal and in fact permeates all of existence. At its deepest level, the same <em>li</em> that constitutes one’s innate moral nature is the universal pattern/order of reality; this is the reason that one’s spontaneous moral concern can extend outward.</p><ul><li>The sage is the one who is ultimately able to understand the unity of self and the rest of reality: realizing metaphysical unity produces benevolence.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a><ul><li>In this way, the School of Names derives the same principle through a different argument.</li></ul></li></ul>","All things in the universe are manifestations of the mind and thus illusory and impermanent":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All things that we believe exist in the universe are in fact only objects of the mind, and thus illusory and impermanent. Therefore, we must rid ourselves of worldly desire.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the central plank of Buddhism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The desires that people have manifest in <em>karma</em>, which causes one to be trapped in the eternal recurring cycle of existence (<em>samsara</em>). All the different schools of Buddhism are essentially trying to solve the fundamental problem of how to bring someone out of a worldly state and achieve <em>nirvana</em>, or enlightenment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many aspects of Buddhism derive from Hinduism\">Many aspects of Buddhism derive from Hinduism</a><ul><li>Regarding the connection between the Buddhism and Hinduism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although humans have free will, our very fundamental “wants/wills” are something that transcends the individual, and is something that ultimately expresses a universal purpose that serves to drive history forward according to divine will.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Hegel’s view of the nature of history, and man’s place in it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Although Caesar and Napoleon had the ability to do what they want, their actions remains, in its totality, in a divine system. Hegel terms this the <em>cunning of reason</em>.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that the justification for the existence of the cunning of reason is very weak. It is true that Caesar and Napoleon could not have known why they were doing what they were doing (i.e., they very underlying motivations) nor their impact on history. However, the evidence in support of the cunning of reason seems just as well to support fate or chance. In other words, the diagnosticity of the data in question is negligible.</p><h4>References</h4>","American democracy is too populist":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>American democracy is too populist, focusing too much on “democracy,” and not enough on constitutional liberalism and the overall wellbeing of the nation.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>This idea presents an interesting critique of American democracy that presents its “democracticness” in a more nuanced view.</p><h4>Rising Populism</h4><p>American politicians are catering to the masses too much, and ignoring the actual needs of the nation and constitutional liberalism.</p>","American white males are underrepresented in academia and media in lower-level positions, and overrepresented in upper level positions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Lower-level positions are increasingly being dominated by minorities and women, while upper-level positions remain dominated by white males, a trend that emerged around 2014. This was the result of established leaders protecting their own status while sacrificing the prospects of younger men to meet diversity quotas.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>As a result, many qualified professionals left their fields or remain stuck in lower-level positions.</p><h4>Examples</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Jacob Savage, <em>The Lost Generation</em></p><p>At <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1LVwbrfB8qcm0SCwaLrBzlZhCcsnbT0VM/view?usp=sharing&amp;ref=compactmag.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Berkeley</a>, as recently as 2015, white male hires were 52.7 percent of new tenure-track faculty; in 2023, they were 21.5 percent. UC <a href=\"https://ap.uci.edu/new-faculty-appointments/?ref=compactmag.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Irvine</a> has hired 64 tenure-track assistant professors in the humanities and social sciences since 2020. Just three (4.7 percent) are white men. Of the 59 Assistant Professors in Arts, Humanities and Social Science appointed at UC Santa Cruz between 2020-2024, only two were white men (3 percent).</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","Amusement is pleasant and choice-worthy, though it is not happiness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Play, games, joking, or other forms of recreation or rest is considered to be amusement, which is choice-worthy and pleasant, but it is not the happiness that one seeks.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This distinguishes amusements from happiness, which is an important distinction to make.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Amusements are not an end; rather they are a sort of means to provide relieve from exertion for ourselves so that we may work.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>The end goal of life is not amusement, and it is not something that one should consider an end at all.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a><ul><li>Rather, the contemplative life is true happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An act is compulsory (otherwise known as \"involuntary\") when the cause of the action is external, i.e., what is compelling the agent to conduct the action does not originate in the agent. Another possibility is that the agent acts out of ignorance, but they are remorseful after the fact.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s definition for what is a compulsory action. He will use this definition in order to justify many aspects of his philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>An act is involuntary if</p><ol><li>They were coerced into committing that action by an external force.</li><li>They acted out of ignorance (of the particular, not of the principle), and they repent for their actions after the fact.</li></ol><p>The agent is never morally responsible for their action if they act involuntarily.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>This is the opposite of the type of action described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>If an action is involuntary, it can never have a quality of virtuousness.</li></ul></li></ul>","An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An act is considered to be “non-voluntary” when an agent acts out of ignorance, but does not feel regret for the action afterwards.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The distinction between “non-voluntary” actions and “involuntary” actions are slim, and this distinction made by Aristotle is often confused.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A non-voluntary action would be if, for example, an agent were to step on someone else’s toes without realizing it, but then showing no reaction at all. Aristotle considers this to be neither morally repugnant (since the agent did not commit the action willingly), but nor does he consider such an action morally praiseworthy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>On the other hand, an action is praise worthy or blameworthy if it is done voluntarily (and if the action concerns virtue or vice).</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a><ul><li>An action is never praise worthy nor blameworthy when it id done involuntarily.</li></ul></li></ul>","An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An action is defined as being voluntary when the agent chooses to engage with the action, with this motivation originating internally. Additionally, even if the final actions are involuntary/compulsory, if it was voluntary action that lead to the involuntary/compulsory action, then the agent is still at fault.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This lays out Aristotle’s definition for when an action is voluntary, which is the other side of the equation (the other side being when an action is compulsory) when determining moral responsibility.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two specific conditions required for an action to be considered voluntary:</p><ul><li>One is acting of one’s own volition.</li><li>One understands the circumstances and particulars of the action that one wishes to engage in.</li></ul><p>Given that, there are also two possible variations for the voluntary action.</p><ol><li>You choose between chocolate ice cream and vanilla ice cream.</li><li>You choose to help a drowning child.</li></ol><p>The difference between the two is that only the second is within the moral domain. Thus, only the second of the two actions can be considered praiseworthy or blameworthy.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Actions that are the result of desire or temper are considered voluntary, since our disposition is something that we can control, and our disposition is the reason for certain desires or tempers. Additionally, moral responsibility tracks from motivation. For instance, licentiousness is more voluntary than cowardice since pain distracts the sufferers, and makes their actions less voluntary. In this way, the person who is more cowardly (in pain) is less responsible for the licentious person.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>In order for the agent to act in a virtuous state, they must act of their own volition, i.e., they must act voluntarily.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a><ul><li>The exact opposite of a voluntary action.</li></ul></li></ul>","An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even if every action is causally determined, the agent may still be responsible for it, because what determines the action is the agent’s own nature, and the agent’s nature is what praise and blame are directed at.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the oldest compatibilist move available, and it relocates responsibility from the moment of choice to the standing character that the choice reveals.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Chrysippus holds the view in this form: one does not control the action, but one is the kind of person whose actions those are, and that is sufficient for the action to be attributable.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>The position this rejects.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive\">Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive</a><ul><li>The same conclusion reached by a different route.</li></ul></li></ul>","An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A conceit sustains one comparison over many scenes rather than exhausting it in a line, which lets a work develop an idea structurally instead of stating it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the device by which a work argues without asserting, and it is what distinguishes a controlling image from ordinary figurative language.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><em>Hamlet</em> is built on several, including the recurring images of disease and rot in the state, which return in different mouths and different scenes without ever being named as the play’s subject.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The general criterion of which the conceit is one technique.</li></ul></li></ul>","An ignorant action is only excusable when it represents an ignorance of the particular, not of the universal or of voluntary ignorance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An action that is the result of ignorance is only excusable given that the ignorant action regarding something particular, not when it is universal and not when it is voluntary ignorance, regardless of the type of ignorance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle defines clearly the types of ignorance and whether they are considered morally repugnant.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Ignorance of the particular: when you shoot a rabbit, but it turns out the rabbit was actually someone’s foot. You are distressed by your actions, and you repent. Here, you were ignorant regarding the particular situation, though you are not ignorant of the universal, i.e., that shooting someone is bad.</li><li>Ignorance of the universal: you shoot someone because you don’t like the way they look. In this case, you are ignorant of the universal moral principle at play.</li><li>Voluntary ignorance: you voluntarily got drunk, and then killed someone in a fight. Here, you could have chosen not to get drunk, so the consequences of the action falls on the you.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards\">An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards</a><ul><li>The ignorance being referred to here is ignorance of the particular.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a><ul><li>The ignorance being referred to here is also ignorance of the particular.</li></ul></li></ul>","An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Ceteris paribus, an ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic. This would maximize their revenue, ignoring any costs that act as a function of quantity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant as this explains why the ordinary monopolist set the price where the demand curve is unit elastic, which explains why there is often a shortage and higher prices in markets a monopoly.</p><h4>Proof</h4><p>Let the revenue function for some monopolist be defined as such: $R(p)=p \\cdot q(p)$, where $p$ represents the price and $q(p)$ represents the quantity supplied as a function of price. To find the price at which the monopolist maximizes profits, then, let us find where $R'(p)=0$. $$ R'(p)=pq'(p) \\cdot q(p) $$ $$ 0 = 1 + \\frac{p}{q(p)}q'(p) $$ The the point-slope definition of elasticity ($\\epsilon$) is $\\frac{p}{q} \\cdot \\frac{dq}{dp}$. Thus, where monopolistic revenue maximization occurs is at $$\\epsilon = -1$$</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points\">The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points</a></li></ul>","An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An unrealized gains tax could force people to sell of assets just in order to pay their tax, and in cases where the asset is illiquid, this could lead to a distortion of the actual value of the asset, meaning that in the long run, investment may be weakened. Additionally, if an asset is overvalued, it may be overtaxed, and if undervalued, undertaxed. Having to valuate the asset also introduces transactions costs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant arguments against an unrealized gains tax, as it would distort the financial markets and cause a decrease in investment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a></li></ul>","Animalism regarding identity states that humans are merely biological animals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that humans are nothing but the biological material that make then up. If the physical body is destroyed, the self is likewise destroyed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant views in personal identity.</p><h4>References</h4>","Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A consumption tax can be made progressive if it is rebated in a progressive manner. Usually, consumption taxes are regressive, since low-income people have a higher MPC, meaning that they would proportionally be taxed more. This can be ameliorated with a progressive rebate system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds complexity to the idea that consumption taxes are totally regressive, since they could be made progressive with the right rebate system.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A revenue neutral carbon tax could be tax at, for example, 10%. With this in place, a low-income individual pays $100 in taxes, while a wealthier people might pay $1000. After taxes are levied, each individual receives $500. This makes the tax progressive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>Describes the nature of the tax system in the US, related the content of this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In the US, direct state expenditures are often &#x27;submerged&#x27; through indirect tax breaks\">In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks</a><ul><li>Describes the nature of the US tax system, and how expenditures are often hidden through tax breaks.</li></ul></li></ul>","Arguments for kingship must collapse to arguments for aristocracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Arguments for kingship are, in fact, better satisfied when viewed through the lens of aristocracies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the argument Aristotle employs in order to reject arguments for kingship.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The main argument for kingship is that the rule of law is context-insensitive, and therefore, is unable to make the best decision for the common good. Accepting that this is true, in order to act in the good interest then, the king must be virtuous. But surely, the judgement of many virtuous people combined is better than the judgement of a single virtuous person? Not only that, a plurality is more incorruptible than a single person. Therefore, aristocracy is preferable to kingship.</p><h4>References</h4>","As history progresses, morality advances as well":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that there is moral progress in the world, and that we are fundamentally moving towards some ethical apex whereby humans will have uncovered the fundamental ethical “truths” extant in the universe.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that is often assumed in contemporary discussions of the nature of morality, especially in the context of past moral wrongs.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that this view has heavy metaphysical implications, since it requires that there is in fact some indisputable moral truths and that we have in fact achieved moral progress towards it. It necessitates a response to several questions:</p><ul><li>How are we orienting our moral compasses correctly?</li><li>Why were humans originally developed in a state of moral ignorance?</li></ul><p>It seems that this idea is inextricable tried up in Christian theology, and the corresponding ideas of Original Sin and humanity’s progress towards God.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>This seems however to be a departure from Christian morality, since most moral progress as been associated with rebellion towards authority and status quo.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>This note is an example of this?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>Would capitalism be the force that holds back moral progress, or does capitalism stand as a testament against the very existence of some necessary moral truths?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is impossible to construct a universal theory of morality for liberalism\">It is impossible to construct a universal theory of morality for liberalism</a><ul><li>It seems then that the idea of moral progress is incompatible with liberalism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ethics is grounded in religion\">Ethics is grounded in religion</a><ul><li>Related to the Christian morality thesis presented here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological\">All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological</a><ul><li>Maybe this is a hint of the ultimate morality.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a><ul><li>Related claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a></li></ul>","As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the modernization thesis, associated with liberal political theory which states that nations generally converge on liberal democracy when they become more prosperous. This thesis has been near conclusively disproven.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was a major hypothesis for a while in political science and international relations, though its since been rejected since there are powerful counterexamples.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Examples that go against this thesis include:</p><ul><li>China</li><li>Singapore</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade brings people into contact, not agreement\">Trade brings people into contact, not agreement</a><ul><li>Trade, which tends to enrich the people, doesn’t necessarily bring them into agreement, just contact.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability\">Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability</a><ul><li>The opposite of the previous claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>It seems that this note is evidence against this claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a><ul><li>Generally, economic freedom is required for people to become rich. However, it seems like there are cases where even though economic freedom has been achieved, political freedom has not.</li></ul></li></ul>","Attaching ancillary requirements to a project prevents it from delivering its core objective":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When every public project is required to serve procurement rules, labour standards, environmental review and local consultation at once, the accumulated conditions make the primary objective unreachable, so the programme delivers none of its aims rather than most of them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This identifies a failure mode that does not show up in any individual decision, since each requirement is defensible on its own and only the stack is fatal.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Klein advances it as a case for supply-side progressivism, which keeps the ends of the political left and abandons the practice of loading each instrument with the whole programme.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>Why the requirements cannot be relaxed case by case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people&#x27;s misunderstanding of what causes the issue\">The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people's misunderstanding of what causes the issue</a><ul><li>The clearest domain in which this occurs.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>The general pattern of which this is an instance.</li></ul></li></ul>","Authoritarian & non-democratic nations likely overstate their GDP growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Satellite night light data shows that authoritarian or non-democratic nations overstate their GDP growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><strong>Autocracy Gradient</strong>: As a country becomes more authoritarian, the same increase in nightlight level translates into higher levels of GDP growth.<ul><li>The gradient is larger before an election, or when growth is low.</li></ul></li><li>GDP growth in authoritarian nations are typically overstated by a factor of 1.15 to 1.3.<ul><li>Over the long term, this represents a 40% inflation of GDP.</li></ul></li><li>China is a major example of this case.<ul><li>Even beyond analysis with nightlights, analysis of GDP growth through the construction of an Engels curve showed the curve shifting over time, indicating overstating and understating inflation by the government over time.<ul><li>The Engel Curve analysis showed the growth was actually underestimated in the 90s, but over estimated from the 2000s onwards. </li><li>In total, the Engel-Adjusted real growth of the Chinese economy has fallen below the official growth reported by the Chinese government, in largely part to the fall in growth during the 2008-2009 crisis.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing\">GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing</a><ul><li>It can be manipulated, for instance.</li></ul></li></ul>","Autonomous vehicles will lead to a decentralization of cities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Autonomous vehicles (AVs), like all previous technological innovations that have made transportation more efficient, will lead to a further decentralization of cities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>While some people say that AVs will lead to further centralization of cities, this note argues the opposite.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some have pointed to the historical occurrence whereby while cars decentralized urban areas, trains served to centralized urban areas. This argument points to the fact that this fact is complicated:</p><ul><li>The railway centralization supposedly occurred during the 19th century, when railroads were constructed in greater numbers, but the reason that centralization increased is that the people who were brought in to work in factors did not earn enough to live outside the city, while those who did earn enough left behind living in the city.</li></ul><p>Another assumption that is made in the claim that AVs will increase centralization is that the entertainment centers are concentrated in the city center, which, while it remains true for some older American cities, is increasingly less the case.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained\">Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained</a><ul><li>Decentralized cities might develop better than centralized ones? Although the type of centralized that is referred to here is geographic, and not political.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities\">Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities</a><ul><li>AVs would not do the same.</li></ul></li></ul>","Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that average utilitarianism is more justifiable to rational agents compared to classical utilitarianism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a prominent advantage that average utilitarianism has over classical utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The main feature of classical utilitarianism that makes it less justifiable to the rational agent than average utilitarianism is the population size problem. Under classical utilitarianism, adding more people below the average utility increases overall welfare. In fact, a society with a massive amount of miserable people with just-above-zero utility could be better, in the eyes of the classical utilitarian, than a world with 100 people of substantially higher utility. Also, from the original position, the result of the principle of insufficient reason mathematical derivation is exactly the same as the utility predicted through average utilitarianism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Still, the difference principle is better than average utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Bad Samaritans (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This book discusses the many downsides to the neoliberal world order that has been established.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important book that counters the traditional, mainstream economic view of neoliberal global policy.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a></li></ul>","Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>On the basic desert conception, an agent deserves blame simply in virtue of having performed the act, independently of whether blaming produces any further good such as deterrence or reform.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the conception of desert that the free will debate is actually about, since consequentialist justifications of blame survive determinism untouched.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The conception carries a strong control requirement: the act must have been up to the agent in some robust sense, because otherwise there is nothing about the agent that the blame is tracking.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some people must deserve something\">Some people must deserve something</a><ul><li>The pre-theoretical intuition that basic desert formalises.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Retributive punishment is never justified\">Retributive punishment is never justified</a><ul><li>Rejects the practical upshot of basic desert.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility\">Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility</a><ul><li>Why this notion sits at the centre of the debate.</li></ul></li></ul>","Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Baumol’s Cost Disease, which is when high productivity sectors force a rise in wages of lower productivity sectors, can slow economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main factors that when new innovations appear which can galvanize the economy, they impact is often muted, as costs are rising across the board.</p><h4>Effects</h4><p>For example, labor will shift from highly progressive (high increases in labor productivity) sectors to stagnant sectors (low increases in labor productivity).  The Baumol Effect has a large effect on sectors such as the performance arts, healthcare, or education, where there isn’t a huge amount is innovation/little efficiency gains. It seems that the effect of this wage equalization would over power the depressed wages that would otherwise result from the influx of labor into low-productivity sectors, since the overall effect is one of wage increase (despite the increased labor leaving high-productivity sectors for low-productivity sectors.)</p><ul><li>I suppose this is an empirical question, since the reason that low productivity sectors would raise wages is due to the fact that workers would otherwise leave for sectors with higher wages, but since there is an influx of new workers, it seems that this effect would be weakened substantially, if not reversed. </li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth\">A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth</a></li></ul>","Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People are resistance to AI largely because AI is moralized and presented as something that is morally reprehensible, even though it would have concrete benefits for people.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could explain why there is so much public resistant to AI despite AI having proven benefits for people.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The conclusion in this note was drawn based:</p><ul><li>Analysis of new sources covering AI, which showed a similar level of moralization as other controversial topics like GMOs and vaccines.</li><li>Surveys which show that people would not use AI even if it produced concrete benefits for them.</li><li>On when people were given tasks to do, they would not use AI, even when it would have aided them.</li></ul><p>There are several reasons why this resistance has developed:</p><ul><li><strong>The Naturalistic Fallacy:</strong> The belief that \"natural\" things are inherently good, and \"artificial\" things are inherently bad.</li><li><strong>Sacred Domain Intrusion:</strong> The fact that AI is intruding on domains that have historically been dominated by humans, such as artistic creativity, romantic companionship, and legal judgement.</li><li><strong>Social Contagion:</strong> Because others also do not like AI, having the same opinion allows the individual to fit into a larger group, overriding individual pragmatism and reality.</li></ul><p>The way to deal with this viewing of AI through a strictly moral lens (which even overrides the utility benefits that AI has), is to explicate the benefits of AI equally in moral terms.</p><ul><li>Ex. AI helps reduce the amount of medical mistakes that could occur, saving more lives.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As the Mongols originated in the plains of Northern China, where the temperature fluctuates widely (-30 C to 40 C), they were adapted to fight in many different areas around the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the Mongols were able to take such a large amount of land, and also shows that conditioning oneself to a variety of situations allows oneself to perform better when those situations inevitably arrive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200\">Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200</a><ul><li>A second climatic factor, though one acting through horse supply rather than through adaptation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe\">The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe</a><ul><li>The social form that this environment selected for.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered\">The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered</a><ul><li>Again demonstrates their adaptability.</li></ul></li></ul>","Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While there were many technological innovations before the Industrial Revolution, there was also a disconnect between practical (prescriptive) knowledge and theoretical (propositional) knowledge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This disconnect between the two forms of knowledge is the reason that the innovations were not able to produce economic growth.</p><h4>References</h4>","Belief can be fixed by tenacity, authority, the a priori method, or by science":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are four ways of settling a belief: clinging to what one already holds, accepting what an institution imposes, adopting what is agreeable to reason or fashion, and testing against evidence that anyone can repeat.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Framing inquiry as a choice among methods rather than as a search for truth makes it possible to compare the methods by what they deliver, which is the move that grounds pragmatism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Peirce concedes that the first three are not irrational. Tenacity provides peace of mind, authority provides social stability, and the a priori method feels like reasoning. Each fails on contact with a person who has settled the same question differently, which is the pressure that pushes inquiry toward the fourth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry\">Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry</a><ul><li>The conclusion this typology is built to support.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A belief is a habit of action\">A belief is a habit of action</a><ul><li>What is being fixed by any of the four methods.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth\">Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth</a><ul><li>A rival account of how disagreement is resolved.</li></ul></li></ul>","Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A belief in meritocracy makes people act more unfairly towards one another.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to support arguments that oppose meritocracy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Scientists have designed experiments where participants play fake games of skill.</p><ul><li>In such cases, they are more likely to distribute winnings to themselves when they believed that they had won as a result for their superior skill compared to when they thought that it was just luck.</li></ul><p><code>But doesn't the very fact that this instinct evolved mean that it is in fact the case that, more likely than not, the more meritorious person deserves more rewards? It seems like we are merely exploiting edge cases in our intuition in order to come up with these results.</code></p><h4>References</h4>","Berkson's paradox explains the existence of false negative correlations between two events":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Berkson’s paradox explains why there often seems to be a negative correlation between two independent variables due to selection bias.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may help us avoid falling into believing false dichotomies.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>There seems to be a negative correlation the attractiveness of a celebrity and their acting prowess. However, this is likely due to selection bias, as those who are neither attractive nor good at acting become actors. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Randomization allows for causation to be determined\">Randomization allows for causation to be determined</a><ul><li>This is a principle in statistics that is important to understand; since Berkson’s paradox is not random, causation cannot be determined.</li></ul></li><li>Designing a Good Experiment<ul><li>When designing a good experiment, make sure that the experience does not fall into the trap of Berkson’s paradox.</li></ul></li></ul>","Bible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An important religion text that sets the foundation for religions like Christianity, Judaism, and Islam.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Understanding the bible is crucial for understanding many works of Western literature, since much of the writing done after the bible is a response to it or builds upon it.</p><h3>Genesis</h3><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Man is created in the image of God\">Man is created in the image of God</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not good for man to be alone\">It is not good for man to be alone</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a><ul><li>Suffering begins with original sin.<ul><li>In deflecting his responsibility of eating from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, Adam cuts off his relationship both to the divine and to the human, since he blames Eve, and in extension, since he claims that Eve is the creation of God, he blames God.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Angel to Hagar</p><p>And the LORD’s messenger said to her, “<strong>Return to your mistress and suffer abuse at her hand.</strong>”… “For the LORD has <strong>heeded your suffering.</strong>”</p></blockquote><ul><li>Hagar is able to “see” the well after she is reminded by the Angel and remembers that Ismael will rule over a great nation.<ul><li>This is basically saying: “yes, one will suffer, but with faith in God, suffering is endurable and not as bad.”</li></ul></li><li>The Binding of Isaac:<ul><li>God’s direct command overrides the other moral precepts that have been laid down by him?<ul><li>Shows God as the creator of morality.</li></ul></li><li>This scene is not only a test of Abraham’s loyalty to God, but also a test of Isaac, and his loyalty to God and Abraham.</li></ul></li><li>The tree of knowledge of good and evil was there<ul><li>To have humans exercise free will</li><li>So that they could eat from it later?</li></ul></li><li>There is a contrast between the Jacob and Esau story and the Cain and Abel story:<ul><li>In the case of the former, the two brothers, although one tries to kill another, the brothers are “enlightened.” Esau does not kill Jacob, and Jacob in a way repays Esau for the trouble that he has caused him.</li><li>In the case of Cain and Abel, Cain successfully kills Abel, and there is no resolution between the two.</li></ul></li><li>In the Joseph story, there is a cycle of betrayal that is broken when Judah offers up himself instead of Benjamin to Joseph as a slave.<ul><li>Joseph was originally betrayed by his brothers since he was his father’s favorite.</li><li>Later, when the brothers arrive in Egypt, Joseph makes them play out what had happened to him with Jacob’s second son by Rachel, Benjamin. </li><li>When Judah offers to sacrifice himself rather than having for the brothers to sacrifice Benjamin, the cycle of sibling betrayal is finally ended, and Joseph reveals himself to his brothers.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Meta</h4><ul><li>The use of repetition in the bible is not an error, but rather intention, to ensure that the reader understands the circumstances and fully context of an event, to highlight the relationship of people to each other in the context of their current actions.</li><li>The betrothal scene between two characters is often indicative of the nature of their marriage.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are three different types of chemical bonds: covalent, ionic, and metallic. However, only covalent and ionic are relevant for biology.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Chemical bonds, along with Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules intermolecular forces, are important in biology.</p><h4>Bonds</h4><p>There are three types of chemical bonds:</p><ul><li>Covalent bonds share electrons </li><li>Ionic bonds transfer electrons</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules\">Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules</a></li></ul>","Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Buddhahood is achieved through Sudden Enlightenment, which is the moment when one becomes one with the <em>wu</em>, or the Universal Mind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a theory of Chinese Buddhism, specifically, the theories developed by Zhu Daosheng.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>All  sentient beings are born with the Universal Mind, though they might not realize it. It is this blindness that prevents one from being able to escape the eternal reincarnation that occur as a result of <em>karma</em>. Eventually, through practice, one will be able to see one’s Universal Mind, at which point one is suddenly enlightened. That is also the moment when one realizes the true nature of the <em>wu</em>.</p><h4>References</h4>","Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The reasons for the decline of Buddhism in India includes the lack of patronage in India, and the fact that Buddhism was overtaken by the <em>Bhakti</em> movement (a spawn of the incumbent Hindu religion), a movement with roots in Hinduism, around the turn of the first milenium.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Many of the elements of Buddhism were eventually incorporated into Hinduism.</li><li>The <em>Bhakti</em> movement made Hindu practice more accessible to the common people, even people of low caste, since it focused on intense worship of deities, rather than philosophical speculation or sacrifices, often conducted by the Brahmins.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The hypothesis that business cycles are the result of the central bank control of interest rate. Essentially, that the central bank control of the interest rate makes it so that interest rates are kept too low during booms, and too high during depressions, meaning that the effects of these interest rate changes are exasperated.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Austrian explanation for the existence of the business cycle, and this explanation differs from the explanations for the business cycle offered by other schools of thought.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Savings and Loan Crisis was caused by interest rate shocks, risky investments, and regulatory failure\">The Savings and Loan Crisis was caused by interest rate shocks, risky investments, and regulatory failure</a><ul><li>The artificial manipulation of the interest by the central bank during this period of time could be fit as the factor that ultimately lead to the collapse of thrift banks.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mainstream economics’ need for assumptions in their models justifies government intervention into the economy\">Mainstream economics’ need for assumptions in their models justifies government intervention into the economy</a><ul><li>Perhaps it is due to these interventions that bubbles are caused.</li></ul></li></ul><p>-</p>","By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Once one is able to become one with the universe, where the boundaries between the individual and the world dissolves, one is essentially rendered immortal and one finds absolute happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the final goal of the Taoist, that is, to become the Taoist sage who has become one with the universe.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When one becomes one with the universe, things the trouble the average person in their daily lives fail to disturb the sage any longer, since they themselves have genuinely been molded into a part of the cosmos. Life and death become much less meaningful, since in the grand scheme of things (from the view of the cosmos), they no longer matter.</p><h5>Zen</h5><p>A similar concept is in Zen Buddhism, since achieving enlightenment means being unable to see the distinction between things in the cosmos, thus becoming one with the Tao.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>This is the means by which one is able to become one with the universe.</li></ul></li></ul>","CHONPSNaCaK are the most fundamental building blocks of biological molecules":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>CHONPSNaCaK are the molecules that are the most fundamental building blocks of life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These molecules are an important aspect of many of the macromolecules that make up life.</p><h4>What Does It Stand For?</h4><ol><li>Carbon</li><li>Hydrogen</li><li>Oxygen</li><li>Nitrogen</li><li>Phosphorus</li><li>Sulfur</li><li>Sodium</li><li>Calcium</li><li>Potassium</li></ol><h4>Notes</h4><p>See Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds which indicates how the molecules described here could bond with other molecules.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds\">Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds</a></li></ul>","Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the future, due to the rise in the ability of capital to entirely replace human labor (rather than be complements), capital’s share of income will drastically increase.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the thesis of Thomas Piketty, and although he had been wrong about the past, this note argues that he will be correct in the future.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While in the past, it has not been the case that capital has been able to largely replace labor (and thus occupy a larger share of national income), as Piketty predicted, mainly due to the fact that capital remained complementary to labor, in the future, especially due to AI, this is unlikely to be the case. AI is able to perform many agentic tasks that require complex cognitive functions, which has been the factor that has ensured that labor is complementary to capital (labor is needed to ensure that the repetitive work that capital does is directed in the right way). This means that they will likely begin to overtake labor’s share of income in the economy.</p><ul><li>This also means that wealth inequality could rise, since the people who originally had more wealth are better able to utilize AI (since they can hire “AI Advisors”), and thus generate wealth, and so on.</li><li>Additionally, while developing nations have traditionally relied on lower labor costs to attract work, now that capital is able to replace labor, these countries lose their comparative advantage there. Naturally resources are the only means for them to grow rich at that point.</li></ul><p>Some ways to potentially mitigate this is a strong wealth tax or inheritance tax.</p><h4>References</h4>","Capitalism and Freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Friedman’s magnum opus, a piece about how political freedom is dependent on economic freedom.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the major works in economics.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The theory that capitalism leads people to abandon morality, become greedier, promotes consumerism, and alienates people. Additionally, civic bonds are weakened and civic virtue declines.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the theory of self-destruction that describes a potential outcome of market capitalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability\">Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability</a><ul><li>This is the opposite.</li></ul></li></ul>","Capitalism undermines democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A common claim among leftists is that capitalism fundamentally erodes our ability to peacefully cooperate, and the greed associated with capitalism engenders inequality and class division which eventually leads to corrupt government and the end of democracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common claim made by leftists.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>Essentially, the things described in this note is what contributes to the current note’s claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a><ul><li>Many identify this as a core issue.</li></ul></li></ul>","Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of careers open to talents does not do enough to rectify the preexistent inequalities in the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Rawls uses this suggest the necessity of a difference principle, namely the principle of fair equality of opportunity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls argues that careers open to talents does not mitigate either social contingencies or natural contingencies. Therefore, we should not adopt it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The same argumentative move applied to a different principle. Both failures push Rawls toward the difference principle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>A main critique of meritocracy is that it is unable to account for the social or natural contingencies that arise, which is captured in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cat-walk (painting)":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>Its quite interesting how the industrial forms are able to be simplified down to nothing, just a collection of lines and shapes and colors.</p><ul><li>It shows something about the artificiality of human architecture.</li></ul><p>Additionally, as you look closer at the painting, you notice that aspects of it don't really line up: some areas are missing shadows, or the perspective is slightly off, or lines may lead nowhere.</p><h4>References</h4>","Cavalry were effective in ancient warfare because of the mobility asymmetry that they created":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Cavalry were really powerful in ancient warfare because of their hypermobility.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that they were able to more easily take advantage of weaknesses in an enemy formation, and exploit these weaknesses in a timely fashion.</p><h4>References</h4>","Cells are typically negatively charged":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the fact that many of the molecules in a cell are negatively charged, most prominently Potassium ions, cells are typically negatively on the whole. They are usually around $-60$ to $-70 mV$, known as its <em>resting potential</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This important for explaining the movement of molecules across the cell membrane, and the membrane potential.</p><h4>References</h4>","Censorship is bad for everyone":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We should never censor someone since it has negative consequences, not only for the people censored, but for everyone.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines the broad case against censorship.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two possibilities regarding the outcomes of censorship:</p><ol><li>either the orthodoxy is right, in which case divergent opinions would merely have tasted and refined the orthodox opinion, or </li><li>if the divergent opinion is true, then we have arrived closer to the truth.</li></ol><p>It seems ridiculous to claim that we are infallible, and therefore, the two previously outlines possibilities always manifest. Therefore, we must always allow for freedom of expression. By allowing freedom of expression everyone benefits from the new insights generated through the process, not just the people who otherwise would have been repressed. We can in fact produce a more accurate definition of infallibility: it is not the state of feeling sure of an opinion; rather, it is making a decision for others, and disallowing others from providing dissenting opinions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>Cancel culture is a form of censorship.</li></ul></li></ul>","Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>$\\Delta$ in pH or salinity is able to break ionic bonds (see Ionic bonds transfer electrons) and hydrogen bonds (see Hydrogen bonds form between polar molecules (or charged molecules)).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may be relevant for an AP Bio test.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>A change in pH does not affect Van der Waals forces occur between all molecules .</p><h4>References</h4>","Charges refer to different particles depending on the context":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Depending on the context, “charge” in physics could refer to electrons, protons, or ions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Often, physics problems just mention how “charges move,” but it is not explicitly stated what that entails. Clarifying this detail would aid with solving these problems.</p><h4>Carriers of Charge</h4><ul><li>Electrons are negatively charged particles that usually move in conductors.</li><li>Protons are positively charged particles that do not move (though in some models, they are shown moving).</li><li>Ions are atoms that have lost or gained electrons, so they carry a net charge.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A proton and electron carry equal but opposite elementary charges\">A proton and electron carry equal but opposite elementary charges</a><ul><li>Specifies the magnitude of charge that each of these carriers holds.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object\">In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object</a><ul><li>Where the mobile carriers described here actually end up in a conductor.</li></ul></li></ul>","China grows its influence with non-expansionary means":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>China has historically been a non-expansionary power, since in its view, it was the only true power around, and there was no civilization that could truly mount a threat against it. This trend is expected to continue being present in Chinese foreign policy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This theory could be used to predict the actions of the PRC.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Though it will not expand militarily, it will instead want to ensure that the nations around it</p><ul><li>Support its territorial integrity (with regards to Tibet, Taiwan, etc.).</li><li>To generate support for Chinese foreign policy.</li><li>To remain aligned with Chinese economic goals.</li><li>To support Chinese culture and Chinese people.</li></ul><p>Part of this diplomacy may be motivated by the fact that China’s foreign policy is continually tinted by its Confucian view, that of the harmony between different power differentials. In this way, China does not come off as outwardly aggressive, though in fact that is China’s stance on the issue, and it is made clear in more subtle ways.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs\">Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs</a><ul><li>This approach to foreign policy may be fundamentally rooted in Daoism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s foreign policy is characterized by &#x27;theatrical diplomacy&#x27;\">China's foreign policy is characterized by 'theatrical diplomacy'</a><ul><li>This is one of the main ways by which China gains influence.</li></ul></li></ul>","China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The historical development of strong state institutions in China allows for highly centralized state action, though at the cost of individual freedom. The lack of such development in India has led to comparatively greater power to the masses and the development of the world’s largest democracy, though the government suffers from debilitating bureaucratic hurdles, local autonomy, and corruption.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This comparison between the two most populous states in the world essentially illustrate the two manner in which states can come to develop: either with strong or weak state institutions, with either of these paths leading to radically different outcomes.</p><h4>Historical Examples</h4><p>The rise of the Chinese state begins with the Legalistic Qin state, which was the first to unite all of China.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that although modern day China is highly centralized, it is up to debate whether this is due to some innate attribute of the way that Chinese state institutions developed, or due to some geographic quirk, or something else. I think that China’s centralization is in part due to its long history, its relative power compared to surrounding states (due to its geography), and the evolution of a national identity. So, because China’s state institutions have had a long time to develop, they were able to slowly establish frameworks for centralizing the state. Although there are periods of instability of fracturing, ultimately, the goal of any fracture state is not to remain independent, but “reclaim China.” Likewise, its relative lack of outsider influence means that it has been able to remain relative isolated and develop a unified identity. Today, modern technology has made it a lot easier to run a highly centralized state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India\">Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India</a><ul><li>This could partly explain the weak state in India.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>The way that the state does not collapse in on itself is through its channels of meritocracy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>This continues to be the case in China, and is perhaps made worse by the strong state control.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>China’s geography influenced the strengthen of its state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Xi Jinping seeks to establish a revolutionary spirit to maintain ideological purity and prevent institutional decay\">Xi Jinping seeks to establish a revolutionary spirit to maintain ideological purity and prevent institutional decay</a><ul><li>This is an example of the strong state, since Xi is actually trying and maybe able to cultivate a revolutionary spirit.</li></ul></li></ul>","China will dominate in East Asia":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that China will be a regional, if not global power through its domination of East Asia.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has implications for US foreign policy towards China.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is fundamentally derived through the regression towards the mean, as China has historically been dominant in East Asia, and has only become weak after its contact with the West. China is predicted to regress towards the mean, i.e., the state whereby China is the dominant power in East Asia.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>China is likely to enforce this dominance through non-expansionary means.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The around 60% of China’s total trade goes through the Malacca Strait\">The around 60% of China’s total trade goes through the Malacca Strait</a><ul><li>This could potentially be a barrier to China’s domination.</li></ul></li></ul>","China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>China’s basic social contract is one which gives the state dictatorial powers, but the people positive rights and economic development.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding the way that the Chinese government works, and the foundations that underlie the Chinese state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>This idea is again present here, as China’s strong state is predicated on the fact that they provide positive rights for their citizens.</li></ul></li></ul>","China's foreign policy is characterized by 'theatrical diplomacy'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>China’s foreign policy approach is marked by their “theatrical diplomacy,” where they engage in essentially risk-free diplomatic gestures which engender other countries’ goodwill towards China.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains an important aspect of China’s foreign policy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“China Gains Favor With Trump by Dipping Into Iran Diplomacy”</p><p>Underneath its peace proposal lies what some call a “China First” policy—a cautious strategy that gives priority to symbolic wins and never assumes significant risk for the sake of global stability. This “theatrical diplomacy” illustrates the limits of Beijing’s influence even as it casts itself as a superpower peer to the U.S. “The prevailing mindset in Beijing is that China is not sticking its neck out for anybody,” said Danny Russel, a former senior U.S. diplomat now with the Asia Society Policy Institute. “China will only engage in mediation that allows it to claim credit without incurring any real risk or cost.“”</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China will dominate in East Asia\">China will dominate in East Asia</a><ul><li>Perhaps part of the way that China will be able to dominate is through a soft power approach aided by this theatrical diplomacy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>Related concept to the idea in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A world dominated by China will be radically different from a world dominated by the US\">A world dominated by China will be radically different from a world dominated by the US</a><ul><li>Extending the logic of this note, a world dominated by China would be one with less public goods provided, which is exactly what this note explains.</li></ul></li></ul>","China’s defeat in the Sino-Japanese war undermined Chinese belief in their cultural superiority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The reason for end of Chinese belief in their cultural superiority can be traced back to the defeat of China during the Sino-Japanese War. This marked the ultimate turning point after a series of defeats at the hands of the West.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a turning point in Chinese history when the prevailing belief of Chinese cultural superiority was shattered.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Prior to coming into contact with the West in an extended degree, all the peoples that lived around China were heavily influenced by Chinese culture and politically and militarily less than or at the same level as the Chinese. Even when this was not true, Chinese culture still often drastically influenced them. With the defeat during the Opium Wars, wars with other European powers such as Russia and France and finally the Japanese, the Chinese ultimately had to realize the possibility of Western culture being something that is valuable to study and adopt elements from. Prior to the Sino-Japanese War, they had only considered Western technology to be superior, not their culture.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a><ul><li>Japan ultimately embraced the influence of the West before China did, however.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>This seems to be one thing that was changed by Western culture, which had a larger focus on positive science.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion\">When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion</a><ul><li>China was ultimately unable to reform, and in the resulting revolution, many people argued for the adoption of many aspects of Western values, such as constitutional liberalism and democracy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Chinese firms' global market shares have increased largely due to subsidies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A large reason for the increase in Chinese firms’ global market shares is due to the large amounts of subsidies that they get from the government, which accounts for around 10% of a firm’s annual revenue. This is compared to the 2% OECD average.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the large amount of market share which Chinese companies occupy in many industrial sectors.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Much of the subsidies were given in the form of “soft loans,” or below-market rate loans from the government, and which are hard to trace. Additionally, this strategy does not increase the productivity of the Chinese firms, but simply makes them unfair competitors compared to other OECD firms.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>For instance, economic domination.</li></ul></li></ul>","Chinese philosophy is inseparable from political thought":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The very nature of Chinese philosophy, and the circumstances under which it emerged dictates that it is inseparable from political theory.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial feature of Chinese philosophy that sets it apart from Western philosophy. It also again shows the pragmatic nature that fundamentally underlies Chinese philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is often a direct link between various Chinese philosophies and the specific politics that they endorse. For instance, Confucianism advocates for a strict social and political hierarchy, while Taoism strives for a more anarcho-primitivist outlook.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation\">Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation</a><ul><li>This seems related, since the state can be a crucial instrument in aiding the individual achieve spiritual elevation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>Again, shows the pragmatic nature of Chinese thought.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>Direct example of Confucianism influence Chinese state politics.</li></ul></li></ul>","Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Chinese philosophy, unless the philosophy of the West, is not concerned with increasing positive knowledge, but rather with searching for super-moral values, an understanding of the world that helps one practically deal with worldly travails and achieve the status of the sage.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This identifies something that structurally separates Western and Eastern philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The end goal of Chinese philosophy, as identified by Fong Youlan, is “sageliness within and kingliness without.” In this way, the enlightened individual is not merely a citizen of some particular polity, but also a “citizen of the universe (天民).”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>It seems that perhaps part of the reason for the development of philosophy in this direction is that this way, philosophy would pose less of a threat to the central power of the state, since it far more focused on understanding humans, which is controllable by the state, whereas an upturning of the metaphysical nature of the world would undermine state power.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>It seems that a similar attitude pervaded Chinese science.</li></ul></li></ul>","Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Chinese society, due to their historically quick adaption of new technologies and the positive benefits of such adaptations, are generally more positive regarding new technologies.</p><ul><li>This explains their largely positive outlook towards AI and its potential. 83% of people are optimistic about AI in China, whereas only 39% of people are optimistic about it in the US.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that the Chinese may receive the benefits of new technologies before other societies do.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some have argued that the cause of this positive attitude to technology is not necessarily a result of techno-optimism. Rather, it is because Chinese history has demonstrated that those who are not able to meet the pace of new technology and adopt it timely fall behind in society.</p><ul><li>This was what occurred in Northern China during the Reform and Opening Up, when millions of people working in government-run industries lost their jobs to the South and East of China, as higher tech industries sprang up.</li></ul><p>Either way, there is higher trust and willingness (whether it is out of genuine techno-optimism or simply FOMO) in China compared to the West. Another possible explanation for this phenomenon is the fact that in China, human lives are valued less, since there is an oversupply of human lives compared to the rest of the world (China has too many people).</p><ul><li>As a society that is not as rule-of-law-based as the US, protection for individual rights are correspondingly far weaker.</li><li>People care less when other people’s jobs are overtaken by AI, since human lives are valued less.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“Involution Without Export Is Wasted Effort”</p><p>When you live in a society so arbitrary, <strong>the only option is to make yourself adaptable.</strong> So when someone in Beijing tells me AI is inevitable and you might as well learn to live with it, I hear their parents’ lives in that sentence. Americans grow up assuming change is something you get a vote on. It doesn’t just “happen to you”, rather you get to decide if it even happens or not.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>Additionally, China’s strong state is able to ensure that new technologies are fostered by the central government.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cholesterol is the starting material for the synthesis of many hormones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Cholesterol is a type of steroid that forms the basis for many different types of hormones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Cholesterol is therefore not merely structural. The same molecule that regulates membrane fluidity is the precursor of the steroid hormones, so one lipid links the physics of the membrane to endocrine signaling.</p>","Cholesterol is used to control the fluidity of membranes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the shape of cholesterol, it is able to fit into gaps in the phospholipid membrane to ensure the right level of structure/fluidity in the membrane.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains one of functions of cholesterol, which is relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cholesterol is the starting material for the synthesis of many hormones\">Cholesterol is the starting material for the synthesis of many hormones</a></li></ul>","Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Christian morality inherently adopts an attitude which makes it obedient to authority.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a new perspective on the issue of Christian morality.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is used by J.S. Mill in order to reply to the claim that Christian morality is entirely right and cannot be otherwise. This aspect of Christian morality which he identifies as an counterargument to that claim.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>Perhaps this feature of Christian morality is not a “bug” but a feature?</li></ul></li></ul>","Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that Christian morality is at some fundamental level, the closest to the a universal moral system, since it has so widely been adapted everywhere.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is used by some to argue for Christian morality.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In fact, many social movements that are avowedly Atheistic nonetheless adopt the morality associated with Christianity.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Couldn’t this just because of the large geopolitical influence that Europe has exerted across the world in the past, and not necessarily a result of the inherent appealingness of the moral system?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>It seems that capitalism has been able to coexist with Christian morality, but why?</li></ul></li></ul>","Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A citizen is whoever is entitled to share in deliberative or judicial office. The definition is functional rather than legal, geographic, or genealogical, and since regimes differ in kind, a citizen in a democracy may not be one in an oligarchy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle’s answer to “who is a citizen?”, prior to defining the city, since the city is a multitude of citizens.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>He rules out three rival definitions:</p><ul><li><strong>Residence</strong>, since metics and slaves share the citizens’ domicile.</li><li><strong>Legal standing</strong>, since parties to a commercial treaty can also sue and be sued.</li><li><strong>Descent</strong>, which cannot cover a city’s founders or later enfranchisements.</li></ul><p><em>Office</em> is stretched to include the juror and assemblyman, who hold no fixed term but wield the greatest power. Entitlement matters, not attendance, which is why children and the aged are citizens only in a qualified sense. The definition fits democracy best and nearly empties out in a narrow oligarchy; regime-relativity absorbs this, but makes “citizen” equivocal.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law\">Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law</a><ul><li>Entitlement to share in office is not the same as being able to exercise it.</li></ul></li></ul>","Civilization is the process by which time preferences diminish":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Hoppes describes the “process of civilization” as one that is defined by diminishing time preferences, whereby people are more and more willing to engage in capital formation and to create durable consumer goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This both explains the importance of time preferences as a means of ensuring the success of civilizations, it also explores the importance of time preferences in ensuring the success/establishment of a working civilization.</p><h4>References</h4>","Civilizational continuity, for the Chinese, requires cultural continuance, not political continuance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For Chinese people, they do not consider a political discontinuity to consist in being conquered by outsiders. Rather, they consider it in terms of cultural continuation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents insight into the way that the Chinese view their state and their views on the importance of culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Even though the Chinese state was taken over my Mongols, and then the Manchus, the Chinese people consider these ethnically different dynasties to be “Chinese” dynasties, since they shared the same culture that Chinese people did (at least, eventually).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a><ul><li>It seems that this note affirms the linked note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered\">The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered</a><ul><li>Thus the Mongols adapted to Chinese culture?</li></ul></li></ul>","Clarity comes in grades, from familiarity, to abstract definition, to practical consequence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Understanding an idea admits of degrees: one may recognise the term when it appears, one may be able to state a definition of it, and one may know what difference it would make in experience if it were true, and only the third counts as full clarity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This gives a test for whether a distinction is real, since two ideas at the third grade of clarity are the same idea if they imply the same practical consequences.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The grades are cumulative and the second is the deceptive one, because the ability to define a term convincingly resembles understanding closely enough to be mistaken for it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act\">A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act</a><ul><li>The criterion that the third grade supplies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We often mistake our own unclearness of thought for some inherent mystery in the subject of our thought\">We often mistake our own unclearness of thought for some inherent mystery in the subject of our thought</a><ul><li>The characteristic error of someone stalled at the second grade.</li></ul></li></ul>","Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>On the classical compatibilist analysis, what opposes freedom is not causal determination but obstruction, so an agent is free whenever nothing external prevents them from doing what they want to do.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the analysis that makes freedom cheap enough to survive determinism, and every later compatibilism is a repair to it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hobbes gives the canonical formulation, defining liberty as the absence of impediments to action not contained in the nature of the agent. The analysis has an immediate cost: it secures the freedom to act otherwise if one wanted to, but says nothing about the freedom to want otherwise, and the attempt to add that generates a regress, since changing one’s will would require a prior will to change it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will\">The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will</a><ul><li>The precise form of the failure described above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom\">One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom</a><ul><li>A constraint any account of freedom has to accommodate.</li></ul></li></ul>","Classical utilitarianism is too demanding":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls argues that classical utilitarianism is only possible in a world where people are perfectly altruistic, since only in such a world would people accept some loss to themselves (especially major disutilities) in order that some other, unknown person may gain even just a marginally greater amount of utility. In short, it is too demanding of people.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Rawls’ objection towards classical utilitarianism, because the assumption of perfect altruism is clearly unreasonable, especially in Rawls’ framework of rational agents.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Another manifestation of the issue here comes in the form of population ethics. Utilitarians would likely too heavily discount the present in order to increase overall utility, and try to mitigate this through the introduction of a morally irrelevant time preference.</p><ul><li>In the first case, it is too morally demanding of the current generation. But to solve this, a morally irrelevant factor must be introduced to make a nonsensical claim: that of pure time preference.</li></ul><h4>Mill</h4><p>Mill counters this argument by saying that, in general, we only have to attend to private or communal utility, and that someone acting as a general benefactor to a huge number of people is going above and beyond. Utilitarianism is therefore not actually that demanding, since all it demands is that we consider the utility of the people around us.</p><h4>Contractarianism</h4><p>A similar counterargument is presented by Scanlon through his <em>Reasonable Rejection of Extreme Sacrifice</em>, whereby an extreme demand for giving others which would leave us destitute would prevent ourselves from being able to carry out our personal projects.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a><ul><li>This is part of the reason why this is true.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pure time preference is irrational\">Pure time preference is irrational</a><ul><li>This explains why pure time preference is irrational.</li></ul></li></ul>","Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity in that it has the potential to reduce biodiversity, which can lead to ecosystem collapse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the main reason that climate change should be avoided, though that are other, secondary reasons as well.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Another way of putting this is that through climate change, we lose access to ecosystem services, which are benefits provided by the environment (clean air, water purification, pollination, etc.).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem\">Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem</a><ul><li>This is way climate change is so hard to solve.</li></ul></li></ul>","Collectivist countries are more lonely less individualist ones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is evidence to suggest that, contrary to popular belief, collectivist states have a higher level of loneliness than individualist ones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges something that is commonly believed regarding collectivist/individualist countries.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>  There seems to be a relatively strong correlation between the collectivity of a state and the level of loneliness in that state. This seems to be because collectivist states are more tribal and people typically don’t associate outside of their “tribe.”</p><h4>References</h4>","Commerce is not ‘natural aquisition’":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle’s idea that commerce is something that is bad, since it is not natural, given that commerce does not use the items that it involves according to its “true” purpose, but rather its secondary purpose, that of being able to be traded.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This laid the groundwork for Marxist economic thought in the 19th century, and was the putative belief regarding the nature of economic for the period before the Enlightenment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle claims that this unnatural acquisition is the result of people merely pursuing life, and not living well. When people merely seek bodily satisfaction, the result is commerce.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>Regards political justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Compensating differentials is the idea that some jobs pay more due to the non-pecuniary benefits (or disadvantages) associated with employment. This is evident for example for lighthouse workers, who receive very high pay as they are isolated and the job is also dangerous.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can explain some of the variation in the job market, and why some jobs pay a lot more, despite being less technical or physical demanding than some other jobs.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","Concord is a pragmatic friendship between citizens of a state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Concord is when people have the same opinion about what is to their interest, choose the same actions, and carry out what they decided in common.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for Aristotle’s discussions on politics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Concord specifically regards an alignment in belief of practical matters that shape life.<ul><li>It regards shared purpose, not just shared opinion.</li></ul></li><li>It is inherently political, concerning common advantage in the state.</li><li>It is only able to be reached by virtuous people, not vicious ones.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>In order for a state to have concord, many people must be virtuous.</li></ul></li></ul>","Conductivity is determined by the resistance of a material":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The more resistance a material, the less conductive it is, and the more thermal radiation is released as waste.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Different conductivities can change the speed at which a charge moves through the material. This is significant for many physics problems.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Resistance is what makes “freely” a spectrum rather than a binary.</li></ul></li></ul>","Conflict is sharpened by differences in culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Conflicts between nations are often made worse or more intense due to civilizational/cultural differences between the belligerents.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may explain why cross-cultural conflicts often result in greater violence and destruction.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>During the wars after the dissolution of Yugoslavia, there was brutal fault line conflicts between the various new nations that formed, including mass genocides of people of different cultures.</p><ul><li>The war in Yugoslavia is an example of a complex fault line war, where there is an intricate network of supporting parties on both sides of the conflict that do not actually fight.</li></ul><p> In the diagram above, $A_{1}$ and $B_{1}$ represent the actual parties engaged in the conflict, while $A_{2}$ and $B_{2}$ are secondary and $A_{3}$ and $B_{3}$ are tertiary actors.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a><ul><li>This would explain why this note is the case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a></li><li>This affirmation of Asian culture could mean that when there are future conflicts between Asian cultures and Western ones, it could be more brutal due to the cultural difference.</li></ul>","Conflicts arise when a rising power begins to overtake an existing hegemon":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Also known as the Thucydides Trap, when rising powers begin to overtake an established power, the two powers often come into conflict.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to explain/predict when conflicts occur.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This effect was first observed by Thucydides, and was noted in his <em>History of the Peloponnesian War</em>, as when Athens began to grow powerful and threaten the dominance of Sparta, the two nations engaged in the Peloponnesian War. Such conflicts naturally tend to be core state conflicts.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes\">History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes</a><ul><li>This is an example of one of the conclusions that Thucydides reached with his method.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucianism divided over whether human nature is innately good or innately evil":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While both were branches of Confucianism, Mencius argued that all humans were naturally good-natured, and Xunzi argued that all humans were naturally ill-natured.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the ideological diversity that existed in Confucianism following its founding, and The domination of Xunzi’s ideas would shape Confucianism until the Neo-Confucian revival.</p><h4>Mencius</h4><ul><li>He developed the idea of the four cardinal virtues:<ul><li>Benevolence</li><li>Righteousness </li><li>Wisdom</li><li>Propriety </li></ul></li><li>Argued that all humans were innately good.</li></ul><ul><li>Also developed further by Xunzi.<ul><li>Argued that all humans are innately evil.</li><li>Argued for the importance of learning to develop oneself and make oneself better.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Why?</h4><ul><li>Part of reason that Xunzi and Mencius have conflicting ideas is that they have different definitions of human nature: Mencius defines it as the <em>potential</em> for what one could become, while Xunzi defines it as one's <em>natural dispositions</em>.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a><ul><li>Xunzi’s thesis.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a><ul><li>Mencius’s thesis.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucianism has a prominent focus on education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An important facet of Confucianism is its focus on the importance of education, reflected in the Civil Service exams (see The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Confucianism’s focus on education allowed for Chinese state institutions to be operated effectively, and for its civilization to produce a variety of advancements.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>The human product of that focus: officials who were also scholars.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>Complicates this note — the exams served co-optation as much as education.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a></li></ul>","Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Confucianism is built upon categorical imperatives, each depend on one’s role in society and the associated duties. There is no particular justification for the “way things are.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves as the core of Confucian philosophy, since it is what determines one’s duties.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Based on the <em>Book of Changes</em>, the natural order of things is found, and not invented by humans. Additionally, it does not exist for any reason. Confucius advocated for doing things because they are the right thing to do, regardless of the outcome of such actions. Often times, things do not work out, and this is attributed to <em>ming</em> (命).</p><ul><li>The idea of <em>ming</em> is that of cosmic indeterminacy and random chance, so that even if try the best that you could, things could still go wrong simply due to chance. Accepting <em>ming</em> means understanding the inevitability of the world but disregarding whether something is successful or not.</li></ul><p>From another viewpoint, in order to better the world, one does not need to be in a position of power. One needs merely to act accordingly, and through sincerely attempting to act well, one is resting in the highest good.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>These imbalances are the result of the categorical imperatives.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction\">Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction</a><ul><li>Xunzi disagrees with the formulation here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucianist culture and a non-communist economic system is correlated with economic success":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There seems to be a correlation between Confucian culture in non-communist states and rapid economic growth, though there are some confounders which make it difficult to draw a clear connection between the two concepts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could serve as a launching point for a deeper understanding of Confucianist culture and/or the rapid development of the Asian Tigers.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>  The above charts seems to demonstrate that there is in fact some correlation between Confucianism and higher levels of economic growth, such that it is statistically significant that Confucianist, non-communist countries have higher levels of growth than non-Confucianist, non-communist countries. There are some things which much be noted, however:</p><ul><li>The sample size is rather small ($n=11$).</li><li>The Confucianist countries begin in a better economic situation than the non-Confucianist ones, though the difference is not statistically significant.</li><li>There are confounders such as the fact that Confucianist non-Communist states tend to be small, guaranteed by Western nations, and had good port access.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Perhaps <em>some</em> state intervention in present, and cannot be discounted in explaining the rapid growth?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations\">Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations</a><ul><li>This fact could be true, but it seems that institutions (communist vs. Non-communist) play a more important role.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a><ul><li>And this rejection led to economic growth?</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucius emphasized righteousness (义) and human-heartedness (仁)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core of Confucian philosophy is centered around 义 and 仁, or righteousness (the “oughtness” in a situation) and human-heartedness (“loving others”).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the fundamental principles of Confucianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Righteousness describes categorical imperatives. Confucius theorizes that everyone in society has certain things that he ought to do, and these things ought to be done for their own sake, because it is the morally right thing to do. Even doing the right thing, if done with ulterior motives, makes an action no longer righteous.</p><ul><li>The concept of <em>yi</em> is opposed by the concept of <em>li</em> (利), or doing things for profit.</li></ul><p>Human-heartedness is the essential duty of “loving others,” which is the idea of treating others as one would want oneself to the treated. There are, more specifically, two aspects to this practice:</p><ul><li><em>Zhong</em> (忠), or the idea of being conscientious to others.</li><li><em>Shu</em> (恕)，or the idea of altruism.</li></ul><p>It is through practicing these two principles that one becomes “one with the universe,” and that “all things are complete within us (万物皆备于我).” <em>Ren</em> would be further applied to politics by Mencius, who argued that the government must act in accordance to this principle as well, not just individuals.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader\">The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader</a><ul><li>The principles of <em>zhong</em> and <em>shu</em> would be applied to the state by Mencius, and develop associated beliefs regarding the nature and legitimacy of the state.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucius reinterpreted existing moral teachings":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>By the time of Confucius, the core classic works that existed in China were already well-established, so Confucius essentially served as a reinterpreter and synthesizer of existing moral ideas, bringing them together into a cohesive moral framework.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Confucius did not invented a wholly new moral framework, but rather reshaped the existing moral fabric.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Based on the existing texts, Confucius gave new interpretations and functioned as perhaps the first teacher in Chinese history who spread his teachings across a great geographic extent. Therefore, his teachings became widespread and influential.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>This could make sense since Confucius was China’s “first great teacher,” so they would focus heavily on education.</li></ul></li></ul>","Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Congress is the only branch of government that has “intrabranch checks,” meaning that its power is checked by its internal parts. This comes in the form of the upper and lower houses, which may disagree about something and thus put a check on the power of Congress as a whole.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The intrabranch checks on power are important, as they ensure that Congress does not get too power, especially if the other branches of government are ever severely weakened.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a></li></ul>","Contemplation is the highest form of happiness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Of all the different things that bring about happiness, contemplation is the one that brings about the most profound/highest form of happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core aspect of the happy and virtuous life.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Happiness is achieved in its highest form with contemplation since:</p><ul><li>Contemplation is the highest, and most continuous of all activities. We are most capable of practicing it, and we can do it anywhere, at anything.</li><li>Contemplation is the most pleasurable thing. Those with knowledge are more pleasured than those still searching for knowledge.</li><li>Contemplation is self-sufficient, and it is something we can do by ourselves.</li><li>Contemplation is appreciated for its own sake. It is not a means to an end; it is the end.</li><li>Contemplation must be the activity of the gods (if only that is because they have nothing else to do).</li></ul><p>Moral activity is secondary happiness, since it engages with worldly and practical things, whereas contemplation is the greatest form of happiness, since it concerns the heavenly/divine.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>At its highest, this means contemplation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a><ul><li>But contemplation is not enough, because one has to first be physically well-off.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>This, although it is important, is not as important as contemplation when it comes to achieving happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Continence, or self-control, is the ability to control one’s desires with reason, so that even though one may have base desires, such a desire is not acted upon due to the restraining influence of reason.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the important moral states that Aristotle identifies.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control\">A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control</a><ul><li>When desire overpowers rationality, one becomes incontinent.</li></ul></li></ul>","Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Continuity of history is something that needs to be actively pursued. Although religion is not something that is by necessity required in order for our preservation of history, the best way to do so in the age of modernity is through ascetic religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Burckhardt’s views on how we are to preserve our history, i.e., through “true” ascetic religion.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Burckhardt does not argue that there is a theology of history, as he dismisses other such unifying theories of history. However, he holds on to his belief in continuity, which, in his view, requires an active maintenance of the past. This, he argues, is incompatible with the unfettered greed and power-hungriness that permeates the modern world in the form of capitalism, nationalism, militarism, etc. What is required, he avers, is “spiritual practice and pure contemplation.” Therefore, he argues that ascetic religion is required in order for history to be continued. And in doing so, he rejects modern Christianity, which he sees as being a religion that lacks any of its original spiritual commitments, and is now a mere action out of habit, exactly opposite his idea of active continuity.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Chapter 1, <em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 30</p><p>The moral strength of the early Christians consisted not least in their unconcern with nature and culture; dominated by an eschatological faith, the Christians of the apostolic age could not have any real interest in them. <strong>\"The end of the world and eternity were at the door, and it was easy to turn away from the world and its delights…”</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>In the modern Christianity, Burckhardt argues that this is gone.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>This is in part what Burckhardt argues.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>This is part of what he argues.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>It is this divine calling that makes Christianity spiritual in Burckhardt’s view?</li></ul></li></ul>","Contractarian reasoning appears to license compulsory bodily sacrifice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Parties choosing behind a veil of ignorance, not knowing whether they will need an organ, seem bound to agree to a scheme of compulsory donation, and since almost nobody accepts that conclusion, the reasoning that generates it is under suspicion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a reductio aimed at the original position itself rather than at any particular principle chosen within it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mankiw draws the further inference: if we hold rights over our own bodies against what the veil would license, it is unclear why we do not equally hold rights over the product of our labour. The available reply is that bodily integrity and income differ in that only the latter is produced by a cooperative scheme, which is the point made in the reciprocity argument.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>The reply that distinguishes income from bodily integrity.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position\">Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position</a><ul><li>The assumption that generates the objectionable agreement.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>The methodological commitment under attack.</li></ul></li></ul>","Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mackinder’s Heartland Thesis argued that whoever controlled the heartland (see below, defined as the “Pivot Area”) would be able to control the Rimlands (defined below as the “Inner or marginal crescent”), and thereby, they would be able to control the entire world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant theory from international relations and realpolitik that informed many later theories regarding geopolitics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> I think that the theory carries some weight, though some historical evidence suggests that the state that controls the Pivot Area is not guaranteed to be able to control the entire world. I think that this weakens the theory, since a theory could really be produced about any region of the world, with the result that specific region has some specific trait that allows it to conquer the world. That being said, there is other historical evidence to support the theory, including many of the nomadic conquerors. It seems that the most important factor in determining the effectiveness of the conquests of the state in the pivot area is its access to transportation technology that is advanced and flexible. In that way, this theory could be considered “technological” more than “geographical.”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li></ul>","Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Nicholas Spykman’s Rimland Thesis holds that control of the Rimland affords its controller the ability to control the entire Eurasian continent, which would then allow the Eurasian power to control the entire world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The Rimland Thesis informed the policy of containment practice by the US during the Cold War, a crucial aspect of American foreign policy in the latter half of the 20th century.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> The Rimland Thesis finds several flaws in its predecessor:</p><ol><li>Most economic and military power is concentrated in the Rimland, rather than the Heartland.</li><li>The geographic features that surround the heartland can act also as a hedge against Heartland expansion outwards. </li><li>The ability for the Rimland to be connected via an inter-oceanic highway makes it just as effective moving large amounts of men, materials, and ideas across its mass as an industrialized Heartland.</li><li>There has never really been a simple land-sea power opposition.</li></ol><p>Therefore, Spykman focuses far more on inter-Rimland conflict, and its implications. Through his analysis, he concludes that the US must act as a mediator of the balance of power for Rimland states, especially in the “core areas,” such as Western Europe and East Asia. By establishing regional international organizations, nations would be able to maintain this balance of power amongst themselves, which they would do actively since the regional nature of such an organization ensures that they have a stake. The US simply acts as an outside mediator that ensure no state or coalition in the Rimland gets too powerful. This theory would come to influence the theory of containment during the Cold War, which would have immense impacts on not only US history but World History.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Kennan’s containment policy sought to prevent the expansion of Soviet power in the Rimland\">Kennan’s containment policy sought to prevent the expansion of Soviet power in the Rimland</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world</a></li></ul>","Credit expansion ends in specie outflow, hoarding, and unemployment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An issue of money beyond what productivity supports raises consumption and import demand, foreign sellers refuse the paper and are paid in metal, the outflow destroys the backing for the paper, holders of capital hoard what metal remains, and manufacturers dismiss workers for want of liquidity until falling incomes kill the import demand and the drain stops.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The sequence is a complete account of a monetary cycle written before the analytical apparatus for one existed, and it identifies the balance of payments as the binding constraint on domestic credit.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Gervaise sets it out as a cycle with a definite terminus: equilibrium is restored not by policy but by the contraction itself. The unemployment is a consequence of hoarding rather than of the price adjustment, which is the part that anticipates later work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A nation cannot permanently hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity\">A nation cannot permanently hold a stock of money that exceeds its relative productivity</a><ul><li>The constraint that drives the first stage.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Currency devaluation causes unemployment because capital holders withdraw from the real economy\">Currency devaluation causes unemployment because capital holders withdraw from the real economy</a><ul><li>The mechanism of the later stages.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank\">Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank</a><ul><li>The modern version of the same causal story.</li></ul></li></ul>","Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society, though it is a factor that can influence political development.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a critique levied at Samuel Huntington’s idea of Cultural Determinism, that culture is that will underly all political differences between states after the end of the Cold War.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The same culture that lead to a progressive parliamentarian democracy in the Netherlands led to Apartheid in South Africa.</li><li>Korea, due to its Asian culture, was thought to be despotic in nature.<ul><li>This idea that Asian states are naturally despotic have even been used by Asian dictators to legitimize their rule.</li></ul></li><li>Turkey, due to its Islamic culture, ought to resemble the other Middle Eastern countries, rather than adopting Western legal and political institutions.<ul><li>I think that this is debatable through, since Turkey has long had a much greater exposure to the West and it’s institutions.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a><ul><li>A relevant distinction that needs to be made in order for this point to stand.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>This could potentially be used as a point to support this argument, since the vastly different cultures of China and India lead to vastly different institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a><ul><li>Again, this would be another point that supports the idea of cultural determinism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Same as above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a><ul><li>Modernization theory is something that, although debated in and of itself, refutes the idea of Cultural Determinism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India\">Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India</a><ul><li>An example of Culture’s impact on institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>Another example.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>A related idea of Geographical Determinism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development\">Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development</a><ul><li>An argument against this idea; a general rejection of Cultural Determinism for a more general idea of conflict being the result of opposing ideologies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Culture has become the most important factor in determining the identity of people in a nation. Nations that are significantly divided between cultures is known as a “torn country.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that a nation’s stability can partly be determined by the cultural homogeneity present in its society.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Culture becoming the most important factor in the way that a society views itself means that</p><ul><li>There is the creation of out-groups, leading to feeling of superiority, fear and lack of trust in people of different cultures, differences in communication, and lack of familiarity.</li></ul><p>The cultural question is one that cannot be negotiated, since it is a zero-sum game. Additionally, generally, it seems that nations with more diversity of culture tends to be more unstable than nations with more homogeneity of culture.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a> <ul><li>Specifically, the culture of the West in decline in the areas of the world previously dominated by Western culture.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself\">A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself</a><ul><li>How a country that is cleavage by culture can reshape itself.</li></ul></li></ul>","Cultures and institutions are different":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference between culture and institutions is threefold: culture arises naturally, while institutions are formed through deliberately constructed systems; cultures unconsciously shape people, while institutions function through formal rules; and cultures are hard to transplant, while institutions can be copied or imposed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial, because the difference between the two concepts is often forgotten, or ignored. Understanding the difference between the two concepts is also crucial for understanding Huntington’s thesis regarding Cultural Determinism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Cultures can influence institutions, but the way institutions develop is not solely determined by culture.<ul><li>The same cultural background can develop into vastly different institutions.<ul><li>Calvinism: pillarization in the Dutch Republic, autocracy in the Prussian state, and Apartheid in South America.</li></ul></li><li>The Athenian institution of democracy has spread and become a global phenomenon, but Athenian culture has not.<ul><li>Democracy was also not something that emerged out of Athenian culture. It emerged as a reaction against Athenian culture at the time.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development\">Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development</a></li></ul>","Currency devaluation causes unemployment because capital holders withdraw from the real economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>During times of monetary contraction, capital holders withdraw from the real economy since they are afraid of inflation eating up interests. As a result, interest rates rise, borrowing becomes difficult, and the economy slows.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a downside of currency devaluation, since it eats at economics growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>High inflation raises interest rates due to the fisher effect, and high interest rates leads to lower investment since people are less willing to borrow money when it is more expensive to do so. Less investment means that there is less growth in TFP, which means that economic growth may potentially slow.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>High inflation is a circumstance of high economics uncertainty.</li></ul></li></ul>","Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core principles of Daoism, and is crucial for understanding its impact on Chinese culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>One should strive for simplicity in their living, abandon education and self-improvement, and be self-sustaining. Additionally, one should withdraw from worldly affairs.</li><li>While Daoists do want to make the world better, it does not seek to do so through direct action, as that would lead to artificiality and the prevalence of self-interest as a motivating factor.</li><li>Argues for \"non-action,\" or 无为.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>During the development of a system, it is better to have decentralization, as it allows for independence and autonomy which can aid with growth. However, when it has been developed to a certain extent, more control should be exerted over the system to ensure no unwanted/misguided growth, decay, or destabilization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important when examining the growth of states and their maintenance.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fragmentation of military power within a state weakens central power\">The fragmentation of military power within a state weakens central power</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Diction that sustains two readings at once compresses a great deal into very little, so that a line can operate simultaneously on the literal, the metaphorical and the topical register.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the mechanism behind literary terseness, and it explains why paraphrase of good writing is always longer than the original.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In <em>Hamlet</em> 1.2.74 and 1.2.76 the word “common” means first what is shared by all humanity and then what is low and trite, within two lines.</li><li>“A certain convocation of politic worms are e’en at him. Your worm is your only emperor for diet” (4.3.22-24) puns on the Diet of Worms, casting Hamlet as Luther destroying Claudius by argument, so the line works metaphorically and religiously at once.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The criterion this device serves.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased\">Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased</a><ul><li>Why the compression cannot be undone without loss.</li></ul></li></ul>","Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Deliberation is defined by Aristotle as what occurs when the right course of action is not clearly defined, and reason/logic is needed to find the correct means.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Understanding deliberation is important for understanding Aristotle’s definition of choice, which is important for his definitions of virtue and vice.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>We only deliberate about the means, and not the end, since ends are set by character and desire. So, for example, we do not deliberate “should I become more healthy?” rather, we deliberate “what should I eat today to become more healthy?” The final result of a deliberation is the “first action,” i.e., the first step in the means that eventually brings one to the end. Deliberation is also not concerned with particular facts, as such facts are the matter and concern for sense perception. For instance, we do not deliberate “Am I feeling something soft?”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end\">A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end</a><ul><li>Choice requires deliberation.</li></ul></li></ul>","Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Democracies are doomed to always be mediocre since the people in power are, in the end, selected by the average (mediocre) people.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Mills argument for tolerance, since in doing so, more people of “strong character” can be formed and thus bring the society out of mediocrity.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American democracy is too populist\">American democracy is too populist</a><ul><li>The reason that populism is bad is because the people who populists appeal to are merely mediocre, and thus don’t truly understand what is good for themselves or the country.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>Again, this could because mediocre people are choosing the people who run the government.</li></ul></li></ul>","Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>Democracy and constitutional liberalism are not the same thing, and can be unlinked from each other.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>Understanding this helps us better analyze the situation present in many nations, where there may be the lack of one or another, but often, we may just say that the country lacks “democracy.”</p><h4>The Difference</h4><p>Democracy refers to the process by which leaders are election, namely, through a free and open election. While this would entail some necessary freedoms, such as freedom of the press and association, it does not require a lot. Constitutional Liberalism refers to the existence of legal-political institutions that are “constitutional” and “liberal.” This would include things like the protection of property rights, the enshrinment of certain political rights such as speech, gun ownership, or religion, or <em>habeus corpus</em> and the rule of law.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Many today are forgetting the important of constitutional liberalism and the importance of process, and I think that this is partly because people are conflating democracy and constitutional liberalism and especially a disregard for the latter. Without the rule of law, the state does suffering from a lack of information and transparency that eventually puts it behind the nations that have rule of law.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>An explication of the contents in this note which puts it into practice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Deontic powers, which are desire-independent reasons for acting, are built upon institutional facts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Deontic power, or reasons for acting that goes beyond simple desire to do something, are created through institutional facts. This is because deontic power relates to the rights, duties, and obligations that come with institutional facts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates the consequences of having institutional facts.</p><h4>References</h4>","Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In general, deregulation and laissez-faire is good for the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a general approach for how the economy should be run.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is especially true when the following three criteria are met:</p><ul><li><strong>Demand is relatively inelastic:</strong> under such circumstances, taxes distort supply without lowering price.</li><li><strong>High costs are common:</strong> high probability of inefficient firms means that many necessary producers could be excluded.</li><li><strong>Low redistributive motive:</strong> there are some other reason for caring about firm profits.</li></ul><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The deregulation of the airline industry in the 1970s:<ul><li>This drastically reduced the price of taking a flight with a decrease in safety. Prices fell by about 1/3.</li><li>Many more routes were offered.</li><li>Quality fell, and airlines moved to competition based on prices rather than quality.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is a reason why regulation would be bad.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"France’s strict regulation, high worker benefits, and high pensions are crippling the economy\">France’s strict regulation, high worker benefits, and high pensions are crippling the economy</a><ul><li>An example of regulation being bad.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>A set of problems with a highly regulated economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Desert and the Basic Structure":"<h4>Question</h4><p>Is desert prior to the institution that distributes, or produced by it?</p><h4>Thesis</h4><p>Aristotle’s proportional justice and Rawls’ reciprocity argument cannot both be held, and it is the concession to personal desert in <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy</a> that makes this my problem rather than theirs.</p><h4>The Map</h4><p>Both accounts treat justice as the governing virtue of distribution, and both hold that shares should track something about persons. They disagree about what that something is and, more consequentially, about whether it obtains prior to the institution doing the distributing. Call the first position <em>pre-institutional desert</em> (p-desert) and the second <em>institutional entitlement</em> (i-desert), following the distinction I take from Feinberg and McLeod in <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">the meritocracy essay</a>. On the Aristotelian side, justice is <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">the sovereign virtue</a>, and distribution within it is governed by <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">proportion to merit</a>. Crucially, that proportional form is not peculiar to political distribution, since it also structures <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality\">unequal private relations</a>; proportionality is therefore a feature of right relation as such rather than an artifact of any particular constitution. The corrective apparatus points the same way, in that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals\">rectification</a> and <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Equity is the rectification of legal justice\">equity</a> both presuppose an antecedently correct distribution to restore, and there is nothing to restore unless the pre-transaction shares were already the right ones. So does the claim that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly\">injustice cannot be suffered voluntarily</a>, which locates injustice in what is done to a person rather than in the rules under which it is done. On this account the institution does not create the desert base; it reads it. Rawls arrives at the opposite conclusion by elimination. Neither <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">careers open to talents</a> nor <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies\">the principle of efficiency</a>, which is the same argumentative move applied twice, leaves the contingencies untouched, and what survives treats <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">natural endowment as a common asset</a>. The load-bearing claim is that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">desert presupposes a cooperative scheme rather than justifying it</a>, so no appeal to desert can be lodged against the basic structure without circularity. Everything else follows from restricting justice to that structure, viz. that it governs <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions\">institutions and not individual dealings</a> and not <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily\">voluntary associations</a>; the internal ordering is then <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">lexical</a> and <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">indexed to primary goods</a> rather than to merit, of which <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Self-respect is the most important primary good\">self-respect is the most important</a>. Here the institution does not read desert off persons; it constitutes the only sense in which they have any. The two clusters collide at a single point. <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Proportional justice</a> makes merit the measure against which a distribution is assessed, and <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">the reciprocity argument</a> denies that merit can serve as a measure of a distribution at all, since merit is intelligible only once the distribution is in place. Neither note links to the other and neither registers the conflict, which is how it has survived.</p><h4>Pressure</h4><p>Three replies are available, and the second is the one that would actually cost me something.</p><ol><li><em>The scope reply.</em> Aristotle describes justice within a constitution he takes as given, so the claim that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">justice is fully realised only in a city</a> carries the weight and proportional justice was never a pre-institutional claim at all. This dissolves the contradiction by making Aristotle an i-desert theorist, at the cost of stripping proportional justice of critical purchase: it could assess distributions inside a constitution but never the constitution itself, which is the assessment that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">the priority of justice</a> demands.</li><li><em>The virtue reply.</em> Merit for Aristotle is virtue rather than talent or product, and virtue is achieved rather than endowed, so the natural-contingency argument never reaches it. The cost here is an empirical commitment, namely that the capacity for virtue is itself independent of natural and social contingency, which <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The world is unfair and amoral\">the amorality of luck</a> denies outright and which <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly\">the experimental result on meritocratic belief</a> gives further reason to doubt.</li><li><em>Rejection.</em> Aristotle is simply refuted by the reciprocity argument. This is the cheapest reply and the least useful, since it leaves unexplained why the proportional intuition is as durable as <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics\">a good conception of justice</a> requires our firmest convictions to be.</li></ol><h4>Verdict</h4><p>I hold the virtue reply provisionally, which commits me to defending a capacity for virtue that survives the contingency objection, and that defence is not written anywhere in this vault. The wider consequence is for <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">the meritocracy essay</a>: it concedes personal desert in order to deflect Zaitchik, and Aristotle is precisely a personal-desert account meant to govern institutional distribution in the polis. Either the concession is broader than I intended, in which case the institutional argument faces pressure from a direction it never addresses, or Aristotle is a counterexample to the clean separation of p-desert from i-desert that the essay relies on. The essay cannot say which, because it never reads Aristotle.</p><h4>Notes to Write</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotelian merit is virtue rather than endowment, and so escapes the natural contingency objection</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Proportional justice cannot assess the constitution that makes proportion measurable</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Personal desert and institutional desert cannot be cleanly separated</span></li></ul><h4>Loose Ends</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle makes false assumptions about people&#x27;s rationality\">The difference principle makes false assumptions about people's rationality</a><ul><li>The other line of attack on the Rawlsian cluster, independent of this one and not used above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>A historical case where institutional merit is the explicit design principle; a test of the scope reply.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Justice</span><ul><li>The canvas laying out the divisions of justice that the Aristotelian paragraph walks through.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>The source note where the desert-based framing originates.</li></ul></li></ul>","Despite some civilizational convergence, there is no universality in culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although some aspects of the world have become more similar, on the whole, civilizational differences still define us more than similarities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes the idea that there is some universal cultural element to humanity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The spread of Western consumption patterns and pop culture does not relate to the creation of a universal civilization.<ul><li>The spread of Western media does not either:<ul><li>There is no data for how widespread it actually is.</li><li>Different cultures could interpret the same Western media in different ways.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Language has also not promoted the creation of a universal culture.<ul><li>English facilitates <em>inter-cultural</em> exchange, but this does not means that there is a universal civilization.</li><li>The frequency of a language’s use is also tied to the relative ability of the mother nation to exert control, and the West (specifically the US)’s control of this is slipping.</li></ul></li><li>Greater commercialization and trade across cultures does not necessarily mean the creation of a universal civilization.<ul><li>Economic interdependence could either by peace-inducing or war-inducing.</li></ul></li><li>Modern civilization could be more similar to each other than traditional ones, however:<ul><li>There is shared technology and fast exchange of ideas, information, and trends.</li><li>It is industrial, rather than agricultural, meaning that geography plays less of a role in determining the way a state is (and thus causing differences between states).</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>This idea somewhat clashes with the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a><ul><li>Specific example of the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests\">The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests</a><ul><li>This is an example of this note in practice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Civilization is the process by which time preferences diminish\">Civilization is the process by which time preferences diminish</a><ul><li>But this seems to be something that happens universally has civilizations become more advanaced?</li></ul></li></ul>","Determinism and free will are incompatible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that determinism and free will are incompatible (i.e., they cannot both be true at the same time). Does not say whether determinism or free will is correct.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the first step to proving either hard determinism, or libertarianism.</p><h4>Arguments</h4><p><strong>The Consequence Argument</strong></p><ul><li>If determinism is true, then our actions are the necessary consequence of the laws of nature and events in the distant past.</li><li>You cannot change the past, nor can you change the laws of nature.</li><li>Therefore, you cannot change the consequences of them (your actions).</li><li>Therefore, compatibilism is false.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Determinism does not entail that future events are predictable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even if a system is deterministic, it seems that such a system is not determined in such a way that the future is able to be predicted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to weaken the thesis of determinism quite a lot, since it deflates what it means for a system to be “deterministic.”</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The fact that a deterministic system might not entail that its future is predictable is from the fact that if knowledge of a prediction is made known in the real world, it seems that there is nothing stopping agents from merely acting in a way that contradicts the prediction itself. If this is the case, then what does it mean for a system to be determined in the first place? Usually, determined means that a future outcome is able to be predicted based on some presents facts, but if this is not possible, then it seems that humans must have free will to some extent.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism\">Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism</a><ul><li>This note seems to argue against this point.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Developing countries can be stuck in the middle income trap":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Developing nations, when they emerge out of low-income status, may get trapped in the so-called “middle income trap,” unable to join the ranks of the high-income nations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common issue for developing nations, as they can remain stuck in the middle income trap if they do not successfully escape the trap.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Nations can be stuck in the middle income trap for many reasons:</p><ol><li>Lack of required technologies needed to advance to high-income status.</li><li>Lack of strong rule of law, means that long-term growth is difficult.</li><li>Lack of human capital.</li><li>Reliance on commodities, which means the country relies on selling raw materials rather than developing a diversified economic base.</li></ol><h4>Successes</h4><p>Countries like the Four Asian Tigers were able to escape the trap due to their investments in education and high tech industries.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">The 'Resource Curse' can often prevent nations from attaining high-income status</span><ul><li>One of the reasons listed in this note for a reason that a country may be trapped in middle-income status.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Examples of economics policies adopted by the nations that successfully escaped the middle income trap.</li></ul></li></ul>","Dirigisme describes a focus on internal management; Neomercantilism describes a focus on external management":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Dirigisme describes a state that manages internal economic affairs, whereas Neomercantilism describes a state that maintains always a current account surplus through government intervention.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The terms are similar in that both describe a state’s control of the economy, but one is specifically referring the management of the economy as an internal measure, and the other describes the management of an economy as the management of some external feature.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism\">Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism</a></li></ul>","Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Apparent disagreements about whether we have free will typically resolve into disagreements about what control condition moral responsibility requires, so the metaphysical dispute is downstream of an ethical one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This reframes the whole debate: one cannot settle whether free will exists without first fixing what it is needed for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The point has a deflationary consequence. If free will just is whatever control condition responsibility requires, then two parties who agree on all the facts about causation can still disagree about free will, because they are disagreeing about desert.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences\">Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences</a><ul><li>The strongest version of the control condition at issue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Retributive punishment is never justified\">Retributive punishment is never justified</a><ul><li>What follows if the required control condition is never satisfied.</li></ul></li></ul>","Disregarding advice grounded in others’ experience is the characteristic failure of self-reliance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The self-reliant person’s distinctive error is not overestimating their own capacity but discounting testimony, since the knowledge that would have saved them exists and is offered, and is refused because of its source.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This separates a defensible independence of judgment from the disposition that makes it fatal, which matters because the two are usually praised together.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In <em>To Build A Fire</em> the protagonist has been told by an old-timer not to travel alone below a certain temperature, and the advice is both correct and free. He rejects it as the caution of an old man.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not good for man to be alone\">It is not good for man to be alone</a><ul><li>The broader claim about solitude that this specifies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced\">Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced</a><ul><li>The source of the testimony being discounted.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time\">Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time</a><ul><li>The capacity that refusing testimony forfeits.</li></ul></li></ul>","Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals since they constantly side track us. A potential effect of this is our eventual dismay at the fact that we have wasted our lives pursuing distractions rather than what we actually wanted to do.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is both a topic covered in various works of literature and also a fundamental aspect of modern society, where distractions abound.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>With so many distractions in the modern world, it becomes increasingly more difficult to not only find one’s purpose, but also stick to one’s purpose without being distracted. In <em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em>, Harry is constantly sidetracked by his worldly pursuits, which eventually leads to his inability to pursue his true art, writing.</p><h4>References</h4>","Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Distributive Justice concerns when things are being divided up. It states that each individual should receive the amount of something relative to their level of merit; otherwise, it is injustice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Distributive Justice is one of the major types of justice that Aristotle discusses.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>It seems that this idea directly contrasts with Rawls’ idea, since argues based on the idea that merit is inherent, and distribution is on top of that. Rawls argues that opposite.</li></ul></li></ul>","Divided leadership compromises the effectiveness of an army":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is often one of the major problems that can compromise an army, since divided leadership means that the army is unable to act as a coordinated whole.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was one of the major factors that cause military failures.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Examples of this can be found all through out the Second Punic war, especially at the battles of the Trebia, Trasimene, and Cannae.</p><h4>References</h4>","Divine providence is nothing but the collective instinct of mankind":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That the idea of providence in human history has been nothing but a representation of the collective instincts of humans.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was proposed by Proudhon as a means of secularizing the concept of providence and providing a secular explanation for the reason of its being.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>History thus does not advance as a result of providence. Rather, it is concrete human progress (replacing antiquated notions and cultural mores with updated ones) that represents true historical progress. In this way, we have to reject the concept of divine providence.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p.63</p><p>Far from being directed by providential destiny, history advances by revolutionary crises that give birth to new conceptions of justice. The first crisis was provoked by Jesus when he proclaimed man's equality before God. The second was inaugurated by the Reformation and Descartes, achieving equality before conscience and reason. The third began with the French Revolution and established equality before the law. The coming revolution, which is economic and social, will mark the end of the religious, aristocratic, and bourgeois age. It will bring about final equality by the \"equation of man with humanity.\" <strong>To effect this ultimate advance, man has to take up the eternal fight between man and God and decide it; for God, or the Absolute, is the one great obstacle to human progress and the one great source of all kinds of absolutism-economic, political, religious.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>Note</h4><p>Yet, the very idea of achieving something greater, the idea that the universe even has a <em>telos</em> at all, is something that is, by nature, Christian. Therefore, Proudhon, though he blasphemed and attacked Christianity, is attempting to merely create an alternative for the eschatological niche carved originally by Christianity. He merely argues for the “defatalization” of the Christian doctrine of providence, not its abolition.</p><h4>References</h4>","Dominant-end moral theories fail":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A dominant-end theory is an approach to rational deliberation whereby the result is one overarching goal, with all other ends being merely subordinate means to that ultimate end. Rawls argues that this view is impossible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument against dominant-end theories in general.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls makes three key points:</p><ol><li>There is no plausible option for the actual dominant end.<ul><li>Happiness cannot be it since happiness is the result of executing a rational plan of life (see the Thin Theory of the Good).</li><li>Many other proposals seem inhuman if they are presented as the overarching goal of human life.</li></ul></li><li>The vagueness of a dominant-end theory means that it does not actually solve the problem that it claims to. For instance, if happiness if the dominant end, then how does one define happiness?</li><li>Human good seems to be heterogeneous, and not convergent upon some ultimate, singular end.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a><ul><li>This unrealistic assumption is related to Rawls’ third objection here, and is part of the reason why it is unrealistic.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>The critiques here are related to Rawls’ 2nd critique in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Double membrane, circular chromosome, bacteria-like ribosomes, and independent replication are evidence for endosymbiosis":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If an organelle has a double membrane, circular chromosome (like a bacteria), bacteria-like ribosomes, and independent replication through binary fission, they are most likely an organelle that was endocytosed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These four pieces of evidence can indicate whether an organelle was originally independent and became an organelle after being endocytosed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some cell organelles are related to bacteria\">Some cell organelles are related to bacteria</a></li></ul>","Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, it leads to competitors attempting to create something that is similar, leading to much duplicate research being conducted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important as this flaw of the patent system is not often recognized.</p><h4>Caveat</h4><p>At the same time, if there was no patent system, it seems that people would lack incentive to conduct research anyways.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a></li></ul>","During Prophase I, homologous chromosomes meet in a process called synapsis":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>After S phase, there are two copies of every chromatid. Since you have two copies for each chromosome (each one having come from your parents) even before that, you are left with 4 chromatids. During Prophase I, the sister chromatids come together to form a tetrad (four chromatids) in a process called synapsis.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important, since if the homologous chromosomes do not meet together during Prophase I, they would not be separated correctly in order for Meiosis II to occur.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When two genes are farther apart, it is more likely that recombination will occur\">When two genes are farther apart, it is more likely that recombination will occur</a><ul><li>What synapsis makes possible. Once the tetrad forms, crossing over can occur, and distance governs how often.</li></ul></li></ul>","Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The first principle in Rawls’ two fundamental principles of justice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is fundamental to Rawls’ theory of justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The more precise statement: Equal person is to have an equal right to the most extensive basic liberty compatible with a similar liberty for others. Additionally, this condition has lexical priority over the second condition (i.e., fair equality of opportunity and the difference principle). This principle is derived from the general conception of justice by a rational agent.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>The second principle, likewise derived via the general conception of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","East of Eden":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A sweeping family saga set in California’s Salinas Valley that follows the intertwined lives of the Trask and Hamilton families across generations. At its center are the Trask brothers, Adam and Charles, and later Adam’s twin sons, Aron and Cal, whose struggle with jealousy, love, guilt, and the search for identity echoes the biblical story of Cain and Abel. The novel explores the idea of <em>timshel</em>—the belief that people are not doomed by fate or sin but have the power to choose between good and evil.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is considered to be Steinbeck’s best work, and one of the best American novels ever written.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral mastery is achieved through choice\">Moral mastery is achieved through choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not good for man to be alone\">It is not good for man to be alone</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Man is created in the image of God\">Man is created in the image of God</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People sin so that they may be loved\">People sin so that they may be loved</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is the root cause of immorality\">Lying is the root cause of immorality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One&#x27;s fate is not determined by their birth\">One's fate is not determined by their birth</a></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Cyrus Trask</strong></p><ul><li>Intelligent, but rash and obstinate. A liar (he lied about his experiences as a soldier) (19).<ul><li>He falls entirely under his own lie, and by living his lie, he creates a life for himself.<ul><li>It is interesting how although he himself was a terrible soldier, he creates this illusion of being a great soldier around him, and he idealizes soldiers as honorable people (24).</li><li>In fact, he forces his children to live under this lie with him.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He has an obsession with discipline.<ul><li>He has a unique sense of justice that seems strict and idiosyncratic.<ul><li>For instance, he seems to love Adam more, since his mother killed herself, and he possibly never forgave himself for her death, and felt that he owed Adam.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>His knowledge/understanding of the world is entirely built upon his understanding of military strategy? (26)<ul><li>He also says how one can drive someone too far, and thus must always leave a way out. This is reminiscent of military strategy.</li><li>“If you can endure the worst of life, you can experience the best of it.”</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Charles Trask</strong></p><ul><li>Disciplined and violent.<ul><li>He seems like a man of contradictions.</li><li>He beats up Adam when they are young.<ul><li>It could be that he beats him up out of love? He is trying to get Adam to fight back, but he never does, and that is why he keeps on beating him up. <ul><li>To get Adam to fight back would mean that he conquered his fear, and thus his father would no longer love him as much?</li><li>This would “equalize” them in Cyrus’ eyes, and perhaps that would make Charles feel better?</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He loves Cyrus, and does not like Adam because Cyrus only loves Adam and not him.<ul><li>However, Cyrus does not love him back, and only seems to love Adam. This is why Charles has such enmity against Adam in the book.<ul><li>Cyrus days that Charles has no fears (and therefore no honor?) and therefore he does not love Charles. But this is not true. <ul><li>Ironically, it is clear that Charles fears not being loved by Cyrus (36).</li><li>It is also stated that he fears women (45).</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Charles seems like a man defeated by the world. He offers no resistance to it. Perhaps this is because everything he does is in an effort to win the respect of his father, but he no longer gets the chance to do this after his father is gone?</li><li>Charles warms up to Adam after he learns about him escaping prison.<ul><li>Perhaps he thinks that Adam has finally “earned” his love from Cyrus. </li><li>Perhaps he thinks that Adam is no longer perfect, and thus would no longer earn the love of Cyrus, and so they are essentially on equal footing.</li></ul></li><li>He is afraid that the money that Cyrus got was illegitimate since that would undermine his perfect conception of his father in his head. This is perhaps why he is always stressing out about the money.</li></ul></li><li>He is able to see through Cathy, where no one else has been able to.</li><li>He is like a combination of Ivan Karamazov and Dimitri Karamazov. He is cold, but at times impulsive and emotional. He embraces the base aspects of the world, but is not entirely caught up in it, since he manages it against his noble side as well.</li><li>Charles’ living alone is akin to him having killed Abel. It is also during this time that he gets his own “Mark of Cain.” (66)</li></ul><p><strong>Adam Trask</strong></p><ul><li>Aimless and weak-willed, but good hearted.<ul><li>He is like Alyosha.<ul><li>He tries to suffer for the sin of others (Cathy)? At least he falls in love with someone with sexually deviant tendencies, much like Alyosha does with Lisa.</li></ul></li><li>He is aimless in life, and seems to simply just wander around.<ul><li>He joins the army, and falls into its routine, even though he hates killing people.</li><li>He rejoins the army because he missed the routine that the army brought him.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He hates Cyrus, though he has faith in him.<ul><li>He hates Cyrus because Cyrus forces him to join the army.</li><li>He has faith in Cyrus perhaps because he has no love for him. He simply understands Cyrus as an authority figure (70). </li></ul></li><li>His experience escaping the prison seems to have changed him, as after that point, he begins to have more goals and aspirations, rather than simply going where life takes him.<ul><li>He is more confrontational with Charles, and he decides to go to California.</li><li>He marries Cathy, but pisses off Charles. He marries Cathy because:<ul><li>Cathy is attractive.</li><li>He felt bad for her.</li><li>He feels a connection with her (he was also beaten up).</li><li>Cathy manipulates him into believing that she loved him.</li></ul></li><li>Adam projects who he wants Cathy to be onto the actual Cathy, and fails to see what lies beneath this false facade (262).<ul><li>Biblical reference: Eve is created from Adam’s rib, but she is also the one that leads him to sin.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Adam never really redeems himself?<ul><li>He continued to live in lies, first the lie from Cyrus, then the lie from Cathy, then the lying to Aron about Cathy. </li><li>It is the lying to Aron that would eventually (though indirectly) cause his death. </li><li>Perhaps he was able to redeem himself on his deathbed?</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Cathy Ames</strong></p><ul><li>Literally the devil incarnate?<ul><li><s>She is a little like Fyodor Pavlovitch.</s> She is far more like Ivan.</li><li>Emotionless and focused on her goals.<ul><li>She shoots Adam not to kill him, but merely because he stood in her way.</li></ul></li><li>She is unable to see/believe the goodness of others, because she herself is evil.<ul><li>Perhaps the reason why her meeting with Adam shook her so much is because she never thought that Adam would do something so good for her. This forced her to leave her ability to isolate herself from the “chain of goodness” that Adam connected to her.<ul><li>This chain started from Sam?</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Sexually deviant, evil, manipulative, and attractive (<em>femme fatale?</em>).<ul><li>Enters the employ of Mr. Edwards to take advantage of him.</li><li>Kills her own family in an act of arson because she dislikes them.</li><li>Marries Adam to take advantage of him.</li></ul></li><li>Feels a connection to Charles?<ul><li>Perhaps deep down, they are the same. Charles just has an aspect of his soul advocating for the noble, repressing his base.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Samuel Hamilton</strong></p><ul><li>Morally righteous, smart, warm, and kind. </li><li>He is the opposite of Cathy, and whereas Adam is also the opposite of Cathy in that he is lethargic and directionless, Sam is merely opposite in his motives.</li><li>He is bad a making good, though he is a good person.</li><li>He is an immigrant from Ireland, and this gives him an outside perspective on things, and allows him to see things for effectively.<ul><li>He believes in nurture far more than nature. He thinks that people can change, it is the upbringing and the people around then that results in the character of a person, not solely their inherent nature.<ul><li>But would Cathy disprove this? She seems to be incorrigible.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Lee</strong></p><ul><li>Wise, intelligent, and contained.</li><li>He puts on a facade so that others won’t realize his actual power and knowledge and abilities.</li><li>He is pragmatic.</li></ul><h4>The Naming</h4><ul><li>Samuel, in his talking about mediocrity and greatness, thinks that being great means taking on responsibility and initiative in your life, but it also means the greatest sense of loneliness.<ul><li>This loneliness could come from the fact that you are individually burdened with responsibility for others and you are essentially suffering for the sins of others.</li></ul></li><li>But at the same time, he acknowledges that there may be benefits to being great.<ul><li>He considers himself as little selfish that he is placing his son Tom onto a path of greatness, which may bring great rewards, but is also isolating.</li></ul></li><li>This is all essentially to warn Adam about the consequences of his lackluster lifestyle. He presents Adam with the choice between taking action and becoming great or simply resigning.</li></ul><p><em>Guilt</em></p><ul><li>Guilt is used as a means of excusing ourselves from responsibility because it connects us with people in the past, all the way back to Cain, who have sinned.</li></ul><p><em>Cain and Abel</em></p><ul><li>“Reject is the thing that he fears.”</li><li>Directly connects to the tale between Charles and Adam.<ul><li>It is a template of a universal story, something that occurs generation after generation.<ul><li><strong>Is every story a story of Cain and Abel, a story of rejection and fear and anger?</strong></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Similar to the idea of sin being hell from TBK. We fear confession because we fear rejection by others. <ul><li>TBK offers a solution for this.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Brothers Karamazov\">The Brothers Karamazov</a><ul><li>This book explores many themes that are explored in <em>TBK</em> as well.</li></ul></li></ul>","Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As history advances, humans have become more rationalistic, and thus, the religious motive is abandoned in the spirit of capitalism, and religious compulsion is no longer needed to justify economic acquisition.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Weber’s final step in his argue for the development of the spirit of capitalism, as once the religious element fades away, all that is left is the modern conception of capitalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>The idea that gets detached.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Once the calling is gone, traditionalism loses its religious opponent.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","Economic conclusions can follow from metaphysical commitments rather than from observation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle’s economic views are built upon the specific metaphysical views that he held, and are not necessarily based on observed/empirical economics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of his economics, and provides context for the reasons that he makes specific claims.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Based on his metaphysical views of the four causes, the formal cause, efficient cause, material cause, and final cause, things essentially have what he considers a “proper use” which is related to the thing’s final cause. Therefore, Aristotle considers money-making to be excessive greed since money does not have a final cause beyond having the ability to be exchanged for other goods. Therefore, it is improper to merely seek money.</p><h4>References</h4>","Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is one of the main theses in Friedman’s <em>Capitalism and Freedom</em>, with him arguing that it is economic freedom and underlies political freedom. Without economic freedom, political freedom cannot stand.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a strong argument for promoting economic freedom, since political freedom is the cornerstone of so many democracies worldwide.</p><h4>References</h4>","Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Economic growth and productivity does not have a direction relationship, that is, there is economic growth doesn’t means that there has been an increase in productivity and vice versa.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common misconception: that economic growth is just driven by productivity growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Economic growth can occur without commensurate increases in productivity: an increase in available resources (labor/capital). At the same time, even if productivity may rise, a decrease in available resources may diminish the effect of productivity growth, or override it all together.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The effects of capital deepening are uncertain\">The effects of capital deepening are uncertain</a><ul><li>Capital deepening can also be a cause of economic growth.</li></ul></li></ul><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth\">A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth</a><ul><li>While this note makes a case that economic growth is not simply productivity growth, productivity growth still plays a large part in economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"SNAP does not cause economic growth\">SNAP does not cause economic growth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it is commonly claimed that low-income individuals are more likely to suffer from subjective well-being and mental health deficiencies, a recent meta-study has shown that this is not the case. There is no correlation between inequality and mental health being undermined.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the common view that inequality contributes to greater mental health problems and lower subjective well-being.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This does not mean that low-income individuals do not always experience greater mental health problems and lower subjective well-being. It is simply stating that the link between these measures are not as clear and robust as one might initially think.</p><h4>References</h4>","Economic liberalization is positively correlated with democratization":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Economic liberalization is often a cause of democratization in many countries, a fact that is proven through empirical evidence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a third view that regards the relationship between capitalism and democracy, and presents an empirical evidence in support of this view.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are at least two main observations that could explain this correlation:</p><ol><li>Capitalist policies promote growth, allowing for newly accrued economic benefits to be distributed to all people in the system. Redistribution does not produce growth.</li><li>Democratic socialism collapses into tyranny, and thus cannot exist. Economic centralization provides too much power to those in power; “power corrupts and absolute power corrupts absolutely.”</li></ol><p>Some argue that countries such as the UAE, Hong Kong, or Singapore stand as proof that economic liberalization does not indicate a growth in democracy, though I would argue that these nations are not truly capitalist, incorporating far more dirigisme and neo-mercantilist elements.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>This seems to refute the claims made in this note, since it seems that while some of these countries have become democratic, or where democratic when such policies were implemented, some have not become democratic.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>Perhaps the fundamental nature of the society plays a larger role. Despite the economic liberalization in China, it has yet to be politically liberalized.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a><ul><li>This seems to be the underlying principle of the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>The claims in the current note seems to align with the idea here, since economic liberalization is something recent, and it does seem to correlate with the rise of democracy in the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>For liberalization to occur, private property is required.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>Huntington would argue that the ideas represented in this note are wrong.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>So maybe this isn’t the case?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism undermines democracy\">Capitalism undermines democracy</a><ul><li>Related to the above.</li></ul></li></ul>","Economic tax incidence is based on relative price elasticity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Who the tax actually affects is the product of relative price elasticities between the consumers and producers. The side that is more inelastic bears more of the tax.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the mechanism that economic tax incidence operates by.</p><h4>Diagram</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For regulators, taxes are generally more effective tools than bans\">For regulators, taxes are generally more effective tools than bans</a><ul><li>This note points out something that regulators should consider.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system\">Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system</a><ul><li>Perhaps tax can be rebated from the side that benefited more to the side that benefitted less?</li></ul></li></ul>","Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Economists tend the overestimate the negative effects of tariffs, since in theory, they are detrimental to the economy. However, other factors may make it so that the effects of tariffs are not as extremely as originally predicted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant since the Trump administration has implemented many tariffs, which economists claim would wreck the US economy, despite that not having happened.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several factors that may explain why the effect of tariffs may not be as bad as predicted:</p><ul><li>Specifically regarding the Trump administration’s tariffs, many of the highest tariffs have been reduced, which means that their economic effect is diminished.</li><li>Exports only make up around 11% of the American economy.</li><li>Booms in specific sectors of the economy may make up for the dent caused by tariffs.</li><li>Businesses may temporarily absorb the increased costs brought on by tariffs.</li></ul><p>However, in the longer-term, tariffs may increase uncertainty in the economy, and thus be detrimental.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul><p>-</p>","Eels":"<h4>Notes</h4><h4>References</h4>","Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a charged item is brought next to a neutral conductor, the conductor is polarized (i.e., the side that is closer to the item accumulates opposite charges.) Such a polarized conductor is said to have an <em>induced charge</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Electrostatic induction will be on the AP test.</p><h4>Connections with Chemistry</h4><ul><li>The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Induction requires free electrons, which is why it needs a conductor.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule\">The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule</a></li></ul>","Elements of Macroeconomics":"<h4>Logistics</h4><ul><li>Office hours: Wednesdays, 10:30 to 11:30.</li></ul><h4>Prior Notes</h4><p>What the vault already claims about macroeconomics. Check a lecture against these before extracting anything, and treat a contradiction as the more interesting result.</p><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a><ul><li>The objectives the whole course is organized around.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing\">GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing</a><ul><li>Worth holding onto while the course uses GDP as its main aggregate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The standard CPI overestimates inflation, since it does not take into account the substitution effect\">The standard CPI overestimates inflation, since it does not take into account the substitution effect</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy\">Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>These two are rival accounts of the same variable, so whichever one the course teaches, the other is the objection.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank\">Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank</a><ul><li>The Austrian account. A standard course will give a different one, which is the disagreement to record.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Quantitative easing is not the same thing as open market operations\">Quantitative easing is not the same thing as open market operations</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long-term bond yields are driven by expectations of inflation, not worries about government debt\">Long-term bond yields are driven by expectations of inflation, not worries about government debt</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation\">The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minimum wage can reduce unemployment\">The minimum wage can reduce unemployment</a></li></ul><h4>Lectures</h4><h5>Lecture 3</h5><ul><li>Wages of the less productive should rise as a result of Baumol’s cost disease?</li><li></li></ul><h4>To Extract</h4><h4>Errors</h4><ul><li>Firms demand labor; they do not supply employment.</li><li>Selling from inventory increases consumption in the GDP calculation, but it also decreases investment, so there is no net effect on GDP.</li><li>When buying from a foreign country, consumption increases, but this is weighed out by the fact that net exports decreases, so there is no net effect GDP.</li></ul>","Elite overproduction could be a cause of social instability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Elite overproduction could be the cause of social instability since when there is an excess supply of potential elite members in society, these elites are not able to achieve the status in society that they were promised, and thus, they will instigate social instability.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These could be a potential reason for social instability.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a><ul><li>This is an example of when this self-correction goes wrong.</li></ul></li></ul>","Encompassing interests mean that an actor's private optimum resembles the social optimum; narrow interests mean that an actor's gains most from rent-seeking":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An encompassing interest is one such that an actor’s private stake is intertwined with the stake of the broader society. This means that the actor will undertake actions that not only benefits itself, but also the entire society in which it exists. A narrow interest is one such that a actor’s private stake is disconnected and opposite the interests of the broader society. This means that it will engage in rent-seeking behavior.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This allows for the categorization of incentives along a spectrum of encompassing to narrow, allowing for the analysis of policy implications along those lines.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A government has an encompassing interest in ensuring stable economic growth, since with economic growth its own tax base expands. Therefore, it will work to ensure that the economy grows. On the other hand, oil companies could be considered a narrow interest. It engages in business activities that harm the environment, which is detrimental to the society around it, although the company itself profits from this damage.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation\">A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide\">Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide</a></li></ul>","Endurance is a crucial component of courage":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Endurance is the ability to withstand pain (whereas continence is the ability to resist the temptation of pleasure). It is closely related to courage, since to the courage, one must first be enduring.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains what endurance is, and what it is important for.</p><h4>Softness &amp; Insensitivity</h4><p>The deficient state of endurance is softness, or an oversensitivity to pain. Such a person flees all hardship, and does not stand their ground. The excessive state of endurance is insensitivity. Such a person experiences pain for no reason.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be courageous is not to be afraid of death\">To be courageous is not to be afraid of death</a><ul><li>To not be afraid of death means not being afraid of pain.</li></ul></li></ul>","Environment variables are global settings that programs can read at runtime":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Environment variables are global settings that programs are able to read when they are run. For example, <code>PATH</code> gives the directories to find executable files.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Environment variables allow for programs to be run on different computers with little changes required (as they are built into all operating systems).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The invention of paper money by the Song allowed for the development of modern economics\">The invention of paper money by the Song allowed for the development of modern economics</a><ul><li>Paper money is analogous to a “global setting,” as it standardizes units of value and makes exchange easier.</li></ul></li></ul>","Equality of opportunity has a formal and a substantive sense, and meritocracy requires both":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Formal equality of opportunity requires that institutions apply their rules impartially to everyone, while substantive equality of opportunity requires that people arrive at the competition with comparable resources, and a meritocracy is a society in which both obtain.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Almost every defence of meritocracy in public argument establishes only the formal sense, which is the weaker of the two and does no work against inherited advantage.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a><ul><li>The Rawlsian version of the substantive requirement.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>Why the formal sense alone is insufficient.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism\">The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism</a><ul><li>The standing obstacle to the substantive sense.</li></ul></li></ul>","Equity is the rectification of legal justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Equity is defined as the rectification of legal justice, meaning the application of the generality of laws to specific circumstances.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Equity is needed to ensure that laws functions smoothly and are able to be used even when circumstances may differ slightly from the one specified in a code of law.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Equity is a kind of justice, and it is better than legal justice (since it rectifies the flaws existent in legal justice), though not as good as absolute justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a> <ul><li>Equity concerns legal justice, which is a subcategory of justice under political justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Essays (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A deeply personal, wide-ranging exploration of the human condition, mixing philosophy, anecdote, self-examination, and reflection on everyday life. Written in the late 16th century, they are notable for their skeptical, curious, and tolerant outlook: Montaigne questions certainty, examines his own thoughts and habits, and argues that understanding oneself is a path to understanding humanity more broadly. Rather than presenting a fixed system, the <em>Essays</em> invite readers into a flexible, wandering form of thinking that values experience, moderation, and honest observation over dogma.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>There are many impactful messages scattered throughout this book, making it one of my most interesting reads.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We have power over nothing except our will\">We have power over nothing except our will</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has to confront their problems head on\">One has to confront their problems head on</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Ethics is grounded in religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That ethics is fundamentally the result of religion, or that the “true” ethics is at least the result of religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the argument made by many religious authorities in order to present religion as something that gives some fundamental order to the world.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It is argued by some that our currently set of moralities is the byproduct of Christianity, such that even when we push back against Christianity today, we are in fact using a Christian set of morality in order to make such an argument.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>But this doesn’t seem to be the nature of the current moral landscape.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>Essentially the argument made here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>Similar to the argument made before.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Even if God exists, we ought not to believe in Him":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the argument that is made by Ivan Karamazov, essentially presenting the idea that because there is so much suffering in the world, the moral order that God is allowing is unacceptable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a main idea that Dostoevsky attempts to take down in his book.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In the novel, these ideas of Ivan are overridden by the philosophy of active love, and also by the fact that they are contradictory. Since Ivan states that there are no laws in the universe, then by what standard is he judging the universe of God?</p><h4>References</h4>","Every action has an end goal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Every action has an end goal, that is, it’s final cause. There are things which are means to an end, and there are also things that are an end in and of themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental premise that underlies Aristotle’s arguments in Book I of the <em>Ethics</em>, since if all things have an end goal, it must be the case that humans have one too.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that things which is an end in and of itself is happiness, or manifestations of happiness is some fashion. It seems that the fact of “all things having an end goal” can only be established through an inductive argument, whereby all things that we observe have an end goal, so, everything must have an end goal.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s two forms of goodness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a><ul><li>There are three potential definitions of happiness for Aristotle, although only one of them, goodness, and the action thereof, Aristotle considers the true definition of happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Everyone in the world should love all other people equally.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the core tenant of Mohism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This principle was proven by <em>Mozi</em> with his “tests of judgement,” i.e., every principle must be judged based on its:</p><ol><li>basis,</li><li>verifiability, and</li><li>applicability.</li></ol><p>Regarding the first, <em>Mozi</em> claims that the principle is endorsed by the gods and the spirits. Regarding the second, he claims that the principle is able to be verified through the senses of the common people. Regarding the third, he claims that the principle has been applied, and it works in practice. Therefore, he believes his principle to be correct. The principle is desirable since its application guarantees the greatest amount of happiness for the greatest amount of people (this is very similar to utilitarian arguments)</p><h4>Note</h4><p>This principle is not something that is innate to humans, but rather needs to be imposed from the outside, via an absolutist monarch.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society\">Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society</a><ul><li>Provides context for which the principles discussed here were derived.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’\">Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’</a><ul><li>This is required for the principle outlined here to be put into practice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>Many of the critiques of utilitarianism could be directed here?</li></ul></li></ul>","Excellencies are positive externalities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Excellencies are essentially an individual’s traits which have broader benefits for the society at large, rather than just benefiting the individual themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>That excellencies are public goods means that we should try to promote them in society. It also has implications for concepts such as shame.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Excellencies are essentially virtues in Rawls’ view, something that is done that is beyond the requirements of conventional morality that is self-centered.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Shame can be natural or moral\">Shame can be natural or moral</a><ul><li>The implications of this for shame.</li></ul></li></ul>","Exempting employer-provided insurance from tax shifts compensation out of wages and expands insurance beyond catastrophic coverage":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because employer-purchased medical care is tax exempt while wages are not, employers pay a larger share of compensation in health benefits than workers would choose, and the resulting pool of pre-paid money turns insurance from protection against catastrophe into pre-payment for routine care.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a feature of American health care that is usually treated as a cultural given, namely that insurance covers ordinary expenses at all.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The exemption is arbitrary on its face, since no comparable exemption exists for food or housing. Removing it would let workers bargain for higher take-home pay and buy the coverage they actually want, which is Friedman’s proposal alongside restricting public programmes to catastrophic cover.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying\">Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying</a><ul><li>The mechanism this tax treatment feeds.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In the US, direct state expenditures are often &#x27;submerged&#x27; through indirect tax breaks\">In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks</a><ul><li>The general pattern of which this exemption is an instance.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same\">Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same</a><ul><li>Why the incidence of the exemption falls on the worker regardless of who nominally receives it.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The primary purpose of fair equality of opportunity is simply to ensure that no one is unjustly treated based on factors outside of their control (i.e., social contingencies).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This reveals the important of justice, since it is placed even above concerns of efficiency.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Rawls elaborates that even if restricting access to positions ultimately would produce better results, such actions would still not be justified. <code>What about for cases such as men wanting to model for female modeling positions? Or patients requesting female doctors but there are equally qualified male doctors? This principle seems awfully vague.</code></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with\">Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with</a><ul><li>Explains why pure procedural justice is used.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that fair equality of opportunity has to be fully satisfied before any consideration of the difference principle.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important in understanding the way that the second principle of justice is to be implemented.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This rules out actions such as undermining individuals’ rights to have the ability to hold any position that they would be qualified through the justification that such a case would not be able to benefit the worst-off. Rawls specifically offers three arguments in favor of this claim:</p><ol><li>Once a certain threshold of material sufficiency is met, people come to recognize that their fundamental interests—particularly in self-respect and freedom of conscience—cannot be satisfied through additional economic gains, because these interests are not fungible with economic ones.<ul><li>This means that under economically stressful situations, economic welfare can be placed as a higher priority than protecting equal liberties.</li></ul></li><li>No one behind the veil of ignorance would be willing to gamble their freedom of conscience and freedom of thought for economic gains.</li><li>A serial ordering allows for inequalities in the socio-economic sphere to generate overall benefits while ensuring that individuals have sufficient primary goods.<ul><li>This relates to the idea of self-respect. It is impossible to secure self-respect through material means, since they are zero-sum, but equal basic liberties can indeed be secured for all. Grounding self-respect in this means that all can enjoy self-respect, and given this, people in the original position would never accept less than equal basic liberties since it would mean undermining the most important primary good, that of self-respect.</li></ul></li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice\">Fair equality of opportunity is a precondition for pure procedural justice</a><ul><li>This then means that pure procedural justice is lexically before the difference principle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>Puts this in a larger context.</li></ul></li></ul>","Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Faith is something that is generated through the lack of visible marks of providence in the world, since if such marks did exist, then faith would not be needed. It would simply be a fact of the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Bossuet’s view of the nature of history.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Bossuet saw all of history as being in the grasp of God, and the lack of signs of providence is not a sign that God does not exist or that providence is not real. Rather, it is this very lack of providential signs that compels a belief in God (faith).</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 205</p><p>Faithful hope does not eliminate the painful conflict between confidence and evidence; <strong>it rather enhances it</strong>. It is the same faith which creates and resolves the ultimate problem of a Christian and yet worldly existence.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments\">One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments</a><ul><li>It seems that this is related, given that faith comes to fill the gaps where reason cannot reach.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A major claim of the Austrian schools is that fiat money derives its value from the fact that it has historically been backed up by commodities. If that were never to have occurred for some form of fiat money, then it would have failed to continue to function.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Austrian understanding of the nature of fiat money.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>This seems a little confusing to me, since the last time the US was on the gold reserve was in 1971, and it has since been 50 years since cash was exchangeable for gold. Yet, the currency continues to function. Something like 77% of the current US population was born after the end of the gold standard, and yet the fiat currency continues to function. It just seems implausible for fiat money to merely be a continuation of commodity-backed money.</p><h4>References</h4>","Financial momentum is predictive of the outcome of a prediction market":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Financial momentum is when due to a change in prices, further prices change occur. This is considered an anomaly in the efficient market hypothesis. This is also empirical evidence that financial momentum does have some relation with the winner of some bet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The fact that financial moment actually as has prediction power when it comes to prediction markets mean that prediction market are not perfect examples of the efficient-market hypothesis. And also, this has practical significance.</p><h4>Empirical Evidence</h4><p>From the bar graphs above, it is evident that financial moment does have a statistically significant impact on the outcome of a prediction market bet. What is interesting is that at some probabilities, a downward change in the daily move can actually lead to the a positive chance in the final result.</p><h4>References</h4>","Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>After the Revolutions of 1848, as well as the many revolutions that have occurred both in the colonies and in Europe (1832, for instance), governments shifted their strategy from repression to reform in order to prevent further revolutions in the future.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a change in the way that state institutions have approached the issue of how to maintain, shifting from a strategy of repression to reform.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Politicians (people like Disraeli and Bismarck) created social programs and expanded the right to vote in order to keep the population happy, draining the desire for revolution. It was not entirely the work of politicians, however, as food prices were dropping at the time and people standards of living gradually increased, meaning that they had more to lose in a rebellion. Nationalism also gained prominence in the period, something utilized by politicians in order to ensure that their populace stays loyal to the state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms\">The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms</a><ul><li>This is something that stopped occurring as a result of the reforms that occured.</li></ul></li></ul>","Following the Crisis of the Third Century, the Roman military became centralized around the emperor":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the increased risks of powerful generals rising up in an attempt to take the emperorship, emperors began centralizing control of the army so that the military moved where the emperor did.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the final evolution of Roman border defense as troops were removed from the border and centralized around the emperor in order to ensure that power was centralized. This made border defense difficult, since it required long distance travel in order to reach a particular spot and only one border defense issue can be resolve at one time.</p><h4>References</h4>","For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For an action to be virtuous, not only does the action it self must qualify as a virtuous action, the intent/action behind that outcome likewise has to be virtuous.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for understanding Aristotle’s definition of virtue, of what it means for someone to act in a virtuous manner.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the agent to have a quality of virtuousness, they must have the various moral virtues. For the agent to act in a virtuous state, they must:</p><ol><li>Act with knowledge of what they are doing.</li><li>Choose an action for its own sake.</li><li>Act with a fixed disposition, namely, that the actor’s virtue must be ingrained in themselves, and not just a fleeting impulse.</li></ol><p>Virtue is not determined by one’s emotions (the way that one feels about the world), nor by one’s faculties (the means by which one can feel emotions), but by dispositions (the way that we generally perceive the world that shapes the way that our faculties function).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This echoes the idea that one has to act with a fixed disposition.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated\">Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated</a><ul><li>This note refers specifically to moral virtue.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>This refers to the consequence of the action, which must be in accordance with the golden mean.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>Virtue is ultimately to achieve happiness.</li></ul></li></ul>","For regulators, taxes are generally more effective tools than bans":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For regulators, taxes are generally better tools than bans, since they make markets more efficient and increase welfare.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues against the use of bans, which are common in policy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>If regulators want to ensure that the price of a product does not rise too high, they don’t have to simply ban firms from raising their taxes above a certain threshold. Rather a more sensible strategy would be to instead place a progressive tax on the revenue of firms that rise about the threshold. This has several advantages:</p><ul><li>This eliminates inefficient firms that have high marginal costs.</li><li>It preserves access for a good, instead of destroying it as in the case of bans.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>At the same time, taxes might not work.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check\">Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check</a><ul><li>Tax can be used as more than an economic tool as well.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Under certain conditions, a wealth tax leads to greater investment in human capital and other intangible assets\">Under certain conditions, a wealth tax leads to greater investment in human capital and other intangible assets</a><ul><li>Another advantage of taxes.</li></ul></li></ul>","For the sage, emotions are external":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For the sage, one’s emotions are something that is external to oneself. As a result, a sage may experience emotion, though such an emotion is something external that enters us, and then passes us.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Neo-Confucianist view (specifically, the view explicated by Cheng Hao) of emotions.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The mind is a mirror for the sage, and simply reflects what is in the world. Therefore, when anger occurs it is merely reflected in the sage, and it not truly a part of him.</p><h4>References</h4>","Form outlives function":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often the case that while some object or process originally possessed an actual, practical purpose, eventually, it becomes ritualized.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that seems to happen across cultures and institutions, and is a good heuristic when analyzing the nature of cultural processes or the process by which institutions develop.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Many of the instruments that were developed for practical purposes, such as the <em>yue</em> or the <em>ding</em>, which began as a practical food storage unit, gradually morphed into objects with greater/ritual significance.<ul><li>Other examples of this in Chinese culture include the <em>ruyi</em> (which began as a back-scratcher) and the fan, which increasingly became more ornate.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\">When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure</a><ul><li>It seems that often times, objects that were originally meant to be used later becomes abstracted into a “target,” which leads to the end of its nature as a good/practice thing/process.</li></ul></li></ul>","France’s strict regulation, high worker benefits, and high pensions are crippling the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>France’s system of strict regulation on business (high working standards), and extremely generous pensions are crippling the French economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This poses an existential threat to the French state.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>France has the 3rd highest tax wedge in the OECD on an average single worker, at 47.2%.</li><li>It is very difficult to fire people.<ul><li>This means that firms are incentivized to hire less people, leading to a high youth unemployment rate (since established, older workers cannot be fire, and new workers won’t be hired since they can’t be fired).</li><li>They have consistently high unemployment rates as a result, being around 7% (across the general population as well, not just youth).</li></ul></li><li>The 35 hour work week led to higher labor turnover rates, since labor costs were essentially higher.</li><li>France has an exceedingly low age of retirement (62), and as the ratio of retiree to worker increases, it is going to get more and more difficult to fund the pension program.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is an example of this concept in practice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>If the French deregulated, their economy would improve.</li></ul></li></ul>","Frankenstein (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Modern classic that explores themes such as the nature of fate, human progress, revenge, existentialism, and religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most acclaimed works of fiction in all of fiction, and a work that (in my opinion) is deserving of it’s reputation.</p><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a> <ul><li>This book serves as a repudiation of this idea, specifically regarding the technological advancements through history. This is also related to the contemporaneous ideas of the Enlightenment that Shelley would no doubt have been familiar with.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a></li></ul><h4>Quotes</h4><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.74</span></p><blockquote><p>One man’s life or death were but a small price to pay for the acquirement of the knowledge which I sought for the dominion I should acquire and transmit over the elemental foes of our race.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>This seems be the main motivation for many characters in this work, and it is also what creates Frankenstein’s monster. </li><li>After coming back and visiting this quote after learning about Frankenstein’s discovery of the ability to create life, I think that the central motif of the story is one of humans attempting to conquer the unknown, although it often comes with dire consequences. This is exactly what the person writing the letters was attempting to do. </li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.93</span></p><blockquote><p>Besides, I had a contempt for the uses of modern natural philosophy. It was very different, when the masters of the science sought immortality and power; such views, although futile, were grand; but now the scene was changed. The ambition of the enquirer seemed to limit itself to the annihilation of those visions on which my interest in science was chiefly founded. I was required to exchange chimeras of boundless grandeur for realities of little worth.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>This hints at his later attempts at “playing God” in his construction of Frankenstein. He was attracted to the grand undertakings of the alchemists and Hermetics, and not the incremental progress of the modern scientists.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.94</span></p><blockquote><p>The ancient teachers of this science,’ said he, ‘promised impossibilities, and performed nothing. The modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be transmuted, and that the elixir of life is a chimera. But these philosophers, whose hands seem only made to dabble in dirt, and their eyes to pore over the microscope or crucible, have indeed performed miracles. They penetrate into the recesses of nature, and show how she works in her hiding-places. They ascend into the heavens: they have discovered how the blood circulates, and the nature of the air we breathe. They have acquired new and almost unlimited powers; they can command the thunders of heaven, mimic the earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with its own shadows.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>A rejection of Frankenstein’s ideals about the nature of science, grounding it in reality and incremental progress rather than mysticism. It will be interesting to see how Frankenstein responds to this idea, and if he adopts it, how it eventually breaks away from him.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.98</span></p><blockquote><p>After days and nights of incredible labour and fatigue, I succeeded in discovering the cause of generation and life; nay, more, I became myself capable of bestowing animation upon lifeless matter.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>This is most likely an early turning point in the book where Frankenstein learns about the secret to life. It is this ability that he eventually uses to construct his monster.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.99</span></p><blockquote><p>Learn from me, if not by my precepts, at least by my example, how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge and how much happier that man is who believes his native town to be the world, than he who aspires to become greater than his nature will allow.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>“Ignorance is bliss.” The unintentional consequences of knowledge. Explores how an endless search for knowledge may have terrible results.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.100</span></p><blockquote><p>Pursuing these reflections, I thought, that if I could bestow animation upon lifeless matter, I might in process of time (although I now found it impossible) renew life where death had apparently devoted the body to corruption.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>Perhaps part of the reason that Frankenstein wants to learn the secret of life so much was the death of his mother right before he left. This event no doubt left something in him, a desire to learn how to create life, so that he could potentially revive his mother.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.103</span></p><blockquote><p>For this I had deprived myself of rest and health. I had desired it with an ardour that far exceeded moderation; but now that I had finished, the beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled my heart.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>While for the longest time, Victor had sought to create life, the moment he creates it, he realizes how terrible it had been for him to actually do this.</li></ul><blockquote><p>(<span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.147</span>) Shall I not then hate them who abhor me? I will keep no terms with my enemies. I am miserable, and they shall share my wretchedness.</p></blockquote><ul><li>This is much like Satan’s attitude after being kicked out from heaven, the idea that if one is not able to get what they want, they will cause others to suffer as well.</li></ul><blockquote><p>(<span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.148</span>) For the first time, also, I felt what the duties of a creator towards his creature were, and that I ought to render him happy before I complained of his wickedness.</p></blockquote><ul><li>This is the first time in the book when Frankenstein has finally confronted his responsibility as the creator of his creature, feeling, if only in the slightest amount, some compassion for the plight that he has experienced.</li></ul><p><span class=\"wl-off\">The injustice of his sentence was very flagrant; all Paris was indignant; and it was judged that his religion and wealth rather than the crime alleged against him had been the cause of his condemnation.</span></p><ul><li>There much hypocrisy in this statement, as the people were outraged that an Ottoman was judged for his religion and wealth, but when they meet the creature, they judge him the same way.</li></ul><p><span class=\"wl-off\">Farewell, Walton! Seek happiness in tranquillity, and avoid ambition, even if it be only the apparently innocent one of distinguishing yourself in science and discoveries.</span></p><ul><li>Victory’s final farewell to Walton, warning him of the dangers of attempting scientific progress. This is likewise a rejection of Shelley of the Enlightenment idea of inevitable human progress and rationalism. Yet, the brutality and unreasonableness of the creature likewise presents itself as a critique of Romanticism.</li></ul><p><span class=\"wl-off\">But it is even so; the fallen angel becomes a malignant devil. Yet even that enemy of God and man had friends and associates in his desolation; I am alone.</span></p><ul><li>The creature expresses his disappointment that he was alone, comparing himself with Satan from <em>Paradise Lost</em>. He attributes this to the idea that he was mistreated due to the way he looked.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them\">A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them</a><ul><li>Oedipus is a figure just like Frankenstein as both characters seek truth. This also means that both characters are bound by their ambition, their pre-determined fate, and the idea that progress/new discoveries is not always a good thing.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>This is a theme constantly present throughout the book.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">The Enlightenment was characterized by belief in rationality and the inevitability of human progress</span><ul><li>This theme is especially related to Walton and Victor’s quests attempting to uncover the nature of the natural world.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free Will":"<h4>Question</h4><p>Does the control that grounds moral responsibility require that the agent could have done otherwise, or only that the agent was the source of what they did?</p><h4>Thesis</h4><p>The dispute over free will is downstream of a dispute over moral responsibility, so the vault’s free will notes cannot be settled until the control condition is fixed, and fixing it decides the metaphysics rather than the other way round.</p><h4>The Map</h4><p>The oldest treatments do not ask the modern question at all. For Plato freedom is <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is the capacity to choose the good\">the capacity to pursue the good</a>; for Aristotle it is <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is the ability to act against immediate desire, which makes it an instrument of character formation\">the capacity to act against inclination</a>, which makes it an instrument of character; for Chrysippus determination is compatible with responsibility because <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character\">the act expresses the agent’s nature</a>; and for Aquinas <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our ends may be fixed while the means toward them remain chosen\">the end is fixed while the means remain open</a>. Each of these answers a question about what a life should be, not about what causation permits. The modern question is narrower, and the reframing that organises it is that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility\">the dispute is really about responsibility</a>. Once that is accepted, two branches open, corresponding to two candidate control conditions. The first branch asks whether the agent could have done otherwise. The cheapest compatibilist answer locates unfreedom in <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism\">obstruction rather than causation</a>, but the analysis that implements it turns out to define <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will\">free action rather than free will</a>. Replacing it with a categorical analysis then walks into <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past\">the Consequence Argument</a>, which is the strongest statement of the claim that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">determinism and free will are incompatible</a>. The second branch abandons alternatives and asks only whether the act issued from the agent, which is what <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities\">the Frankfurt cases</a> are for. If they work, responsibility attaches to the actual sequence. They face the dilemma objection recorded in that note, which is why the branch is not obviously safer than the first. Underneath both sits the question of what is being secured. Responsibility is not one thing, since it <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral responsibility divides into answerability, attributability, and accountability\">divides into three senses</a>, and only the accountability sense requires the strong control condition, because only it licenses <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences\">basic desert</a>.</p><h4>Pressure</h4><p>The hardest position in this vault is not compatibilism but the claim that neither branch succeeds. <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism\">The Basic Argument</a> holds that sourcehood is unattainable, since being the source of one’s acts requires being the source of the character they issue from, and <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism\">the Luck Objection</a> closes the indeterminist escape. If both hold, the accountability sense is never satisfied, <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Retributive punishment is never justified\">retribution is never justified</a> follows directly, and the claim that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive\">responsibility survives</a> holds only in the attributability sense.</p><h4>Notes to Write</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Sourcehood is a weaker control condition than alternative possibilities, and is satisfied more often</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Attributability survives determinism even where accountability does not</span></li></ul><h4>Loose Ends</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a><ul><li>Bears on whether the control condition can be met by anyone, and is not used above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil\">One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil</a><ul><li>A consequentialist account of blame, which sidesteps this map entirely.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We have power over nothing except our will\">We have power over nothing except our will</a><ul><li>The Stoic position, adjacent to Chrysippus but doing different work.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Free trade increases the size of the total surplus of the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a fundamental concept in economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In trade, the international price that is set is lower than the domestic price, therefore increasing consumer surplus. While this also decreases producer surplus, the decrease is not commensurable with the increase. This, however, poses the fundamental problem in trade, which is how to recompense the losing party.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>Trade requires private property.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often claimed that it would be really difficult to establish claims about responsibility in the case where people don’t have free will, though this note argues otherwise.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this note is the case, then we could potentially make the determinist worldview more likely in the face of its unlikeliness due to the exact problem that this note highlights before.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There have been many arguments made to prove this point:</p><ol><li>Peter Strawson puts forward the thesis of “reactive attitudes,” which means that responsibility is found in the appropriate change in treatment or attitude towards an offender based on their breach of a relationship.</li></ol><h4>References</h4>","Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Free will is impossible, regardless of if we accept determinism or not, since either way, we are not able to self-determine.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this argument succeeds, the debate over compatibilism is rendered null, and free will is determined to be impossible.</p><h4>Argument</h4><p>The Basic Argument goes as follows:</p><ol><li>You do what you do because of the way you are.<ul><li>Your actions can be thought of as a function $\\alpha$ which takes many states, i.e., the way that you mentally are, your location, your height, etc. into account, which together determine your actions. $$</li></ul></li></ol><p>\\alpha(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}, \\dots, x_{n}) = Your \\ Actions $$</p><ol><li>To be truly responsible for what you do, you must be responsible for the way you are. You also must explicitly choose the way that you are.<ul><li>In other words, you must be able to determine $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$, etc. But this requires some prior state, $P_{1}$, which regards your attitudes towards a certain range of subjects relevant for the states of $\\alpha$.</li></ul></li><li>You are the way you are because of circumstances outside your control.<ul><li>However, for $P_{1}$ to actually determine anything, it must also be the case that you choose $P_{1}$, based on some other set of principles, $P_{2}$.</li><li>This results in infinite regress.</li></ul></li><li>Therefore, you are not responsible for your nature, and thus not responsible for your actions.<ul><li>True moral responsibility is impossible, since it requires true self-determination, which is impossible.</li></ul></li></ol><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The Basic Argument is not to say that you cannot change yourself, but that you cannot change yourself in a way that matters for moral responsibility.</p><ul><li>In the moment, it seems that we always have the freedom of choice and that we have moral responsibility.</li></ul><p>It seems true to say that, in general, we believe that moral responsibility for what one does is somehow involved with responsibility for the way that one is.</p><ul><li>An understanding of moral responsibility comes from an understanding of one’s ability to true shape oneself.</li></ul><h5>Possible Responses</h5><ol><li><em>Compatibilism</em>: rejecting premise (2) of the argument by saying that you can be morally responsible for what you do even if you do not choose the way that you are intentionally, i.e., even if determinism is true.<ul><li>It seems that this does not really dispel the central moral intuition that we are only responsible for something if we ourselves are self-determined.</li></ul></li><li><em>Libertarianism</em>: argues that determinism is false, and therefore, we have moral responsibility.<ul><li>But it seems that this does not fundamentally solve the issue, since if determinism is false, then indeterminacy must be true. And it seems that indeterminacy does not rescue moral responsibility.</li></ul></li></ol><p>We could also attempt to introduced a factor $S$ that exists beyond our “determined” factors ($CPM$, or character, personality, mental state, etc.). However, how this $S$ is formed is again called into question.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism\">The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism</a><ul><li>Closes off the libertarian escape route specifically, where the Basic Argument closes off both.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>This note makes the two notes above irrelevant.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities\">The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities</a><ul><li>Another critique of free will.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free will is the ability to act against immediate desire, which makes it an instrument of character formation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Freedom consists in the capacity to do what is immediately unpleasant or undesirable, which means that its exercise is what builds character rather than merely expressing it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This account makes free will continuous with virtue, since repeated exercise of the capacity is precisely how a disposition is formed.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle holds the view in this form. It differs from the Platonic account in that the object of freedom is not the good as such but the capacity to override present inclination, whatever its object.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>The mechanism by which the exercise of this capacity becomes a settled state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>The same capacity, described as a state rather than as a freedom.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free will is the capacity to choose the good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>On the oldest conception of free will, freedom is not the bare ability to pick between options but the capacity to pursue what is good, so that a choice against the good is a failure of freedom rather than an exercise of it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This makes free will an evaluative rather than a metaphysical notion, which is why the ancient treatments of the problem look so unlike the modern ones.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Plato holds the view in this form: to be free is to be governed by the rational part of the soul, and the person ruled by appetite is enslaved even when unobstructed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive\">The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive</a><ul><li>The psychology that this conception of freedom presupposes.</li></ul></li></ul>","Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Friendship that is based on utility or pleasure are easily dissolved, since these things can be fleeting, and once they are gone, there is no reason for these friendships to continue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial factor that separates friendship based on utility or pleasure from those based on virtue.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><strong>Utility</strong>: A friendship based on a business relationship may end after a deal is reached.<ul><li>These friendships are often formed between people who are opposites, since they each have something the other does not (e.g., rich and poor, dumb and smart).</li><li>In this type of friendship, the value of a service is determined by the utility conferred to the recipient of a service (not by the conferrer of the service).</li></ul></li><li><strong>Pleasure</strong>: A friendship based a mutual enjoyment of 18th-century furniture may end because of the participants find a new interest.</li></ul><p>They are easily dissolved since in these types of friendships, people only care about their own utility or pleasure, and not anything about the other person. So the moment they stop gaining anything from the relationship, they end it.</p><ul><li>Most quarrels between friends occur because the individuals are mistaken about the basis of their friendship (i.e., one persons believes the friendship to be one of utility, and the other, pleasure).</li></ul><p>Truly good people do not make friendships of the kinds outlined in this note, only friendships of the virtuous kind.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>A friendship based on moral virtue, unlike what is described here, lasts longer.</li></ul></li></ul>","Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle defines friendship as when two people have affection for each other, wish well for one another, and both mutually recognize their affection for one another. If these conditions are not met, it is not a friendship.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Understanding the definition of friendship is crucial for further argumentation on the matter.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>When applied to the three types of friendships:</p><ul><li>For utilitarian friendships, all three conditions are contingent on the fact that the other person is useful to you. </li><li>For pleasurable friendships, all three conditions are contingent on the fact that the other person is pleasant to you.</li><li>In the case of a virtuous friendship, all three conditions are contingent on the fact that the other person has good character.</li></ul><p>Additionally, friends of virtue don’t need justice between them (not to say that it does not exist, but simply that justice is not needed as an external check). Justice is only applicable between friends of utility.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>Understanding the definition of friendship is crucial for understanding the best form of friendship.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved\">Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved</a><ul><li>While these forms of friendships are lower than the one based on virtue, the definition of friendship still applies to them.</li></ul></li></ul>","Friendship is more necessary in adversity, but more honorable in prosperity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Regarding when we should make friends, Aristotle says that it is more important that we have friends when encountering adversity, since grief is lighted by friends. At the same time, it is more honorable to have friends when you are prospering.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle outlines the duty of friends here.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>We should try not to be open about our grief with our friends, since that would cause them pain. However, we should share our moments of triumph with friends, so that we can experience that together.</li><li>That being said, we can call our friends to aid when in times of need, and when it least disrupts them. We should aid our friends when we notice them suffering.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection\">Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection</a><ul><li>Being friends is doing the things that are outlined in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>Only virtuous friendships are able to achieve the things laid out in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Fulfillment can be found within history":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The standard Christian reading of history, such as the one that is espoused by St. Augustine, argues that history is but an interim period for humans after the death of Christ and his second coming. Joachim of Floris, on the other hand, argues that fulfillment can be found within history, specifically, through the third dispensation, i.e., the transformation of the world into a more spiritual one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This view was later adopted by the Franciscans and was used against the existence of the church (who would play no part in this more spiritual world where every one was in tune with religion).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Ultimately, this theory would influence the creation of secular theories of history in the future, such as the ones of Proudhon or Marx. This is because it moved the end goal of history from an extra-historical dimension within history itself, which was easily adopted by other secular conceptions of history.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 155</p><p>The Christian doctrine from Augustine to Thomas had mastered history theologically by excluding the temporal relevance of the last things. This exclusion was achieved by the transposition of the original expectations into a realm beyond historical existence. <strong>Joachim viewed everything in a historical perspective.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a><ul><li>This was one of the ideas that influenced Marx, though he may not have known it.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a><ul><li>That a final stage of history can even exist on the Earth is something that is borrowed from Joachim’s conception of history.</li></ul></li></ul>","Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A city should be composed “as far as possible, of equals and similars.” The very rich cannot obey and the very poor cannot command, so a city split between them is one of masters and slaves rather than of citizens, whatever its laws say.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Explains why extending the franchise is insufficient, and why the distribution of condition is a constitutional question.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The argument runs through character, not resources directly. Those with excessive goods of fortune are neither willing nor able to submit to authority, never having learned obedience; the very poor are too degraded to do anything but obey. Neither extreme readily follows reason, and the resulting contempt and envy destroy the <em>philia</em> a city depends on. Democracy has legal equality by definition, since it reckons equality by number. But that secures nothing about condition, and both extreme democracy and pure oligarchy arise “where some possess much, and the others nothing.”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office\">Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office</a><ul><li>Supplies the formal entitlement this note argues is insufficient alone.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society\">Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society</a><ul><li>The constructive half of the same argument.</li></ul></li></ul>","GDP (per capita) is an inaccurate predictor of wellbeing":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>GDP or GDP per capita is a poor predictor of the level of wellbeing in an economy, since it cannot take into account things like inequality or other seemingly non-quantifiable characteristics of wellbeing (aside from monetary success).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common claim made often by economists and non-economists alike.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It’s been shown, however, that high GDP correlates relatively highly with non-quantifiable factors such as high happiness or low loneliness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Authoritarian &amp; non-democratic nations likely overstate their GDP growth\">Authoritarian &amp; non-democratic nations likely overstate their GDP growth</a><ul><li>This is perhaps a possible distorting factor when it comes to measuring such correlation? However, I would imagine that in such countries, measurements for happiness may also be overstated.</li></ul></li></ul>","Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Genghis Khan was able to take advantage of the fighting ability of the Mongols and pit them towards external enemies rather than focusing on internal squabbling (see Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This, combined with the raw firepower of the Mongol warriors, was what allowed them to take over so much of Eurasia.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe\">The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe</a><ul><li>The structure being unified, and why the strongest leader naturally accrued power.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200\">Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200</a><ul><li>A material condition that made this unified force more effective.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division\">Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division</a></li></ul>","Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Geography can play an important role in determining the way that states can come to form, as different geographic factors can lead to different challenges for civilizations, whether directly as a result of the geography, or due to related causes.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant as geography has influenced the way that many civilizations have formed, and the acknowledgement of geography’s influence can better help us analyze the reasons for specific reasons that a civilizations developed in a specific way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Rashidun conquest was made possible by geography and unique political economy\">The Rashidun conquest was made possible by geography and unique political economy</a></li></ul>","Gerontocracies are bad":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rule by the elderly is bad, for a variety of reasons. For instance, they may not be able to keep up with contemporary developments in economics, culture, and politics, and thus not accurately represent the interests of the wider population.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Gerontocracies are increasingly the norm in democratic societies across the world, and thus note outlines a case against them.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions\">Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions</a><ul><li>Perhaps if the government consisted of more youthful members, the whole tense period leading up to the debate could have been avoided?</li></ul></li></ul>","Given that basic rights are protected, inequality ought to be reduced as much as possible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls’ famous idea that is related to his Veil of Ignorance. Essentially places basic rights and freedoms as the first-order requirement that must be satisfied before attempting to reduce inequality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental axiom that underlies Rawls’ political and moral philosophy.</p><h4>References</h4>","God saved humans, while not directly changing their psychology, from sin":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The main way in which divine providence manifests is through the fact that although people act for and through their personal self-interest, the results of such action are all a part of God’s providence such that they result in justice and stability for societies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows a major way in which God affects human life and what divine providence means.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, the way in which the fundamentally beastial nature of humans are tempered and made into what allows for things like justice and stability in societies is the fact that people are checked by their fear of God, or at least this was the initial impulse that allowed for this to occur. At first, God had to deceive people into believing false gods in order for the true God to eventually show himself.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one\">The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one</a><ul><li>This note is in some ways related.</li></ul></li></ul>","Good institutions are the source of economic growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Good institutions are the source of economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this were true, then we would want to ensure that are institutions are good as possible in order to maximize economic development.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of ‘good institutions’ is either tautological or unsupported\">The concept of ‘good institutions’ is either tautological or unsupported</a><ul><li>This note refutes the stuff set out in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle rejects the idea of a uniform “goodness.” He argues that goodness is a heterogenous concept whereby there is not a common characteristic corresponding to one idea.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>With this idea, Aristotle grounds ethics. Goodness is no longer some abstract Idea or Form. Rather, it is something actionable.</p><h4>Argument</h4><p>We first must separate the idea of goodness into things that are <em>good in an of themselves,</em> and things that are <em>good as a means to secure something that is good in an of itself</em>. Here, only the former is examined, since the second sense of goodness is not the sense that we ought to strive for in life, i.e., the second sense of goodness is contingent and lower in importance. With respect to the first kind of good then, if there is one overarching Platonic Idea of “goodness” that underly all concepts we consider good (e.g., honor, intelligence, etc.), then there must be some kind of similarity between these in how they all seem to approach the same concept of “goodness.” Yet this does not seem to be the case. Therefore, we must conclude that goodness is different for each of its incarnations, and all of its incarnations do not approach on some central, underlying concept of “goodness.” Rather, goodness is something that changes and adapts to the specific concepts that it is currently being applied to.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>In this section of the text (specifically, 1096b26) Aristotle raise the question of for what reason, if any, can all these different concepts be called “good” given that they do not all approach on a central concept of “goodness.” He does not address this issue explicitly in the rest of his philosophy. However, Aristotle does define a broad conception of goodness: “an activity of the soul that is in accordance with virtue.” This could be put forth as a solution to the problem presented above, as while it is not a strong, Platonic definition of good, it does bring a sense of unity to the concept of goodness. His argument for such a definition is as follows:</p><ol><li>The function of a person is an activity of the soul in accordance with a rational principle.</li><li>A good human is a rational human.</li><li>Something done well is done according to its excellence.</li></ol><p>$\\therefore$ What is good is an activity that is in accordance with virtue.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a></li></ul>","Goods of the soul are most valuable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A pursuit for what is good the soul, above what is good for the body or external goods, should be prioritized.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Aristotle’s argument for why one should be virtuous.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are a few arguments for this:</p><ol><li>This is the only way to acquire virtue; goods of the body or external goods cannot bring about virtue.</li><li>External things have a limit, so an excess of them is either harmful or neutral. Goods of the soul is the opposite, whereby the more of it you have, the better.</li><li>All the other goods serve the good of the soul.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should desire less\">We should desire less</a><ul><li>It seems that Taoism almost as an entirely opposite claim regarding that one ought to do.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Goodwill is not friendship":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle distinguishes between “goodwill” and “friendship,” where the former occurs when we feel some merit and good towards someone, but the other person is not aware of these feelings and these feelings are largely superficial.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It is important to distinguish between friendship and good will since many of the things that apply to friendship do not apply to goodwill.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection\">Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection</a><ul><li>The contents of this note describe something that is different from the content of this link.</li></ul></li></ul>","Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is common to observe how once government institutions are created, it is difficult for them to be ended, since there are so many stakeholders, and there is no incentive for efficiency.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that over time, governments can become more inefficient and bloated.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The USDA got involved with the Farmers Home Administration in the 1940s in order to support farmers and improve farming. However, even as farms became big businesses over the next few decades, the USDA continued to work in housing, shifting from farmers explicitly to “rural communities.”</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Activist organizations often shift priorities in order to self-perpetuate\">Activist organizations often shift priorities in order to self-perpetuate</a><ul><li>Related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>in most cases, the government should not interfere in the affairs of private citizens, since such intervention is either inefficient or actively harmful.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core ideas in Mill’s <em>On Liberty</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Government intervention is not justified for the following reasons:</p><ol><li>Individuals can do tasks better without government intervention.</li><li>It is good for individuals to do something, even though the government may do it ore efficiently, because it helps to develop the citizenry.</li><li>It unnecessary to add to the powers of government, which can be abused.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Freedom is the only non-contradictory political idea</span><ul><li>Government intervention makes the system internally contradictory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>Another reason why government should not intervene.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a><ul><li>When intervention occurs, it has negative consequences.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Great Britain was narrowly able to prevent a revolution in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill which drastically updated the medieval (literally) electoral laws in place at the time, which greatly disenfranchised the masses.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>An example of when reform prevented revolution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions\">Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions</a><ul><li>The other governments of Continental Europe did not reform in the same way that Britain did, and thus faced revolutions, though the revolutions were crushed.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians\">The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians</a><ul><li>The failure of the Roman Senate to reform was what doomed them.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A work has depth when its content cannot be extracted and restated without loss, which typically shows up as the availability of two readings that the work sustains at once.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the criterion that distinguishes art from illustration, since an illustrated thesis is by construction paraphrasable.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The test case is a work that supports a reading against its own apparent intention. <em>Parasite</em> is ostensibly about structural inequality yet goes out of its way to make the poor unsympathetic, and it is good partly because it sustains both readings. Plato’s <em>Apology</em> can be read as a condemnation of Socrates rather than a defence.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The structural feature that makes multiple readings possible.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption\">Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption</a><ul><li>An instance of a work sustaining two readings of its own ending.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy\">Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy</a><ul><li>Another case where the surface reading and the available reading diverge.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Good work sets up relations between its elements, both within a level and across levels, and these recurrences hold the work together whether or not they carry any determinate meaning.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why symbolism can be effective without being interpretable, which is otherwise puzzling on any account that treats symbols as encoded messages.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The recurrence of the number three throughout Kafka’s <em>Metamorphosis</em>, and of ears throughout <em>Hamlet</em>, are examples where the pattern does structural work regardless of what it is taken to mean. The requirement pairs with surprise: echo without surprise is formula, and surprise without echo is noise.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art is surprising\">Great art is surprising</a><ul><li>The complementary criterion.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content\">Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content</a><ul><li>A specific device by which echoes are produced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A minor incident can encapsulate a larger movement in a plot\">A minor incident can encapsulate a larger movement in a plot</a><ul><li>Layering across scales rather than repetition within one.</li></ul></li></ul>","Great art is surprising":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A work is good to the extent that its parts are not predictable from one another, so that each element withholds something the audience could have supplied itself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This gives a criterion for aesthetic quality that can be applied locally, at the level of the sentence or the brushstroke, rather than only to the work as a whole.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In prose the failure case is the adjective already implied by its neighbour, as in describing a sunset as interesting and incredible, where the second word adds nothing. The positive techniques are defamiliarisation, in which the commonplace is described as an outsider would encounter it, and syntactic misdirection, in which a construction sets an expectation that the next clause withdraws.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The second criterion, which constrains this one: surprise alone would license mere randomness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur\">We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur</a><ul><li>The same mechanism operating in humour.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art\">Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art</a><ul><li>A tradition that makes withholding the central principle rather than a technique.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hamlet (play)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of Shakespeare's four great tragedies. Considered by many to be Shakespeare's best, and the best play ever written.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is required reading for AP Lit. But also, I have always been interested in reading Shakespeare's work.</p><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Literature can dramatize metacognition by depicting characters reasoning about their own reasoning\">Literature can dramatize metacognition by depicting characters reasoning about their own reasoning</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work\">An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Oedipus complex appears in many works of literature\">The Oedipus complex appears in many works of literature</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content\">Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed\">The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>There are many similarities between these two plays.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hannibal was an excellent tactician, but poor grand strategist":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While Hannibal was a more than competent military tactician, as can be seen through the various strategems he employed at battles such as the Trebia or Cannae, he was not a great grand strategist.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the Carthaginian defeat during the Second Punic War.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Despite the many victories achieved by Hannibal during his campaign in Italy, he was enable to capitalize on the victories in a strategic sense. In the end, the war turned into a constant grind between Hannibal’s slowly dwindling forces and the Romans, as Hannibal by the latter half of the Italian campaign was unable to force decisive victories on the Romans. He was never able to use his battlefield victories to push the Romans into capitulation.</p><h4>References</h4>","Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are different conceptions of what happiness entails, and Aristotle categorizes them into three major camps: those who think that pleasure is happiness, those who think that political success/honor is happiness, and those who value contemplation as happiness. Aristotle also states that it would be “grossly disconcerting” if it were the case that happiness was “random.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle believes that true happiness can only be acquired through the final means: that is, contemplation. This forms the backbone of his philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle simple dismisses pure hedonism, presumably since it is something that does not involve “being an expert humans.” as pure hedonism is something that is achievable (at least in theory) by non-human organisms. Aristotle rejects the definition of happiness as honor, since it is too “superficial.” “Honor is felt to depend more on those who confer than on him who receives it.” Perhaps honor is something that is too passive, and is not really an <em>activity</em> of the soul. Additionally, people honor others because they are good (virtuous). This means that goodness, and not honor, is the root of happiness.</p><ul><li>I don’t know if this point is the most convincing. It seems possible that one can still be happy with the praise, not one’s intrinsic virtue. </li></ul><p>Aristotle ultimately argues that the contemplative life is one that is able to produce happiness.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>An important note to make clear here is that Aristotle’s conception of contemplation. His conception of “contemplation” is not one that promotes active discovery and curiosity towards new knowledge. Rather, it is reflecting on what one has already learned. This is not to say that the Aristotelian contemplator does not seek out new knowledge, but that he does not constantly seek out new knowledge, since that is not what brings him happiness. It is rather the process of reflecting on knowledge that has been acquired that is the basis for happiness under this conception.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s definition of the highest form of happiness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s two forms of goodness.</li></ul></li></ul>","Happiness is linearly related to log(income), not just income":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Happiness is linearly related to log(income), meaning that the same absolute change in income derives a real but increasingly smaller amount of additional happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the correlation between income and happiness.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This correlation seems to exist wherever you look, including within countries, between countries, and between historical time periods. There are some exceptions to the pattern, such as hunter gatherers, who had a surprisingly high level of happiness for the amount of real wealth that they owned.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>This seems to undermine Aristotle? Or maybe, since the results of the studies merely prove a correlation and not causation, what Aristotle says may be a potential confounder?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Having a middle class is supposed to have many benefits for society, including ensuring both political and economic stability and reducing class conflict.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea, first espoused by Aristotle.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that having a large middle class allows a city to better achieve its <em>telos</em>, since there is less factional conflict (as there would be between a clear upper and lower class).</p><ul><li>The middle class align with his doctine of the mean, since they are not subject to the often times degrading influence of being a member of the upper class, and also not the struggles of the lower class.</li><li>The middle class is most likely to follow reason.</li><li>The upper class is too haughty and the lower class too slavish. Therefore the middle class are then the best citizens and are best able to contribute to the city.</li></ul><p>In a polity is where a large middle class is most likely to emerge, and this is also where political participation (citizenship) is extended to the most amount of people.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>In a way, having many middle class people is the political manifestation of the golden mean.</li></ul></li></ul>","Having a moral personality is sufficient for the full protections of justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A moral person with a moral personality is someone who is capable of having a conception of the good and someone who is capable of having a conception of justice. Having a moral personality is sufficient for being treated with the principles of justice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of morality and why people would rationally want to be moral.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rawls states that only the people with moral personalities are the ones who ought to be subject to demands of the principles of justice. There is the specific case of someone whose naturally constituted such that their plan of life is fundamentally incompatible with the principles of justice as a result of their moral psychology, e.g., their sense of the good is built upon a desire to dominant others and engage in zero-sum competition. In this case, they would be punished by their society.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This claim faces many obvious problems, such as the fact that whether someone has a moral personality is a natural contingency, and thereby is morally arbitrary. However, the difference principle is unable to solve it in this case. Additionally, this also means that people who are mentally impaired therefore don’t have rights accorded to them via the principle justice, and must rely on people’s humanity and kindness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society\">The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society</a><ul><li>In this case, it does not.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The intolerant must mostly be tolerated\">The intolerant must mostly be tolerated</a><ul><li>These two ideas are actually compatible.</li></ul></li></ul>","Heritability makes intergenerational income correlation consistent with equality of opportunity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Parents and children share genes as well as circumstances, so a correlation between parental and child income is exactly what one should expect even in a society with fully equal opportunity, and the correlation alone therefore does not establish that opportunity is unequal.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the strongest empirical defence available against the standard mobility statistics, and any argument from mobility data has to answer it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mankiw presses the point in this form. The reply is that the argument proves too much, since it would equally license inferring genetic transmission from any persistent group difference, and it does not distinguish heritable traits that constitute merit from heritable traits that merely correlate with reward.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Income mobility is becoming more difficult to achieve\">Income mobility is becoming more difficult to achieve</a><ul><li>The finding this claim is designed to reinterpret.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The Rawlsian response, which grants heritability and denies it is morally relevant.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Variability Hypothesis states that males exhibit a wider spread in many traits\">The Variability Hypothesis states that males exhibit a wider spread in many traits</a><ul><li>Another argument of the same structure, and vulnerable in the same way.</li></ul></li></ul>","High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When there is high levels of inflation, nominal interest rates rise, which means that costs for businesses and individuals seem to also rise and become less predictable, which leads to less investment overall.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument against high inflation, since increased uncertainty leads to less investment in the economy, which, in the long term, depresses economic growth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>There is uncertainty regarding the effect of uncertainty on the economy in the short/long run.</li></ul></li></ul>","High levels of tribalism is associated with lower levels of democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Higher levels of tribalism and patronage is associated with lower levels of democracy, since society like this are typically less able to cooperate on a larger scale.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why countries which are much more tribalistic have trouble adopting democracy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specific proxies, such as cousin marriage, can be pointed to show the effects of tribalism on democracy. In fact, there is a high negative correlation between cousin marriage and the level of democracy. Typically, collectivist societies are the ones with higher levels of tribalism, and they perform worse on many of the metrics by which we judge a country.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Collectivist countries are more lonely less individualist ones\">Collectivist countries are more lonely less individualist ones</a><ul><li>Another point on collectivist countries.</li></ul></li></ul>","Highland Light (painting)":"<h4>Notes</h4><p>The way that the frame is slightly tilted creates a sense of incoherence and confusion for the viewer, and also, as one observes closely, the lighthouse is way to big for the background of the view, which means that the perspective would only make sense if the lighthouse were placed at the bottom of a cliff such that only the top of the lighthouse shows. These inconsistencies create a sense of the disquiet in the viewer.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cat-walk (painting)\">Cat-walk (painting)</a><ul><li>See the above painting for similar comments.</li></ul></li></ul>","Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the tolerant nature of Hinduism, which offers many “different paths to the same top of the hill,” religion conflict is reduced in India.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrate how particular viewpoints towards the religion, more specifically, the acceptance towards religious values different than yours, may shape a civilization in ways beyond the immediate influence of the religion itself.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hinduism is very tolerant of other religions, leading to very little religious conflict in India throughout history, except when outside religions attempted to move in (usually headed by a hostile army).</p>","Historical judgment is relative":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Historical judgement is necessarily something that is relative and dependent on the perspective from which you look at a topic.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This should impel a sense of epistemic humility when we both cast judgment on the past or just when we make general claims about events that occurred in the past.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Orosius used this to support his argument regarding the importance and omnipresence of suffering in the world. For instance, even when the Romans civilized Europe, the barbarian tribes were harmed.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 179</p><p>Thus when pagans say that Rome happy in the times of her continuous triumphs, victories, imposing processions, and wealth, one has to point out to them that whenever Rome conquered and was happy the rest of the world was conquered and unhappy.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>Even just accepting the trivially true premise that morality changes across time, this is something that we should look out for.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth\">The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth</a><ul><li>This, for example, could be example of a perspective on history that is tainted by modern perspectives.</li></ul></li></ul>","History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Thucydides’ approach to history in his <em>History of the Peloponnesian War</em> scientized the study of history compared to the approaches taken by his predecessors, most famously Herodotus and Homer. Rather than focusing on divine intervention and inspiration, Thucydides seeks concrete evidence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Thucydides’ work laid the groundwork upon which future histories were created, as he set the standard for good history for the future.</p><h4>References</h4>","History is inherently theological":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Meaning in history is something that can only be found through theological interpretations of history; secular history is incommensurate with theological history.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the perspective of history presents in the Bible.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This manifests through the fact that while the Bible makes a lot of claims about the coming salvation for humanity and theological events and their implications, it provides no concrete guide at all with regards to how these theological claims are to be related to secular events.</p><ul><li>When people have attempted to do this, such as Boussuet, or the Franciscans (building off of the ideas of Joachim), they have failed and demonstrated the weakness of their theology.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>It seems that this thesis cannot be held in tandem with the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes\">History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a><ul><li>In this sense, Marxism concedes to the claim made in the current note.</li></ul></li></ul>","How characters are framed in a shot can demonstrate the relationship or the mental state of characters":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>How characters are positioned within shots, especially as it relates to how they are framed together with other characters, gives an insight into the relationship between the characters. Additionally, the way they are shot can also give us an indication of the mental state of the character.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is cinematic technique used by films such as <em>The Third Man</em> or <em>Fargo</em>.</p><h4><em>Fargo</em></h4><p> In the film, Margie is always filmed in the same frame as norm, indicating their close relationship. Additionally, when Mike moves closer to her during their conversation, she explicitly asks him to move to the other side of the table, moving them out of the same frame.   Marge is always portrayed in the interview scene with Jerry as seen over his shoulder, showing her connection with others and her connection with the world at large. On the other hand, Jerry is shown in a claustrophobic manner where the camera gets in his face and there is nothing to locate him in a space (it is merely a blank wall behind him).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Dutch Angle can be used to convey a sense of disorientation and confusion with the surrounding world\">The Dutch Angle can be used to convey a sense of disorientation and confusion with the surrounding world</a><ul><li>This is a specific example of this principle in action.</li></ul></li></ul>","Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>East Asian countries have higher levels of intelligence on average, which explains the success of these countries. It is not their industrial policy, as is commonly claimed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes the common idea that it is industrial policy that allowed East Asian countries to prosper.</p><h4>GDP per capita PPP vs. PISA</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4>","Human social relations are non-trivial":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that human social relations are non-trivial and go beyond mere prudential concerns.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This builds upon the contractarian moral framework by appending the consideration that people’s relation with each other are non-trivial.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In order to ultimately reach one’s full potential, a community is required. In other words, the whole community together outweighs each individual by themselves added together. There are two sub-claims related to this idea:</p><ol><li>That successful, just institutions are the shared and final end.</li><li>That such institutions are prized as goods in themselves, rather than merely instrumentally.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance\">Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance</a><ul><li>This is an example of non-trivial social relations.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humanity is insignificant":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that humanity is insignificant in the vastness of existence. This is a core belief in philosophical schools such as the existentialists.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea in many existentialist schools of philosophy and works of visual art and literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In the plays of Sophocles, such as Oedipus the King, Oedipus at Colonus, or Antigone, humanity is always portrayed as entirely subservient to the gods, as they are essentially bent to the will of the gods in their every action. <ul><li>In fact, the entire plot of Oedipus, first of him as king, and later, when he was at Colonus, was essentially dictated by the gods.<ul><li>If Oedipus does not find out about the truth of his family, the city will continue to suffer from the plague. He is forced, then, to find out the truth.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus at Colonus\">Oedipus at Colonus</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The world is unfair and amoral\">The world is unfair and amoral</a><ul><li>This is a common theme or deduction based on this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humans are born as blank slates, gaining knowledge a posteriori":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core idea that binds the Enlightenment Empiricists together, namely, that humans are born without knowledge of the world, though with some a priori tools for understanding the world with.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core thesis of the Enlightenment Empiricists, a core group of thinkers that would come to influence later thinkers.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is two aspects of empiricism that need to be differentiated here:</p><ul><li><em>Concept empiricism</em>: that all our ideas trace back to experience.</li><li><em>Judgment empiricism</em>: that all knowledge is justified a posteriori.</li></ul><p>There is far more agreement regarding concept empiricism among the Empiricists than regarding judgement empiricism, where while all agree that we are born with some innate tools to help us understand the world, the nature and scope of such tools is disputed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology\">Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology</a><ul><li>Decartes was opposed to the Empiricist school, being a rationalist.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humans are the most ‘political’ animal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Humans are the most political of all the different species of animals. Here, political means having a single/common goal in mind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason for the writing of the books.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Humans are the type of animal who are best able to cooperate in order to achieve some grand goal. Additionally, humans have the ability for speech, which fosters this politicality, and they have a sense of morality, which also strengthens this feeling.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics\">Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics</a><ul><li>This is one elaboration on humans’ political nature.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>Justice in politics according to Aristotle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences\">Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences</a><ul><li>The practice of politics is complicated and involves various parts.</li></ul></li></ul>","Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something that seems to be uniquely humans is the ability to preserve information and be able to pass in through time to our descendants. In this way, we preserve information for our descendants and thus cooperate with them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that is unique to humanity, and it gives us an edge when it comes to combating existential risks.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since we are able to interact with the future (at least in some non-trivial sense), it means that we are far more effective at dealing with problems that concern long terms than other species. However, this characteristic has its limits too, and it seems that currently, humanity does not think long-term <em>enough</em> in order to deal with the existential risks that abound. Another consequence of this is the existence of the study of population ethics, or how we ought to treat people yet to exist.</p><h4>References</h4>","Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that humans have rapidly grown to be able to wield advanced technologies, while our sense of how to use them lags behind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This proses a significant challenge, since it means that while we yield significantly more power than before, we still do not realize the how to use this power effectively and responsibly.</p><h4>References</h4>","Hungary's economy is suffering from a lack of demand, deficit spending, and corruption":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Hungary is suffering an economic downturn due to a lack of demand for their products, their excessive deficit spending, and the corruption in Orban’s government.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This economic downturn could potentially end Orban’s regime in Hungary.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Polish economic miracle is largely a result of EU benefits and a diversified economy\">The Polish economic miracle is largely a result of EU benefits and a diversified economy</a></li></ul>","Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Most famously through his work <em>The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order</em>, Huntington established the importance of political culture as a driver of international relations, arguing that post-Cold War, political developments were more likely to be along the lines of conflicting political cultures.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This marks a significant change from the previous study of international relations, as the study of political culture had largely been abandoned since the late 19th century and the early 20th century due to it being seen as unscientific, too Western orientated, and, in general, too weak as a predictor of anything.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Huntington predicted that in the aftermath of the Cold War, the various ideological blocs that had formed would break apart and reform based on culture instead (called \"civilizations\").  He points to several pieces of evidence in order to support his claim:</p><ul><li>The break up of Yugoslavia was very much on cultural lines, and Great Powers often came into conflict with one another due to conflicts between the civilizations that they supported in the conflict.</li><li>ASEAN’s attempts at establishing itself as a unified entity failed, largely due to the incompatibility of the Buddhist and Islamic civilizations.</li></ul><p>Later, another piece of evidence seemed to support his claims:</p><ul><li>The Western intervention in the Middle East failed, which is a clear example of how despite Western attempts to establish institutions in the Islamic civilization, due to their fundamental differences in culture, such attempts were doomed to fail.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a><ul><li>There must be some non-trivial level of correctness to his theory, since it seems to be the case right now, especially when it concerns the division between the US and Europe.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political attitudes is one&#x27;s opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much\">Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much</a><ul><li>Clearly, culture plays some role.</li></ul></li></ul>","IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A big reasons why IBM’s market share has fallen in many different industries is because of their failure to invest in emerging technologies, which meant that other companies with began with much smaller market shares were able to out compete them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This demonstrates the importance of keeping up with technological innovation, as falling behind could mean losing a large part of your market share, especially in industries where it is easy to switch to a competitor.</p><h4>Notes</h4><ul><li>The industries that they lost in include: database software, operating systems, the PC market, semiconductors, etc.<ul><li>Much of its revenue comes from middle software and services (revenue growth has slowed here, however).</li><li>Its intense stock buyback program hurt growth in the 2010s.</li><li>Their investment in Linux was a good decision, since Linux has come to dominate the server industry, and their Red Hat serves as a main service that provides open source software to companies<ul><li>They adopt a flexible approach that doesn’t lock companies in when they choose to use their software, which they claim that customers wouldn’t want. <ul><li>This is interesting though, since they did something similar when they put DOS and OS2 on their computer, and it failed. This seems to have similar intentions.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage\">When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage</a><ul><li>The general claim that this case illustrates.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs\">Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs</a><ul><li>A structural version of the same failure, located in corporate form rather than in a single firm’s decisions.</li></ul></li></ul>","Ideas are getting harder to find":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>New ideas are getting more difficult to find.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could have massive implications for productivity growth in the future, as more and more resources are required to acquire new information.</p><h4>Graphs</h4><p> </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors\">Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors</a><ul><li>Related to the idea of productivity. Mostly likely since it is easier for technological advancements to benefit manufacturing than it is to benefit service sectors.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth\">A drop in productivity growth is hugely detrimental to overall economic growth</a><ul><li>Illustrates the impact that the events described herein could have.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth\">Economic growth is not solely caused by productivity growth</a><ul><li>At the same time, it should be recognized that economic growth is not complete related to productivity growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a><ul><li>A related idea that describes how low productivity could have an effect on the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A social truth is one which is able to the challenged by others. A “truth,” even if its actually true, which is never (able to be) challenged is a sort of “accidental” truth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains one of the important functions of freedom of expression.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Therefore, one of the important functions of freedom of expression is the creation of <em>robust social truths</em>, since freedom of expression encourages people to challenge “accidental truths.” An idea/system of thought which goes unchallenged “rots” over time and loses the its soul/spark. Eventually, the disciples to the thought mere begin to rote memorize the tenants of the thought and all vitality is lost. This is also why it is typically better to learn from the real world than just from textbooks or teachers, since the real world does not merely offer strawman versions of alternative truths, but is filled with people who vehemently defend their alternative visions.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>There are <em>logical truths</em> which do not need to be challenged in order to be proven to be true. However, these truths are merely products of deduction and logic, and are not the type of social truths discussed here.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a><ul><li>This is <em>not</em> the type of truth dealt with here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition\">Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition</a><ul><li>This is essentially the principle that is advocated here, applied to metaphysics rather than epistemology.</li></ul></li></ul>","Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People are defined more by what they are not than by what they are; what they are not is more important to politics than what they are.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a view of looking at politics and the way that politics happens different from the typical view.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Ideology is no longer the most important part about politics. </li><li>Rather, it is culture: ancestry, religion, language, history, values, customs, and institutions.</li><li>And with regarding these specific factors, people identify against the people who different from them in these specific factors, rather than specific identifying with people who share the same factors.</li></ul><h4>Christianity</h4><p>The same concept can be applied to the history of Christianity. Christianity has languished since there is no genuine “paganism” out there in order to sharpen and shape Christianity today. Contemporary Christianity has been so diluted and adulterated by secular influences and actions that it is not the Christianity that it once was.</p><ul><li>Joachim served the function of reinvigorating Christianity by challenges the status quo.</li><li>Other figures, such as St. Paul, Tertullian and St. Augustine, and Pascal have all served to preserve Christianity through their defense of what is “Christian,” though that term is so diluted now that it would be like punching at air.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Things get their meaning and identity from their relations within a structure</span><ul><li>This structuralist idea is supported here with Huntington's claim.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one\">Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one</a><ul><li>Again supported by/related to this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion\">Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion</a> <ul><li>Related to the discussion of Christianity here.</li></ul></li></ul>","If a superintelligence is able to rewrite its own utility function, it would be neutralized as a threat to humanity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If a superintelligence is able to rewrite its own utility function such that it can reach its own bliss point, there would simply be no reason for such a being to endlessly consume resources and thus eliminates it as a threat to human survival.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents a solution to the problem of AI superintelligence that is typically not considered.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that human societies have already moved in a similar direction. Rather than endlessly increasing our consumption of physical resources, we’ve shifted more towards services. Additionally, if AI is able to achieve a bliss state, it would mean that it has violated local nonsatiation. By designing AI in this manner, we will be able to mitigate the possibility that AI endlessly consumes resources that humans need.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that if we design AIs such that they attempt to satisfy their goals doing as little as possible, their utility functions would be designed such that it is as easy as possible to satisfy. Therefore, it will ensure that AI does not compete with humanity for the same resources.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI represents a new epistemological category\">AI represents a new epistemological category</a><ul><li>It could be the case that designing an AI with capped capabilities as described here could prevent it from successfully manifesting its purpose to the fullest extent?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a><ul><li>This note provides a reason why perhaps they should not be afraid of AI.</li></ul></li></ul>","If an molecule has and O or N, bonded to C or H, it is likely polar":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>To determine polarity, use this simple method: if there is a “strong” molecule ($O$ or $N$), and they are bonded to a “weaker” molecule (that has less electronegativity), the molecule is polar.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>This is an important concept in biology, since it is important to know if a molecule is polar. For instance, The more polar a molecule, the more soluble it is in water is an example of how this Zettel can be applied.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>If a “strong” molecule bonds with another strong molecule, it is non-polar. If a \"strong\" molecule bonds with a \"weak\" molecule, then it is polar. The bonds that are formed here are covalent bonds (see Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds). Strength here is determine by electronegativity, as The more electronegative an atom, the more it wants electrons.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds\">Biology relies on covalent and ionic bonds, not metallic bonds</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The more electronegative an atom, the more negatively charged it is\">The more electronegative an atom, the more negatively charged it is</a></li></ul>","Immigrants of all types are a net benefit to the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All types of immigrants contribute to helping reduce the federal debt, because they consume less resources than native citizens, and sometimes contribute more in tax revenue than native citizens.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the ideas that immigration is bad for the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>From 1994 to 2023, immigrants generated $24.2 trillion in tax revenue compared to triggering only $13.6 trillion in costs. When factoring the amount saved in interest, this means that immigrants generated $14.5 trillion in surplus.<ul><li>In fact, without immigrants, the US debt-to-GDP ratio would have surpassed 205% of GDP, which is widely considered to be the threshold beyond which a country enters an economic crisis.</li><li>A lot of government spending is fixed, and does not expand based on the population. This means that immigrants actually help diffuse the costs of such programs among the US population.</li></ul></li><li>The average immigrant generates 23% more taxes than the citizen. This is because<ul><li>Immigrants are more 20% more likely to be prime working age, meaning that the state does not have to pay for their education, nor their retirement benefits (yet, and even then, not all immigrants qualify) and</li><li>Immigrants are 12% more likely to be employed.</li></ul></li><li>The majority of the taxes that immigrants pay are hidden, which is a result of the nature of the US tax system.<ul><li>This manifests through payroll taxes, property taxes (passed through to renters), and corporate incidence (corporate tax generations driven by labor expansion).</li></ul></li><li>In fact, even illegal immigrants contribute to the surplus, since while they have a much lower tax compliance rate, they receive 75% less in old-age benefits and use half the educational resources of the US on average.</li><li>Second generation immigrants are likewise lucrative, since they have a the highest educational rate.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In the US, direct state expenditures are often &#x27;submerged&#x27; through indirect tax breaks\">In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks</a><ul><li>Likewise, many tax receipts are not properly documented due to the existence of things like payroll taxes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay\">Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay</a><ul><li>Some of these immigrants may be more likely to take more dangerous jobs.</li></ul></li></ul>","Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A main point of difference between Imperial Chinese astronomy and the astronomy that was occurring in the West was that while Chinese astronomy was largely predictive, i.e., people studied astronomy in order to <em>predict</em> the arrange of celestial elements, the Wests’ study was more <em>casual-theoretic</em>, i.e., investigations into the reasons for particular motions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This may explain the lack of a scientific revolution in China.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While Chinese astronomy was focused on predicting future events, European astronomy was much more focused on understanding the reasons for why things worked. This was largely because of cultural differences and the fact that only the predictive aspect was of interest to the Chinese state (since it formed part of the Mandate of Heaven of the ruler to be able to predict astronomical events), and it was banned for private individuals.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese state’s monopoly on knowledge prevented a scientific revolution\">The Imperial Chinese state’s monopoly on knowledge prevented a scientific revolution</a><ul><li>Chinese astronomy was monopolized by the state.</li></ul></li></ul>","Implementing a minimum wage is ineffective for gig workers, since their supply is so elastic":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is ineffective to place a minimum wage on gig workers, since they supply is so elastic that it can adjust easily to any such practice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This offers a critique of why minimum wages should not be implemented for gig workers.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>For instance, is a minimum wage is implemented for Uber drivers, because there are so many people with a car, the supply of driver would simply increase and there would be less work for each Uber driver, essentially keeping their overall net hourly wage the same. When the experiment was done in Seattle, the following was noted:</p><ul><li>Consumers tipped less.</li><li>Each full time diver experienced a -26% change in the number of monthly tasks, due to the decreased demand (from high prices) and more drivers.</li><li>All in all, the net change in the amount earned by the drivers was 0.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minimum wage can reduce unemployment\">The minimum wage can reduce unemployment</a><ul><li>Not in this case.</li></ul></li></ul>","In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In Receptor Tyrosine Kinases, signals are amplified through kinase cascades, which the receptor itself triggers (by phosphorylating a kinase itself). In G-Protein-Coupled Receptors, an enzyme like adenylyl cyclase creates many cAMP (second messenger), which amplifies the signal.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two different signal transduction pathways have different means of amplifying the signal, which is something that I often forget.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In signal transduction pathways, enzymes cause signal amplification\">In signal transduction pathways, enzymes cause signal amplification</a></li></ul>","In a Rawlsian society, people would be educated for autonomy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a Rawlsian society, people would not be brainwashed into having Rawlsian ideas, but rather, would simply be educated in autonomy, such that they are able to derive the principles of justice themselves from the original position.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to side-step accusations of brainwashing in the Rawlsian society.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems unlikely to be that even if educated in autonomy, people will be able to all come to the same conclusions that Rawls did, because Rawls’ logic relies on people having specific utility functions which would naturally differ between people.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position\">Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position</a><ul><li>Something like this would be an assumption that wouldn’t really be able to be made.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>The issue is that people’s assumptions of the original position might differ.</li></ul></li></ul>","In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Under a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not undertaken merely as a means to satisfy some need. It is also (largely) taken as an end in and of itself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of Weber’s argument in The Protestant Work Ethic.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>I think that while this is true to a certain extent, “in and of itself” is too overarching of a statement to me. More accurately, it would be that capitalism incentives economic acquisition as a means of providing more economic acquisition in the future (the accumulated wealth being thought of as economic capital). In a sense, Weber is right that the accumulation of capital can be thought of as being in and of itself (since capital is useless on its own), though this explanation makes it more clear.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism\">The Protestant Work Ethic</a></li></ul>","In a conductor, the charged particles remain on the edges of the object":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a conductor, because the charged particles want to stay away from each other, they move to the edge of the conductor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that on the inside, a conductor is in electrostatic equilibrium.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Free electron movement is the precondition for charge reaching the surface.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Strong induction sees electrons move throughout the entire object; local polarization effects only see the shifting of electron clouds\">Strong induction sees electrons move throughout the entire object; local polarization effects only see the shifting of electron clouds</a><ul><li>Explains why whole-body redistribution is possible in a conductor but not an insulator.</li></ul></li></ul>","In a democracy, the adjudicative power and the executive power should not be vested in the same person":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a democracy, one must be able to separate the adjudicative and the executive powers, since allowing the same person to hold both positions gives them too much power, and decentralizing the power in this way means that competing incentives between the two people holding respective positions weakens the influence of both.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle mentioned this as being especially important when it comes to assigning offices in a democracy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This can manifest in the practice of makes the multitude the deliberative body (or having people from this group hold the power of deliberation) while having the notables, i.e., the virtuous, be the executive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Power corrupts those who possess it\">Power corrupts those who possess it</a><ul><li>When a person has too much power, as here, it ends badly for others/their enemies.</li></ul></li></ul>","In empires, the north is typically where the political control is based, and the south is typically where the economic power is":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is often the case in large land-based empires that the political control originated from the North, while the economics prosperity originates from the South.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a general pattern that can be observed in many states and can explain their formation/the things that they do.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>China</li><li>US</li><li>Russia</li><li>India</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>This is an example of this principle in practice.</li></ul></li></ul>","In life, one has nothing to regret":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is nothing for us to regret in life, since even if we had acted differently, things would probably have turned out the same way. In other words, in most circumstances, things are simply not as important as it might be suggested in the moment.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that, once understood, changes the way that we look at life. A lot of things that previously seemed like a big deal suddenly become a lot more manageable.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The direction of ones life is not easily changed, and by struggling against one, one will never be able to “conquer life.” Rather, a more sensible strategy is to accept the ebbs and flows that life presents us, and remember that even though things might seem earth-shattering at the moment, in the long run, they are not that influential. A reason for this is that our life is built up through small moments, a constant accumulation. Our life is more akin to Darwinian evolution than Lamarkianism. We change slowly, and there are very few moments that actually decide our life directions, even though it may seem that way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a><ul><li>It seems that this is also something implied by the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Life is too short for regrets\">Life is too short for regrets</a><ul><li>Something else implied by this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is the little moments that shape us in life, not mainly the big, especially memorable ones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Although the big events in life are what we remember, the big events are merely culminations of the little moments.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>What is most valuable in life is to live everyday with intention and vitality, since they are what ultimately shape us for the big moments in life. But the big moments is ultimately not the important part either. What actually matters for quality of life and satisfaction is the routines.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Right now, it seems to me that to live life well in this sense means to constantly see life through a new lens, to discover the undiscovered, and to approach life with a fortitude and endurance.</p><h4>References</h4>","In order to recapture economic growth, the EU must deregulate and standardize market rules":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the past 50 years or so, Europe has been unable to recapture the explosive economic growth that characterized the post-war boom. This is because of the excessive regulation and fragmentation of the European Common Market.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates the negative impacts of regulation on economic growth, as many European regulations, such as the GDPR, despite the best intentions, have greatly reduced economic growth.</p><h4>Possible Reforms</h4><p>Currently, regulations are often conducted on a national basis, which introduces a barrier when trading across borders. This introduces a \"regulatory thicket\" whereby there are overlapping regulations and confusing laws, adding to the difficultly of international trade. Additionally, there is a lack of specialized commercial courts that slow down market enforcement. At the same time, the EU should rely on existing international organizations, as to not create duplicate organizations that only add bloat to the system. Lastly, regulation should be subject to greater rigorous cost-benefit analysis, as well as introducing sunset clauses, rather than having the regulations remain indefinitely.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The laws and regulations of the EU often end up spreading outside its borders\">The laws and regulations of the EU often end up spreading outside its borders</a></li></ul>","In proteins, secondary structure describes interactions between backbones, and tertiary structure, R groups":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a protein, secondary structure is specific referring to the interactions between the backbones of a polypeptide chain. Tertiary structure refers to the interactions between the R groups of a polypeptide chain.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein\">Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When the polarity of an amino acid is discussed, it is specifically referring to the polarity of the R group\">When the polarity of an amino acid is discussed, it is specifically referring to the polarity of the R group</a></li></ul>","In signal transduction pathways, enzymes cause signal amplification":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In signal transcription pathways, enzymes are the locations where the signal is amplified.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant for AP Bio, especially understanding how signal pathways work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers\">In RTKs, signals are amplified through kinase cascades; in GPCR, signals are amplified through second messengers</a><ul><li>The two receptor families in which this amplification is realized differently.</li></ul></li></ul>","In the US, direct state expenditures are often 'submerged' through indirect tax breaks":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it may seem like the US federal government spends less than other, similar Western governments, much of the “spending” is hidden in tax breaks for individuals and businesses.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why it seems like the US government spends so little money compared to other world governments.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a></li></ul>","Income mobility is becoming more difficult to achieve":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Some evidence that suggests that it is more and more difficult for children to be able to rise and earn more than their parents has seen a dramatic decline in the US over the last half-century.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This supports the idea that income inequality is going to worsen.</p><h4>Evidence</h4><ul><li>For children born in 1940, 90% earned more than their parents, but for those born in the 1980s, that number plummets to just 50%.</li><li>Most of this decline was from the industrial Midwest.</li><li>It was primarily caused by the unequal distribution on the GDP growth, not the fact that there was not GDP growth.</li></ul><h5>Intergenerational Mobility</h5><ul><li>Economists have found a “Great Gatsby curve” whereby countries such as the US or UK, where there is relatively higher income inequality, have lower intergenerational mobility compared to Denmark or Norway, where there is much higher income mobility.</li><li>There have theorized to be several causes for this:<ul><li>Richer families are able to have greater human capital investment in their children, ensuring that their children have more educational resources and thus more human capital.</li><li>Societies with a higher “college premium” tend to have lower mobility.</li><li>Rich people have more connections and can engage in nepotism.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><em>Defending the One Percent</em><ul><li>Contrasts with the above note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>While this article does not attack the idea of meritocracy, it provide evidence that meritocracy is failing.</li></ul></li></ul>","Industrial policy requires wage suppression":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Often, successful industrial policy has relied on states to implement wage suppression.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that, while industrial policy can work, it is difficult to do since it requires extensive state intervention and the suppression of labor rights.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>We can look to India and Japan for an illustration of this. At the beginning of the 20th century, the two countries had approximately equal levels of wages and productivity. However, due to the more luddite views of Indian laborers, and the fact that there is an inelastic supply of labor in India, Japan was able to surpass in Indian in terms of (lower) levels of wages and higher productivity. This led to successful industrialization in the country.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This does not mean that wages did not grow, but that they did not grow as much as they could have.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Many Asian countries also employed industrial policy in their rise to prominence.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations\">Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations</a><ul><li>This could also serve as a reason for success of East Asian nations.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inefficient taxes are a means of keeping the power of the government in check":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Inefficiency in taxes are a means by which the people resist against the encroachment of state power. The people want to be taxed less, so they put up less resistance to taxes that are more inefficient, and since they put up resistance to taxes that are more efficient, the government chooses not to tax those, and instead tax the easier-to-tax but more inefficient options.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the persistence of inefficient taxes in the economy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>This is an example of when people resist taxes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation\">The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation</a><ul><li>The reason that the LVT has not been implemented could be that it is too efficient, and thus by implementing it, the government would gain too much power.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inequality has dropped globally, though increased within countries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Inequality has not skyrocketed in the world, despite what is often claimed in the media.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This disproves a common myth that is spread that inequality has been continually rising around the globe, but supports the idea that inequality has increased intra-nation.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> Since the early 2000s, and especially with the 2008 Financial Crisis, inequality has been falling across the world. Global income inequality has also been falling. At the same time, inequality has risen in individual countries. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>A potential cause of the inequality is outlined.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a><ul><li>A fallacious claim about inequality is that it harms mental health.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that there is constant race between education/human capital advancement and technological innovation. While human capital advancement reduces inequality (since it empowers labor), technological innovation worsens inequality (since it is capital heavy).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant since it offers an explanation for why inequality exists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The following explains the importance of education in income. </p><h4>Note</h4><p>Mankiw presses the same explanation against the rent-seeking account of the top one per cent: the earnings differential between skilled and unskilled workers over the postwar period follows the same path as the income share of the top one per cent, which is what the technology story predicts and what a rent-seeking story does not.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a><ul><li>Related to economic inequality, namely that it does not really harm the individual mentally.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Heritability makes intergenerational income correlation consistent with equality of opportunity\">Heritability makes intergenerational income correlation consistent with equality of opportunity</a><ul><li>The companion argument, applied to mobility rather than to the level of inequality.</li></ul></li></ul>","Innovation in fields such as green energy and pharmaceuticals are slowing down":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The rate of pharmaceutical innovation and green energy innovation has slowed, producing what is known as “Eroom’s Law” (reverse of Moore’s Law).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a potentially dangerous trend, because it means that productivity growth in the pharmaceutical sector is slowing.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are four causes for specifically pharmacuticals:</p><ol><li>Increasing competition from generic brand drugs.<ol><li>It is difficult for newly created drugs to compete effectively against generic brand drugs that often achieve the same purpose, but are priced far lower.</li></ol></li><li>Increasingly stricter regulations.<ol><li>Over time, the regulation surrounding the development of new drugs accumulate and the barrier becomes more difficult to overcome, since there are now higher standards.</li></ol></li><li>An increasing amount of money is being spent.<ol><li>In a stochastic process, more money does not mean greater efficiency.</li></ol></li><li>A bias towards “reductionism.”<ol><li>A trend towards looking at things through molecular perspective, rather than a more macro perspective. But this doesn’t really translate to the creation of new drugs.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Ultimately, it comes down to the fact that there are increasing restrictions on the ability of firms to innovate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is also a reason for the problem described in this note?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Producers capture almost none of the surplus generated through innovation\">Producers capture almost none of the surplus generated through innovation</a><ul><li>Is this the case here?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas are getting harder to find\">Ideas are getting harder to find</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is the reason why?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This note provides an example in support of this point.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>For instance, regulation my slow down innovation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage\">When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage</a><ul><li>If there is a lack of innovation then, it would also mean more entrenched advantages for existing players in the fields described in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Instant-runoff voting solves the spoiler effect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Instant-runoff voting (alternative voting) is a plurality voting system where the candidates are ranked. It solves the spoiler effect since people rank the party that they favor most as number one, and do not strategically vote by choosing the party that they dislike the least.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a significant advantage that instant-runoff voting has over standard SMP voting.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This being said, instant-runoff voting still shares many problems in common with SMP:</p><ul><li>Cannot prevent gerrymandering.</li><li>Is not proportional.</li><li>Does not elect the Condorcet Winner.</li><li>Both also trend towards two parties</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Instinct outperforms intellect under extreme conditions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Reasoning is a slow instrument that requires information and time, and in conditions that supply neither, an inherited disposition that encodes no reasons at all will outperform it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This bounds the claim that rational deliberation is the superior guide to action, and locates the boundary at the point where the cost of being wrong once is total.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The contrast in <em>To Build A Fire</em> is between a man who can calculate temperatures and a dog that cannot, and only one of them survives. The general form is that tacit knowledge is robust where explicit knowledge is brittle.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love\">Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love</a><ul><li>A parallel limit on the authority of reason, in a different domain.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>The same superiority of dispersed and tacit knowledge, at the level of a system.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should strive to overcome emotion with reason\">One should strive to overcome emotion with reason</a><ul><li>The counsel this claim qualifies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Institutional investors ensure that individual firm are correctly priced":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For the undiversified investor, since the market price does not actually account for the firm level risk of a firm (since institutional investors usually hold diversified portfolios), the market price is usually overvalued for the undiversified investor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why an investor should not be undiversified.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the investor looking to invest in a specific company, the company’s price will almost always be above the rational amount that they would be willing to pay since the institutional investor is willing to pay a higher price since they do not account for the firm risk (which is balanced out through their diversified portfolio).</p><h4>References</h4>","Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something that is repeatedly observable when viewing the historical process by which institutions were created is that often, new institutions created in order to rectify an existing flaw serves merely to reproduce the flaw in a different location.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the futility of attempting to create new institutions in order to solve fundamental flaws.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When institutions are created with the purpose of making up for some flaw within existing institutions, it is likely that they themselves will merely reproduce the flaws downstream. For example, the censorate of the Chinese civil service exams were designed to be able to catch corruption in the system and prevent unfairness in the exam system. However, it itself become an instrument of corruption and court politics.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>Part of the reason why institutions designed to solve a problem often don’t.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>So if they fail to solve the problem, it can lead to the creation of new institutions to solve the unsolved problems, as the size of the government drastically grows.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>One way a flaw in an institution could show itself.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient\">The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient</a><ul><li>Note regarding the example about the imperial civil service exams.</li></ul></li></ul>","Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All stable and successful institutions in history have had robust self-correcting institutions such that it is able to correct itself without the need for external stimuli.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core, identifying factor of successful institutions, which may help us understand why existing institutions work and design better institutions for the future.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Per Claude Opus 5:</p><ul><li>British Reform Acts of 1832, 1867, and 1884. Acemoglu and Robinson read these as elites extending the franchise under threat of revolution, because a promise to redistribute while keeping power was not credible. The 1832 Act raised the electorate from roughly 478,000 out of 24 million to 813,000, and the decades after saw radical social reform and a falling Gini in England and Wales. Pressure converted into rule change rather than rupture.</li><li>Westminster removing sitting prime ministers mid-term (Thatcher 1990, Truss 2022) without a regime crisis.</li><li>US 17th Amendment (1913) responding to legislature-appointed Senate corruption; the 13th–15th after 1865.</li><li>German Basic Law (1949) explicitly engineered against Weimar's failure modes: constructive vote of no confidence, 5% threshold, Constitutional Court.</li><li>Post-Watergate: FEC, FOIA amendments, Church Committee.</li><li>Pecora hearings into Glass-Steagall (1933).</li><li>Ostrom's design principles for long-enduring commons institutions include graduated sanctions, rapid access to low-cost local arenas for conflict resolution, and nested enterprises, with monitors who are accountable to users or are the users themselves. Japanese irrigation associations are the standard case.</li><li>Swiss cantons plus the referendum; US federalism. Errors are localized and cheap to reverse.</li></ul><h4>Homeostasis</h4><p>A contrast can be drawn between good self-corrections and the idea of homeostasis in biology. An instruction can be analogized to be a single person, where our internal system is able to self-correct only with the need for external stimuli. On the other hand, good institutions are ones that are able to self-correct without the need for external stimuli, mostly because such external stimuli is almost always negative and harmful for the overall stability of the institution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>This note is a specific example of the broader pattern identified in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>Good institutions don’t do this? Perhaps self-correction allows the flaw to be minimized.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>Important for the design of good institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>Perhaps whether an institution has self-correcting mechanisms can affect the level of trust in that institution.</li></ul></li></ul>","Insurance companies are not the main reason for the high costs in the US health care system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Health care insurance providers are not the main cause of the high prices of US health insurance. Rather, the high cost is due to a combination of the providers and the AMA.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides an alternative argument for the reason that health care costs are so high in the US.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several things which point to the fact that the reason for the high cost of health care is not the insurance companies:</p><ul><li>The profits of insurance companies are low compared to the average in the S&amp;P 500, with the largest insurance company having a profit margin of 6%, and the second largest having between 2 and 4%. The average profit margin of a company on the S&amp;P 500 is around double that.<ul><li>The paltry margin means that if the largest insurance company, UnitedHealth Group, were to donate every single dollar of its profits to buying Americans more health care, they would only be able to buy 9% more.</li><li>Even if we were to look at the return on equity, the ROE of health insurance companies are still lower than that of the general population of companies in the S&amp;P 500. Regardless, the financial success of these companies don’t matter, since neither way their small profit margins means that they can’t pay out that much extra health insurance anyways.</li></ul></li><li>The majority of the cost comes from the higher cost by providers.<ul><li>The doctors and hospitals that actually provide care at the largest driver of health care costs, which demand some government intervention in order to negotiate the lower cost of drugs (through if done poorly, this may reduce innovation).</li><li>Due to the litigious nature of the US health care system, doctors will practice defensive medicine even in ambiguous situation. </li></ul></li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>Americans actually pay a smaller amount out-of-pocket compared to many other rich countries. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized\">The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized</a><ul><li>Even though their system is highly socialized, it seems that the Japanese pay more out of pocket compared to Americans.</li></ul></li><li><em>How to Cure Health Care</em><ul><li>Some of the points made here are also made in the linked article.</li></ul></li></ul>","Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that intellectualism and rationalism can only be used to rationalize selfishness. The only way for love to occur is through impulsiveness and emotion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important ideas from <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In <em>TBK</em>, Ivan continues to rationalize, and he does so to such a degree that he is genuinely unable to love.</p><ul><li>For instance, when he comes upon the freezing peasant the second time, in an impulsive decision, he carries the man to the police station and hopes for his recovery.</li><li>He also originally at the desire to confess about his role in the murder of his father, and Dimitri’s innocence, but he rationalized this action away, saying that he will do it tomorrow (after the trial starts).</li><li>However, when he does go in tomorrow, his account doesn’t make anymore sense, since if he were truly responsible, he would have confessed to it earlier.</li></ul><p>Dimitri’s impulsiveness and baseness is eventually what allows him to love others.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular\">It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular</a><ul><li>Whereas the former love is an intellectual love, the latter love is “true love,” in the sense that it is joining humanity’s shared connection.</li></ul></li></ul>","Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Interlocking patents, when very minute pieces of information are able to be patented, stalls innovation rather than promoting it. This is because new inventions are built off of old ones, and if old technology is continually patented, it leaves no room for new innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a step in Chang’s argument against the strict patent and IPR laws that are in place.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>I think that there are some holes with this argument. Chang doesn’t specify what he means when he says that a piece of information is “minute.” Who is to say whether Amazon’s one click purchase is too small of an invention to patent? When does an idea become no longer minute?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a></li></ul>","Intermolecular forces interact between non polar molecules":"<h5>Summary</h5><p>Some of the key intermolecular forces include hydrogen bonds and Van der Waals interactions.</p><h5>Significance</h5><p>Intermolecular forces are important since they play an inportmant role in biology, for example, in the formation of amino acids.</p><h4>Forces</h4><ul><li><strong>Hydrogen bonds</strong>: a result of partial chrages of polar molecules.</li><li><strong>Van der Waals</strong>: a weak attractive force that occurs between all molecules.</li></ul><p>Hydrogen bonds allow for the formation of secondary structures, as the various R-structures of polypeptides interact with each other to create $\\alpha$ -helices and $\\beta$ -sheets. They are most prominent in non-polar molecules, since such molecules have no other interactions, but are present in all molecular interactions.</p>","International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the high cost of running a patent office and ensuring that all inventions are account for, this may result in a drag on the economies of developing nations (the ones that don’t yet have technology). As a result, this system only serves to benefit countries that are already hubs of innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This fits into Chang’s general argument regarding the weakening of existing patent and IPR laws.</p><h4>Quote</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Martin Wolf, <em>Why Globalization Works</em>, pg. 217</p><p>[Patents and similar forms of protection] is a rent-extraction device for [developing nations], with potentially devastating consequences for their ability to educate their people (because of copyright), adapt deigns for their own use (ditto) and deal with severe challenges of public health.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a><ul><li>The individual-level behaviour that produces the incumbent advantage described here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li></ul>","Interpersonal relations in the Confucian tradition are characterized by power imbalance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The “Five Relationships” that Confucianism followed are examples of religiously entrenched power imbalances designed to “preserve harmony.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could explain the vast disparities in power that exist in modern China, by tracing the normalization of such power disparities to a keystone in Chinese culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Society consists primarily of unequal relationship; i.e., father and son, husband and wife, ruler and subject. At the same time, positions of power could be achieved meritocracy.<ul><li>This provided justification for inequality in society, but also set the expectation that those in power would yield the power justly and care for those below them.</li></ul></li><li>By maintaining these relationships, harmony would prevail.</li></ul><p>In this way, Confucian ethics can be said to emphasize gradations, where some (such as one’s parents) are loved more than others (like a stranger). This is in contrast with Mohism’s teachings.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives\">Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives</a><ul><li>It is these categorical imperatives that maintain the rigid social order.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>This is perhaps the reason for the strictly hierarchical Chinese state.</li></ul></li></ul>","Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While new patents have to be new and original, inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality. This leads to a system of interlocking patents, which make new innovation difficult.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial aspect of the argument that Chong sets forth in his book.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>The international-level result of this incentive, where established innovation hubs capture the gains.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a></li></ul>","Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Following the fall of the Srivijaya Empire in the 13th century, Muslim merchants and proselytizer were able to convert much of the areas former Buddhism into Islam.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason that Islam is so prevalent in Southeast Asia.</p><h4>References</h4>","Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Unlike the West, which places its emphasis on loyalty to the nation-state, Islamic cultures have a much heavier emphasis on loyalty on the clan level, and beyond the nation-state level, regarding the <em>ummah</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the lack of inter-state conflict in the Islamic world. This also means that the West’s focus on the nation-state level may be misguided.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Western civilizations, when viewing their loyalty, can be seen to place the largest emphasis to loyalty on a specific nation-state, not one’s immediate community or the large religious community that one is part of. Islamic civilizations on the other hand, emphasize exactly the opposite. Of note is that while the <em>ummah</em> explicitly presupposes the illegitimacy of the nation-state, it would only be able to be united through the actions of such a state. Otherwise, there is no political unity.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest\">Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest</a><ul><li>Perhaps it is this ignorance of the supremacy of the nation-state that allowed Islam to be spread to effectively through trade?</li></ul></li></ul>","Islamic states are far more likely to engage in violence than non-Islamic ones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It has been observed than Muslim states are more likely to engage in violence than non-Muslims ones, both towards other Muslim countries and non-Muslims countries.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could explain the relatively higher amount of conflicts between Muslims, other Muslims, and non-Muslims.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are many possible reasons for this being the case that have been proposed:</p><ul><li>Historical culture which glorified conquest for Muslims.</li><li>Due to their historical conquests, Muslims often live in close proximity to people of different cultures.</li><li>Muslim culture is less tolerant of non-Muslims, drawing a clear distinction between <em>Dar Al-Islam</em> and <em>Dar Al-harb</em>.</li><li>Muslims victimization by the West (although this would not explain Muslim conflicts against non-Muslim minorities).</li><li>The absence of a core state in the Muslim world.</li><li>Youth bulge, meaning there is an excess in the amount of population of fighting age.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a><ul><li>This could be part of the reason for the conflict, since conflict based on religion is common.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest\">Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest</a><ul><li>Although they might have spread through trade, violence is now erupting.</li></ul></li></ul>","Issues involving justice involve high levels of utility":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Claims of justice and injustice are especially weighty, utility-wise, compared to other, non-justice claims. In other words, they are incommensurate with other issues of mere expediency.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This makes justice <em>sui genris</em> as an utilitarian phenomenon, and thus requiring special treatment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This idea by Mill is not claiming that there is some lexical priority which must be given to the principles of justice, as Rawls does. Rather, he merely claims that, <em>in general,</em> claims regarding justice are heavier ethical claims, which means that they would then be considered beyond matters of mere expediency.</p><h4>References</h4>","It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This was an idea expressed by a speaker in one of Zossima’s stories. In essence, it argues that while it may be difficult to make grand propositions of one’s love of humanity in general (even being willing to sacrifice oneself for humanity), it seems much more difficult to actually love particular people, because the annoyance of mundane existence overpowers this more noble sense of universal love.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This highlights a principle that is often neglected when we think about what it means to “love” humanity. The difficulty comes down to loving individual people, not the abstract idea of “humanity.”</p><h4>Quote</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Father Zossima, from <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></p><p>\"It's just the same story as a doctor once told me,\" observed the elder. \"He was a man getting on in years, and undoubtedly clever. He spoke as frankiy as you, though in jest, in bitter jest. 'I love humanity,' he said, 'but I wonder at myself. <strong>The more I love humanity in general, the less I love man in particular.</strong> <strong>In my dreams,</strong>' he said, '<strong>I have often come to making enthusiastic schemes for the service of humanity, and perhaps I might actually have faced crucifixion if it had been suddenly necessary; and yet I am incapable of living in the same room with any one for two days together, as I know by experience.</strong> As soon as any one is near me, his personality disturbs my self- complacency and restricts my freedom. In twenty-four hours I begin to hate the best of men: one because he's too long over his dinner; another because he has a cold and keeps on blowing his nose. I become hostile to people the moment they come close to me. <strong>But it has always happened that the more I detest men individually the more ardent becomes my love for humanity.</strong>'\"</p></blockquote><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This seems to connect to the overall idea in <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em> of the contrast between the base and the noble. It is an example of when the base (the annoyance with others in the mundanity of life) overpowers the noble (an idealistic desire to love humanity universally). Father Zossima tries to do both, taking responsibility for all of humanity’s sins while seeking to love them individually. Ivan is almost like the man that is talked about in this passage. He has a penchant for viewing things through a spectator point of view, gaining enjoyment out of that, but when viewing things in particular, he begins to despise humanity. This is what drives his argument in “Rebellion.” We have these romanticized notions about the character traits that humanity has with regards to the good (love, compassion, etc.), though this image breaks down when we view individuals in particular.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Even if God exists, we ought not to believe in Him\">Even if God exists, we ought not to believe in Him</a><ul><li>This is the argument that Ivan makes in “Rebellion.”</li></ul></li></ul>","It is impossible to construct a universal theory of morality for liberalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>With Rawls’ A Theory of Justice and Mill’s works as examples, while it seems that both attempt to construct a moral theory which is able to exist in a liberal political framework, though they both fail. Therefore, it seems that it is just impossible to construct a universal moral theory for liberalism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this is true, then people should stop attempting to do so.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The moral theories proposed in <em>A Theory of Justice</em> and Mill’s works, such as <em>Utilitarianism</em> or <em>On Liberty</em> seems to have unsolvable flaws which undermine the very nature of the frameworks. The fact that they have both failed seems to suggest that it is simply impossible to construct a moral framework for liberalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>This work is mentioned here as an example for the impossibility of constructing a moral theory for liberalism.</li></ul></li></ul>","It is not always good to know the truth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that someone, while searching for something, lead themselves to their own doom.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in many works of art and literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h5>King Hamlet</h5><ul><li>Hamlet’s primary motivation throughout the entirety of the play is searching for truth, to the extent that one can pin truth as the bedrock of his motivation to do anything at all. At the same time, his enthusiasm for truth is not met in part by his actual actions, as he does not actually act on his true beliefs.</li><li>This is relevant for understanding Hamlet, the main character of Hamlet the play. By understanding what drives Hamlet, we are better able to understand the reasons for the plot functioning in the way that it does.</li></ul><h5>Victor Frankenstein (Robert Walton)</h5><ul><li>Both Victor and Robert’s desires for truth are what undergirds the adventures that they go on.</li><li>Victor’s search for the means of creating life, to become the “modern Prometheus,” is eventually what leads to his downfall, as he creates a monster. He also dies in his journey to kill this creature that he creates.</li><li>Robert Walton’s desire to explore the North Pole is similar. He is driven by a desire to advance human knowledge, and would have continued on his quest if not for Victor’s tale.</li><li>The creature also expresses this sentiment: <ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I cannot describe to you the agony that these reflections inflicted upon me: I tried to dispel them, but sorrow only increased with knowledge. Oh, that I had forever remained in my native wood, nor known nor felt beyond the sensations of hunger, thirst and heat!</span><ul><li>While meeting humanity gave him access to many different experiences that are positive, he also found out about his true nature as a hideous creature, something that, had he never met humanity, he would never have found out.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Increase of knowledge only discovered to me more clearly what a wretched outcast I was.</span></li></ul></li></ul><h5>Oedipus</h5><ul><li>Oedipus searches for the truth through the entirety of the play, and the same search for the truth is also what ultimately brings him down.</li><li>This dedication to the truth is what eventually brings Oedipus down, as he find out about the truth regarding the relationship between him and his wife/mother, and the fact that he kills his father.</li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">TIRESIAS: None of you knows— and I will never reveal my dreadful secrets, not to say your own. OEDIPUS: What? You know and you won't tell? You're bent on betraying us, destroying Thebes?</span><ul><li>Here, it is the case of Tiresias having the truth. Yet, due to having the truth,  he is yelled at and blamed by Oedipus.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">OEDIPUS: Who primed you for this? Not your prophet's trade. TIRESIAS: You did, you forced me, twisted it out of me.</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">OEDIPUS: What—give up now, with a clue like this? Fail to solve the mystery of my birth? Not for all the world! JOCASTA: Stop—in the name of god, if you love your own life, call off this search! My suffering is enough.</span><ul><li>Jocasta, having heard that Oedipus was not the true son of the Corinthian king and queen, and having heard that he was given to them by a shepherd, begins the realize the truth and begs Oedipus to stop his search. Yet, Oedipus is adamant about finding out the terrible truth.</li></ul></li></ul><h5>Jack Burden</h5><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, p. 590</p><p>So I went back down and stood in the garden among the black magnolia trees and the myrtles, and thought how by killing my father I had saved my mother's soul. Then I thought how maybe I had saved my father's soul too. Both of them had found out what they needed to know to be saved. <strong>Then I thought how all knowledge that is worth anything is maybe paid for by blood. Maybe that is the only way you can tell that a certain piece of knowledge is worth anything: it has cost some blood.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>The truth is not something that can be wield and sought and acquired willy-nilly. It’s something that people will defend with blood and tears and something that can ruins others completely.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Frankenstein (book)\">Frankenstein (book)</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects\">A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects</a><ul><li>This is a theme that is present in many of the cases described in this note, as a strict deference to the value of honesty and the safety of the people (Oedipus), or a strict intellectual rigor (Hamlet), or scientific discovery and human technological advancement (Frankenstein) ultimately brings them all down.</li></ul></li></ul>","It is not good for man to be alone":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Given my God to Adam in the Garden of Eden, God says how people ought to live in a community, and not just isolate themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a commandment that God gives.</p><h4>Bible</h4><ul><li>This is a key insight into what it means to be human, since it is NOT GOOD that man is alone. <ul><li>This is a contrast with the “and it was good” form the previous verses.</li></ul></li><li>Man is set up as a mediator between the earthly and the heavenly, answerable to both God and other humans.</li><li>A person’s identity is defined in terms of his relationship with others. <ul><li>Adam is only able to name himself “ish” after he names Even “isha”</li></ul></li></ul><h4><em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></h4><p>This commandment relates to the idea that Dostoevsky explicates in <em>TBK</em>, where he essentially advocates for Christian Socialism and existentializes the process of confession into something earthly, specifically, public/group confession. He also states explicitly that isolation from others (especially as a result of sin/guilt) is the existentialized version of hell.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Brothers Karamazov\">The Brothers Karamazov</a></li></ul>","Japan's economy is sustained by a highly leveraged sovereign wealth fund":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Japan is able to sustain its economy through the central bank’s leveraging of the huge amount of savings in the country, whereby domestic savings are reinvested into higher-return, international assets.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The BOJ is able to enact their strategy due to the low interest rate in Japan, as well as the depreciated nature of the yen. However, if interest rates were to rise, or if the yen were to appreciate, this would likely lead to extremely, if any at all, margins. Ultimately, this system, though effective so far, puts the savings of ordinary Japanese in huge danger.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized\">The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Japanese companies care fundamentally structured differently than Western ones, making them better at incremental progress than drastic breakthroughs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Japanese companies have dominated some fields while Western companies dominate over ones.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Overall, the Japanese system is more decentralized and shareholder focused. For instance,</p><ul><li>unions are company based, and not industry based.</li><li>workers are more generalized than in Western firms, so they can solve problems all along a production process.<ul><li>The <em>andon</em> cord is something unique to Japanese manufacturing, whereby anyone alone a production line can pull the string if they spot any flaws in the product.</li></ul></li><li>there is very little equity from investors, and its mostly from other companies. This means that companies are not in pursuit of short-term profit, but can rather focus on long-term goals.</li><li>people who typically work their whole lives at a single company, and there is never any mass layoffs.</li><li>promotions based on seniority, not productivity.</li></ul><p>All of these facets of the system are interconnected and present as a unified system whereby they cannot be implemented individually. And all of this means that the Japanese firm is able to expand into a variety of industries that range widely.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage\">When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage</a><ul><li>The general mechanism this corporate structure runs into.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries\">IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries</a><ul><li>A Western case of the same outcome, arrived at through decisions rather than through structure.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice and injustice can manifest in-whole, discretely, or through sum-threshold criteria":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>(In)justice can manifest through various forms, including</p><ul><li>in its total form at once, </li><li>in a discrete form in a particular rule in an institution or in an institution among many institutions, or </li><li>apparently non-existently, though in sum, produces a visible effect.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the various forms that justice could take.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns</a><ul><li>When considering this, then, we have to also consider what form justice takes.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice and the Meritocratic State":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Presents a philosophical defense of meritocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important work that informs the philosophical nature of meritocracy as a defending point of view.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>Central Thesis: Justice is Realized when People Receive what They Deserve.</h5><p>Mulligan is a pluralist about morality, but a monist when it comes to justice.</p><ul><li>Justice and morality are not the same thing.</li><li>Additionally, being a pluralist about justice means that you can value different things (such as justice or efficiency) fairly, and not have an overwhelming bias towards one thing specifically.</li></ul><h5>Three Requirements of Justice</h5><ol><li>A theory of justice should suggest an economy that is reasonably efficient.<ol><li>Essentially, since there is always going to be an equity/efficiency trade-off, we should not sacrifice all efficiency in the pursuit of equity (as Rawls might suggest).</li></ol></li><li>A theory of justice should suggest morally attractive public policies.<ol><li>This is arrived through a process of reflective equilibrium.</li></ol></li></ol><p><code>I think that this point is relatively weak. While Mulligan claims that a refutation like 'our moral intuition may be fickle when it comes to tax policy' I don't believe that there is an 'intuition' in the same sense when it comes to tax policy at all. There may be an intuition that 'equal distribution is good', but an intuition of the kind 'the rich should be taxed x%' seems far stretched.</code></p><ol><li>A theory of justice must pass the Singer Test.<ol><li>The <strong>Singer Test</strong> states that moral opportunity costs of any public policy must be factored in. He quantifies this in terms of the number of lives potentially saved.</li></ol></li></ol><h5>Ideal and Non-ideal Justice</h5><p>There are essentially four challenges that non-ideal theories pose towards ideal theories, which Mulligan states the ideal theories (such as meritocracy) is able to meet.’</p><ol><li><strong>Feasibility:</strong> A theory of justice must be reachable from the actual world given available resources.</li><li><strong>Policy guidance:</strong> A theory of justice must be generate concrete and sensible policies that actually can serve as a policy guide. I.e., the policies that a theory creates must not unreasonably clash with our moral intuitions. ^policy-guidance</li><li><strong>Utopianism:</strong> A theory must not be utopian in nature, meaning that it must not demand too much from human beings.</li><li><strong>Justificatory incompleteness:</strong> A theory must be able to specify, down to the last details, its policy recommendations, and not remain in the realm of the abstract.</li></ol><h4>Empirical Evidence</h4><p>There is plenty of evidence to suggest that desert is a concept that is well-embedded in the human psyche, ever since we are born. It is even observed in many animals, suggesting a genetic origin. Likewise, we can present a rejection of Rawlsian justice here, since empirically, people rarely choose to subscribe to his theories. He then gives some reasons why we ought not to disregard intuition for desert specifically:</p><ol><li>If it is the case the philosophy simply ought to systematize concepts that are already implicit in our intuitions (as a metaphilosophical principle) then it is the case that since there are already a plethora of empirical evidences that demonstrate the existence of intuition for meritocracy, our work is done.</li><li>Given reflective equilibrium, the role of intuition remains important.</li><li>A theory that does not have the backing of intuition cannot serve as a justified guidance to justice. See <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice and the Meritocratic State\">Justice and the Meritocratic State › ^policy-guidance</a> .</li><li>It is <em>dangerous</em> to violate intuitions about desert, since when people live under a system that they consider to be unjust, there is more likely to be social turmoil, destabilizing social cooperation.</li><li>(This last point seems to be an elaboration on the earlier intuition point)</li></ol><h5>A Defense of Desert</h5><p>First, he sweeps away two of Rawls’ other concerns regarding desert.</p><ol><li><em>Desert is impracticable, since we can never truly find out who truly deserves something.</em><ol><li>But this is not true, since there are some deserts that are clearly observed (e.g., the fastest runner deserves the gold medals, and this is quantifiable).</li></ol></li></ol><p><code>Even in cases where it is not immediately observable who has merit, we can look to the market for such an determination (e.g., the best artist is the artist whose art we are willing to pay the most for).</code></p><ol><li><em>Desert is incompatible with public reason, that is, any distributive schemes must be justifiable to all reasonable citizens.</em><ol><li>The best theory that is compatible with public reason is meritocracy.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Then, he outlines Rawls’ argument in the following manner:</p><ul><li>P deserves x on the basis of y only if P deserves y.</li><li>If y originated in fortunate circumstances, then P does not deserve y.</li><li>All putative desert bases originated in fortunate circumstances.</li><li>Therefore, P does not deserve anything.</li></ul><p>There are currently two standard responses to the above arguments:</p><ol><li><strong>Zaitchik’s Moorean:</strong> The above principle proves too much, since it would mean that no one ever deserves anything at anytime, which is clearly absurd (reductio).<ol><li>If I baked a cake, I do not even deserve to have my cake because it was due to fortuitous circumstances that I am even able to bake it in the first place.</li></ol></li><li><strong>Sher:</strong> Rejects premise 1, claiming that the regression of desert stops at a certain point, such as effort.</li></ol><p>Mulligan proposes the following argument to strengthen their case: <strong>he rejects premise 4, that all putative deserts originate in fortune. Drawing on the concept of the essentiality of origin, some properties of ourselves are simply metaphysically necessary so that we are ourselves. Therefore, it is not the case that <em>all</em> desert bases originate in fortune.</strong></p><ul><li>The error with Rawls is that he believes that people have robust identities totally independent of their traits, meaning that you could totally alter a people’s traits, but still come out with the same person. </li><li>This line of thinking also illustrates an intuitive difference between establishing equal opportunity through either:<ul><li>Social policies, which mean perfectly reasonable,</li><li>And genetic modification of embryos to equalize natural abilities, which seems outrageous.</li></ul></li><li>An objection to the argument might be that since the assignment of genetics is purely arbitrary, it makes no sense to base desert off-of genetic assignment either.<ul><li>But this argument runs into the same issue that the previous one did, namely, that it fails to consider genetics as something metaphysically necessary for the person.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All philosophy reduces to metaphysics\">All philosophy reduces to metaphysics</a><ul><li>Perhaps that argument about the which Mulligan uses to refute Rawls can be seen as an example of this.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice as fairness only applies to institutions, and does not apply to individual dealings.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial caveat to the application of justice as fairness, namely, that it not cover the aspects of life that are not voluntary.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice as fairness is just a theory of the basic structure of society, and has a uniquely pervasive and non-voluntary influence on individuals’ life prospects. Additionally, applying this principle to individuals on every case would be too demanding.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony\">Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony</a><ul><li>This is more possible given that the focus of justice is regarding institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>Aristotle also relates justice to state institutions, specifically, the laws of a state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>Is justice impossible for institutions?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This would be the an example of an institutions that is in need of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice as fairness fosters self-respect since the public institutions affirms the sense that one’s rational, long-term plan in life is worth carrying out. Also, this leads us to respect others, which in turn means others respect us. This eventually leads to more societal harmony and efficient social cooperation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a part of Rawls’ argument for justice as fairness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions\">Justice as fairness does not apply to individual dealings with specific people, only to institutions</a><ul><li>This explains why we deal with institutions, and thus its institutions which support our plans.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Self-respect is the most important primary good\">Self-respect is the most important primary good</a><ul><li>This is why it matters that Justice as Fairness promotes self-respect.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice as fairness is specifically designed for a closed, well-ordered society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls' theory of justice is specifically designed without the consideration of private associations or international relations. Additionally, it assumes a well-ordered society, which he considers an idealization of actual societies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These are the assumptions that Rawls uses, and thus they can be challenged to challenge the entirety of his theory.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>He makes these assumptions in order so that once a theory based on the assumptions have been established, the theory can more easily be expanded outwards to cover more intellectual ground. He does not claim that this theory holds for all cases.</p><h4>References</h4>","Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls' view of justice, he claims, would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons from his conception of the original position.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds a contractarian element to Rawls' Justice as Fairness theory, which makes it vulnerable to critiques of those views.</p><h4>Explantion</h4><p>Rawls' view of the original position most prominently includes the veil of ignorance, which ensures fairness in the original position. The fact that this is fair results in the moral payoff of his system, which is that agents which are a part of the system mutually cooperate and engage with each other under the understanding that they were free and equal persons whose relations with respect to each other were fair.</p><ul><li>In this way, the society is almost voluntary, since it free and rational agents would indeed accept such a society based around justice as fairness.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns, such as coordination, efficiency, stability, etc.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the importance of the idea of justice in Rawls' philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Rawls</p><p>\"laws and institutions no matter how efficient and well-arranged must be reformed or abolished if they are unjust.\"</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","Justice in the political sphere corresponds to friendship in the household":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle compares political systems (particularly with respect to their treatment of justice) and analogizes them to situations within a household. In doing so, he finds similarities between the two.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for understanding how to approach friendships within a household.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><strong>Monarchy</strong>: <em>father-son</em>; the father rules over the son like a benevolent king, having absolute authority but exercising it for the benefit of the son.</li><li><strong>Aristocracy</strong>: <em>husband-wife</em>; the most natural relationship, where each person focuses on their strengths, where they rule the household based on their respective merit in different tasks.</li><li><strong>Timocracy</strong>: <em>brother-brother</em>; the most equal relationship, the closest to a friendship of virtue found between unrelated peers (since the brothers are roughly equal, have a shared history, and if one is good the other is also likely good since they were raised together).</li></ul><p>The first two are virtue friendships too, but of secondary quality, since they lack equality. Additionally, an implication is that since there are “corruptions” of these forms of government (tyranny, oligarchy, and democracy respectively), the relations in the family can likewise be corrupted. The worse case of such an corruption is tyranny in the household, which is also, in regards to political systems, the worst form of corruption.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one\">Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one</a><ul><li>Aristotle seems to hold this view, given that he analogizes different forms of the state based on their different ethical approaches to politics.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>How one engages with others is precisely what friendship is about, so the connection between friendship and politics is natural.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice is an individual’s moral right":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mill’s ultimate definition of justice is that it is someone’s moral right, and it represents the rules of conduct applicable to all and intended for the good of all. Justice is a perfect duty.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Mill’s definition of justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice is essentially derived from the natural desire for retaliation when some social harm has been done. Importantly, the harm must be social (involving the general interest), or else, the retaliation is no longer a form of justice. Injustice occurs when:</p><ol><li>Someone is deprived of their liberty, property, or any other such lawful thing.</li><li>Taking something away from someone where that thing is a moral right.</li><li>One does not obtain what they deserve.</li><li>One breaks faith (a promise).</li><li>One is partial when one ought not to be.</li></ol><p>When there are conflicting claims of justice, utilitarian calculus is used to resolve the tension. For instance, if we must steal in order to save a life, the utilitarian calculus would allow for the stealing in order to preserve someone’s life.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having\">A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having</a><ul><li>What a right is.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Law is not constitutive of justice\">Law is not constitutive of justice</a><ul><li>The disconnect between the law and justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice is considered by Aristotle to be the “sovereign virtue,” since it encompasses all of the virtues that he has described, and it regards the way that one interacts with others under the law.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s definition of universal/general justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice the process of aiming at the mean state, while injustice is aiming at the extremes. There is a difference between the concept of justice and “political virtue,” despite both terms regard the stance of the individual towards the regime. Full political justice, justice as the common advantage, is achieved only in a correct regime, and perfectly only in the best regime where civic virtue and complete human virtue coincide. But personal justice does not require a good regime, and the mere presence of civic virtue guarantees nothing, because it is exactly the kind of virtue a bad regime can put to work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Law is not constitutive of justice\">Law is not constitutive of justice</a><ul><li>A refutation of this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice, Equal Opportunity, and the Family":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Identifies Fishkin’s trilemma, a fundamental problem with liberal societies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This work was an integral part of the debate over meritocracy and the possibilities for the existence of a truly meritocratic society.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Three core tenants of liberal thought cannot be maintained together simultaneously:</p><ol><li><strong>The Principle of Merit (M):</strong> That positions in society should be allocated based on relevant qualifications, disregarding irrelevant characteristics.<ol><li><em>This draws on the concept of procedural justice, which is most well explicated as the concept of pure procedural justice by Rawls. Fishkin directly draws on [@barryPoliticalArgumentReissue1990, p. 97-98]</em></li></ol></li><li><strong>Equality of Life Chances (ELC):</strong> That one’s position in society is not determined by the circumstances of their birth.<ol><li>See <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy\">A Diachronic Rejection of Meritocracy › ^precise-ELC</a></li></ol></li><li><strong>Autonomy of the Family (FA):</strong> That parent’s are permitted to substantially influence the development of their children.<ol><li>So this basically just comes down to a matter of parenting style.</li></ol></li></ol><p>Now, any combination of two of these principles is crucial for a liberal society in any conventional sense of the word. Essentially, the idea that any realistic large-scale modern society will have substantial social and economic inequalities. Yet, when any two are indeed synthesizes, the result is that the third principle is excluded:</p><ul><li>M + ELC entails that children must develop under equal conditions, which requires overriding FA.<ul><li><code>any empirical studies about how/whether there could be the possibility of having some kind of a problem where the state ensures equality through developmental programs that are compatible with FA?</code></li></ul></li><li>M + FA entails that children from more well-off families will systematically develop superior qualifications, violating ELC.<ul><li>This is documented in [@markovitsMeritocracyTrapHow2019].</li></ul></li><li><em>ELC + FA is a unique case, because it is self-defeating without even needing to invoke M. ELC states that all aspects of children that are arbitrary ought to be equalized, yet that would mean equalizing the family situation which undermines FA.</em><ul><li><em>In other words, such a society simply cannot exist, let alone be liberal.</em></li><li>I have empirical evidence that differences in family circumstance leads to differences in outcome of the child.</li><li>Even if this is not the case, ELC + FA and assignment of employment according to some lottery would undermine liberty of employment.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism\">The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism</a><ul><li>Underscores FA.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s contribution to the idea of the importance of family, related to FA.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>A general coverage of the concept of meritocracy, related to M from this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Kennan’s containment policy sought to prevent the expansion of Soviet power in the Rimland":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>George F. Kennan’s “containment” policy which shaped American foreign policy during the Cold War was inspired by Spykman’s Rimland Thesis, in that Kennan wanted to prevent the Soviets from controlling as much of the Rimland as possible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This places the US policy of containment during the Cold War as it existed in the context of previous theories that would have justified a policy such as containment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian heartland dictates control of the world</a></li></ul>","LLM hallucinations are 'shameless guesses,' not 'hallucinations'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that LLM \"hallucinations\" are not actually hallucinations in the way that we think about a hallucination. Rather, it is just a guess the LLM made.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that we have to rethink how we conceptualize mistakes that AI make. This has implications for how we think about the nature of AI agents as being bullshit machines.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The common perception of “hallucinations” is that is a form of <em>sui generis</em> mistake that AIs make which is entirely foreign to humans.</li><li>Another way to view this, however, is to see hallucinations as probably guesses that the AI makes because its utility function does not take into account “shame” that someone might feel if they made a wild guess.</li><li>This lack of shame is due to the nature of the training process, where the AI is rewarded for correct guesses, but it is not punished for wrong ones.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","LLMs are not good at counting and manipulating individual characters":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the tokenization mechanism behind LLMs, they are not able to complexly manipulate individual characters in strings of text, nor are they good at counting.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why although LLMs are often able to answer high level problems, they often struggle with simple operations such as counting or spelling.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Due the nature of tokenization, where words/strings of characters are often represented as one token (so individual manipulation of characters is difficult for the LLM to understand), it is difficult for the model to be able to count or spell well.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"LLM hallucinations are &#x27;shameless guesses,&#x27; not &#x27;hallucinations&#x27;\">LLM hallucinations are 'shameless guesses,' not 'hallucinations'</a><ul><li>So the result of tasks involving those described in this note can be categorized as such.</li></ul></li></ul>","Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Labor productivity grows faster in manufacturing than service sectors, since technological innovations lead to larger productivity gains when the sector is more capital-heavy (i.e., manufacturing).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is cause of Baumol’s cost disease, since it pushes up wages in relatively less productive sectors, putting a drag on economic growth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a></li></ul>","Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the minimum efficient scale (MES) is relatively large relative to the overall demand, there is likely to be high concentration. On the other hand, when it is relatively small, there is unlikely to be high concentration.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>By looking at the MES, we are able to predict roughly how concentrated an industry is, which may be useful.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p><strong>Small MES</strong> If the overall demand for bread in a city is 200,000 loaves in a month, and a bakery is able to produce 200 loaves in a month at its MES, then, since 200 is a relatively small proportion of 200,000, there is likely perfect or near perfect competition in the industry.</p><p><strong>Large MES</strong> If the overall demand for diamonds is 200 per month in a city, and a single jewelry store is able to produce 150 diamonds per month at its MES, since 150 is a relatively large proportion of 200, the market is likely to be highly concentrated.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a></li></ul>","Law is not constitutive of justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The laws of some state does not constitute justice. In other words, it is not the case that whatever the laws say is exactly what justice is.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This fundamentally weakens the justificatory power of law and the nature of the legal system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is proven by the fact that there are and have been obviously unjust laws, and therefore, laws could not possibly be the only valid interpretation of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>It seems the Aristotle here is making a claim opposed to this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Leaders often use external conflict to overcome internal division":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A common tactic used by leaders to maintain control when there is internal division is to turn your attention outwards.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one possible motivation for leaders to seek external conflict, and may explain the underlying cause of some conflicts.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.bing.com/ck/a?!&amp;&amp;p=e9adc0cd945de2565d39f8b76f7f46bea866252ed164dfc404f27d1a9a20d612JmltdHM9MTc1NzYzNTIwMA&amp;ptn=3&amp;ver=2&amp;hsh=4&amp;fclid=02b3cf74-2a65-6907-1060-d9392b216882&amp;psq=charles+x+invasion+of+algeria&amp;u=a1aHR0cHM6Ly9lbi53aWtpcGVkaWEub3JnL3dpa2kvSW52YXNpb25fb2ZfQWxnaWVyc18oMTgzMCk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Invasion of Algiers (1830)</a>: Charles X invaded Algiers in order to distract from domestic problems and restore his prestige.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Falklands_War\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Falklands War (1982)</a>: Argentina’s government ordered an invasion of the Falklands in order to distract form their popularity and mismanagement of the nation.</li><li><a href=\"https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Russo-Japanese_War\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Russo-Japanese War (1904-1905)</a>: Russia attempted to attack Japan in order to distract from its internal problems. However, they were defeated.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Legal regulation is not context sensitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Any sort of regulation enforced by law is, by its nature, context-insensitive. For example, a law may prevent <em>x</em>, even though there might be (rare, but) generally acceptable conditions under which it would be moral permissible to <em>x</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the often unintended side effect of regulation, which can lead to a slew of unintended consequences, misallocated resources, and general harm.</p><h4>Aristotelian Political Theory</h4><p>Aristotle in fact brings up this point in his <em>Politics</em>, arguing that rule of law is context insensitive. In democracies, especially when the rule of law is weak, this can lead to the rise of demagogues who seek to bypass the rule of law and instead rule by decree.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society\">Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society</a><ul><li>A more specific claim regrading licensures and why they are generally detrimental.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>A specific manifestation of legal regulation, therefore, directly benefit incumbents in a market.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>If not legal regulation, then deregulation.</li></ul></li></ul>","Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Fukuyama, in <em>The End of History and the Last Man</em>, argued that with the end of the Cold War, we had reached the “end of history,” essentially that there would be nothing else that could challenge liberal democracy as the final evolution of ideology.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was a very influential idea which has been widely cited and critique in the coming decades.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>This is a very Hegelian idea (Hegelian Dialectic): thesis, antithesis, and synthesis.</li><li>Saw the end of the Cold War as evidence in support of modernization theory.</li><li>In this way, he was also opposed to the idea of political culture being the final form of political “war” to be fought.</li><li>He later argued that culture was still not a major force against the tide of global liberal democracy: - <strong>liberal democracy still had no serious ideological rival</strong>.<ul><li>Ex. Islam does not have a global appeal; it was only regionally powerful</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</a></li></ul>","Liberality (generosity) is the result of giving to others under the right conditions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Liberality, or generosity, mean giving the right amount, at the right time, at the right place, and most importantly, to the right person. This person also does not feel pain when they engage in the act of spending/giving money.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides a definition for a virtue that Aristotle talks about.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Liberality is the virtue of wealth management, not just wealth disposal. It means governing well both the inflow and the outflow of wealth. Like all other virtues, liberality depends of the disposition of the liberal person, not on the amount or quality of things given. Additionally, of the two vices that surround liberality, illiberality and prodigality, it is better to be prodigal than illiberal, though much more people are illiberal than prodigal.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Liberality is one of these virtues that must be repeatedly practiced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth\">Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth</a><ul><li>A variation on the virtue of liberality.</li></ul></li></ul>","Liberty can only be restricted in order to protect or strengthen the overall system of equal basic liberties":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Under Rawls’ framework, the only legitimate reason to restrict the rights of some is in order to protect the overall system of equal basic liberties or the strengthen it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important principles that Rawls establishes, since it ensures that liberty is the basic principle that no other principle may disturb.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are roughly two ways in order liberty may be diminished:</p><ol><li>Liberty can overall be less extensive, but still maintaining equal liberty. This requires that some specific liberty be narrowed for everyone equally, leading to a net gain in liberty for the representative individual.</li><li>Liberty can be made unequal. This is canonically with the case of children, since they loss of liberty enhances their freedom and allows them to develop properly.</li></ol><p>There are also two ways in which restrictions in liberty may arise.</p><ul><li>Natural limitations: there is something about the nature of the society/the place that the society inhabits that necessitates limitations on the liberty of some or the whole.</li><li>Existing injustice: this is the case whereby existing social arrangements already violate the principles of justice. This concerns the domain of non-ideal theory.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The intolerant must mostly be tolerated\">The intolerant must mostly be tolerated</a><ul><li>The intolerant could have their rights reduced if they go too far.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a><ul><li>This is the fundamental principle that this note is trying to establish.</li></ul></li></ul>","Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Licensure is, under the vast majority of circumstances, unnecessary.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Today, over 20% of jobs in the US require some form of licensure. This results in a situation where only the incumbents benefit, thus reducing economic competition, and increases prices for consumers, resulting in economic inefficiency.</p><h4>Argument</h4><ul><li>It is the case that many jobs that are dangerous, or would cause dangers to others should be licensed to ensure public safety. However, this does not justify an across the board licensure of many different fields.</li><li>Licensure in many harmless fields only result in the incumbents being kept in power, as they can control the amount of people who receive the license, thus holding an economic monopoly on the supply of some occupation. This results in economic inefficiency.<ul><li>In many cases, the board that determines whether some people would receive the license represents a clear conflict of interest, and by artificially restricting the amount of people that are entering a profession, it represents a sort of democratic backsliding, as the interests of the people are not being represented. </li></ul></li><li>A <a href=\"https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/sites/default/files/docs/licensing_report_final_nonembargo.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">2015 study by the White House</a> concluded that most licensing does not results in a higher quality of service provided.</li><li>Licensing should only be used when <strong>necessary, targeted (at specific professions), and competently administered (avoiding conflicts of interest).</strong></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>More licensure could be a part of the reason that this is happening?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>So this is similar to licensure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>A licensure system should be on where only the incumbents benefit, and thus would be a bad system.</li></ul></li></ul>","Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Licentiousness (the embodiment of vice) is acting wrongly, but fully conscious and choosing to act in this way. This is due to the licentious person’s understanding of universal morality being corrupted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This helps us understand why some people act wrongly and contrasts this moral state with other moral states.</p><h4>References</h4>","Life is too short for regrets":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Our lives are too short for us to spend a huge amount of time thinking about what could have been. Rather, we should focus on the future, and what can be.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This change of mindset can help one reorient and improve quality of life.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Remaining mired in the past only slows us down. It keeps us trapped and unhappy, since it requires that we constantly take responsibility for past actions. However, one must realize that past actions are gone, and there is nothing that we can do to change it, and thus taking responsibility in this way is futile. Rather, we must focus on the future, something that remains in our hands to shape.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, one has nothing to regret\">In life, one has nothing to regret</a><ul><li>Related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Literature can dramatize metacognition by depicting characters reasoning about their own reasoning":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A work can take thought itself as its subject by staging characters who examine the structure of their own inferences, which turns the audience’s attention from what is concluded to how it is concluded.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the point at which drama becomes philosophical rather than merely dealing with philosophical themes, and it is a technique with a datable origin in English literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The gravedigger in <em>Hamlet</em> reasons explicitly about the structure of an act: “if I drown myself wittingly, it argues an act, and an act hath three branches, it is to act, to do, to perform. Argal, she drowned herself wittingly” (5.1.10-13). The joke is a demonstration of formal reasoning, and the audience is watching someone reason about reasoning.</li><li>Shakespeare does this considerably more after <em>Hamlet</em>, which is part of why the later plays read as more philosophical.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work\">An extended metaphor can carry a single idea across an entire work</a><ul><li>Another structural device by which a work can argue rather than assert.</li></ul></li></ul>","Long term goodness is the means to a happy life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A good life, as Aristotle argues, is also a happy one. This is because a good life is a life whereby the individual achieve their duties as a human well, and since the final end of humans actions is happiness, then goodness must eventually arrive upon happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important argument Aristotle uses in order to arrive upon the idea of goodness, especially as a means of achieving happiness.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>So, goodness it the <em>mean</em> by which we can live a happy life, which is the <em>end</em>.</p><ol><li>Virtue (goodness) → right action.</li><li>Right action → expertly fulfills the function of a rational being.</li><li>Fulling the function well → produces happiness.</li></ol><p>Therefore, goodness produces happiness.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Aristotle clarifies that one must be good in a sustained manner, as being good “\\[demands] not only complete goodness \\[for a limited period of time], but a complete life \\[of goodness].”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li></ul>","Long-term bond yields are driven by expectations of inflation, not worries about government debt":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Long-term bond yields are driven primarily by expectations of long-term inflation, not worries about the government defaulting on its debt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues against the thesis that long-term bond yields are the result of people’s fears of government default.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Debt levels do matter somewhat for countries with credible central banks, though the main driver of long-term bond yields is still inflation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"TIPS yields are not a good proxy for real interest rates\">TIPS yields are not a good proxy for real interest rates</a><ul><li>Even if this note is true, it is not immediately applicable in the case of TIPS.</li></ul></li></ul>","Loser's consent is a crucial aspect of democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Loser’s consent is the idea that the losers in an election will not deny the results of that election. This is crucial for an democracy, as it ensures that the losers of any election will not simply deny the results and prevent a peaceful transfer of power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant aspects for a stable democratic system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are three key factors that underlie loser’s consent:</p><ol><li>Belief in a common nation: without this, there is no incentive for the defeated party to not attempt to hold on to power.</li><li>Belief in a fair electoral system: without this, there is no reason for the defeated party to accept the results of the election.</li><li>Belief in the existence of future elections: without this, defeated parties would remain defeated, which would incentive a coercive hold on power now.</li></ol><p>Many are arguing that today, American democracy is failing to meet the three standards set about here, and thus, that American democracy is backsliding.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American democracy is too populist\">American democracy is too populist</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a></li></ul>","Lying is a 'formal wrongdoing' and not a material wrong":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the classic case of the axe murder, where an axe murder comes to your door and asks for the location of your friend with the intention of murdering them, Kant says that you are obligated to tell the truth. By lying, however, the harm done is a formal one, not a material one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This clears up a common point of misunderstanding regarding Kant’s legal and ethical views.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A common interpretation of Kant’s condemnation of lying to the murderer is to interpret Kant’s desire not to lie to the murderer as one that is characterized by the murderer having a “right to truth.” Therefore, in lying, you are doing a “material wrong” to the murderer. However, this doesn’t actually seem to be the case. Rather, you are actually simply doing a “formal wrong” in the sense that you are wronging society as a whole by eroding, if only ever so slightly, the structures of trust that underlie a society. This erodes the argument that Kant’s original claim was “morally repugnant,” since it is no longer any potential harm to the murderer that we are referring to. Rather, it is a more general damage to society that is the wrong which the lying produces.</p><h4>References</h4>","Lying is the root cause of immorality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that lying, both to oneself and to others, is the source of immorality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that is present in various works of literature and philosophy.</p><h4><em>The Brothers Karamzov</em></h4><p>Elder Zosima says that lying is the root cause of evil since it means that the liar is no longer able to tell the truth everywhere, and thus loses respect for all others, including himself.</p><ul><li>Perhaps the claim about not being able to tell the truth lies in the fact that the liar has the prescience to the fact that everyone is (could be) lying, so he no longer trusts anyone, including those who actually speak the truth.<ul><li>Fyodor Pavlovich is the demonstration of this in practice. He has no respect for Elder Zossima.</li></ul></li></ul><h4><em>East of Eden</em></h4><ul><li>Lying is bad because it undermines the ability to see/believe in the truth.<ul><li>The lying to Aron about the nature of the mother, the lying to Adam and Charles by Cyrus, the lie of Cathy’s love to Adam (lying to himself).</li></ul></li><li>Ultimately, it leads to sin and bad outcomes.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is a &#x27;formal wrongdoing&#x27; and not a material wrong\">Lying is a 'formal wrongdoing' and not a material wrong</a><ul><li>Lying as a formal wrongdoing seems to be the way that lying harms the world in many of the examples describes in his note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Magnanimity is the 'crown' of the moral virtues":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To be magnanimous means to have every other virtue. Additionally, it means knowing that you are virtuous in every way, and acting in an apt manner. The magnanimous person cares (in the lightest sense of the word) only for honor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the most interesting of Aristotle’s virtues, since it is the encapsulation of all other virtues in one.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The deficient vice of magnanimity is being pusillanimous, and the excessive vice is being conceitful. The magnanimous man does not particularly care about honor, though he accepts honors from good people (since it is what he ought to receive), but shuns honors from lower people (since they are irrelevant to him). He views external events as beneath his dignity, and thus does not react irrationality when circumstances outside his control falter. Additionally, to achieve this virtue means having access to power, good birth, and wealth. However, power and wealth only matter to him because of the honor that are associated with having them.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth\">Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth</a><ul><li>Like magnificence, magnanimity is a virtue only achievable for some.</li></ul></li></ul>","Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is interesting because it is in contrast with our putative morality today, which makes morality egalitarian. On the other hand, Aristotle claims that this virtue is not achievable by all.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The excessive vice of magnificence is vulgarity, meaning spending too much in a tasteless fashion. The deficient vice of magnificence is pettiness, meaning spending too little, and when spending, spending in a tasteless fashion. Since it requires having great wealth to be magnificent, this is a virtue restricted to those who have the means to acquire great wealth.</p><h4>References</h4>","Mainstream economics’ need for assumptions in their models justifies government intervention into the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mainstream economics makes many claims about the assumptions which must be true in order for its economic models to be applied and thus the economy to be managed. Consequently, using the fact that their models typically fail to be satisfied, they justify state intervention into the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an implicit argument against mainstream economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The critique is essentially that mainstream economic is focusing too much on models and math, and failing to see that they are too focused on their models and not enough on the actual behavior of people and the applicability of their models. In fact, Vernon Smith demonstrated that many of the typically assumptions in mainstream economics are in fact unnecessary.</p><h4>References</h4>","Man is created in the image of God":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The theistic idea that humans were created in the image of God.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has many ethical and theological implications, including for morality.</p><h4>Bible</h4><ul><li>Adam goes about and names the animals, something that God ought to do. This symbolizes how humans have dominion over the Earth.</li><li>Humans have a special moral status, since they are in the image of God, and thus murder is prohibited.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ethics is grounded in religion\">Ethics is grounded in religion</a><ul><li>The current note would support this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Countries such as China, Japan, and India are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist in that they adopt capitalist methods of production, but many aspects of the economy are controlled by the state or are directly influenced by the state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds nuance to the usual characterization of East Asian countries as either capitalist or socialist, or some other vague term.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h5>Japan</h5><ul><li>Business cooperated deeply with the government:<ul><li>Many of the biggest international Japanese companies were subsidized by the government.<ul><li>Mainly automobile, technology/manufacturing, and steel.</li></ul></li><li>Many were government monopolies, though encouraged (through low tariffs) by the government to trade internationally.</li></ul></li><li>High savings rates in the country:<ul><li>40% of savings rate; promoted low interest rates and growth of infrastructure.</li><li>Foreign savings depreciated the yen as well.<ul><li>Promoted the success of Japanese businesses.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Deep investments in education (human capital):<ul><li>Made the country technologically sophisticated.</li></ul></li><li>Great political stability.<ul><li>Predictable future means that investment is encouraged (perceived lower risk).</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism\">Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized\">The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li></ul>","Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many are arguing that 2006 was the apex of the current wave of democratization, and that democratization has be in a downward decline following that point (around the same time as the Great Recession).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a backsliding of democracy again, as has happened in the previous waves, although this time, Democratic Backsliding has become more insidious, as authoritarians are allowing Competitive Authoritarianism and the slow corruption of democratic values, rather than more direct means, such as a violent coup.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>One way in which this manifests is in the rise of competitive authoritarianism, whereby authoritarian strong-men vie for political power within an ostensibly democratic system though when they gain power, they begin stripping away at the democratic substructure of the state. These figures, somewhat controversially, include:</p><ul><li>Vladimir Putin,</li><li>Victor Orban (now removed from power),</li><li>Nicholas Maduro.</li><li>Alexander Lushenko.</li><li>Donald Trump</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a><ul><li>People might be confusing the two terms here. It seems more accurate to say that constitutional liberalism is what is being threatened, not “democracy.”</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism undermines democracy\">Capitalism undermines democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini&#x27;s Rise\">The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals\">The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li></ul>","Many aspects of Buddhism derive from Hinduism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are many similarities between Buddhism and Hinduism, and that is largely because many elements of Buddhism are derived from Hinduism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains many of the origins of the practices from Buddhism that are reminiscent of Hinduism. It also explains why Buddhism made such a large impact when it was introduced in India.</p><h4>Buddhism &amp; Hinduism</h4><ul><li>Many aspects of Buddhism were derived from Hinduism, and Buddhism can be seen as a simplified and standardized version of Hinduism.</li><li>At the same time, Buddhism challenged the inequalities that were introduced by a Hindu caste system, arguing that there are no barriers to enlightenment.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there\">Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD\">The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD</a></li></ul>","Maoism advocates for a 'bottom-up' revolution, while Leninism argues for a 'top-down' revolution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since there was a distinct lack of industrial workers in China that concentrated in working areas, China needed the revolution to spring from the people in the countryside, thus creating a “bottom-up” revolution that is counter to Marx’s original intentions. Importantly, administration remains “top-down.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a key difference between the Maoists and Marxist-Leninists.</p><h4>References</h4>","Marginalism is declining in importance as a theoretical framework":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that marginalism is a method of economic that is declining.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has profound for the future direction of economics, and identifies a trendline, a direction of movement for the field of economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Tyler Cowen identifies five reasons that marginalism is declining:</p><ol><li>Empirical science is now displacing theoretical science, meaning that the more theoretical nature of marginalist thinking has been replaced by the data-driven approach of more empirical methods.</li><li>Price theory is declining in importance.</li><li>Finance, which was seen as the ultimate applicant of marginalist thinking, has switched to more empirical science.</li><li>Machine learning is making marginalism obsolete, since it can directly find the structure in data, rather than needing to have a framework like marginalism guide the process of finding the structure in the data.</li><li>Economics is increasingly turning to the left, and people on the left are generally more uncomfortable with the conclusions made by marginalism.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The consumer chooses to consume the amount that makes the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods equal\">The consumer chooses to consume the amount that makes the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods equal</a><ul><li>This is an application of marginal thinking.</li></ul></li></ul>","Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Markets have three crucial functions: they help allocate resources where resources are needed, put savings to use, and help fund new innovations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the core purpose of markets, and the importance they place in ensuring that capital is utilized effectively to produce the best outcome.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a></li></ul>","Marx is more idealistic than Hegel":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While the conception of the two philosophers is that Hegel is the idealistic one that created the framework which Marx then put into practice, a better way of looking at things is that Marx is actually the idealist, and Hegel, the realist.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the ultimate hypocrisy that is in Marx’s philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In Hegel’s analysis, the existing religious and political history is already reasonable, and the authorities that already exist are already what is rational. However, Marx looks at the existing history and argues that it is against reason. He argues for the complete transformation of the world according to his conception of reason.</p><h4>References</h4>","Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While Marx claims to lead a new, secular revolution, the underlying structure of his claims and the meaning of his action is able to be precisely modeled upon a Christian perspective.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Lowith’s analysis of Marx’s historical viewpoints.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are many similarities between Marx’s view of history and the Christian eschatological view of history.</p><ul><li>Marx sees the proletariat as the world-historical instrument that exists outside existing society and serves as the driving force that actualizes the world’s potential for a classless, absolute society.<ul><li>It is only with a eschatological view of history that Marx is able to state that the proletariat is the “heart” of history, while his ideas are the “brains” of history.</li></ul></li><li>Marx’s view of exploitation is the supreme moral fact that the world revolution is justified upon.<ul><li>It is merely an ethical judgement, and is not empirical, as Marx claims that his theory overall is. If the very fundamental nature of a theory is merely ethical, then it hardly seems plausible to call the overall theory “empirical.”</li><li>“Exploitation” can be equated to the “original sin” in Christian theology.</li></ul></li><li>The classless society prophesized by the <em>Communist Manifesto</em> is the Kingdom of God on Earth.</li><li>Marx generalizes his critique to being one that is against all “idols,” not just Christianity specifically, but also capitalism. A fight against earthly idols is not what scientists do; it’s what prophets do.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Chapter 2, <em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 42</p><p>[A] Kingdom of God, without God and on earth, […] is the ultimate goal and ideal of Marx's historical messianism. […] <strong>Though perverted into secular prognostication, the Communist Manifesto still retains the basic features of a messianic faith: the assurance of things to be hoped for.</strong> […] It would have been quite impossible to elaborate the vision of the proletariat's messianic vocation on a purely scientific basis and to inspire millions of followers by a bare statement of facts.</p></blockquote><p>Marx critiques religion by simply dismissing it, thinking that the work has already been done by other scholars. He fails, however, to analyze the reason for the existence of religion in the first place. And by destroying religion, Marx is essentially supplying a substitute in the form of Marxism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos\">People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos</a><ul><li>This is what is said in the quote above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marx is more idealistic than Hegel\">Marx is more idealistic than Hegel</a><ul><li>This can be seen through Marx’s critique of religion, which says essentially that humanity needs to entirely override our existing conceptions of religion and… replace it with a whole other “correct” religion.</li></ul></li></ul>","Matching people to roles by merit may be less efficient than letting them choose":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Allocating positions by measured merit assumes the allocator knows where each person is most productive, whereas allowing people to select their own occupations uses information about preference and local circumstance that no central assessment can capture.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This turns the standard efficiency argument for meritocracy against it, and it does so on the meritocrat’s own consequentialist ground.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hayek makes the argument in this form, and it is the same objection he raises to central planning generally, since in both cases the planner must aggregate knowledge that exists only in dispersed and tacit form.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>The general form of the objection.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>The same argument in its original economic setting.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>Why this objection is fatal only to the consequentialist defence.</li></ul></li></ul>","Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Material incentives are only part of the reason that people may produce new ideas. Other large justifications for the production of new ideas include scientific curiosity, as well as a general desire to benefit humanity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common argument when attempting to point out flaws in the patent system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>I think that this is true to a certain degree. However, I think that this is more so true in the past than today. In the past, often those who were able to conduct high-level/cutting edge scientific research were singular, wealthy people who had time on their hands. Today, not only do new scientific breakthrough take more equipment, they also often require large teams of people working in tandem. The high cost of equipment and the difficulty of uniting the motivations of many people (who are working for a wage, not for leisure) means that financial incentives would far outweigh other incentives in the academic environment today.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one\">The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one</a></li></ul>","Meaning in History":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Argues that modern Western philosophies of history—including the ideas of progress developed by thinkers such as Hegel and Marx—are secularized versions of the Judeo-Christian belief in salvation and the end of time. Although modern thinkers reject religious explanations, they preserve the same basic structure: history is linear, the past prepares the way for the future, and humanity is moving toward a final fulfillment. Löwith contrasts this view with the cyclical conception of history found in ancient Greek thought and argues that secular progress cannot provide a genuine meaning for history because it transfers religious hopes into the worldly realm without retaining their transcendent foundation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This work is influence in the philosophy of history and seems to relate to many modern discussions regarding the influence of Christianity on the contemporary cultural zeitgeist.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"That history entails progress is a modern conception\">That history entails progress is a modern conception</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world\">Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a></li></ul><h5>Burckhardt</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Spiritual continuity is the primary concern of history\">Spiritual continuity is the primary concern of history</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion\">Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion</a></li></ul><h5>Marx</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marx is more idealistic than Hegel\">Marx is more idealistic than Hegel</a></li></ul><h5>Hegel</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will\">Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The philosophy of history is essentially an elaborate theodicy for all of human existence\">The philosophy of history is essentially an elaborate theodicy for all of human existence</a></li></ul><h5>Proudhon</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Divine providence is nothing but the collective instinct of mankind\">Divine providence is nothing but the collective instinct of mankind</a></li></ul><h5>Comte</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action\">The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Secular philosophies of history can only address humans as collectives, and not individuals\">Secular philosophies of history can only address humans as collectives, and not individuals</a></li></ul><h5>Condorcet &amp; Turgot</h5><ul><li>Condorcet’s philosophy of history was one that was supremely optimistic, and portrayed the philosophy as one whereby humans would inevitably reach ultimate happiness.</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The process of history follows naturalistic laws that can be determined by humans\">The process of history follows naturalistic laws that can be determined by humans</a></li></ul><h5>Voltaire</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The universality of history is a product of Christian history\">The universality of history is a product of Christian history</a></li></ul><h5>Vico</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Since humans constitute history, the principles that undergird history must then be accessible to humans\">Since humans constitute history, the principles that undergird history must then be accessible to humans</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"God saved humans, while not directly changing their psychology, from sin\">God saved humans, while not directly changing their psychology, from sin</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"That history entails progress is a modern conception\">That history entails progress is a modern conception</a></li></ul><h5>Bossuet</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world\">Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world</a></li></ul><h5>Joachim</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fulfillment can be found within history\">Fulfillment can be found within history</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition\">Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition</a></li></ul><h5>Augustine</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"That history entails progress is a modern conception\">That history entails progress is a modern conception</a></li></ul><h5>Orosius</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Historical judgment is relative\">Historical judgment is relative</a></li></ul><h5>The <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Bible\">Bible</a></h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History is inherently theological\">History is inherently theological</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moby-Dick (book)\">Moby-Dick (book)</a><ul><li>An example of the death of Christianity eluded to in this book.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics (book)\">Politics (book)</a><ul><li>Representative of the circular nature of ancient views of history.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)\">The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Merchants were viewed as the lowest social class in Imperial China since they were viewed as being merely people who exchanged items and did not create anything productive. They did not work as the peasants did.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Perhaps this derogatory view of merchants was what ultimately halted the development of a free market in China compared to Europe.</p><h4>References</h4>","Merit is contextual and internal, so it cannot be read off outcomes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whether someone is meritorious depends on the context that defines the relevant merit, and merit is a property of the person rather than of what happens to them, so outcomes are at best defeasible evidence of it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the concession that makes meritocracy defensible in principle and unworkable in practice, since the only measure available to an institution is the outcome.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly\">Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly</a><ul><li>What happens when outcomes are treated as direct evidence of merit anyway.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure\">When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure</a><ul><li>The mechanism by which a proxy for merit degrades once it is used to allocate.</li></ul></li></ul>","Meritocracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An essay that describes in broad strokes the arguments and positions relating to the concept of meritocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding the broad topics and arguments surrounding the idea of meritocracy.</p><h4>Historical Survey</h4><p>Meritocracy has a long tradition in East Asia, mainly due to the perceived consequentialist benefits.</p><ul><li>“People will be more virtuous, and better cared-for, if they are ruled by the meritorious.” (Mulligan, 2023)Other thinkers, such as Plato and Aristotle have also participated in the tradition of favoring meritocracy.However, while Plato and Eastern thinkers mainly concerned themselves with meritocracy when it comes to governance, A. deals generally with the distribution of social goods.</li><li>For A. specifically, he believes in proportional equality, meaning that the distribution of social goods is proportional to the merit of the persons.</li><li>He defines merit roughly as how well something to achieves their intended goal.This idea is also related to the idea of the American Dream.</li></ul><h4>Merit</h4><p>Merit is contextual, i.e., whether someone is meritorious depends on the context which defines their merit. Merit is internal, and is distinct from the outcomes which result from the merit.</p><ul><li>We can’t judge someone’s merit merely based on outcomes.We must choose between two definitions of merit:</li><li><strong>Consequentialist</strong>: one chooses meritocracy because of the results it produces; it is only contingently valuable.<ul><li>But the issue is that there might be things that are not related to merit that might produce a better outcome.</li></ul></li><li>\\<em>\\*Categorical: \\</em>\\*one chooses meritocracy because running a system based on merit is inherently good.<ul><li>But the issue then is that you are not always able to produce the best results.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Merit and Desert</h4><p>There is no generally agreed upon definition for the two terms. Desert generally is pre-institutional; it is what people deserve that decides institutions/the rules that govern society. It is a concept that can be used to judge institutions to see of they are fair/good or not.</p><ul><li>A libertarian view would argue that this does not matter, and as long as individuals chose to participate in the institutions, they must abide by the institutions and external considerations of desert are ignored.Desert is also often thought of as having two properties:</li><li>Aboutness: the basis for desert must be “about” the subject of a desert-claim. This is often construed as responsibility.</li><li>Fitness: there is a natural connection between the basis for desert and the object being awarded.</li></ul><h4>Equal Opportunity</h4><p>There is two conceptions of equal opportunity:</p><ul><li>Fair/substantive: people start with the same amount of resources (in the broadest sense of the term) in the initial position.</li><li>Formal: the institutions in society apply equally and fairly to all.A meritocracy is merely a society where there is equality of opportunity in both senses.There are two salient debates when it comes to equal opportunity that is relevant here:</li><li>Whether there is a moral difference between social advantages (being born into a rich family) and natural advantages (being born intelligence).</li><li>The extent to which the state can interfere with family affairs to ensure equal opportunity.“The philosophical issues here are to explain what is problematic and what is not, and to draw the line between the government’s interest in establishing equal opportunity and the liberty of parents to raise their children as they see fit.” (Mulligan, 2023)</li></ul><h4>Governance</h4><p>There are three questions principally related to the idea of governing with a meritocracy:</p><ol><li>How do you define merit?</li><li>How should the meritocrats rule?</li><li>Is meritocracy justifiable?</li></ol><h5>New Confucian Meritocracy</h5><p>These theories are based on a mixture of Western liberal political theory and traditional Chinese Confucian political theory.</p><h5>Restricted Suffrage</h5><p>Another set of theories that are broadly related to the concept of restricting the right to make political decisions to those who are sufficiently competent.</p><h5>Plural Voting</h5><p>A set of theories which argues that people should be given differing amounts of votes based on their competence. This has consequences, such as the fact that:</p><ol><li>Some people with lower allocations of votes would de facto be restricted from suffrage.</li><li>There is no unique assignment of votes (all votes could be multiplied by 2, and the result would be the same).</li></ol><h4>Justice</h4><p>There is obviously an instrumental connection between meritocracy and justice, since many systems of thought see most of the time that putting the most competent in positions of power is the best means of achieving some end. However, the most vocal proponents of meritocracy claim that meritocracy works as a deontological principle as well. One response is to say that since in a meritocracy, people get what they deserve, and getting what one deserves is just, that meritocracy is just. Additionally, meritocracy is intuitively appealing for people, and it is grounded in autonomy, meaning that we consider only the relevant facts (whether someone is able to perform some tasks well) and thus we treat them as agents whose purposeful acts can make a difference in the world (we respect them). Another idea is the the very nature of social goods points to merit based distribution.</p><ul><li>When the goal is to produce bagels, it is inherently (intuitively) true that the person who should get this job is the person who is best at making bagels. The fact that the person who is best at making bagels gets the job is “primitive” and arises as aesthetic experience does.</li></ul><h5>Distribution of Jobs</h5><p>When it comes to a just distribution of jobs, the meritocrat would argue that only merit be considered, and all non-merit factors ought not to be considered. However, it is difficult to ascertain what exactly constitutes a merit factor.</p><ul><li>For instance, does beauty count as a merit factor? If a customer is more likely to buy from a sales agent who is beautiful, should that be a factor of consideration in the hiring process?Affirmative action is a policy that is disliked by meritocrats because it violates the principle of equal opportunity, and is discriminatory.</li></ul><h5>Distribution of Income</h5><p>There are a few arguments made in favor of a meritocratic distribution of income:</p><ul><li>Desert; people who work harder deserve more income.There are multiple ways of measuring economic contribution: WTP, consumer surplus, marginal product.A complication is how to disentangle the contribution of labor vs. capital in the production of something. And it seems that how the capital was acquired is of moral consideration as well.Economic rents is also a problem, since rents are incomes not associated with any sort of economic contribution. For instance:</li><li>Executive self-dealing</li><li>Luck-based income</li><li>Rent-seeking behavior (capturing value without creating it)</li><li>The beauty premium</li></ul><h4>Objections</h4><h5>Rawls</h5><p>Rawls claims that meritocracy is only contingently and instrumentally useful in the case that it furthers the Difference Principle. More specifically, he has four critiques of meritocracy:</p><ol><li>Targeting desert-based theories broadly, he claims that no one “deserves” anything, since all differences between people are either due to social circumstances or natural differences in ability, neither of which is morally relevant.</li><li>He views it as being impossible to accurately track people’s merit and thus distribute things on a basis of merit.</li><li>He thinks that it is possible that meritocracy is the result of comprehensive doctrines, and not the result of bargaining between people. Therefore, it would lack universal justification to serve as a shared political base.’</li><li>It is insufficiently democratic.</li></ol><h5>Relational Egalitarianism</h5><p>Believes that the fundamental principle of society is equality, and since meritocracy violates that principle, it is morally repugnant.</p><h5>Democratic Theory</h5><p>The first group in this camp that rejects meritocracy are the <em>epistemic democrats</em>, who argue that democracy produces good decisions. For instance, they use Condorcet’s Jury Theorem to argue the point of the \"wisdom of the crowd.\" The meritocrat can respond in the following three manners:</p><ul><li>Deny the relevance of the model, since its assumptions are violated in the real world (no independence condition met, or reject that assumption that the average  $p &gt; 0.5$  ).</li><li>Concede that the theorem is true, but that meritocracy does better, since it gains all the benefits of the theory (from aggregation), but suffers none of the downsides.</li><li>Argue that this supports meritocracy, since if one where to give the more informed more votes, the limit of the aggregate probability of the right decisions increases as the amount of voters approaches infinity is faster than in the democratic case.</li></ul><p>$$ \\prod_{n=0}^{\\infty} p_{n} \\ \\text{where} \\ p \\in [0,1] \\text{ and }p_{n} \\text{ is more informed than} \\ p_{n+1} $$</p><pre><code>The above approaches 1 faster than merely:</code></pre><p>$$ \\prod_{n=0}^{\\infty} p_{n} \\ \\text{where} \\ p \\in [0,1] $$</p><pre><code>Assuming that $\\frac{\\sum p_{n}}{n} &gt; 0.5$ in both cases.</code></pre><p>For <em>procedural democrats</em> all that matters is that the principles associated with democracy are carried out, and whether the end effect is better or worse does not matter. <em>Epistemic proceduralism</em> attempts to marry the two schools of thought into one.</p><h5>Libertarianism</h5><p>The meritocratic distributive rule of hiring on the basis of merit violates the libertarian right to contract. Additionally, Hayek gives the critique that a meritocratic economy may not be the most efficient, since letting people choose what they want to do maximizes surplus, while choosing based on merit might not.</p><h4>References</h4>","Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One may favour merit-based allocation because it produces good results, in which case its value is contingent, or because allocating by merit is intrinsically right, in which case one must accept it even when the results are worse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two justifications are usually run together, and separating them shows that most defenders of meritocracy cannot have both.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The consequentialist is vulnerable to the case where some non-merit factor produces the better outcome, and must then abandon merit. The categorical defender is vulnerable to the case where merit-based allocation predictably underperforms, and must accept the loss.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Merit is contextual and internal, so it cannot be read off outcomes\">Merit is contextual and internal, so it cannot be read off outcomes</a><ul><li>Sharpens the problem for the consequentialist, who needs merit to be measurable.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Matching people to roles by merit may be less efficient than letting them choose\">Matching people to roles by merit may be less efficient than letting them choose</a><ul><li>The efficiency case that the consequentialist justification has to survive.</li></ul></li></ul>","Meritocracy is grounded in autonomy, since it treats persons as agents whose acts make a difference":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Allocating by merit considers only what a person has done and can do, which is to treat them as an agent whose purposeful action changes the world, and this respect for agency is a deontological rather than an instrumental ground for meritocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the best version of the categorical defence, since it does not depend on merit-based allocation producing good outcomes.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>The distinction this claim sits on the categorical side of.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our identity is defined by our nature as agents\">Our identity is defined by our nature as agents</a><ul><li>The general account of agency that this defence draws on.</li></ul></li></ul>","Mexican immigration to the US is unique compared to immigration to the US from other countries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Mexican immigration to the US is different from immigration from other parts of the globe to the US for a few reasons:</p><ul><li>There only a small geographic distance between the two countries, making it easy for immigration to (continue to) occur, legally and illegally.</li><li>Mexican immigrants show a particular aversion to assimilation to local culture.</li><li>Because many part of the US used to be Mexican, and thus have strong cultural roots to Mexico, Mexican immigration to the US could potentially arouse revanchist sentiments.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a few of the complications regarding Mexican immigration into the US.</p><h4>References</h4>","Minimum wage drives capital substitution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A minimum wage drives the substitution of labor for capital, since it increases the relative marginal product of capital increases with higher marginal product of labor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a downside (for the worker) of minimum wage.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>A 10% increase in the minimum wage is equivalent to an 8% increase in robot adoption.</li><li>Large manufacturing firms are the drivers of the substitution.<ul><li>This is because manufacturing is relatively easier to automate.</li><li>And this is because large firms have more capital to invest in automation.</li></ul></li><li>This is a trend seen in the US, China, Turkey, and Germany; it is likely true everywhere.</li><li>A conclusion that can be drawn from this is that labor policy shapes technological diffusion.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minimum wage can reduce unemployment\">The minimum wage can reduce unemployment</a><ul><li>This is not true in this case.</li></ul></li></ul>","Misaligned systems persist due to competition with misaligned incentives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the major causes of bad systems which, although they may operate very efficiently, maximize the wrong output, is that the competition inherent in the system is maximizing something that is not generally human-value aligned.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major cause of some bad systems extant in the world.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Within a system, there may be multiple equilibria such that when competition induces people to make processes more efficient, it merely incentives them to move to the most accessible equilibrium, which may not, in absolute terms, be the optimal outcome. The classic example of this is the prisoners dilemma and races to the bottom.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>I think that this claim may be too broad to be helpful, since some things in the world clearly are correctly aligned. What’s more, it’s not clear that human values are, in general, internally consistent. People may support higher wages for workers, for instance, and demand that the prices of basic necessities be held constant. Yet these two inclinations are not always coherent. Advocating for higher wages for minimum wage workers could make the prices of basic necessities increase, and so, we are never able to fully satisfy human values.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A viable system must be internally consistent\">A viable system must be internally consistent</a><ul><li>The issue is that, in many cases, the axioms that underlie a system are not internally consistent.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a><ul><li>This could be an example of a misaligned incentive.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moby-Dick (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Ishmael, a young sailor who joins the whaling ship <em>Pequod</em> under the command of Captain Ahab, an obsessive and vengeful man determined to hunt the great white whale Moby Dick, which previously destroyed his ship and severed his leg. Ahab’s pursuit ultimately brings about disaster: Moby Dick destroys the <em>Pequod</em>, kills Ahab and nearly the entire crew, while Ishmael alone survives to tell the story.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the greatest works of American (world) literature, exploring themes of friendship, fate, nature, obsession, and humanity’s desire to understand the unknown.</p><h4>Raw Notes</h4><ul><li>This book is anti-Enlightenment; reality itself does not process clear lines. Human nature and reality cannot fully be rationalized.</li><li>“I promise nothing complete; because any human thing supposed to be complete, must for that very reason infallibly be faulty.”</li><li>Onto-theology: the idea that the universe can be neatly tied together where there is one correct interpretation of the world and one correct categorization.<ul><li>Everything has a reason, place, and a moral purpose.</li></ul></li><li>The danger is when we mistake our maps for reality. This is the fundamental idea of the book.</li><li><strong>Whiteness</strong>: there are two conceptions<ol><li>The sterility of the universe/the underlying logical structure of the universe that is devoid of human meaning. It’s the dead void of science.</li><li>“Blankness that is full of meaning.” By attempting to capture all the meaning there is in the world, we simply create “blankness/whiteness,” which is the most ambiguous of all. This mirrors the very physical nature of light itself.<ul><li>Humans should be able to deal with the radical variety that exists in the world.</li><li>We inherently want to pursue a grand theory of everything.</li><li>The weaver god’s loom is all of the interconnection of human existence and culture. However, if one wants to synthesize all of this, its just chaos (whiteness).</li></ul></li></ol></li><li>The solution is learning to live with the multifaceted world. It means to have distance with the weaver god’s loom. It means to be able to see that the whiteness of the world is, in fact, the combination of all the colors of the rainbow.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Meaning of the Work?</p><p>It seems that this work is an argument against the established onto-theological view of the world and the rationalism of the Enlightenment. It’s saying that such a view of the world is fundamentally both unable to fully account for the complexities of the world, and also leads us down unproductive and ultimately destructive roads. Melville argues that we should find meaning in the world through concrete, local traditions rather than seeking a single, unified answer in the world at large.</p><blockquote><p>There is, of course, some inherent complexity and impossibility of picking out a single idea from this work, since the most comprehensive one is also the one that undermines the centrality of itself.</p></blockquote></blockquote><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li>Ahab is the <em>Peqoud</em>; the activities and states of the <em>Pequod</em> mirrors that of Ahab.</li><li>Ahab is a slave to his passions; every is a slave to fate/the natural world in some sense</li><li>Ahab is like ambergris?</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality\">Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry\">Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Ishmael</strong></p><ul><li>Impelled by a sense of disillusionment with the world, and thus sought adventure and some meaning in life through the treacherous lifestyle of a whaleman.</li><li>It seems like his character is almost used as a negative foil against which the meaninglessness of the cosmos is portrayed. There are many chapters where Ishmael tries in vain to categorize and rationalize the world, or attach some grand universal meaning to specific aspects of the whale or the business of whaling (e.g., the cetology chapters, the chapter on the glory of the profession of whaling, etc.). However, this is ultimately just used as a device to show that such attempts are in vain.</li><li>I feel like there is an inherent tension in the narrative, however. <em>The very fact that Ishmael survives the journey, and then pens a book about it</em> seems to me, in some sense at least, an knowledge of the existence of a force that arranges the world in a way that Ishmael is trying to deny.<ul><li>It seems that this is either: one, intentional, or two, <strong>a fundamental flaw with any intellectual work that seeks to prove non-existence of some overarching meaning/ordering principle in the world. They are, in a way, self-undermining.</strong></li><li>Ishmael seems to survive because of his refusal to search for a single meaning in the world.</li><li>At the same time:</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ishmael?useskin=vector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ishmael (biblical figure)</a></p><p>It is a [theophoric](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theophoric \"Theophoric\") name translated literally as \"God (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/El_\\(god\\\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">El</a> \"El (god)\")) has hearkened\", suggesting that \"<strong>a child so named was regarded as the fulfillment of a divine promis</strong>e\".</p><blockquote><p>This is perhaps a simpler explanation for the fact that he survives?</p></blockquote></blockquote><p><strong>Ahab</strong></p><ul><li>He is driven mad by the loss of a leg from Moby Dick.</li><li>It seems that he is unable to understand how meaningless the universe truly is, since he seems incapable of understanding that his loss of a leg is something that is just fundamentally without meaning and a cruel twist of “fate.”</li><li>It is precisely this lack of ability to square the seemingly meaningless nature of the world and the immense suffering of the individual that turns Ahab into the wolfish, heartless creature that he becomes.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Moby-Dick</em>, ch. 36</p><p><em>That inscrutable thing is chiefly what I hate</em>; and be the white whale agent, or be the white whale principal, I will wreak that hate upon him.</p></blockquote><ul><li>Ahab is used in the text as the prototypical figure that holds on to onto-theological ideas. He sees the world as being imbued with meaning, and thus, he is unable to come to terms with the fact that such is actually not the case.<ul><li>Again, an esoteric reading of this could be the following: Ahab does not invent a “theodicy” for the loss of his leg, as is the standard Christian practice. Rather, he often times invokes curses against heaven and engages in other paganistic and blasphemous activities. Melville’s critique then makes the greatest sense as claiming that Christianity places humans in a onto-theological mindset, which can be corrupted and both turned against its creator and also drives ourselves insane (?).</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Moby-Dick</em>, p. 532</p><p>“Ego non baptizo te in nomine patris, sed in nomine diaboli! \\[<strong>I do not baptize you in the name of the Father, but in the name of the devil!</strong>]”</p></blockquote><ul><li>Ahab can be contrasted against the captain of the <em>Samuel Enderby</em>, who suffers the same fate but does not become an Ahab-like figure.</li><li>The biblical Ahab was a devil worshipper.<ul><li>Or perhaps <em>Moby-Dick</em> is a tale akin to an anti-Book of Job, where Ahab is the anti-Job.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Question regarding Ahab</p><p><strong>What to make of the fact that Ahab is seemingly aware of his own insanity and irrationality?</strong> As he says himself on page 530: “In no Paradise myself, I am impatient of all misery in others that is not mad.”</p></blockquote><ul><li>Ahab’s death is a phenomenon of <em>higher-order indifference (whiteness)</em>. <ul><li>Indifference merely means the lack of meaning in the world, but a higher-order indifference is manifest through the existence of various meanings in the world (the condition for the possibility of many different meanings in the world all to be true), but the failure for all these meanings to be able to be synthesized into one.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Pip</strong></p><ul><li>Driven made by his seeing of the fundamental fabric of the world after he is thrown out of the whaling boat.<ul><li>This is the reason that Ahab was afraid of Pip “curing him.”</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Moby-Dick</em>, ch. 129</p><p>There is that in thee, poor lad, which I feel too curing to my malady. Like cures like; <strong>and for this hunt, my malady becomes my most desired health.</strong></p></blockquote><p><strong>Queequeg</strong></p><ul><li>Melville’s example for how to live a fulfilling life.</li><li>He lives without the onto-theology that is associated with Christianity, though he is “poisoned” by Christianity in the book, and thus goes down with the ship at the end of the book.<ul><li>What is telling, however, is the fact that his coffin, upon which he carved all of his esoteric symbols, does not go down with the ship. It is the only part of the ship which survives, and which saves Ishmael.</li><li>The sinking of the <em>Pequod</em> is representative of the death of onto-theology.</li></ul></li><li>Queequeg physically embodies his tradition. It is something that he physically lives by. He has a physical idol as a manifestation of his faith.<ul><li>This concreteness of his religion is what allows for him to be so content. He does not need to pursue philosophy or a meaning for the world, because all of the meaning is already physically present with him.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Moby Dick</strong></p><ul><li>The three days of the final chase is representative of the three different, equally valid interpretations of the nature of the whale.<ol><li>The malice of the whale: the whale acts with cunning in order to wreck Ahab’s boat.</li><li>Awkwardness of the whale/randomness: the whale thrashes and wrecks Ahab’s boat.</li><li>Indifference of the whale: Ahab’s harpoon catches around his neck and kills him.</li></ol></li></ul><h4>Note</h4><ul><li>There are many analogies made in the book to the Bible.</li><li>It seems that if the central meaning of the book is to find meaning through local, concrete traditions, then this book itself must be an exception to the rule? <ul><li>Perhaps this point and the previous point is connected?</li></ul></li><li><strong>The Sacred (Ordinary and Extraordinary):</strong> When the singular “mono-god” collapses, it leaves behind two surviving forms of the sacred that can heal human lives:<ol><li><em>The Extraordinary Sacred:</em> Local, fragile, and temporary religious rituals (like Queequeg's worship of his idol, Yojo).</li><li><em>The Ordinary / Everyday Sacred:</em> The deeply fulfilling everyday practices highlighted in the “Squeeze of the Hand” chapter—such as friendship, a good meal, a bed, a wife, and a fireside.</li></ol></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meaning in History\">Meaning in History</a><ul><li>The point that Melville makes about faith in Christianity is covered in a more scientific way by Lowith. In fact, <em>Moby-Dick</em> is a good case study for the degradation of the idea of faith in Christianity. The characters in the book try to concretize the religion (which Melville views as something that is natural to human nature/a fundamental flaw with Christianity), which is ultimately met by failure.</li><li>In this way, <em>Moby-Dick</em> and <em>Meaning in History</em> have, in a very broad sense, the same thesis: that Christianity cannot survive without its spiritual element, and once earthly elements are introduced (the philosophy of history of Christianity is secularized, while preserving the same Christian framework for understanding history), the entire system implodes.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Bible\">Bible</a><ul><li>Many references are found in this work.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>There are many similarities between these two works in the way that they both approach the topic of man vs. The divine.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All the King&#x27;s Men\">All the King's Men</a><ul><li>There are some similarities in the discussion regarding the fate of humans.</li></ul></li></ul>","Modesty is not a virtue; rather, it is a feeling":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle says that modesty is not a virtue. Rather, it is a feeling, something that is readily mutable, rather than a disposition or a state. He defines it as the “fear of disrepute.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Normally, modesty is defined as a virtue. Aristotle, however, defines it a feeling. He also has some very specific thoughts regarding the nature of modesty, viz. who it is good for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that modesty is good for young people, but bad for virtuous adults. Regarding virtuous adults, he makes the argument that:</p><ol><li>Virtuous people does not do shameful things.</li><li>You only feel modesty (shame, as defined by Aristotle) when you are shameful.</li><li>$\\therefore$ Virtuous people do not feel modesty.</li></ol><p>Virtuous people only “feel” modesty “in hypothesis,” in other words, they can imagine themselves feeling shame if they were to do something not virtuous. In young people, however, since they live by “passion” rather than “reason,” they are prone to making mistakes due to their inexperience. Then, having the modesty to admit their mistakes and change themselves becomes a boon to their development. Modesty is better than shamelessness, but still not as good as not being modest (for the adult).</p><h4>References</h4>","Mohists were mainly derived from the lower classes of society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The core precepts of Mohism, that of universal love, was something that was greatly appeal to the lower classes, who had something to gain from a moral levelling out with the aristocracy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the major support group and believers of Mohism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are a few main critiques of Confucianism leveled by Mohism, and these critiques are motivated by the social position of the Mohsists:</p><ol><li>Confucianism does not believe in the existence of the gods or spirits.</li><li>Confucianism is too ritualistic (despite the fact that they are not believers in the gods or spirits).</li><li>Confucianism advocated for predestination (命), which caused people to be lazy and resign themselves to their fate.</li></ol><p>The third criticism is obviously misdirected, since although Confucianists knew the circumstances are allotted, one’s moral achievements are not. One tries their best to be moral, though <em>ming</em> may, in the end, disrupt their progress.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>A defense of the argument presented in 2 says that Confucian rituals were mostly to pay respects to the ancestors. Additionally, its seems that the reason for Mohism’s assertion of the existence of gods and spirits is merely in order to provide <em>ethos</em> for the arguments of <em>Mozi</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>Education was something that was only accessible to people with means.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives\">Confucianism operates upon categorical imperatives</a><ul><li>These categorical imperatives would enforce the existing social hierarchy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Money is a psychological construct":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Money is a psychological construct since the currency itself has no intrinsic value and its use is merely something to make transactions easier.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument from Harari’s <em>Homo Sapiens</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the value of the money itself is nil, the entire value of a piece of money comes from the psychological beliefs associated with the money. This, Harari argues, was a part of the Cognitive Revolution that he hypothesizes.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems to me however that many of the currencies of the ancient past did have value, such as gold, since they were in great demand and they were scarce.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities\">Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities</a><ul><li>This is essentially my critique.</li></ul></li></ul>","Money is not a psychological construct, nor is it simply 'debt'":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Yuval Noah Harari claims that money is a psychological construct that purely the result of our imagination, and David Graeber claims that money is simply “debt.” However, both these points of view are incorrect, since money is an actually economic good.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a rebuttal to the ideas that money is a psychological construct, or that it is simply “debt.”</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Money is a commodity that naturally evolved from barter. It has intrinsic value as a unit of value, a store of value, and as a unit of exchange. Things become money because they serve these purposes well.</p><ul><li>At some point in the past, money had to have value, or was tied to value. It is rather ridiculous for people, at some point, to simple assign value to something that is inherently worthless, since that is impractical, especially on a large scale. <ul><li>You cannot simply gather a group of people and say, “Let's pretend this piece of blue paper is worth one cow.” No one would trust it.</li></ul></li><li>It is simply impossible for there to even be debt before the existence of money, since debt must be denominated in money. <ul><li>Additionally, the state did not money in order to pay its soldiers, since if that was the case, there is no reason for the state to choose gold as its means of paying its soldiers. They must have done so since gold already had inherent value as money.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Money is a psychological construct\">Money is a psychological construct</a><ul><li>This is the idea introduced by Harari regrading the nature of money.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities\">Fiat money only has value because it was historically backed by commodities</a><ul><li>This is the Austrian explanation for way fiat money currently has value.</li></ul></li></ul>","Mongol success was partly driven by the warming period around 1200":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Part of the reason that the Mongols were so successful may have been that due to the warming weather at the time, the supply of available horses increased.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This adds complexity to the perspective that it was wholly the Mongols themselves, rather than their environment, that facilitated their success.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere\">Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere</a><ul><li>The other climatic explanation, running the opposite way: harshness produced the adaptability, warmth produced the horses.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies\">Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies</a><ul><li>The political precondition. Extra horses only matter once the tribes are pointed outward.</li></ul></li></ul>","Monopolies should make more money":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that is would both be more efficient (a given considering basic microeconomics) and more fair to have monopolies make more money, especially through price discrimination.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to address a common misconception today that price discrimination is always bad, since it reduces consumer surplus.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The author points to the fact that the use of things like coupons are technically price discrimination, and in that case, having a little bit of price discrimination is actually beneficial, since it also creates consumer surplus. This is better than the situation where the people who would otherwise not have purchased without the coupon does purchase, and thus both consumers have more surplus, and the monopolist has more surplus. Additionally, having more price discrimination means that the monopolist has more money that it is able to invest into R&amp;D, which may increase allowing for greater technological innovations. If it is the case that innovation is getting more and more difficult (which is backed up by empirical evidence), then firms will have to save up more and more of their surplus in order to actually make new innovations.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Brian Albrecht, <em>Monopolies don't make enough money</em></p><p>Without price discrimination, the profit from selling the good is not large enough to justify investing in the new product, so innovation does not occur. But price discrimination makes it profitable to innovate.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic\">An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic</a><ul><li>This describes the basic nature of the monopolist, and one of the reasons that monopolists are not efficient.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a><ul><li>A reason for the formation of a monopoly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>This is another potential barrier to innovation, that could potentially make innovation more difficult in the future.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>If this is the case, then maybe monopolists should remain more inefficient if they want to reduce inequality?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas are getting harder to find\">Ideas are getting harder to find</a><ul><li>The empirical evidence that ideas/innovation are getting more difficult to find.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moral mastery is achieved through choice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Moral mastery, the ability to choose when one is faced with good and evil, isn’t determined when one is born. It is something that one develops through time or otherwise acquires by choice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea in philosophy and literature.</p><h4><em>East of Eden</em></h4><p>The idea of <em>timshel</em> or “Thou Mayest” is present throughout the book and it is something that the characters consistently struggle with.</p><ul><li>Adam struggles with the ability to overcome his love for Cathy and love on, until he finally confronts her and stands his ground against her.</li><li>This is an idea that Samuel Hamilton espouses during his discussion of naming Adam’s children.<ul><li>He says that we are not determined by our birth, and we are able to shape who we become.</li></ul></li><li>Lee also goes through this process, abandoning her use of pidgin as a means of protecting himself and actively beings to help the Trask family.<ul><li>He reveals intimate details about his family past in order to help Adam embrace <em>timshel</em>.</li></ul></li></ul><h4><em>The Brothers Karamzov</em></h4><p>The idea of “active love” that is expressed in this book fits in with the philosophy of the importance of individual choice.</p><ul><li>One ought not to simply fall into sin and debauchery, as Fyodor does.</li><li>Although the Karamazovs are born with the Karamazov spirit, they must be able to conquer it and not fall entirely under its influence.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>Aristotle argues that in order for someone to be responsible for something (and thus be considered virtuous for it or just praised for it), they must have choose it. In this way, his idea of virtue connects with the idea from this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moral principles originate from rational choice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the basis for all contractarian theories, whereby what is moral is decided through collective bargaining.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the underpinning of contractarian moral through.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This essentially reduces morality to rational decision making, which is an idea that some find appealing, while others reject. Theories that are in this vein include Rawls’ Contractualism, Contractarianism, and it is even related to Kant’s theories, since Kant argues that morality is something that people <em>rationally</em> agree upon.</p><h4>References</h4>","Moral responsibility divides into answerability, attributability, and accountability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To hold someone responsible may mean demanding their reasons (answerability), judging the act as expressing who they are (attributability), or holding them liable to praise and blame (accountability), and these three come apart.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Arguments about responsibility often equivocate between the three, so distinguishing them dissolves a class of apparent disagreements.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Accountability is the demanding sense, since it licenses the reactive attitudes of resentment, indignation and guilt. Attributability is the weakest, since an act can be attributable to a person’s character without their being liable to blame for it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character\">An agent remains responsible for determined actions that express their character</a><ul><li>A view that secures attributability while leaving accountability open.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a><ul><li>Bears on which of the three senses survives a determinist picture.</li></ul></li></ul>","Moral truths arise internally and naturally":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Moral truths do not originate from some metaphysical nature outside of oneself. Rather, it arises naturally out of physical substance of our minds.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the view towards the nature of morality for the School of Mind, a branch of Neo-Confucianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Crucially, the School of Mind breakdown the division between <em>li</em> and <em>qi</em> such that there is no longer a clear metaphysical separation as in the School of Principle. Rather, the heart-mind (心) is the <em>li</em>.</p><ul><li><em>Li</em>, therefore, is a characterization of the heart-mind when it responds accordingly to circumstances as dictated by the <em>liangzhi</em>.</li></ul><p>The ultimate goal of the School of Mind, therefore, is to be able to act without external motivation and purely according to the innate and infallible <em>liangzhi</em>. In doing so, one begins to understand the <em>li</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All things are one and the same\">All things are one and the same</a><ul><li>This is the fundamental principle that underlies the thought of the School of Mind.</li></ul></li></ul>","Morality exists in order to apportion satisfaction":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Xunzi argues that morality exists in order for individual satisfactions to be apportioned among individuals.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Xunzi’s justification for the existence of morality.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since people do not all desire different things equally, and the things that people do desire are not infinitely plentiful, and because the world is limited in size, <em>li</em> (礼), which are moral guidelines for individual action, must be established in order to set limits on how much individuals can satisfy their desires; otherwise, there is conflict and war. In this way, he departs from the Confucian orthodoxy, since he claims that morality is not <em>sui generis</em>, since it could have been otherwise, if this “otherwise” led to the same final result.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a><ul><li>Both this linked note and the principle described in the current note are the results of a type of rough utilitarian calculus.</li></ul></li></ul>","Morality is relative":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that there is no objective set of moral truths in the world. Rather, different people or groups create their own versions of morality which are all equally valid.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a viewpoint that had been held by many intellectuals throughout time.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>The Rationalist School of Neo-Taoism</h5><p>Guo Xiang argues that since there is disagreement regarding the truth of moral claims, that there must then not be objective morality.</p><h5>The Sentimentalist School of Neo-Taoism</h5><p>Simply claims that one should live as they want to, without regard for the customs and morals of one’s time. This does not mean descending into sensual pleasure, however. It merely means living according to one’s impulse, which is often highly refined. This is expressed in the concept of <em>feng liu</em>. Most of the time, people of this schools were sentimental regarding nature or the cosmos, rather than their personal tragedies or successes.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Guo Xiang’s argument is clearly fallacious, since such an argument can easily be applied to the very debate at hand, and thereby refute Guo’s position.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry\">Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry</a><ul><li>This note opposes the linked note, at least for ethical truths.</li></ul></li></ul>","Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although there are some crucial industries that require patents to generate new knowledge, such as pharmaceuticals or chemicals. Beyond these industries, however, it seems that patents are usually not the main driver of innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial part of the argument made by Chang in his book, as this increase in patent prevalence is dragging innovation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research\">Due to the winner-takes-all nature of patent systems, waste is created through duplicate research</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a></li></ul>","Most profits from AI have gone to producers of hardware":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The primary limiting factor (the most limited complementary factor) for AI is hardware, since the supply is quite constricted. This also means that most of the gains from AI does to hardware companies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the apparent non-effect of AI on the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is pretty strong evidence, mainly in the double digit growth of Taiwan’s GDP in Q1 and Q2 of 2026, that most of the financial gains of AI have gone to the chip producers.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capital&#x27;s share of income will rise in the future due to AI\">Capital's share of income will rise in the future due to AI</a><ul><li>This is supported by this claim, since physical capital (and extremely valuable capital at that) is required in order for the development of AI.</li></ul></li></ul>","Music can influence your confidence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Playing the right music can boost your confidence and charisma.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This fact could be used to achieve maximum confidence/charisma in the right situations. By playing music that makes me feel confident before an important event, I will be able to perform better.</p>","NAFTA increased mortality and lower living standards in certain areas of the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Although aggregate statistics calculated that there was a +0.11% real wage gain as a result of NAFTA trade, this overlooks the localized negative consequences.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the importance of looking at issues that are localized and making appropriate judgements with such information.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There was a sharp increase in mortality when trade barriers are lifted for manufacturing sectors especially.</p><ul><li>Following 1994, there was a 0.68% increase in annual mortality for areas that were exposed to NAFTA.</li><li>This burden fell most heavily on working age men (8.9% increased in mortality for them).<ul><li>Ripple effect for widows.</li></ul></li><li>There are higher levels of reported “fair/poor health,” higher levels of smoking, and a surge in Medicare coverage among the affected demographic.</li><li>Accounting for all of this, the net welfare is actually a loss (-0.45% of annual consumption for a 45-year-old male).</li></ul><p>Trade shocks specifically effect people in manufacturing sectors, and cause adverse effects. There are studies that show how recessions actually make people healthier, but recessions (such as the one in 2008), typically largely affect service and non-manufacturing roles.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved\">Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved</a><ul><li>This note would contradict this linked note.</li></ul></li></ul>","NATO militarily intervened in Bosnia in 1995 due to the Srebrenica massacre":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The July 1995 massacre of Bosnians by the Serb army was the worst massacre post-WWII, and led to the involvement of NATO forces in Bosnia in order to prevent further war crimes from being committed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the immediate spark for NATO intervention in the Bosnian War.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete\">The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete</a><ul><li>A competing account of the same intervention. That note reads it as civilizational betrayal driven by naivete, not as a response to atrocity.</li></ul></li></ul>","Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Huntington claims that typically, nations Westernize first, and at the same time, they modernize, and then they reject the West.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major pattern that Huntington identifies.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>First, we have the define the West. Huntington identifies the following as elements of the West (importantly, he says that the West is not defined by its modernization, but by its culture):</p><ul><li>It is defined by these values/institutions specifically (though this list is by no means exhaustive):<ul><li>The classical tradition</li><li>Catholicism &amp; Protestantism</li><li>Rule of Law</li><li>Separation of church and state</li><li>Representative bodies</li><li>Individualism</li><li>Social pluralism: autonomous groups beyond kinship groups that exert influence in society.</li><li>Common language: the West is influenced collectively influenced by Latin.</li></ul></li><li>Westernization first leads to modernization. After modernization, as the state gains its own power and influence, it rejects the West and embraces its indigenous culture and religion once again.</li><li>Modernization has both a societal and individual effect that contributes to cultural and religious resurgence:<ul><li><strong>Societal</strong>: modernization leads to the state becoming more powerful in terms of its industry/economy, leading the people to gain a sense of cultural assertiveness.</li><li><strong>Individual</strong>: modernization leads to <em>anomie</em>, increasingly making people turn to religion.</li></ul></li><li>Nations today are becoming less Western and more modern.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>This could be part of what non-Western nations took from the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion\">The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion</a><ul><li>This was also adopted by the countries not in the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests\">The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests</a><ul><li>This shows Asian nations pushing back against Western institutions and ideology.</li></ul></li></ul>","Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Natural virtue is one’s potential to become virtuous and it is innate, unreasoned, and incomplete. In order for natural virtue to become true virtue, prudence is needed. Virtue cannot be achieved without at least some innate inclination towards virtue, i.e., natural virtue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Highlights the importance of prudence to acting morally, since it is prudence that elevates natural virtues to true virtues.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a><ul><li>The virtue of prudence is required in order to turn natural virtues into true virtues.</li></ul></li></ul>","Nature is destructive and beautiful at once":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The same indifference that makes the natural world lethal is what makes it beautiful, so the two aspects are not in tension but are the same property described from two positions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is why the literature of exposure so often reads as reverent rather than hostile, and it distinguishes the natural sublime from mere danger.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>London makes the point through the manner of the death in <em>To Build A Fire</em>, which is rendered as drowsy and almost peaceful, against a landscape whose beauty is a direct function of its emptiness.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humanity is insignificant\">Humanity is insignificant</a><ul><li>The premise the indifference rests on.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The world is unfair and amoral\">The world is unfair and amoral</a><ul><li>The same indifference described in moral rather than aesthetic terms.</li></ul></li></ul>","Naval strategy can seek either decisive battle or the disruption of enemy communication":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While Mahan pursues the elimination of the enemy fleet through decisive battle, thus establishing absolute naval control, Corbett argues for the disruption of enemy supply and communication lines without decisive battle in an attempt to establish relative naval control.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This contrasts the difference between Mahanian and Corbettian naval strategies, as while one focuses on complete and absolute control, the other focuses on disrupting the ability of the enemy to wage war more indirectly.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A limited war is best fought on the strategic defensive and the operational offensive\">A limited war is best fought on the strategic defensive and the operational offensive</a><ul><li>A related idea of Corbett.</li></ul></li></ul>","Neo-Confucianism was Confucianism influenced by Buddhism and Daoism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Neo-Confucianism (宋明理学) was an attempt to create a more rationalist version of Confucianism, free from the influence of Daoism and Buddhism. At the same time, it incorporated many elements of Daoist and Buddhist vocabulary.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Neo-Confucianism’s development in the Tang and Song dynasty was an attempt to rid Confucianism of its Daoist and Buddhist roots, and at the same time, revive and reinvigorate an old tradition.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs\">Daoism argues for self-sufficiency and withdrawal from worldly affairs</a><ul><li>The core ideas of Daoism that influenced the Neo-Confucian revival.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">The Yin-Yang symbol represents the duality and synthesis between Daoism and Confucianism</span><ul><li>Perhaps this relates to the Neo-Confucian revival?</li></ul></li></ul>","Neo-mercantilism is the modern adaptation of historical mercantilism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whereas historical mercantilism had a single-minded focus on maintaining a trade surplus and accumulating precious materials, neo-mercantilism is more complex, as it integrates capitalistic elements.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important distinct to make, since many countries today are considered to be “neo-mercantilist,” though this does not mean that they are mercantilist in the 16-17th century sense.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p><strong>Neo-mercantilism:</strong></p><ul><li>Focuses on national economic strength.</li><li>Favoring exports over imports, though with nuance.</li><li>State-guided capitalism.</li><li>Focus on industrial policy; technology, infrastructure, and innovation are prioritized.</li><li>State-subsidized/directed industries (which are considered to be crucial for the state).<ul><li>Low domestic competition.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Mercantilism:</strong></p><ul><li>Resource extraction from colonies that also serve as markets for the mother state.</li><li>Heavy economic regulation and government monopolies.</li><li>Hoarding of precious mineral wealth.</li><li>Maximize export and minimize imports.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom\">Economic freedom is the precursor to political freedom</a></li></ul>","New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A polypeptide chain’s N-terminus is created first, so new amino acids are attached via peptide bonds on the C-terminus.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that might appear on an AP Bio test. This is also analogous to how New nucleotides are added to the 3' end of a DNA or RNA.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"New nucleotides are added to the 3&#x27; end of a DNA or RNA\">New nucleotides are added to the 3' end of a DNA or RNA</a></li></ul>","New nucleotides are added to the 3' end of a DNA or RNA":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When new nucleotides are added to a DNA or RNA, it is added to the 3’ end of the chain of nucleotides. Thus, DNA or RNA is always read from the 5’ to the 3’ end.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This information might appear on an AP Bio test. This is also analogous to how New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain\">New amino acids are added to the C-terminus on a polypeptide chain</a></li></ul>","Nicomachean Ethics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A foundational work of ethics that introduced the theory of Virtue Ethics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has had a tremendous impact on the way that future ethicists approach their discipline.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><h5>The Good and Happiness</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good\">Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself\">One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive\">The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative\">The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative</a></li></ul><h5>Moral Virtue and the Mean</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Moral States</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated\">Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do\">The things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Acting virtuously is not painful\">Acting virtuously is not painful</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself\">Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal\">The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a></li></ul><h5>Voluntariness, Choice, and Deliberation</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful\">An act is compulsory when the cause of the action is external or the agent acts out of ignorance but is remorseful</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards\">An act is non-voluntary when an agent acts out of ignorance and feels no regret afterwards</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An ignorant action is only excusable when it represents an ignorance of the particular, not of the universal or of voluntary ignorance\">An ignorant action is only excusable when it represents an ignorance of the particular, not of the universal or of voluntary ignorance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily\">Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end\">A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a></li></ul><h5>The Particular Virtues</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be courageous is not to be afraid of death\">To be courageous is not to be afraid of death</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Endurance is a crucial component of courage\">Endurance is a crucial component of courage</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself\">Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberality (generosity) is the result of giving to others under the right conditions\">Liberality (generosity) is the result of giving to others under the right conditions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth\">Magnificence is liberality when one has great wealth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Magnanimity is the &#x27;crown&#x27; of the moral virtues\">Magnanimity is the 'crown' of the moral virtues</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Modesty is not a virtue; rather, it is a feeling\">Modesty is not a virtue; rather, it is a feeling</a></li></ul><h5>Justice</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Justice</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals\">Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction\">There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Equity is the rectification of legal justice\">Equity is the rectification of legal justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be just, one must have a disposition towards just action and be able to make deliberate choices\">To be just, one must have a disposition towards just action and be able to make deliberate choices</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly\">One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a></li></ul><h5>Intellectual Virtue and Prudence</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of art is concerned with making, where the end goal is the product, not the action itself\">The virtue of art is concerned with making, where the end goal is the product, not the action itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation\">The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence\">Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect\">Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced\">Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Practical Syllogism motivates us to perform some action\">The Practical Syllogism motivates us to perform some action</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences\">Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences</a></li></ul><h5>Continence and Incontinence</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control\">A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Obstinacy is different from continence\">Obstinacy is different from continence</a></li></ul><h5>Friendship</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection\">Friendship is having affection for another, wishing well for them, and having the other recognize your affection</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved\">Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality\">A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality\">A friendship between people of different status is based on status-weighted proportional equality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goodwill is not friendship\">Goodwill is not friendship</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Concord is a pragmatic friendship between citizens of a state\">Concord is a pragmatic friendship between citizens of a state</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice in the political sphere corresponds to friendship in the household\">Justice in the political sphere corresponds to friendship in the household</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The benefactor typically loves his beneficiary more than the other way around\">The benefactor typically loves his beneficiary more than the other way around</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our best friend is ourselves\">Our best friend is ourselves</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtuous man needs friends\">The virtuous man needs friends</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship is more necessary in adversity, but more honorable in prosperity\">Friendship is more necessary in adversity, but more honorable in prosperity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should not have more friends than we can get intimate with\">We should not have more friends than we can get intimate with</a></li></ul><h5>Pleasure and Contemplation</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle on Pleasure</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not a process\">Pleasure is not a process</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life\">Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Something that is pleasurable is not necessarily bad\">Something that is pleasurable is not necessarily bad</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is pleasurable to the good man is truly pleasurable\">What is pleasurable to the good man is truly pleasurable</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Amusement is pleasant and choice-worthy, though it is not happiness\">Amusement is pleasant and choice-worthy, though it is not happiness</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics (book)\">Politics (book)</a><ul><li>The continuation of the argument: the Ethics establishes what makes an individual good, and the Politics asks what kind of city produces such individuals.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Desert and the Basic Structure\">Desert and the Basic Structure</a><ul><li>The map that sets the account of distributive justice given here against the Rawlsian one.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism (book)\">Utilitarianism (book)</a><ul><li>The main rival account of the human good, and the one against which virtue ethics is usually defined.</li></ul></li></ul>","Non-compete clauses are bad for the worker and the consumer":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is evidence to suggest that there are adverse effects to having non-compete clauses, since they reduce wages for laborers and raise prices for the consumer.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a potential argument for bans of non-compete clauses.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>When non-compete clauses disappear, workers wages rise by 3-4% across a variety of fields.</li><li>Although NCC encourages training, the worker does not share in the gain, as the ROI of the training is captured entirely by the firm.</li><li>It hurts small businesses since they are not able to hire many people due to non-compete contracts.</li><li>It also hurts consumers since NCCs lead to reduced labor mobility, high market concentration, and thus higher prices.</li><li>Bans of NCCs lead to knowledge spillovers.</li><li>They are also not any more effective in preventing information leakage than NDAs.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The labor market is not perfectly competitive\">The labor market is not perfectly competitive</a><ul><li>The underlying condition. Non-competes can only suppress wages because labour markets have monopsony power to begin with.</li></ul></li></ul>","Non-price discriminating, monopolistic firms prefer technological innovations that cut costs rather than increase outputs":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the fact that as one sells more, the price of a good goes down, firms that experience downward-sloping demand curves tend to prefer technological innovations that cut costs rather than innovations that increase outputl.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes a fact of firm behavior for monopolistic, non price discriminating firms.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic\">An ordinary monopolist sets the price where the demand curve is unit elastic</a><ul><li>They would not want to move the price below this point, for instance.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Monopolies should make more money\">Monopolies should make more money</a><ul><li>But if firms price discriminate, this would not be a concern?</li></ul></li></ul>","Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to nonpolar molecules being hydrophobic, they are driven away from $H_{2}O$ and toward the interior of the protein, while polar and charged R groups face outward into the solvent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the principal driver of tertiary structure. The fold is largely determined by which residues must avoid water, which is also why a change in solvent, pH or salinity can unfold the protein.</p>","Objective truths exist, and can be accessed via scientific inquiry":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The claim that objective truths in fact exist, and they can be arrived upon through rigorous applications of logic and the scientific method.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This forms the core of the pragmatist philosophy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Peirce gives the following as proof that the objective (or as he terms it, “Reals”) exists*:</p><ol><li>If through scientific investigation, we cannot prove that there are Reals, it at least does not prove the opposite.</li><li>Nobody can doubt that when there are two claims which are contradictory to each other, that there must be one which is correct, or more correct.</li><li>The scientific method is already widely used to find Reals, except when people don’t know how to apply it.</li><li>Historically, the scientific method has been successful in finding Reals.</li></ol><h4>Note</h4><p>This is far more a social than logical argument, ironically. Most of these premises are, if considered strictly, fallacious.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>The comparative argument is stronger than the four premises above. Science is preferable to tenacity, authority and the a priori method not because it is certain but because it is the only one of the four that contains a mechanism for its own correction; the other three are stable precisely because nothing in them responds to being wrong.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief can be fixed by tenacity, authority, the a priori method, or by science\">Belief can be fixed by tenacity, authority, the a priori method, or by science</a><ul><li>The typology this conclusion is drawn from.</li></ul></li></ul>","Obstinacy is different from continence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Obstinacy is not the same thing as continence, though they may share some similarities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Continence is an important moral state, and it is important to not get it mixed up with obstinacy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Obstinacy can mean that someone is “hard to persuade” or “not easily persuaded.”<ul><li>In the former case, it means that they are difficult to change their mind. </li><li>In the latter case, it means that it is difficult to change their minds once they are already set on something.</li></ul></li><li>There are three types of obstinacy:<ul><li><strong>Opinionated</strong>: relates to pleasure and pain; such a person like being right too much. It causes them pain when they are wrong.</li><li><strong>Stupid</strong>: such people are not able to figure something out because they can’t think rationally about a problem, or they don’t have the relevant knowledge.</li><li><strong>Boorish</strong>: such people are stubborn due to lack of sensitivity or social awarness.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>Continence is different from obstinacy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Oedipus at Colonus":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows the blind, exiled Oedipus as he arrives at Colonus with Antigone and seeks sanctuary at a sacred site, where he confronts his suffering, fate, and past crimes. As Creon tries to persuade—or pressure—him to return to Thebes, Oedipus refuses, distrusting his motives, and the conflict escalates into threats against his daughters. Theseus, king of Athens, offers protection, and the play explores themes of justice, mercy, redemption, and the moral duty to protect the vulnerable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This play furthers exploration of the themes that were present in Oedipus Rex and explores from new themes as well.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humanity is insignificant\">Humanity is insignificant</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy\">Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li></ul>","Oedipus the King (play)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Oedipus, the confident ruler of Thebes, as he tries to save his city from a devastating plague by finding the murderer of the former king, Laius. As he investigates, he uncovers a terrible truth: he himself unknowingly killed Laius and married his own mother, Jocasta, fulfilling the prophecy he had spent his life trying to avoid. The play is a tragic exploration of fate, truth, and the limits of human knowledge, ending with Jocasta’s suicide and Oedipus blinding himself and accepting exile.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A major work of literature that has shaped philosophy, future literature, and theses in psychology.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them\">A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The blind are able to see what the sighted are not\">The blind are able to see what the sighted are not</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humanity is insignificant\">Humanity is insignificant</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed\">The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed</a><ul><li>In this respect, Hamlet is very much like Oedipus, in that both characters seek the truth in the world. Yet, it is this truth that eventually drives them mad and leads to their downfall.</li><li>Yet, there is a crucial difference between the character of Hamlet and Oedipus: while the former is driven by his search for truth, he never acts on the result. On the other hand, Oedipus acts with no hesitation when he learns the truth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a><ul><li>This is exactly the case in <em>Oedipus Rex</em>.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy\">Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy</a><ul><li>There is some semblance of optimism at the end of this play, especially in the way that Oedipus accepts his own exile.</li></ul></li></ul>","Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Compared to all the others arts, music is the best able to bring about different dispositions of character, and thus help people learn more about these dispositions, ultimately allowing them to achieve virtue through this knowledge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the reason Aristotle then argues that music should be part of the mandatory education.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>His premise is that in rhythms and tunes there are likenesses particularly close to the genuine natures of anger and gentleness, courage and moderation, and their opposites. Then the explicit contrast: no likeness of characters is present in the other perceptible things. In touch and taste there is none, and in visible things it is present only slightly. Figures and colors are not likenesses of characters but only indications of them, and only as character is manifested by the body under passion. Whereas in tunes by themselves there are imitations of characters.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that in the modern day, film would also be considered virtue-inducing?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Music can influence your confidence\">Music can influence your confidence</a><ul><li>It seems that modern research somewhat backs this up?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Maybe music can help with this process.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates\">People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates</a><ul><li>This note is the reason Aristotle argues that people should be trained in music.</li></ul></li></ul>","Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because people who are older have more experience with particulars, and this experience of particulars is what allows one to develop prudence, older people are generally more prudent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important, since this explains why we typically put older people in positions of power.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Gerontocracies are bad\">Gerontocracies are bad</a><ul><li>Aristotle would disagree with this, since he would consider older people to be more prudent than younger ones.</li></ul></li></ul>","Olley-Pakes decomposition produces a more accurate representation of market competitiveness compared to other measures":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Olley-Pakes decomposition, which measures whether consumers are switching to more productive firms, offers a clearer view into the efficiency of a market compared to measure such as HHI (which just looks at size).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This offers a methodological improvement to the problem of ensuring efficient and productive markets. This is a methodological improvement because more market concentration doesn’t necessarily imply reduced innovation and stagnating productivity growth for firms. By looking at Olley-Pakes, we can gain a better understanding into if the concentration is due to the higher productivity of one firm compared to another, or something else.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The difficulty with this method is just that it requires a lot more data. And in order for this method to be effective, data needs to be collected constantly to ensure that things like monopolies temporarily cutting prices in order to remove competitors from the market is not happening.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Monopolies should make more money\">Monopolies should make more money</a><ul><li>If they are more efficient, it would make sense for them to make more money.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry</a><ul><li>What if we investigated natural monopolies with this?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition\">Large MES relative to industry demand indicates consolidation; small MES indicates competition</a><ul><li>But more competition isn’t necessarily better.</li></ul></li></ul>","On Liberty":"<h4>Summary</h4><h4>Significance</h4><p>Taken together the three are the core of nineteenth-century liberalism, and reading them as one argument rather than three books is what makes Mill's position intelligible: the political conclusions in the second and third works are supposed to follow from the moral theory in the first.</p><h5>I. Introductory</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We ought not to interference with the personal liberties of other individuals\">We ought not to interference with the personal liberties of other individuals</a><ul><li>The harm principle, which the rest of the essay is an elaboration of.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a></li></ul><h5>II. Of the Liberty of Thought and Discussion</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality\">Ideas must be challenged so that they maintain their vitality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth\">Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Truths which are important to society ought to be the ones exempt from skepticism\">Truths which are important to society ought to be the ones exempt from skepticism</a><ul><li>The objection Mill answers, not his own position.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a></li></ul><h5>III. Of Individuality</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society\">We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people\">Democracies are doomed to mediocrity since they are ultimately, on average, subject to mediocre people</a></li></ul><h5>IV. Of the Limits to the Authority of Society over the Individual</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases\">Government should not intervene in the affairs of private individuals in most cases</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil\">One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil</a></li></ul><h5>V. Applications</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom\">One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom</a><ul><li>The limiting case: liberty cannot be used to alienate liberty.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state ought to prevent childbirth in cases where the parents would not be able to support the child\">The state ought to prevent childbirth in cases where the parents would not be able to support the child</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>There is some overlap between these two works, specifically in the way that they both treat the nature of people’s (moral) actions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Another work of liberal political theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality\">A friendship between people of equal status is based on equality</a><ul><li>The Aristotelian account of equal friendship that <em>The Subjection of Women</em> arrives at from the opposite direction.</li></ul></li></ul>","Once enlightenment is achieved, the sage returns to normal life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Once the Zen sage has achieved enlightenment, they return to living their normal lives, though they have been changed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is what the Zen doctrine instructs its students to do.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Once one has achieved enlightenment, they return to living their lives, such that while their actions remain the same, they are no longer the same person. With the achievement of enlightenment, the sage loses their attachment to everything.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment\">Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment</a><ul><li>This is the process by which one achieves enlightenment.</li></ul></li></ul>","Once the threshold potential is reached, voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, and depolarization begins":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the dendrite receives a collection of different charges which average to around $-50mV$, voltage-gated $NA^+$ channels open, and cause a spike in the charge through a process known as depolarization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial part of how muscles and neurons operate, as this is the beginning of a signal that is sent down the cells (in a neuron).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Positive sodium ions that spread through passive diffusion causes a signal to be transported through a neuron\">Positive sodium ions that spread through passive diffusion causes a signal to be transported through a neuron</a></li></ul>","One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is impossible for someone to voluntarily choose to be treated unjustly. To be treated unjustly is a bad thing. And since no one truly wishes for what is bad for them, no one can voluntarily accept injustice. Also, justice must be conducted with more than one person present.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial argument for Aristotle to make, since otherwise, there is a contradictory possibility for someone to be unjust to themselves (for instance, through giving themselves too little when it comes to distributive justice; in such a case the person is gaining something else, perhaps honor).</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Committing suicide, which seems to be an injustice against oneself, is indeed an injustice against the state when viewed through the lens of legal justice. Metaphorically, parts of one’s soul is able to engage unjustly with other parts of the soul, though this is not true “justice” and “injustice” in the sense used elsewhere.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>There are some specific exceptions to the typical rules of justice, which are outlined here.</li></ul></li></ul>","One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We cannot voluntarily choose to lose our freedom.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It’s unclear why Mill makes this point, this point being something that is relatively weak as well.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This note seems obviously false. There does not seem to be a good reason that one cannot voluntarily give up one’s freedom.</p><h4>References</h4>","One has no other obligations except to oneself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of egoism, that there are no other interests one needs to serve other than oneself’s.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core belief of the early Taoists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>From this concept that oneself is the most important thing (人人为自己), we can derive the other important precept of the early Taoists, that of “despising things and valuing life (轻物重生),” since all that one should care about is oneself, and what is more core to oneself than one’s life, one should naturally place one’s life above all else.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Lüshi Chunqiu</em> (吕氏春秋), “The Importance of Self”</p><p>今吾生之为我有，而利我亦大矣。论其贵贱，爵为天子，不足以比焉；论其轻重，富有天下，不可以易之；论其安危，一曙失之，终身不复得。此三者，有道者之所慎也。 The life I possess is truly my own, and its value to me is immense. In terms of worth, even the rank of Son of Heaven cannot compare to it; in terms of weight, not even the wealth of the entire realm could be exchanged for it; in terms of security, once lost at the break of dawn, it can never be regained in a lifetime. These three aspects are matters that the wise treat with the utmost caution.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","One has to confront their problems head on":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is important to confront problems head on.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that is put forth often in books regarding ethics, or what one ought to do.</p><h4>In <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Essays (book)\">Essays (book)</a></h4><p><em>To consider and estimate the danger is in a way the reverse of being haunted by it.</em> Montaigne urges us to, instead of shying away from a danger, to confront it directly. And so, to deeply consider and estimate the danger is the best way to ensure that you are not affected by it.</p><h4>References</h4>","One must discard knowledge":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That in order to truly become enlightened, one must discard knowledge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the core procedures in Taoism for one to achieve enlightenment. Same thing in Zen Buddhism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>Taoism</h5><p>The ordinary use of knowledge is to make distinctions. However, in order to truly become a part of the cosmos, the individual must abandon concepts of differentiation and embrace the undifferentiated aesthetic continuum. A further distinction must be made between having <em>no-knowledge</em> and <em>having-no</em> knowledge. The latter is a sort of innocence that is innate to children. The former, however, is the goal of the sage, who must discard the knowledge that they have accumulated to return to a sort of enlightened innocence.</p><h5>Zen</h5><p>When one comes to be able to perceive the Tao is when one has achieved enlightenment. This occurs when the individual loses their knowledge of the distinction between the knower and the known. Therefore, one while one is not without knowledge, the only type of knowledge one then possess is non-knowledge; it is a state in which all distinctions are gone.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao\">By becoming one with the cosmos, one finds the Tao</a><ul><li>In order for this to occur, one must first abandon knowledge.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment\">Buddhahood is achieved via Sudden Enlightenment</a><ul><li>It is when we lose all our knowledge that Sudden Enlightenment becomes possible.</li></ul></li></ul>","One ought no be held responsible if in not doing so, they act better, or if doing so produces more evil":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While usually one should be held responsible for their actions, they should not be held responsible when:</p><ol><li>by holding the person responsible, we end up producing more harm than good, or </li><li>the person is likely to act better while not compulsed by others.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains exceptions to the commonly held precept that people ought to be held responsible for their actions.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>The exceptions noted here are clearly utilitarian in basis. A deontologist would hardly accept either of these exceptions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible\">One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible</a><ul><li>Aristotle would clearly disagree with the exceptions which are made here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones\">The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones</a><ul><li>As seen here, the application of utilitarian theory produces from questionable results which feed into the congruence problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","One ought to be useless":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the best thing one can do is to be useless, since it doing so, once is best able to preserve one’s life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant viewpoint during early Taoist thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>People who are too good become powerful, and are thus susceptible to corruption and envy from others, causing problems. People who are too evil are also hated by others and face many problems. Ultimately, it is only be being useless and not being either too powerful or evil that one is best able to live. This does not actually mean actually “doing nothing,” but rather, doing less and acting without artificiality or arbitrariness.</p><ul><li>Doing less is good because overdoing is bad. We should only do what is necessary, and not over-do.</li><li>This also part of Laozi’s critique of the Confucian virtues, which he says are against these principles and therefore against 德 and 道.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite\">To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite</a><ul><li>When we act, then, we must act with this principle in mind.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has no other obligations except to oneself\">One has no other obligations except to oneself</a><ul><li>Since this is true, we can derive the current note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should desire less\">We should desire less</a><ul><li>Ultimately, we should just desire less, and that would just make us do less.</li></ul></li></ul>","One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One should choose to do the things that good for them (in their particular circumstances), not what is “good in themselves,” i.e., honor, wealth, power, pleasure, etc.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explicates Aristotle’s theory of virtue, as he says that the most important thing is that one cultivates their overall virtue, not the amount of something that one has.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The unjust person is one who pursues things that are “good in themselves,” and not necessarily what is “good for them” in the specific circumstances that they are bound to. In this way, since it is the pursuit of what is good for oneself at the specific moment and under specific circumstances that brings the individual virtue, by not engaging in such pursuits, but are no longer virtuous and thus become unjust.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>This means that one must pursue what is good for them, not what is good in itself.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>One should be seeking moral goodness, but technical goodness in this case.</li></ul></li></ul>","One ought to focus on the present and not remain bogged down in the past":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that it is important to focus on the present and not be contained by what happened in the past.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is both a common trope in literary works and also something that is empirically demonstrated.</p><h4>In Economics</h4><p>In economics, this manifests in the form of the concept of <em>sunk cost</em>, or costs that have already been incurred and which are irreparable. Indeed, the sunk cost fallacy is the inability to see this by operating in a manner which suggests that the costs are not actually sunk. The correct decision is to ignore sunk cost and operate on marginal costs.</p><h4>In Literature</h4><p>This is also a theme in many works of literature, for instance,</p><ul><li><em>The Sound and the Fury</em> is in large part about the failure of the Compson family to move past the Antebellum South.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","One receives external moral truths":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That one’s sense of morality is something ultimately derived from external truths in the world, and not a result of one’s internal, natural, nature.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the position of the School of Principle of the Neo-Confucianists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>They argued that the mind, as with all other individual things, is the embodiment of <em>li</em> with <em>qi</em>, where <em>li</em> is the moral truths that exist in the world, independent of the mind, and <em>qi</em> is the substance that is required in order for the <em>li</em> and the <em>qi</em> to be bound together and create the mind. In this interpretation, the four constant virtues are of <em>li</em> and the four beginnings are of the operations of the mind. This theory also explains the nature of evil/bad/imperfect things in the world. What is bad is simple <em>qi</em> that does not align with the <em>li</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral truths arise internally and naturally\">Moral truths arise internally and naturally</a><ul><li>This is the theory subscribed to by the opposite school of thought, the School of Mind.</li></ul></li></ul>","One should act according to one’s natural abilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A development of the Taoist doctrine of <em>wu-wei</em> states that people should not simply “not act,” but rather that they should act according to their natural abilities.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a development of Taoist theory, specifically, of the concept of <em>wu-wei</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The core issue, as identified by Guo Xiang, is that of imitation; i.e., attempting to imitate the sages and accomplish great things when it is not in one’s fundamental nature to do so. Guo rather claims that one should do what is inherently in one’s nature. Imitation, Guo claims, is useless, fruitless, and harmful.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to be useless\">One ought to be useless</a><ul><li>This note represents a development of this Taoist concept.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>Guo redefines knowledge as that which is beyond one’s natural ability.</li></ul></li></ul>","One should act differently depending on one's relative size compared to the overall system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When one is a small and inconsequential actor in a system, one should act from generally applicable and utility generating principles (rule utilitarianism). Only when one becomes such a major actor in a system that one’s actions generate profound and reverberating effects in an system that one should act with consideration of the other actors in the system (act utilitarianism).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a simple heuristic that helps with understanding the way that we should act in different situations.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When you are such a negligible part of the overall system, the best way of acting is a non-consequentialist one, i.e., acting in a way that is in accordance only with personal utility, and not overall utility of the system. This is because one’s actions at this stage is unlikely to have large effects that perpetuate in the system very far and thus adversely affect oneself now or in the future (there, of course, are exceptions to this, via coordination problems/races to the bottom). In cases where one is in fact in a influential position in a system, one should then begin to consider one’s consequences in the system, since it will not begin to directly and majorly affect oneself.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems analysis works best when the system is static\">Systems analysis works best when the system is static</a><ul><li>The issue perhaps with this heuristic is that since systems are constantly changing, it is difficult to get a precise estimate of the size of the system. Although it is rare for there to be systems that fluctuate extremely violently in size.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>Perhaps one of the factors that lead to this equilibrium is this heuristic in action?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained\">Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained</a><ul><li>Decentralized systems are systems whereby individuals are able to act with personal discretion and according to their own individual heuristics.</li></ul></li></ul>","One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One should not attempt to analyze a belief in a supreme power based on rigorous, logical argumentation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a viewpoint expressed by some when it comes to topics relating to the philosophy of religion.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>An example of this in literature is in <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>, where Fyodor attempts to analyze his faithlessness with an argument about how there are no hooks in hell, since they don’t have furnaces to make iron and because there isn’t a ceiling in hell for the hooks to hang on. He seems to find a contradiction in whether hell has a ceiling or not.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Fyodor Pavlovich, <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></p><p>You see, however stupid I am about it, I keep thinking, I keep thinking — from time to time, of course, not all the while. It's impossible, I think, for the devils to forget to drag me down to hell with their hooks when I die. Then I wonder — hooks? Where would they get them? What of? Iron hooks? Where do they forge them? Have they a foundry there of some sort? The monks in the monastery probably believe that there's a ceiling in hell, for instance. Now I'm ready to believe in hell, but without a ceiling. It makes it more refined, more enlightened, more Lutheran that is. And, after all, what does it matter whether it has a ceiling or hasn't? But, do you know, there's a damnable question involved in it? If there's no ceiling there can be no hooks, and if there are no hooks it all breaks down, which is unlikely again, for then there would be none to drag me down to hell, and if they don't drag me down what justice is there in the world?</p></blockquote><p>It seems that by attempting to justify something with argumentation, one can only trivialize it. Dostoevsky seems to make the case that this is not the right way to approach religion, this sort of materialist, trivially-rationalist approach.</p><h4>References</h4>","One should strive to overcome emotion with reason":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that one’s emotions can be overcome with the use of reason, since many of the things that we feel strongly about, if reasoned correctly, would reduce our feelings towards it, keeping us more serene and in line with the Tao.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a trait that the Taoist sage embodies, and therefore, people should strive for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Any (negative) emotion can essentially be rationalized away, since it is either the result of one’s own doing, in which case one must then just rationally learn from one’s mistake, or it was outside one’s control, meaning that one couldn’t have done anything anyways, so there is no reason to grieve about it. Once one is able to rationalize away one’s emotions, then one’s happiness is not dependent on outside factors, which is considered absolute happiness.</p><h4>References</h4>","One's fate is not determined by their birth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that one’s fate and course in life is not determine by the circumstances of their birth/growing up.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common idea reiterated in society and present in specific works of literature as well.</p><h4>East of Eden</h4><p>This is a main idea of East of Eden.</p><ul><li>It is first expressed during the naming ceremony by Sam Hamilton.</li><li>This is evident in the case of Adam, and Cal.</li></ul><p>There is large contrast between the way that Adam and Cyrus’s treatment of this idea, specifically regrading their respective offspring.</p><ul><li>Cyrus forces Adam into his own mold, giving him his own definition of a good life and morality, rather than giving him real moral choice.</li><li>While Adam fails in much the same way that Cyrus does throughout most of the book, in his final moment, he is able to redeem himself. His “timshel” blessing for Cal frees Cal from the moral determinism that has chased the members of the family throughout the rest of the book.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a><ul><li>The metaphysical version of the question. Freedom from one’s birth circumstances is compatible with one’s ultimate fate still being fixed.</li></ul></li></ul>","One’s initial moral sensitivity is irrelevant to whether one ought to be considered morally responsible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One’s initial moral outlook is irrelevant to how one should be assigned moral responsibility.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a point of clarification that Aristotle makes when he defends against the objection that some people are just naturally less able to be moral than others.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that if we somehow absolve some people of moral responsibility since they are less able to perform moral actions due to some innate factor, it would be like saying that people who do act virtuously are somehow just innately predisposed to act in the same way and thus should not be praised. This is clearly false.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>Acting virtuously is always virtuous, and so someone’s initial conditions when it comes to their ability to act morally doesn’t actually matter to whether they ought to be held morally responsible.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>In order for one to become more virtuous if they do not begin as being virtuous, they have to continuously act virtuously.</li></ul></li></ul>","Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that an absolute monarch is needed in order to people moral and prevent them from degrading into anarchy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This line of thought has been advocated for by a variety of thinkers, including Thomas Hobbes and <em>Mozi</em>.</p><h4>Mohism</h4><p>An absolute monarch is needed in order to maintain the order in the world and prevent a state of complete anarchy. They are needed to propagate and enforce the doctrine of all-embracing love. The entire purpose of the state, in fact, is to “unify the standards (一同国之义),” or police the morality of the nation and ensure that it remains homogenous. The legitimacy of any such ruler is necessarily born out of both the will of the people and the Will of God.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination\">Everyone in the world should love everyone else without discrimination</a><ul><li>This is the principle that the absolute monarch would enforce.</li></ul></li></ul>","Only around the beginning of modernity have people begun to see romantic love as something that makes life worthy and significant":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As the values that define our lives begin to fade during the Enlightenment, people sought new ways to define meaning in life. One form of this was the emergence of romantic satisfaction as a means of finding meaning in life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the important that our society places upon romantic affection, since it is a means by which people find meaning and satisfaction in their existence.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Anthony Giddens, <em>The Transformation of Intimacy</em></p><p>The other, by being who he or she is, answers a lack… And this lack is directly to do with self-identity: in some sense, the flawed individual is made whole.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a><ul><li>This could potentially be related to the ideas discussed in this note, that a sense of meaninglessness pervaded public sentiment during and after the Enlightenment, leading people to seek new ways to find meaning in life.</li></ul></li></ul>","Only realized institutions can be judged to be just or unjust":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Only realized institutions, i.e., institutions which are effectively and impartially administered in reality, can be considered to be unjust or just. To judge an abstract institution means to judge the realized version of that institution.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that in order to consider some institution, we have to consider the way that it would function in reality (though still idealized), and not just consider it as an abstract concept.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism\">The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism</a><ul><li>When judging this institution, we then must judge the realized role of this institution (perhaps though empirical evidence) and not the idealized, abstract version of the institution.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This may come from the fact that there is a discrepancy between the abstract institution and the realized institution.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Weak and Kafkaesque institutions created a large informal economy in Latin America\">Weak and Kafkaesque institutions created a large informal economy in Latin America</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Only when moribund do we have a clear perspective on our own life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that death is somehow profound and brings about an evaluation of the way that we live which is deeper than would otherwise have been able to be achieved.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be something that is suggested by many works of literature, and makes us aware of the fact that when we are not about to die, we may not have the fullest perspective on life.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>This idea has been referenced in many works of literature, including:</p><ul><li><em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em>: where Harry gains a deeper understanding of the “sins” during his life during his death.</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"East of Eden\">East of Eden</a>: when Adam was on his deathbed, he was finally able to understand <em>timshel</em>, the experiences of his life, and the experience of his sons.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>The claim needs a qualification. Clarity about a wasted life is a cognitive achievement and not a moral one, so the deathbed recognition does not by itself discharge what preceded it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><em>Youth</em><ul><li>I think that this tale presents a complementary message about the nature of youth, and how our explorations during youth have some greater meaning, despite the struggles that we might face.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption\">Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption</a><ul><li>The qualification stated as its own claim.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our best friend is ourselves":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One’s best friend is themselves, meaning that one loves oneself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle’s idea of self-love is an important aspect of his philosophy that is rather rare.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>A virtuous man is able to be virtuous to others, so he must be able to be virtuous to himself.</li><li>Importantly, the “self-love” described here is not referring to a type of immoral self-love whereby we attempt to secure for ourselves the maximum amount of money, or honor, or bodily pleasure. <ul><li>This is referring acting virtuously towards oneself.</li><li>This also means that a bad man cannot have self-love in the true sense, and the bad man hates himself.</li></ul></li><li>Since we are our own best friend, it means that we also love ourselves (virtuously) most.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Our ends may be fixed while the means toward them remain chosen":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A teleological account can hold that the end toward which we move is not up to us while the route we take toward it is, which preserves a domain of choice inside a fixed order.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that determinism about ends and freedom about means are compatible, and it is the standard medieval settlement of the problem.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aquinas holds the view in this form, with the rational end given and deliberation operating over the means. The structure recurs wherever a system fixes an objective and leaves implementation open.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a><ul><li>Deliberation is precisely the faculty this account leaves free.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Every action has an end goal\">Every action has an end goal</a><ul><li>The teleological premise the account rests on.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our fate is determined from birth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that our ultimate fates are ones that we do not get to determine. Rather, it is already determined, whether through from theological/divine process or a purely naturalistic one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a central idea in many faiths and belief system, and many works of art, so it is worth exploring.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h5>Oedipus Rex</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I would be blind to misery not to pity my people kneeling at my feet.</span><ul><li>This is a clever bit of irony that the play immediately throws at the audience, since at the end of the play, Oedipus is indeed blind, except he would be the one in misery, not his people.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">He said thieves attacked them—a whole band, not single-handed, cut King Laius down. OEDIPUS: A thief, so daring, so wild, he'd kill a king?</span><ul><li>Interesting how Oedipus mishears what Creon says and instead of referring to thieves, refers to a single, thief. It is as if he knows his fate, that he is that “thief” referred to.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Whoever killed the king may decide to kill me too, with the same violent hand—by avenging Laius I defend myself..</span><ul><li>First of all, this does not come true, at least in <em>Oedipus Rex</em>. Regardless, it is ironic that the “violent hand” that eventually kills Laius’ killer is Oedipus, the same person who states that this would occur.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Whoever he is, a lone man unknown in his crime or one among many, let that man drag out his life in agony, step by painful step—</span><ul><li>Oedipus not only makes reference to a single person to commits the crime, which is incongruous with the story told to him by Creon, he also predicts his future fate, where he is himself cast out of Thebes and forced to “drag out his life in agony, step by painful step–.”</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I curse myself as well . . . if by any chance he proves to be an intimate of our house, here at my hearth, with my full knowledge, may the curse I just called down on him strike me!</span><ul><li>This line perhaps hits too close to home for Oedipus, as he will soon learn.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I hold the throne that he held then, possess his bed and a wife who shares our seed . . . why, our seed might be the same, children born of the same mother might have created blood-bonds between us if his hope of offspring had not met disaster—</span><ul><li>Clearly, this is a prophecy Oedipus tells and which gets fulfilled by the end of the play.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">So ,I will fight for him as if he were my father</span><ul><li>Another bit of irony, as Laius is actually his <em>real</em> father.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Let no crops grow out of the earth for them— shrivel their women, kill their sons,</span><ul><li>This is indeed something that occurs in Antigone, as the sons of Oedipus kill each other, and Antigone herself is killed by Creon. Only Ismene survives.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Laius was killed, they say, by certain travelers. OEDIPUS: I know—but no one can find the murderer.</span><ul><li>Yet another instance of Oedipus (perhaps Freudian slipping) and stating that there was one murderer, instead of many.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Blind who now has eyes, beggar who now is rich, he will grope his way toward a foreign soil, a stick tapping before him step by step.</span><ul><li>Tiresias predicts Oedipus’ future, and states it to him.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">But reject a friend, a kinsman? I would as soon tear out the life within us, priceless life itself.</span><ul><li>This is exactly what Creon does in Antigone, as he kills Antigone. Therefore, it is rather ironic that he is moralizing Oedipus about this now.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Then let him go, even if it does lead to my ruin, my death or my disgrace, driven from Thebes for life.</span><ul><li>In a way, Creon is the one that eventually ruins Oedipus, since he did call Tiresias to meet with Oedipus, who reveals the nature of Oedipus’ relationship with his truth mother and father.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I heard all that and ran. I abandoned Corinth, from that day on I gauged its landfall only by the stars, running, always running toward some place where I would never see the shame of all those oracles come true.</span><ul><li>Oedipus attempts to escape his fate, and not see it come true. Yet, it is simply the case that he cannot see the truth, not that he is particularly able to escape the truth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">MESSENGER: Polybus was nothing to you, that's why, not in blood. OEDIPUS: What are you saying—Polybus was not my father? MESSENGER: No more than I am. He and I are equals.</span><ul><li>A chance meeting with a messenger (in fact, the very one that first brought the child Oedipus to Polybus) revealed the truth of Oedipus’ relationship with Polybus and Merope, showing the inevitability of fate.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">MESSENGER: He called himself a servant of . . . if I remember rightly—Laius. JOCASTA turns sharply.</span><ul><li>This seems to be the moment when Jocasta begins to piece things together, leading to her realization about Oedipus and her suicide.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">but I, I count myself the son of Chance,</span><ul><li>It is interesting how often Oedipus reference chance, here, he references himself as the child of chance. It seems that the very opposite is true, given the miraculous set of events that have occurred so far as to eventually lead Oedipus to find out the true nature of his birth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">My wife— she gave it to you?</span><ul><li>This is probably the deciding moment for Oedipus that made him actually realize the truth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Apollo, friends, Apollo— he ordained my agonies—these, my pains on pains! But the hand that struck my eyes was mine, mine alone—no one else— I did it all myself!</span><ul><li>It seems that although our fates are the determined, the way that we may react to our fates is not. This complicates the debate regarding free will, and how we are able to act in a world that is predetermined, yet still have moral consequences and be responsible for our own actions.</li><li>Clearly, Oedipus has to be responsible for his own actions (that of continuing to search for the truth), yet the truth itself has already been determined.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">It's mine alone, my destiny—I am Oedipus!</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Well let my destiny come and take me on its way!</span><ul><li>Oedipus accepts fate, at last (and with his eyes gone).</li></ul></li></ul><h5>Willie Stark</h5><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 89</p><p>Or it is possible that fellows like Willie Stark are born outside of luck, good or bad, and luck, which is what about makes you and me what we are, doesn’t have anything to do with them, <strong>for they are what they are from the time they first kick in the womb until the end</strong>. And if that is the case, then <strong>their life history is a process of discovering what they really are</strong>, and not, as for you and me, sons of luck, a process of becoming what luck makes us. And if that is the case, then Lucy wasn’t Willie’s luck. Or his unluck either. <strong>She was part of the climate in which the process of discovering the real Willie was taking place.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Willie Stark is said to essentially create his own luck (to the extent that the concept of “luck” doesn’t even meaningfully apply to him) since he goes through life with such brutal force and unmitigated determination.</li><li>All the other characters in the story are essentially part of the setting that revolves around Willie Stark.</li><li>It seems that the main motivation behind Stark’s unfiltered intensity is that his pursuits are, to him at least, almost divine callings. <ul><li>When Stark’s original parochial worldview had been shattered by Tiny Duffy’s offer, he gained almost a fanatical dedication to his life’s goal. </li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One&#x27;s fate is not determined by their birth\">One's fate is not determined by their birth</a><ul><li>Despite the near-identical titles, these are different claims. That note concerns social and familial circumstance; this one concerns metaphysical predetermination.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>It may well be the case that this endless pursuit of the truth is what ultimately determines our fate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a><ul><li>The fate that we are determined to, more often than not, could be one characterized by suffering (ex. Oedipus).</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The blind are able to see what the sighted are not\">The blind are able to see what the sighted are not</a><ul><li>Our fate is determined often because we are not able to see its warning signs, or we don’t even realize that our fate is determined. This could be because we are able to “see” in the normal sense, while to be able to “see” our fate requires a different sense.</li></ul></li></ul>","Our identity is defined by our nature as agents":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whereas Parfit deflates both the metaphysics and the normative importance of the conception of the self, Korsgaard argues that, from a practical standpoint, the second cannot be disregarded.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>She offers a counter-perspective to the argument that Parfit puts forth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Korsgaard performs a partial Moorean shift. She argues that the metaphysics does not matter, and that only the normative implications are relevant.</p><ul><li>In this way, she even accepts the more conservative and deflated version of the metaphysics of the self, that is Parfit's reductivism.</li></ul><p>She argues that the normative claim must be preserve because if it is not, one would not be able to (or have the motivation to) plan for the future, and rational agency would be incoherent. But she replaces this with a normative claim that we ought to only think of ourselves as only one thing (regardless of whether we actually are) since:</p><ul><li>We have only one body, and therefore, we have to collectively act.</li><li>The agent is the source of the actions that they conduct.</li></ul><p>A future person is only \"you\" because you were able to plan for them.</p><ul><li>A radical change in one's identity that does not originate in the agent disrupts one's identity, but this is not the case if one chooses to change themselves.</li></ul><p>Additionally, collective entities can be people.</p><h4>Refutation</h4><p>But Korsgaard's conception of the self has been challenged via empirical evidence.</p><ul><li><strong>Sperry and Gazzaniga split brain research</strong>: they gave an instruction to one of the brain of a split brain subject, and after the action had been performed, and the other hemisphere of the brain is asked why they had did it, the other half of the brain would post hoc come up with a reason for their action.</li></ul><p>The existence of confabulation undermines the assumption of people as rational and future-thinking agents, which means that it undermines the nature of a person defined as such.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self\">Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self</a><ul><li>More about Parfit's metaphysics.</li></ul></li></ul>","Out-migration taxes are a method of preventing tax flight":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While just implementing a higher wealth tax may lead to substantial tax flight, by implementing out-migration taxes, you are able to regain much of the lost tax revenue, since people are much less incentivized to leave the country when there is a out-migration tax.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a strategy that states can use to ensure that they are able to maximize tax revenue even when they raise taxes on the rich.</p><h4>Graphs and Charts</h4><p> As shown in the data, when a wealth tax is combined with an out-migration tax, the net effect is a positive one, since tax revenues rise in general, and so does aggregate output.  This shows the effectiveness of out-migration taxes, since it pushes down the out-migration rate of the most productive business owners down to levels equivalent to the unproductive business owners.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes\">An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes</a><ul><li>Another tax proposal that is related to the idea of taxing the rich. Perhaps that proposal likewise has some solution that mitigates the negative consequences?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a><ul><li>Something that is clearly demonstrate here, as when profits lower (via the out-migration tax), business owners are incentivized to remain in the country.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pareto improvement does not take into account the relative strength of utilities or disutilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of a pareto improvement is that it leads to an utility gain while leading to no disutility as a result, at all. An action is not considered a pareto improvement even if it raises utility by $100 \\ utils$, if the action lowers utility by even $1 \\ utils$.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>I think it is a little strange for the concept of pareto improvement to be defined this way, considering many other concepts in economics have a focus on maximizing overall welfare.</p><h4>References</h4>","Pass-through businesses are taxed once, at the level of the owner’s income":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A pass-through entity is not taxed on its profits directly; the profits are attributed to the owners and taxed as their personal income, so unlike a corporation the business bears no separate layer of tax.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The distinction determines who a change in business taxation actually reaches, since a measure aimed at corporations leaves pass-throughs untouched and a measure aimed at personal income captures them.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The 2017 deduction of twenty per cent of qualified pass-through income was defended on this basis, as an offset to the reduction in the corporate rate that pass-throughs could not benefit from.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same\">Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same</a><ul><li>The incidence principle that determines who bears the difference.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>The schedule that pass-through income is assessed under.</li></ul></li></ul>","Paternalistic decisions are to be guided by an individual’s settled preferences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Paternalistic decisions are to be made so that they align with the settled preferences of the individual. If this is not possible, then the decisions should be made as to maximize the possible primary goods available to the person.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is what Rawls says about when paternalistic decisions must be made for another individual.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Paternalism is only justified given the subject’s failure/absence of reason and will.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a><ul><li>Primary goods are what allow for the development and carrying out of this long-term life plan, which leads to goodness and happiness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them\">The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","People are a product of their times and circumstance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The way people are often is the result of the circumstances either familial or social that they were born into.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves both as an ethical question when it comes to assigning blame to such individuals when they commit wrongs that were normalized in the circumstances of their upbring, and also is a common theme in literature that heightens tragedy.</p><h4>Example</h4><p>In the film <em>Ran</em>, both the aging warlord and his sons are are the product of their times, since they all lived in the Sengoku Jidai period. As a result, the warlord is ruthless and bloodthirsty, and this eventually comes back to bite him when his sons go after him to secure their own power. Another example of this is in <em>All the King’s Men</em>. Stark, although he was originally someone who was innocent and good hearted, developed into the ruthless dictator that he later became through the influence of the world of politics.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some people must deserve something\">Some people must deserve something</a><ul><li>Even though people are shaped by their environment, and therefore lower their level of responsibility, they still must deserve something?</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotelian merit is virtue rather than endowment, and so escapes the natural contingency objection</span><ul><li>Aristotle would argue that despite one’s environment, one’s virtue is one’s own, and thus, a truly virtuous person would be able to overcome any such difficulties.</li></ul></li></ul>","People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People are far more able to be inspired by argumentation that appeals to their inner sense of pathos than to strict, logical argumentation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can profound implications for politics, since this means that people can be easily swayed by demagogues.</p><h4><em>All The King’s Men</em></h4><p>This is something that is specifically noted for Willie Stark, since when he began his speeches, much of it was simply tax figures and numbers that seemed entirely uninspiring to people. However, as his career progressed, he learned to harvest the spirit of the people through fiery polemics and generally invoking pathos far more than logos. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People value present goods more than future goods\">People value present goods more than future goods</a><ul><li>This is something that demagogues often exploit.</li></ul></li></ul>","People are not apportioned a greater share of liberties beyond a certain limit of their capacity for justice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the Range Property Argument. People are not apportioned a greater share of justice based on their capacity for justice beyond a certain limit.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Explains the way that the principles of justice are to be applied.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Once someone meets a certain limit in their capacity for justice, they are no longer awarded further considerations of justice corresponding to above that limit. Of course, it is difficult to pin down the exactly threshold above which one is no longer entitled to greater justice.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>An obvious objection is that it seems arbitrary that people who pass beyond a specific threshold should not receive more justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a><ul><li>The reason for the thing in this note is to ensure this.</li></ul></li></ul>","People often obsess over abstractions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People often obsess over what are ultimately just abstractions or mere ideals.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These abstractions/ideals often serve as significant motivating forces for people’s real life actions.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>People often follow specific dogmatic beliefs/ideologies dominated by abstractions of ideas in the world.<ul><li>This comes in the form of specific political ideologies, i.e., Marxism.</li></ul></li><li>This is also the case in some literary works, such as the Sound and the Fury, where the three Compson brothers are all obsessed with their sister Caddy, even though she has been “exiled” by the family.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Training in the arts should be something that is required of all youth, since without such training, they are aesthetically inexperienced, and thus are both unable to attain full virtue and also fully enjoy the pleasures which result from the arts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle makes this point in the Politics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>According to Aristotle, the citizenry should be trained to play an instrument that is not to difficult (those instruments are reserved for professionals, who are slaves) so that they are able to cultivate their own enjoyment of art. Aristotle specifically argues that people should be educated in music, since music is most able to improve the soul (it arouses strong emotions in the viewer, and is most capable of changing one’s dispositions and thoughts).</p><ul><li>Music also has therapeutic and pleasure-inducing purposes.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul\">Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul</a><ul><li>This is the reason Aristotle choses music as the thing to educate the youth with.</li></ul></li></ul>","People sin so that they may be loved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>People can motivate their sinful actions by the fact that they want to be loved.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is apparent through the actions of many people throughout different novels.</p><h4><em>East Of Eden</em></h4><p>Charles Trask offers this example when he beats up Adam because Cyrus loves Adam more than Charles. Caleb Track offers an example of this in the next generation, since he engages in the bean business, making a profit at the cost of the nation (price gouging), in order to please Adam.</p><ul><li>After this, he takes Aron to see Kate, which shatters Aron’s worldview.</li></ul><h4><em>All The King’s Men</em></h4><p>Stark engages in ruthless political tactics in order to get what he wants, with is praise from the people (but at the same time, it seems that he genuinely also wants to help the people).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>It seems that if utilitarianism view, this view of this note cannot be supported, and is a behavior that should be discouraged.</li></ul></li></ul>","People value present goods more than future goods":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of time preference in economics. This note explores various theories of time preference.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Time preference is one of the most important concepts in economics, since it explains things like interest rates and people consumption behavior.</p><h4>Theories</h4><p>Some theories to explain the existence of time preference includes:</p><ol><li>(Böhm-Bawerk) Current goods are able to provide both for current consumption and future consumption, while future goods are only able to provide for future consumption.</li><li>(Böhm-Bawerk) People have a lack of will, an imaginative mind, or are uncertain of the future, and thus would prefer to consume today than in the future.</li><li>(Böhm-Bawerk) Receiving a consumption good today means freeing up resources for investment in capital goods, which provides long term benefit. Receiving a consumption good in the future does not allow this.</li><li>(Productivity theory) </li></ol><h4>References</h4>","Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that someone’s birth into a situation with any non-negative utility cannot to have been said to have been harmed by society. This is the non-identity problem.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the foremost problems in population ethics.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A slave who is born with barely positive utility does not have the right to claim that they have been harmed by the people who gave birth to them, even if they knew perfectly well the circumstances into which their child would have been born. This is because their utility, despite being low, is still non-negative, which is better than non-existence.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>I think that this paradox is actually quite weak, for two reasons:</p><ol><li>It relies on the assumption of utilitarianism, which has many flaws.</li><li>It seems to exploit our in ability to fully comprehend the idea of “utility.” It seems that having a non-negative utility in life would, almost as a tautology, make that life worth living, even though intuitively, that might not seem to be the case.</li></ol><h4>References</h4>","Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes because they are amphipathic, with their hydrophilic heads facing outside a cell, and the hydrophobic side remaining on the inside.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Phospholipids are an important part of AP Bio, and their purpose is important for cells to function.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Unlike triglycerides, phospholipids only have <strong>two</strong> fatty acid chains.</p><h4>References</h4>","Plate 16 from 'The Disasters of War', or 'They make use of them'":"<h4>Notes</h4><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Plate 9 from &#x27;The Disasters of War&#x27;, or &#x27;They don&#x27;t like to&#x27;\">Plate 9 from 'The Disasters of War', or 'They don't like to'</a><ul><li>From the same series.</li></ul></li></ul>","Plate 9 from 'The Disasters of War', or 'They don't like to'":"<h4>Notes</h4><h4>References</h4>","Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The only desirable things in the world are pleasure and freedom from pain.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core precept of utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is the fundamental principle that all utilitarian theories fundamentally subscribe to. Something is only desirable if it gives one pleasure, saves one from pain, or is a means to achieving those two. Some critics claim that happiness is unattainable. However, it must be said that:</p><ul><li>at least unhappiness is bad, and </li><li>happiness is achievable to some extent, and therefore, the claim that happiness is unattainable is at least exaggerated.</li></ul><p>Also, according to Mill, we do desire things like virtue, though this only means that happiness has something like virtue as a constituent.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth\">Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth</a><ul><li>This point seems to strengthen the un-paradoxical-ness of the non-identity problem.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>In this way, Mill would disagree with Aristotle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life\">Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life</a><ul><li>A related claim about pleasure.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle on Pleasure</span><ul><li>Aristotle’s views on pleasure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>Aristotle has a different conception of the end goal of life, though it is in some ways similar.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a><ul><li>It seems that Rawls’ view regarding the good life is pretty radically different.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pleasure is not a process":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pleasure is not a process, or something that brings us from one state to another.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for understanding the refutations against the claim that pleasure is bad.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>It is complete at every moment that it is experienced. </li><li>A process has steps that it is divided into but you cannot divide pleasure. </li><li>Pleasure does not take time, while processes take time.</li></ul><p>This contributes to the argument that pleasure is not something that is merely for returning to an equilibrium state, or many other arguments that frame pleasure as a process, and therefore, not something that is good or worthy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>This being said, pleasure is NOT the Good.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pleasure is not solely the Good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pleasure is not the sole thing that determines whether something is good or not. Rather, it can enhance/contribute to goodness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that we should still strive for pleasure, although it is not our sole aim in life.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The Good should be something that is determinant and unchanging, while pleasure could be had as a result of different activities and indifferent degrees.</li><li>Additionally, there are something that don’t necessarily bring pleasure that it would be good to have anyways, even if we have pleasure. This means that pleasure cannot be the sole thing that is good.</li><li>Also, not every form of pleasure is desirable.<ul><li>Bodily pleasure in the excess, for instance, is not good.</li><li>Also, Alien pleasure is not good, and are in fact analogous to pain, since they distract us from being able to experience the proper pleasure in a situation.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life\">Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life</a><ul><li>What pleasure is.</li></ul></li></ul>","Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pleasure accompanies and completes and activity when a well-functioning faculty, perceptual or intellectual, is active towards its appropriate object.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the value/meaning of pleasure.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Pleasure perfects an activity, making the activity better and more fully realized than it would otherwise be.  Thus, pleasure perfects life for the individual, since it perfects activities, and life is an activity. <em>When we feel pleasure:</em></p><ul><li>We don’t constantly feel pleasure due to fatigue. </li><li>Or, something, we only felt pleasure at something for its novelty, which, after it wears off, means that we no longer feel the same pleasure.</li></ul><p><em>How pleasure can change:</em></p><ul><li>Different activities produce different pleasures, since they involve different actions.<ul><li>Pleasure intensifies their respective activities, increasing enjoyment and allowing us to improve at it.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>This being said, pleasure is not the sole aim/good of life.</li></ul></li></ul>","Plural voting admits no unique assignment of votes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Schemes that allocate votes in proportion to competence cannot fix the allocation uniquely, since multiplying every allocation by a constant leaves the ordering and the outcome unchanged while altering who is effectively disenfranchised.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an internal defect rather than a moral objection, and it applies to any proposal that weights political power by a measured trait.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The indeterminacy matters because the absolute level does have a consequence at the bottom of the distribution: under some admissible scalings a citizen’s allocation rounds to nothing, so a purely formal rescaling silently converts plural voting into restricted suffrage.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ranked-choice voting system cannot satisfy all four conditions of fairness\">A ranked-choice voting system cannot satisfy all four conditions of fairness</a><ul><li>Another structural impossibility result about voting rules.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law\">Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law</a><ul><li>The objection from the other direction.</li></ul></li></ul>","Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political attitude is one’s opinions on the degree of political change, how much of it should occur, and how fast it should occur. It can determine the direction towards which a society advances.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant for understanding under political system, since understanding political attitude allows for the understanding of why a political shift is occurring when it is occurring.</p><h4>References</h4>","Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political culture represents the norms and values that society holds when they conduct their politics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Political culture may explain the success of some societies compared to others, or, as according to Huntington’s thesis, it may shape the nature of international relations.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are four commonly recognized types of political culture:</p><ol><li><strong>Parochial:</strong> People barely recognize the government, and there is minimal political participation. Common in tribal societies.</li><li><strong>Subject:</strong> People see the government as powerful, but as something that they cannot influence; they simply obey the authority of the government.</li><li><strong>Participant:</strong> People are active, engaged, and believe that they can influence politics.</li><li><strong>Civic Culture:</strong> A mix of the previous; enough participation to ensure accountability, but enough passivity to ensure stability.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture\">Huntington revived and reshaped the importance of political culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics\">Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political attitudes is one&#x27;s opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much\">Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much</a></li></ul>","Political institutions must constantly change in order to meet the needs of the times":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political institutions cannot remain the same across time. They must constantly change and keep up with the times, since morals and cultural values change over time, and institutions should match these.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents a development in the political theory of the Taoists, who now implicitly accept the existence of a state.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>As opposed to Laozi or Zhuangzi, Guo Xiang argues that political institutions ought to exist, but that they should exist while abiding by the nature of the times, and should not remain stuck in the past. This is supported by his theory of <em>wu-wei</em>, or acting in one’s natural abilities and going with the flow.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>The issue, practically, is that once institutions are established by the government, they are extremely difficult to change.</li></ul></li></ul>","Political leaders should not be able to profit from their offices":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political leaders should not be able to profit from their office for a variety of reasons.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that Aristotle makes very clear.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several explanation for the prevention of political leaders from taking advantage of their positions:</p><ul><li>It means the people running of office are not people who are greedy.</li><li>It increases trust in the government.</li><li>It shifts the goals of the people in power to actually running the state well, rather than ruling for personal benefit.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a><ul><li>This means that political leaders would rule in a way that benefits them, not the wider society.</li></ul></li></ul>","Politics (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A foundational work of political philosophy in which he argues that the state exists by nature and that human beings are “political animals” who achieve their fullest flourishing through life in a community governed by law. He studies different forms of government—monarchy, aristocracy, polity, and their corrupt forms—judging them by whether they serve the common good, and he generally favors a mixed constitution that balances the interests of the many and the few. He also examines the household, citizenship, education, property, and the virtues needed for a stable regime, insisting that politics should aim not merely at power or survival but at creating the conditions for ethical life and human happiness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the most important and foundational works of political science.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>Nature, Teleology, and the City</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is an inherent ontological hierarchy in nature\">There is an inherent ontological hierarchy in nature</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are the most ‘political’ animal\">Humans are the most ‘political’ animal</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The city is the fundamental political unit\">The city is the fundamental political unit</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A city is by nature a plurality\">A city is by nature a plurality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of the city is to foster virtue\">The goal of the city is to foster virtue</a></li></ul><h5>Household, Property, and Acquisition</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic conclusions can follow from metaphysical commitments rather than from observation\">Economic conclusions can follow from metaphysical commitments rather than from observation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Commerce is not ‘natural aquisition’\">Commerce is not ‘natural aquisition’</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Property should be privately held, but commonly used\">Property should be privately held, but commonly used</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues\">Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The ills usually blamed on private property come from vice, not from ownership\">The ills usually blamed on private property come from vice, not from ownership</a></li></ul><h5>Justice and Citizenship</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office\">Citizenship is defined by sharing in deliberative and judicial office</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law\">Full political participation requires citizens who are equal in condition, not merely in law</a></li></ul><h5>Regime Taxonomy and Design</h5><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Government</span></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristole's Three Parts of a State</span></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Arguments for kingship must collapse to arguments for aristocracy\">Arguments for kingship must collapse to arguments for aristocracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society\">Having a large middle class is beneficial for a society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a democracy, the adjudicative power and the executive power should not be vested in the same person\">In a democracy, the adjudicative power and the executive power should not be vested in the same person</a></li></ul><h5>Rule of Law</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree\">Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Small transgressions of the rule of law are most dangerous\">Small transgressions of the rule of law are most dangerous</a></li></ul><h5>Regime Preservation and Office-Holding</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A regime should prevent its defining style of organization from being pushed too far\">A regime should prevent its defining style of organization from being pushed too far</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political leaders should not be able to profit from their offices\">Political leaders should not be able to profit from their offices</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Select into office the person with the greatest amount of the trait required and which is the binding constraint\">Select into office the person with the greatest amount of the trait required and which is the binding constraint</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tyranny makes its population uneducated, mistrust one another, and incapable\">A tyranny makes its population uneducated, mistrust one another, and incapable</a></li></ul><h5>The Best Regime and Education</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goods of the soul are most valuable\">Goods of the soul are most valuable</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should be entirely in charge of education\">The state should be entirely in charge of education</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates\">People should be trained in the arts so they have refined aesthetic palates</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul\">Of all the arts, music is most able to improve the character of the soul</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>The ethics is the prerequisite for the Politics, and contains discussion of what makes an individual good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"On Liberty\">On Liberty</a><ul><li>Also discusses the correct role of the state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Ultimately, politics is a debate over axiology and morals, not a debate over which object policy is the “best,” since any such judgements are based fundamentally on moral assumptions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This reframes the nature of politics from one of factual, impassionate debate over what is “objectively” the best policy into a debate about what we ought to value.</p><h4>References</h4>","Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Politics is the virtue of prudence when applied to ruling a state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Politics is what Aristotle considers to be the most important of the activities that an individual ought to engage in.</p><h4>Legislative vs. Political</h4><ul><li><strong>Legislative Science:</strong> deals with the general outlines of running a state well, i.e., writing laws, etc.</li><li><strong>Political Science:</strong> deals with the particulars that arise when running a state, and thus engaging in the action (last step in the deliberative process). </li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Population decline makes markets less competitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Population decline has made markets less competitive and more prone to concentration of power by a few.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Another reason why population decline would be bad for economies beyond the macro effect (such as the effect on the labor market).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The correlation between labor force growth and startup entry rate is 0.74.</li><li>There are less entry into markets, the average firm size has been increasing, and the share of young firms is decreasing.</li><li>Without new firms/ideas, there is less creative destructions, and so firms hold onto products longer and allow markups to drift up.</li><li>The economy also in the process loses from the new variety creation that comes with new entrants into markets.</li><li>This calls for greater anti trust laws in order to prevent the formation of monopolies.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rising populations in the mid-1700s led to social discontent\">Rising populations in the mid-1700s led to social discontent</a><ul><li>At the same time, perhaps it had the opposite effect as described in this note?</li></ul></li></ul>","Positive sodium ions that spread through passive diffusion causes a signal to be transported through a neuron":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, the sodium outside the cell (which is positive and existing in the higher concentration gradient), floods into the cell, causing depolarization. Through this process, they passively diffuse around the point of entry, causing nearby areas to reach the threshold potential, thereby causing depolarization.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the crucial process by which a signal is transported down a neuron.</p><h4>References</h4>","Power corrupts those who possess it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Power is something that begins to change the people who wield the power in negative ways.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme both in political theory and also in literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A main theme of <em>All the King’s Men</em> is the idea that power corrupts individuals, most notably Willie Stark, who, although he began as an individual genuinely trying to help those around him while abiding by moral boundaries, those moral boundaries eventually dissipated, leaving him naked with ambition and a taste for “improving the world” by whatever means necessary. In <em>Ran</em>, the sons of the warlord, especially the first son Taro, is corrupted by the power that he holds as a result of his father’s abdication. As a result, he oversteps his bounds and eventually gets himself killed when he attempts to capture his father at the Third Castle.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a><ul><li>It is in our nature that we are susceptible to such corruption, or is susceptibility to such corruption a result of the evil influence of society?</li></ul></li></ul>","Predicted fiscal surplus lowers the risk of inflation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that when there is predicted to be a fiscal surplus, the risk of inflation is lowered.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explain a mechanism by which inflation is controlled.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This phenomenon is true for several reasons:</p><ol><li>There is overall less spending in the economy. When there is a surplus, it means that either government spending decreased, or tax rates increased. Either way, the total amount of money being spent in an economy is decreased, meaning that inflation is slowed.</li><li>When governments run fiscal surpluses, investors and the public view the government’s currency as backed by real future assets, i.e., that the government won’t have to print future money to pay off its debts. This makes the currency more valuable now, increasing its purchasing value, and driving down inflation. <ol><li>Note: this theory is predicated on the Fiscal Theory of the Price Level.</li></ol></li><li>When the previous theory is applied internationally, it means that the currency rises against other currencies (since it would be viewed as being more stable), meaning that the currency appreciates, and drives down inflation.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a><ul><li>Describes an impact of inflation, if it occurs.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a><ul><li>When there is certainty (in this case, when the government is predicted to run a budget surplus), inflation is lowered, providing support to the claim above.</li></ul></li></ul>","Prediction markets have a tendency for longshot bias":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Prediction markets have a tendency, especially in high-engagement categories like sports and entertainment, to overestimate the odds of low probability events.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This acts as a counter against the existence of an efficient market in predicts markets, and a highlights a potential opportunity for arbitrage.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>All contracts priced below 20 cents underperform their odds, while those above 80 outperform.</li><li>This phenomenon is most observable in specific categories:</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Taker Return</th><th>Maker Return</th><th>Gap</th><th>N trades</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>World Events</td><td>-3.66%</td><td>+3.66%</td><td>7.32 pp</td><td>0.2M</td></tr><tr><td>Media</td><td>-3.64%</td><td>+3.64%</td><td>7.28 pp</td><td>0.6M</td></tr><tr><td>Entertainment</td><td>-2.40%</td><td>+2.40%</td><td>4.79 pp</td><td>1.5M</td></tr><tr><td>Crypto</td><td>-1.34%</td><td>+1.34%</td><td>2.69 pp</td><td>6.7M</td></tr><tr><td>Weather</td><td>-1.29%</td><td>+1.29%</td><td>2.57 pp</td><td>4.4M</td></tr><tr><td>Sports</td><td>-1.11%</td><td>+1.12%</td><td>2.23 pp</td><td>43.6M</td></tr><tr><td>Politics</td><td>-0.51%</td><td>+0.51%</td><td>1.02 pp</td><td>4.9M</td></tr><tr><td>Finance</td><td>-0.08%</td><td>+0.08%</td><td>0.17 pp</td><td>4.4M</td></tr></tbody></table><ul><li>There is a consistent advantage for NO contracts by makers (people who set limit deals and provide liquidity for the market).</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">NO contracts are better than YES contracts</p><p>Despite the market being zero-sum, dollar-weighted returns are -1.02% for YES buyers compared to +0.83% for NO buyers, a 1.85 percentage point gap driven by the overpricing of YES contracts.</p></blockquote><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Efficient Market Hypothesis cannot account for market anomalies\">The Efficient Market Hypothesis cannot account for market anomalies</a><ul><li>This note describes something that the EMH cannot account for, even outside of market anomalies.</li></ul></li></ul>","Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are main three problems with price controls: supply shortages, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>These represents the large issues that manifest when price controls are implemented.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Price controls cause:</p><ol><li>Supply shortages.</li><li>Misallocation of resources, since people have to spend time looking for alternatives, waiting in line to get something that they need, and the people that ultimately get something are often not the ones that value the thing the most.</li><li>Rent-seeking, since people will engage in activities such as attempting to bribe the people who are selling the good.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people&#x27;s misunderstanding of what causes the issue\">The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people's misunderstanding of what causes the issue</a><ul><li>Rather than supporting pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis, people believe that rent control is the way to solve the problem, while there are many problems associated with price controls.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a><ul><li>When there are price controls, the market is not able to operate efficiently and accomplish the things it would be able to accomplish otherwise, such as the things listed above.</li></ul></li></ul>","Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The originally monetarist thesis (now largely accepted in the mainstream) that the amount of money in an economy is the driver of inflation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core concept that underlies Monetarist thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The effect can be described by the following equation: $$ MV=PY $$ or, equivalently: $$ \\Delta \\% M + \\Delta \\% V = \\Delta \\% P + \\Delta \\% Y $$ , where $M$ is the money supply, $V$ is the velocity of money, $P$ is the price level in the economy, and $Y$ is the real GDP. Given this, and holding $Y$ and $V$ constant, we can see that a change in $M$ corresponds to a commensurate change in $P$.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>An alternative theory to explain inflation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a><ul><li>The effect of a change in price level.</li></ul></li></ul>","Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is the central idea of the Fiscal Theory of Price Level (FTPL), which emphasizes fiscal policy’s role in generating inflation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presenting a contrasting theory to the Monetarist theory of inflation which attributes inflation solely to the amount of money in the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The FTPL is essentially represented by the following equation: $$ \\frac{B_{t}}{P_{t}}=Et \\sum (\\frac{S_{t+j}}{(1+r)^j}) $$ In other words, $$\\frac{\\text{Government Debt}}{\\text{Price Level}} = \\text{Expected Future Surpluses}$$ This means that a rise in the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus leads to a rise in the price level. The reason that this relation is true because when there is a positive difference between debt and EFS, the debt is not fully backed up by tax dollars, so people will spend more in order to convert their money into real goods before the value realizes its lack of backing, causing demand to rise and the price level to also rise. The opposite is also true. When there is greater EFS, people realize that there is more surplus than the amount of debt, they will spend less to prepare for the future taxes that will generate those surpluses, causing demand to drop and the price level to fall.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy\">Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy</a><ul><li>The competing Monetarist idea that the amount of money present in the economy is the sole determinant of the price level in the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Prices give us information about the relative scarcities of various goods, allowing for resource allocation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the most important things for an individual to understand is the relative scarcities of the goods that he is seeking. Knowing this, he is able to choose the optimal bundle of goods which maximize its utility or minimizes his average total cost.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental purpose of the price system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This feature of the price system is what makes it valuable. No other system is able to propagate information about the availability of various goods or capital as quickly and efficiently as the price system.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>The Use of Knowledge in Society</em>, Hayek</p><p>The whole acts as one market, not because any of its members survey the whole field, but because <strong>their limited individual fields of vision sufficiently overlap so that through many intermediaries the relevant information is communicated to all</strong>. The mere fact that there is one price for any commodity—or rather that local prices are connected in a manner determined by the cost of transport, etc.—brings about the solution which (it is just conceptually possible) might have been arrived at by one single mind possessing all the information which is in fact dispersed among all the people involved in the process.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking\">Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking</a><ul><li>This is the reason why when the price signal is distorted, that misallocation of resources occur.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>Socialism seeks to centralize the power of the market in a central planner, but they are unable to aggregate all the information in a market.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>Mises states that it is not even that a socialist state can’t aggregate the information, but that the information is produced by the market. Without the market, there is no information.</li></ul></li></ul>","Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Private property specifically allows for the preservation of two virtues: moderation (in women), and liberality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the arguments that Aristotle makes in favor of private property.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Private property allows for the preservation of moderation since</p><ul><li>you are especially mindful of utilizing things which are yours, and thus you will not over-indulge, and </li><li>only when there is private property can there be liberality</li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems to be that this again is specific to Aristotle’s set of ethics. If it’s the case that these two moral characteristics are merely instrumental or are morally irrelevant, then this argument seems to hold very little weight.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>This is what the moderation point produces on an macro-scale?</li></ul></li></ul>","Private property facilitates trade":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Without the concept of private property, no trade would happen. Trade is the fundamental driver of capitalism, as it is the prerequisite for the existence of markets.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Private property is the fundamental basis upon which capitalism is based.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li></ul>","Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This describes Mises’ Economic Calculation Problem, which states that without a market price (and thus private property) for capital goods, rational economic planning is impossible, and resources will not be optimally allocated.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most fundamental arguments against planned economic systems, since they face this problem of being unable to achieve allocative efficiency.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Prices reflect the scarcity of a resource, reflect demand for a product, and allow for the calculation of profit and loss. All these things allow people to understand if resources are being effectively allocated, and prevent waste. Prices allow you to compare things that are normally incomparable (3 hours of labor vs. 1 ton of steel), and send signals through markets about what people actually want. Prices also allow for the calculation of profit and loss, which can be used to determine if something creates or destroys value.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a><ul><li>This relates to the ideas about markets that are described in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>Another idea that relates to the concepts discussed herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>Without private property, the state has to perform economic calculation, which it cannot.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Securing a good may increase its value, but security does not necessarily mean greater value\">Securing a good may increase its value, but security does not necessarily mean greater value</a><ul><li>This describes some particularities with private property.</li></ul></li></ul>","Producers capture almost none of the surplus generated through innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Producers capture very little of the surplus that is generated through innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the difficulty of innovation in a market capitalist system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Only about 2.2% of total social surplus is captured by innovators.</li><li>The rest of the 98% actually goes to the consumer in the form of lower prices, better products, and higher standards of living.</li><li>Markets typically overestimate Schumpeterian profits and underestimate the decay of such profit.<ul><li>Technology has increased the decay speed, since information is difficult to create, but easy to re-create.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Property rights should not be too powerful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If property rights were too powerful, then it may actually reduce the amount of development that could be done and thus lower the total level of innovation and social welfare.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues in favor of people not advocating for property rights to be made to powerful.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Estate laws in England before the Glorious Revolution meant that hereditary holdings were not able to be cultivated or split up very easily for a fear that allowing such things would lead to the gradual destruction of estates as they are simply split among the descendants or are simply squandered by one generation of the family, leaving nothing to a future generation.</li><li>Zoning laws which are too strict and which allow local homeowners to make radical decisions about the composition of their neighborhoods restricts development and thus leads to inefficiency. </li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Property should be privately held, but commonly used":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Property should be privately held, since doing so establishes particular virtues that would otherwise simply be missing. However, people should continue to act with the virtue of liberality and allow their property to be used by others most of the time.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>While Aristotle completely rejects the idea that property should be coercively commonly distributed, he does believe that people should share their property with others under most circumstances, out of their own virtue.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues\">Private property allows for the preservation of specific virtues</a><ul><li>This is the reason that property is kept private.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>Modern economics backs up Aristotle’s proposition that property should be held privately.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property facilitates trade\">Private property facilitates trade</a><ul><li>It seems that Aristotle’s solution would prevent trade from occurring in the traditional sense, since people could just borrow from others.</li></ul></li></ul>","Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem since our globe is in fact a commons, and while it may cost a nation a significant amount in order to mitigate an existential risk, the benefits accrue to all other members of the planet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the impossibility of solving the issue of existential risks without some strong form of global cooperation in order to eliminate free-riders.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Another way to think about this is that there is an over-supply of risk, since the benefit of the risk can be localized, but the cost cannot (it creates a negative externality). When it comes to existential risks, there is also the added temporal dimension, since most of the effects of the risks concerns our descendants, not us right now. Therefore, one can consider it actually an <em>intergenerational global public good</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation\">A tragedy of the commons cannot be solved with moral suasion or condemnation</a><ul><li>This is perhaps important when considering solutions to the problem.</li></ul></li></ul>","Psychological continuity exists on a continuum":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the continuity of existence is something that exists on a spectrum. As we collect new experience and loss old experience, we thus gradually become more distant from our original self.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that as people's acquired stimulus changes, we slowly become different people, and this solves the problem of \"when we become a different\" person after some period of time.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Parfit argues that:<ul><li>Personal identity isn‘t what we should care about from a self-interested perspective.</li><li>We should care about there existing a future being psychologically connected with us in the right way, in that the future self have some of the same values, personality, beliefs, and intentions.<ul><li>What matters comes in degrees: whether someone is psychologically connected to you in this way is a matter of degree.<ul><li>This psychological distance is the result of the build up of new experiences (or loss) not time.</li></ul></li><li>You at 15 and 70 are more different than between you at 15 and 30.<ul><li>We can‘t have the conscience to look forwards to the future, because you will not be the same person.</li></ul></li><li>An implication of this view is that you have a moral responsibility for your future self.</li></ul></li><li>A good analogy for this can be found in Sorites Paradox.<ul><li>It is a gradation of you. Its like instead of define a “pile” you only define “x degree of a pile”. This is the reduction. There is no true “pile”.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Pure procedural justice is the most practical form of justice to evaluate the basic structure with":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pure procedural justice, since it does not involve judging the result against an independent criteria. Doing this would be impractical since the basic structure would produce too many individual distributions and transactions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason that Rawls adopts pure procedural justice in analyzing the basic structure.</p><h4>References</h4>","Pure time preference is irrational":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of pure time preference, or that we value something less merely because it is in the future (not taking account things such as the increased risk of the future or other factors like that) is irrational.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important for specific argument in population ethics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that we could value all period of our life the same. There is no reason to do otherwise. This also implies that we should not discount future civilizations, and thus we should not over consume now and doom future generations.</p><ul><li>But it seems that many of the disregarded factors do play a role here. For instance, it seems likely that the longer in the future, the likelier that humanity will go extinct. Therefore, it seems makes sense to discount.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Quantitative easing is not the same thing as open market operations":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Quantitative easing is a far more radical form of open market operations, as it involves the large scale purchase of long-term government treasuries. This is as opposed to open market operations, which sees the purchase of short-erm government treasures.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two are different, since while the first is used commonly to make large adjustments to macroeconomy, usually in the aftermath of a major recessions, the latter is used commonly to make small changes to the money supply.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li></ul>","Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Descartes’ famous saying of <em>cognito ergo sum</em> (\"I think, therefore I am\") seem to be at the end of an intense and highly skeptical process of deconstructing the accepted epistemology.  However, at the end of this process, Descartes constructs a new theory upon the old one, this time, based on the divine.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This process of first deconstructing our epistemology, and then reconstructing it upon the divine is the core project of Descartes’ <em>Meditations on First Philosophy</em>.</p><h4>References</h4>","Ran":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows the aging warlord Hidetora Ichimonji, who divides his kingdom among his three sons, expecting a peaceful retirement. Instead, his sons betray and fight one another for power, driving Hidetora into madness and exile. As the kingdom collapses through violence, treachery, and revenge, the film portrays the destructive consequences of pride, ambition, and familial disloyalty.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A film that addresses themes regarding the nature of humanity, and the ability for power to corrupt.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are a product of their times and circumstance\">People are a product of their times and circumstance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Power corrupts those who possess it\">Power corrupts those who possess it</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Randomization allows for causation to be determined":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Only when an experiment is randomized can we draw conclusions related to causation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant concept from statistics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Randomization reduces bias and confounding variables, allowing for causation to be determined.</p>","Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls’ claim that the agents in the original position would be risk-averse, since they don’t know whether their future selves would want the primary goods, or the amount that they would want. Therefore, they would be conservative and ensure that everyone has a sufficient amount of primary goods rather than being risk-neutral and risk that some people don’t have access to primary goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the nature/behavior of rational agents from behind the veil of ignorance.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This would essentially mean the adoption of a maximin strategy. Rawls argues that this is rational since we do not know the probability distribution of our future circumstances, so we can’t adopt a utilitarian view towards this can calculate the expected value. Also, only the minimum amount of primary goods is required to satisfy the rational agent’s rational, long-term plan.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>The result of accepting this is that people are risk-averse, it seems.</li></ul></li></ul>","Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning, whereas most models use SFT":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning to optimize the reasoning process, in addition to being first trained using supervised pretraining. On the other hand, normal models also first train with the supervised pretraining, then they are  subject to reinforcement learning, but simply to optimize their outputs.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This elucidates the difference between reasoning and normal models.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Reasoning LLMs, in their post-training phase, focus on optimizing their reasoning process through RLHF (reinforcement learning from human feedback) or RLAIF (reinforcement learning from AI feedback). On the other hand, normal models will simply have their outputs optimized with the same tools.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Clear sight of what one has done wrong is a cognitive achievement rather than a moral one, and treating the recognition as though it discharged the failure understates what the failure cost.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This qualifies the common literary claim that dying clarifies a life, since clarity about a wasted life is compatible with the life having been wasted.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The ending of <em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em> can be read either way. The flight to the summit is unitalicised, which invites reading it as genuine deliverance; but Hemingway never revises Harry’s judgment of himself, and a redemption granted for the recognition alone would be too cheap for the life that preceded it. The ironic reading is the stronger one, and the text sustains both.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only when moribund do we have a clear perspective on our own life\">Only when moribund do we have a clear perspective on our own life</a><ul><li>The claim this qualifies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Small neglected problems compound into the shape of a whole life\">Small neglected problems compound into the shape of a whole life</a><ul><li>Why the recognition arrives too late to do any work.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle’s defines rectifictory justice as justice that undos the effects of a transactions (without regard to the relative merit of the parties involved).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is another type of justice that Aristotle defines, and a major type within the class of particular justices.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are further variations of rectifictory justice:</p><ul><li><strong>Voluntary</strong>: when people under dealing of their own volition: sale, loan, fire, deposit, etc. Rectifictory Justice here means cancelling unfair gain and loss. </li><li><strong>Involuntary</strong>: when at least one party is not consenting, since they are being wronged: theft, assault, fraud, etc. In such a case, the involuntary party is not unjust, and only the party perpetrating the involuntary action is unjust.<ul><li><strong>Clandestine</strong>: when wrongs are done covertly: theft, adultery, poisoning, etc.</li><li><strong>Violent</strong>: when wrongs are done openly by force: assault, imprisonment, murder, etc.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit</a><ul><li>This is another type of particular justice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction\">There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction</a><ul><li>Rectifictory justice deals with how to rectify bad transactions.</li></ul></li></ul>","Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Reductivism regarding person identity means that the self is merely a bundle of experience, accumulated over time.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This view of personal identity improves upon the egoist theory of the self in Parfit‘s view.</p><h4>Reductivism</h4><p>This is Derek Parfit‘s view of personal identity.</p><ul><li>In this view, the self is a bundle of experiences, where the self is determined by psychological connectedness.<ul><li>Psychological connection is continuity of experience. This psychological connection is the causal connection is the glue that binds everything together? <ul><li>Ex. The sight of a chair causes a belief in the existence of the chair.</li></ul></li><li>Our life is a series of bundles of mental states at any moment (your beliefs, our desires, your thoughts, etc.)</li></ul></li></ul><p>Rather than claiming the existence of an egoist self, Parfit argues that the self is defined by the accumulation of experience in an individual over time.</p><ul><li>The ego view states that there is a special \"me-ness\" to someone.</li><li>Time is not the best proxy for this, since there could be really radical change in a very short period of time.</li></ul><p>Parfit argues that personal identity is not what we should care about from a self-interested perspective. All that matters is continuity of existence.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Psychological continuity exists on a continuum\">Psychological continuity exists on a continuum</a><ul><li>Parfit elaborates this thing that we argues we should actually care about.</li></ul></li></ul>","Regulation in favor of workers is more often just than regulation in favor of employers":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because employers are fewer, better organised and under a standing inducement to hold wages down, rules that constrain them correct an asymmetry, whereas rules that assist them compound one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This asymmetry gives a principled way to distinguish two kinds of intervention that a blanket presumption against regulation treats identically.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Smith states it directly: when the regulation is in support of the workman it is always just and equitable, but it is sometimes otherwise when in favour of the masters, and he describes a tacit and constant combination among employers to keep wages low.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The labor market is not perfectly competitive\">The labor market is not perfectly competitive</a><ul><li>The modern statement of the asymmetry this rests on.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The overwhelming power of labor unions in the post-war consensus harmed the UK economy\">The overwhelming power of labor unions in the post-war consensus harmed the UK economy</a><ul><li>The case where the asymmetry runs the other way.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay\">Compensating differentials partly explain why jobs have different pay</a><ul><li>The competitive model that the asymmetry departs from.</li></ul></li></ul>","Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In many different religions/belief systems, the idea of reincarnation is an important facet.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows a connection between many different belief system in the world, helping us find similarities between otherwise disparate ideas.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In Hinduism, As the human soul migrated from body to body across lifetimes, <em>karma</em>, depending on the \"pureness\" of one's actions, may allow oneself to be reborn to a higher caste, eventually, to achieve Brahman (see The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman\">The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman</a></li></ul>","Religion is compatible with science":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that religion/faith is able to be reconciled with science, despite the typically dogmatic approach that faith holds towards belief.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a project that has been attempted by many.</p><h4><em>The Brothers Karamazov</em></h4><p>This is something that Dostoevsky attempts to accomplish in <em>TBK</em>. He attempts to existentialize religion to make it both not coerce belief (the existence of miracles, in his view, would coerce belief), but ensure that the individual can still believe. He does this through the extraction of the existential core of Christian rituals. Some examples of the rituals that he is able to existentialize include:</p><ul><li><strong>Confession</strong>: public/communal confession of sin to all of the world, since one is responsible for everything.<ul><li>The moral “butterfly effect.”</li></ul></li><li><strong>The incarnation</strong>: someone receives a divine spirit when they embrace agape and the spirit of loving all that is around them.</li><li><strong>Ordination of priests</strong>: God enters first Markel, then Zossima, then Alyosha.</li><li><strong>The crucifixion</strong>: when people experience suffering, they can<ul><li>“lacerate” themselves, they “crucify” themselves to feel a sense of moral superiority to others. But is equivalent to being crucified, and staying that way.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Immortality</strong>: ?</li><li><strong>Angels</strong>: someone who is able to, with agape, bring out the good in others.<ul><li>Ex. Alyosha to Grushenka.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Devils</strong>: someone who desires to bring out the worst in others.<ul><li>Ex. Rakitin when he tried to make Alyosha sin.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Miracles</strong>: when something unexpected situation is created through love.<ul><li>Ex. When Alyosha saves Grushenka.</li></ul></li><li>After Zossima dies, the book gives specific, scientific reason for why the body stinks. This ruined the miracle of the death of Zossima.<ul><li>Tangentially, this also leads to a lot of disbelief, as people prove right the thesis of the GI, that they are weak and need guidance and miracles for faith.</li></ul></li><li>But eventually Alyosha is able to overcome this through a realization of his own, something that comes internally.</li></ul><p>Religion is not observed through miracles that break the laws of physics; rather, it is observed through human interconnection.</p><h4>References</h4>","Remedies to meritocracy either widen access to the competition or reduce what the competition allocates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Proposals for repairing meritocratic societies fall into two families: those that make the contest fairer by removing barriers to entry, and those that make the contest matter less by taking goods out of its reach.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The division is useful because the two families have opposite implications for whether meritocracy is worth keeping, and proposals are often advanced without saying which family they belong to.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Widening access includes removing barriers to higher education and withdrawing the privileges of incumbent institutions. Shrinking the stakes includes distributing non-market goods on non-meritocratic criteria and moving goods out of the market altogether. Only the first family is a defence of meritocracy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Equality of opportunity has a formal and a substantive sense, and meritocracy requires both\">Equality of opportunity has a formal and a substantive sense, and meritocracy requires both</a><ul><li>What the first family of remedies is trying to secure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly\">Belief in meritocracy makes people behave more unfairly</a><ul><li>The finding that motivates the second family.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rentier states typically have high inequality and low political participation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rentier states, which are states that rely primarily on income from natural resources, tend to have high inequality and low political participation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains crucial properties that rentier states typically have, which allow us to analyze such states with the appropriate context.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Since the resource wealth of natural resources tend to be concentrated (there are high fixed costs to extracting natural resources), the wealth tends to flow towards only the upper class.</li><li>Since there is a lot of wealth coming through with the natural resources, the state does not have a reason to raise taxes. This decreases the incentives for people to participate politically, since taxation is typically a very politically salient issue.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics\">Political culture defines the norms by which a society conducts politics</a><ul><li>A rentier state would have a political culture that does not stress political engagement, since people don’t really have an incentive to do so.</li></ul></li></ul>","Retributive punishment is never justified":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that retribution punishments are never justified, which is often justified by invoking several theses (together):</p><ol><li>determinism (or some form of compatibilism) is true such that we are not at least fully responsible for our actions, </li><li>suffering is never intrinsically good, </li><li>we can punish the crime-doer not directly through retributive punishment, and </li><li>punishment’s main goal should be deterrence and prevention.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant perspective on criminal justice that is shared by philosophers such as Scanlon.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Scanlon argues that while we cannot wish for an offender to suffer, we are justified in being indifferent to their misfortune. While theses responses may cause the offender to suffer, we are justified in doing this since the offender has already broken the contract of interpersonal relations (i.e., indifferent to the interests of others).</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Nagel notes that there is an intuitive unappealingness with the fact that we cannot believe in the fact that the wicked should suffer.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive\">Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive</a><ul><li>This is one of the parts of the argument presented here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Returning unspent medical funds to the patient restores price sensitivity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If a portion of the deductible is placed in an account that the patient may keep whatever they do not spend, then the patient bears the marginal cost of routine care and has a reason to compare prices, without losing protection against catastrophic expense.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the standard design for reintroducing a price-sensitive buyer into a third-party payment system, and it is the constructive half of the argument against pre-paid insurance.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying\">Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying</a><ul><li>The problem this design addresses.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Prices give us information about the relative scarcities of various goods, allowing for resource allocation\">Prices give us information about the relative scarcities of various goods, allowing for resource allocation</a><ul><li>What is lost when no participant faces the price, and what the account restores.</li></ul></li></ul>","Returns to intelligence are diminishing, though we still have far to go before hitting the asymptote":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Returns to intelligence are diminishing, though it seems that the gains of extra intelligence remains high for now.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines the potential benefits that future AI development could bring for us, while at the same time acknowledging the potential limits.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>That returns to intelligence are diminishing can be explained via a few possible avenues:</p><ul><li>That there is a limited amount of knowledge in existence, and therefore, it becomes harder for the same amount of intelligence to be effective.</li><li>That due to chaos, discovering the marginal piece of knowledge becomes more and more difficult the more we learn.</li></ul><p>There are three things that more intelligence, specifically from AI, could continue to do for us, however:</p><ol><li>Artificial intelligences can be replicated easily, and could potentially be more powerful than human intelligences. Additionally, they are divisible and allow for more precise allocations of compute.</li><li>Artificial intelligences could potentially be better at capturing diffused tacit knowledge (little pieces of knowledge that is distrusted across a process/supply chain), since they are able to centrally process a far larger amount of data, and thus they would be able to optimize many production processes.</li><li>Artificial intelligences could potentially be able to formulate the existence of “fuzzy/cloud laws” whereby the relationship between factors cannot not specifically be described by one deterministic model, necessitating more complex means of modeling in order to see the relationship between multiple variables. AIs have this capacity.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI represents a new epistemological category\">AI represents a new epistemological category</a><ul><li>This is the reason that AI represents such a huge jump in capabilities in so many fields, including in the processes described here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets\">AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets</a><ul><li>Related to its ability to process large amounts of information.</li></ul></li></ul>","Rising populations in the mid-1700s led to social discontent":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the increasing prevalent of the potato as a food, and the ease with which it can be grown, population boomed beginning during the mid-1700s, leading to social discontent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This social discontent coincided with Enlightenment ideas, and thus produced solutions and reform movements.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>This had many effects during the century:<ul><li>Aristocrats could not find posts for all of their sons (leading to their discontent)</li><li>Peasants couldn’t leave their land to all their children (creating a class of landless peasants)</li><li>This surfeit of population would fuel the armies during the French Revolutionary Wars.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Rituals help us manage scarcity, process emotions, form group bonds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rituals are things that help us to maintain order in the presence of scarce resources (for instance, lining up in a queue helps us orderly manage our scarce resource of time), manage our emotions (as a funeral does when a death occurs), and helps us become human and form group bonds.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes some of the importance that rituals provide for humans.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Xunzi specifically argues that many of the rituals that are ostensibly without use (dancing a rain dance in order to bring about rain) actually serve a purpose, specifically as it is highlighted here.</p><h4>References</h4>","Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rule of law is better than rule by decree since laws are cold and objective, meaning that it is incorruptible, unlike men’s souls.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s argument for the rule of law.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a><ul><li>This seems to be the beginning of the traditional of constitutional liberalism in Western government.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a><ul><li>The rule of law can exist independently from democracy, and this was already identified even in Aristotle’s works.</li></ul></li></ul>","SNAP does not cause economic growth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>SNAP does not boost GDP as although it gives people more money to spend, this can, at best, lead to a temporary spike in GDP, but it cannot lead to sustained growth of the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fallacious argument that many discuss regarding the efficacy of food stamps.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>SNAP redistributes wealth from the relative more wealth to the less wealthy, and since poorer people have a higher MPC, this would naturally lead to a temporary boost in GDP. However, a dollar spent is a dollar not saved, and, without the savings that would otherwise have existed, long-term economic growth is stunted.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"SNAP does not drive up food prices\">SNAP does not drive up food prices</a></li></ul>","SNAP does not drive up food prices":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>SNAP does not raise food prices, as some on the right have claimed. While typically a subsidized good would lead to increased demand for the product, and thus drive up the cost, this is not the case for SNAP as a result of the way the program is structured.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a misconception that many have who argued against the efficacy of food stamps.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While typically, subsidies lead to higher prices since they raise demand for something, this is not the case of SNAP. Or, at least, SNAP does not have an effect on the economy that a normal transfer payment would not.  This is because SNAP essentially makes food free to purchase up to $300. So, although households may spend some of the surplus from the $300 on further food items, it may also be spent on other items. In the end, this means that food stamps simply have an effect on the economy that any transfer payment would.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"SNAP does not cause economic growth\">SNAP does not cause economic growth</a></li></ul>","Scope creep makes government services ineffective and inefficient":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Scope creep, or the gradual expansion of the goals of a project beyond its original scope, usually wrecks the efficiency and effectiveness of government services.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an argument for the privatization of certain government projects, or at least the checking of certain government projects to ensure that they actually carry our their original responsibilities rather than have the problem of scope creep.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>One major example of this is public transportation, especially in the US. While public transportation is intended to mitigate the fact that some low-income households do not own vehicles, due to scope creep, this does not often occur.</p><ul><li>The introduction of electric buses increases the cost of running the vehicles, meaning that less vehicles can be deployed, lowering ridership.<ul><li>The ironic thing is that it is very likely that emissions would have been lower if more investments had just been made such that routes were improved and ride frequencies were improved. </li></ul></li><li>Public transportation is extremely important for people to be able to have access to better schools and for them to be able to access better jobs.</li></ul><p>Ultimately, if the public transportation service just sought to provide the best service possible, they would have been able to tackle their main goal of increasing ridership far more successfully. However, due to scope creep, they move further away from achieving their original objectives.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“American transit is bad because of scope creep”</p><p>Every dollar spent complying with Buy America \\[(a law that requires the purchasing of American made products)] or staging community meetings is a dollar not spent <a href=\"https://worksinprogress.co/issue/the-united-states-needs-fewer-bus-stops/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">improving</a> frequency or coverage. Assuming the median cost per hour for operating a bus in the US is <a href=\"https://www.transit.dot.gov/ntd/data-product/ts21-service-data-and-operating-expenses-time-series-mode-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">$131</a>, and that the median US bus carries 14.3 passengers per hour, <strong>the money for electrifying one bus could fund roughly 2,300 additional service hours, carrying at least 30,000 additional riders.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>Related idea about the entrenched nature of the government and why it is difficult to solve the problem of scope creep once it happens.</li></ul></li></ul>","Sea power translates into national power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>At core of Mahanian naval strategy is that naval power translates into national power, i.e., who controls the seas is able to exert heavy influence on other nations. This dictates, then that nations strive to lock the opposing side in their ports, without allowing them to have any chance at control of the sea, which then naturally leads to the idea of “decisive battle” to decide control of the sea.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Mahanian naval warfare influenced many people, including Teddy Roosevelt, Wilhelm II, and the Japanese military. The core of theory originates with the idea of naval control being the prerequisite to national power.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Naval power is the primary determinant for national power as control of the seas allows for isolation of the enemy from their allies, from supplies, and from strategic maneuvers involving the sea. This idea of the preeminence of naval strategy naturally leads to Mahan’s concept of “decisive battle,” where through a single decisive battle, control of the sea, and therefore and in large, the nature of the war, would be decided.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world\">Control of the Eurasian rimland dictates control of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a></li></ul>","Secular philosophies of history can only address humans as collectives, and not individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the failures of the many secular philosophies of history that have existed is that they fail to address the individual specifically; rather, they are often just addressed towards “humanity” in general.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the critiques the Lowith levels at the secular philosophies of history (Marx, Comte, etc.).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, I think that this critique is relevant when comparing these other secular philosophies of history with Christianity, since salvation in Christianity refers uniquely to the individual soul. Other secular philosophies of history, such as the positivist philosophy of Comte or the economic materialist philosophy of Marx, must take as their subject either \"humanity\" or the \"Occident\" in the case of the former, and the \"proletariat\" or the \"bourgeoisie\" in the case of the latter. But none of these exists except in individual men and women, and so none of them can be the bearer of a destiny. These philosophies therefore adopt the universal element of the Christian understanding of history while eliminating the concern with persons that alone made that universality intelligible.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a><ul><li>This is referenced in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a><ul><li>This idea of Comte is also referenced in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Securing a good may increase its value, but security does not necessarily mean greater value":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Securing a good may increase its value (i.e., gold in a vault is more valuable than gold that is just lying in the open), but security does not necessarily mean value will be enhanced. Generally, the greater the inherent value of the good, the greater security may enhance its value.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the effect that security may have on a good, namely that given the good is inherently valuable, security would increase its value.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Given a good that is inherently value-less, like dirt, increased security does not mean that the value of the dirt is greater. One would not prefer dirt that is locked in an ultra secure vault compared to dirt that is scattered randomly on the ground.</p><h4>References</h4>","Select into office the person with the greatest amount of the trait required and which is the binding constraint":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When it comes to deciding how to pick people to fill out a government position, it should be based on selecting the person who has the greatest amount of some skill relevant to the position and which represents a binding constraint.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is helpful heuristic that Aristotle provides for guiding people to put the best people into office.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle gives the example that, for instance, if we are selecting someone to be the manager of the treasury, we should select the person with the most honesty, since honesty is the binding constraint. There are other things required for the job, such has knowledge of finance or general wisdom, but the binding constraint is the most important factor, since its the rarest.</p><h4>References</h4>","Selective incentives are excludable benefits or sanctions that groups use to overcome the free-rider problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Selective incentives are benefits given out on an individual/household level as to incentivize people to contribute to some cause, or as to discourage people from not participating.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the existence and strength of many different political interest groups. Additionally, it solves the free-rider problem in large groups.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Labor unions: While all workers benefit from the wage negotiations carried out by labor unions, those who join the union get additional benefits, such as union-only legal support or strike pay.</li><li>Professional associations: While such organizations create public good as they enforce stronger profession-wide regulations, members are the only ones that are able to benefit from their networking events and get access to the services that the association offers at a discounted rate.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Self-respect is the most important primary good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls argues that the most important primary good is self-respect. Self-respect means that one is confident that one’s conception of the good is worth carrying out, and that one has the capability to carry out that rational plan.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Self-respect is an important aspect of Justice as Fairness. Since it is the most important primary good, our political systems should be designed to promote it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Self-respect has two components:</p><ol><li>One’s own conviction that his conception of the good is worth carrying out.</li><li>A confidence in one’s ability to fulfill one’s intentions.</li></ol><p>The absolute achievement of something that is spelled out in our rational plans is unimportant, with the more important thing being our sense of worth as the actor of the achievement.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them\">The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them</a><ul><li>So then it is most important to index the level of self-respect.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony\">Justice as fairness fosters self-respect and societal harmony</a><ul><li>Gives the mechanism by which a well-ordered society produces the self-respect that this note claims is most important.</li></ul></li></ul>","Shame can be natural or moral":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls identifies two types of shame: moral and natural.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the two types of shame that is possible for someone to have, and their different implications.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Moral shame arises from a failure to manifest or betrayal of some virtues that is explicated in one’s long-term rational plan of life. Natural shame comes from feeling deficient relative to one’s own ideals of excellence.</p><h4>References</h4>","Shock therapy failed in Russia due to lack of functioning market institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Shock therapy failed in Russia due the lack of well-functioning market institutions that would have allowed the transition from state-run economy to capitalism smoothly. Rather, it lead to asset stripping by the Russian elite.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the failure of Russian shock therapy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A good contrastive example of “shock therapy” working is the privatization of the zaibatsus during the Meiji era. Similar sales were made by the government to an elite selection of families in the public. However, the prices were not artificially deflated in the sale (which in Russia, led to asset stripping instead of reinvestment) and there were well-functioning state institutions in order to carefully enforce property rights and ensure that the whole privatization process was carried out successfully.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>This note is some evidence against this idea, since it is often the result of weak state institutions, and not capitalism itself, which is the cause for the negative effects.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>Directly supported by the current note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Since humans constitute history, the principles that undergird history must then be accessible to humans":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since humans are the ones that act in history (history is thus generated by humans), humans should consequently be able to infer the axioms that underly history.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is essentially Vico’s justification for why he is able to construct a philosophy of history.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Vico does not entirely secularize his theory, since he adds that it is through this process of discovering history that we are able to reach God. The entirely material aspects of the world is what constitutes providence, and thus, it remains entirely accessible to humans. There is no supernatural forces that are involved and which constitute providence.</p><ul><li>To me more specific, the material of history is human, meaning that since we are able to understand the way that humans think and behave and react to stimuli, we are able to understand, at the more intimate and fundamental level, the driver of human history.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>It seems that perhaps Vico’s claim is defeated by the fact that morality advances through time, and therefore, we might not be able to actually understand the way of thinking of other parties across time.</li></ul></li></ul>","Since the Industrial Revolution, inventions have occurred more readily":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since the industrial revolution, inventions have appeared at a rate that is closer to the theoretical maximum.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It is unclear whether this is causation or merely correlation, but it is nonetheless important to note the increase in the rate of innovation with the Industrial Revolution.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> It seems likelier that the Industrial Revolution was the <em>cause</em> of the shortening time between the theoretical earliest possible time of invention and the actual time of invention than that there was merely a correlation. This is due to the fact that the industrialization created many new tools and materials that were able to make tools, leading both the ability to mass produce an invention and in case also the ability to create the invention in the first place.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge\">Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge</a><ul><li>These two notes seen to be highly correlated, and part of the reason why it seems that the Industrial Revolution was the <em>cause</em> of the increased innovation.</li></ul></li></ul>","Sincerity means coming to terms with one’s baser side":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that being sincere to oneself requires coming to terms with one’s baser side, accepting that it exists but not letting it overtake you.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important way to ensure that one is able to accept oneself and sees oneself clearly and fairly.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is what father Zossima argues that one ought to do in <em>The Brothers Karamazov.</em> This is the thing that Dimitri attempts to do all throughout the book. One when holds secrets about the dark thoughts that go through their head, it isolates the person from the rest of society.</p><h4>References</h4>","Small neglected problems compound into the shape of a whole life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A failure that would have been trivial to correct at the time can, left alone, come to stand for a pattern of avoidance that determines everything after it, so the scale of a neglect is no guide to its consequences.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the structure that makes regret intelligible, since what is regretted is rarely a large decision and usually a small omission that turned out to be load-bearing.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hemingway builds <em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em> on the figure: Harry’s gangrene begins as a scratch he does not treat, and it stands for the talent he traded away in exchange for comfort, one small accommodation at a time.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals\">Distractions prevent us from fulfilling our life goals</a><ul><li>The mechanism by which the accommodations accumulate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a><ul><li>The same claim in its positive form.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Life is too short for regrets\">Life is too short for regrets</a><ul><li>The opposing counsel.</li></ul></li></ul>","Small transgressions of the rule of law are most dangerous":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Small transgressions of the rule of law are the most dangerous since they are the ones that people typically care very little about, though they can effectively serve as precedence for bigger transgressions of the rule of law.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is why is it bad that there should be any transgressions of the rule of law, and that any transgressions should be punished harshly.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Small violations of the rule of law are often easily justified and often not noticed, though such instances can both serve to embolden the transgressor and desensitize outsiders from such activities, eventually leading to the end of rule of law.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree\">Rule of law is preferable to rule by decree</a><ul><li>Since this is true, we would want to preserve the rule of law through preventing any transgressions.</li></ul></li></ul>","Social Security contributes to the federal debt":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Social Security program, unlike what some claim, does indeed contribute to the overall federal debt, as since 2010, it has run a deficit, meaning that the amount it receives in payroll taxes is less than the amount that the program spends on benefits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This demonstrates the great necessity for reform in the Social Security system, as over the next decade, Social Security could contribute 4.1 trillion to the overall federal debt.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Often, it is explained that Social Security has not contributed to the debt because they ran surplus in the past, the products of which is stored as the Social Security Trust Fund. The idea is that Social Security could dip into these funds when they run deficits, and thus, not add to the deficit. However, view is facile for several reasons:</p><ol><li>The trust fund is expected to run out by the early 2030s.</li><li>The trust fund is not actually consisting of liquid assets. Rather, it is made up of special-issue Treasury bonds, which means that when Social Security needs to withdraw some amount from the trust fund, the federal government actually needs to find the money to for the trust fund through either tax hikes or increases to the federal deficit.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security is a progressive system where higher income earners receive a lower share of their lifetime earnings\">Social Security is a progressive system where higher income earners receive a lower share of their lifetime earnings</a><ul><li>The distributive structure that produces the deficit described here, since the taxable ceiling caps revenue.</li></ul></li><li><em>How to Cure Health Care</em></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a></li></ul>","Social Security is a progressive system where higher income earners receive a lower share of their lifetime earnings":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Social Security paid for by payroll taxes, though only the first $168,600 of income is taxed in this manner. However, high income earners also receive a lower share of their total lifetime earnings in terms of their benefits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of the Social Security system.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>The fiscal counterpart. Progressivity concerns who bears the cost; that note concerns whether the programme is solvent at all.</li></ul></li></ul>","Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A more precise statement is that social and economic inequalities are to be arranged so that they are both reasonably expected to be for the advantage of all, and attached to positions and offices open to all.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the second aspect of Rawls’ conception of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a><ul><li>The first principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","Social contract theories place heavy epistemic demands on the parties to the contract":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Both Hobbes’ and Locke’s theories of government are incredibly demanding with regards to their necessary epistemic assumptions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves to weaken both theories of the origins/functions of government.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>One of the core assumptions of Hobbes’ and Locke’s theories is their assumption of a “state of nature,” whereby humans are wholly unorganized and in constant conflict (Hobbes) or in harmony (Locke). This is already a heavy epistemic assumption. There is no empirical evidence to support even the existence of a “state of nature,” as humans have always been organized in structures beyond the individual. In addition to this, there are further assumptions such as that the establishment of a government would actually solve the problems that the two thinkers identify in terms of preventing conflicting or ensuring certain rights.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader\">The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader</a><ul><li>A similar thing is probably true of Confucianist theories of state too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’\">Only an absolute monarch can keep people out of a ‘state of nature’</a><ul><li>This is essentially Hobbes’ view.</li></ul></li></ul>","Socialism cannot deal with change":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even if socialism somehow manages to solve the knowledge and economic calculation problems, it still faces a problem of dealing with change. To be able to adequately deal with change, they would have to constantly recalculate the preferences of people and the marginal value of capital.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major problem which faces socialism is economic calculation, but solving that, there is still this problem.</p><h4>References</h4>","Socialism has a knowledge problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The biggest problem that Hayek identified for the socialist economy is the fact that the central planners lack the sufficient knowledge to be able to allocate resources in an economy effectively.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This both differs from the economic calculation problem that is presented by Mises, but also represents an attack of socialism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Fundamentally, the way this argument differs from the economic calculation problem is that this only states that there is an epistemic problem. There is an unknowableness of the necessary information in order to run command economies in command economies. It does not state that such information does not exist in command economies. Specifically, Hayek refers to the “knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place” as the information that the central planner is fundamentally missing. Since circumstances for a specific individual are constantly changing, it is in fact impossible for the central planner to aggregate all this information.</p><ul><li>Part of this is Polanyi knowledge, which is knowledge that is intangible and unable to be quantized in any way.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“A simple model of AI-aided economic growth”</p><p>The Solow model has its uses, but it fails when it comes to major changes stemming from AI.  Consider instead an economy with (at least) two factors of production:</p><blockquote><ol><li><strong>Intelligence</strong>.  Yes, formal smarts.  Playing chess, proving math theorems, and doing well on evals.  Don’t forget humans can do those things too, though AIs are now a huge boost here.</li><li><strong>Polanyi knowledge</strong>.  Michael Polanyi, that is.  This refers to knowledge of time and place, inarticulable knowledge, custom and habit, and many other particularities that you can read about in Hayek and Polanyi and in many other social scientists, anthropologists too.</li></ol></blockquote></blockquote><p>What’s more, by the very nature of aggregated information, the official statistics, minute variations are smoothed over. Yet it is specifically these specific pieces of information that is valuable in the first place.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>This presents the economic calculation problem.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI represents a new epistemological category\">AI represents a new epistemological category</a><ul><li>It seems that even this new epistemological category might struggle with Polanyi knowledge though?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation\">Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Society corrupted innately good humans":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rousseau had the idea that humans are innately good, but society, specifically bad societal institutions, corrupted them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This connects with Rousseau’s other thoughts regarding moral and political philosophy, as well as his views on children and civilization.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Rousseau’s views here play into his views regarding moral and political philosophy, as he believed that change needs to occur at the societal level, not the individual level, which connects to his idea of the social contract. He also viewed children in a much more benevolent light, arguing that education must be imparted on young people to prevent the corrupting influence of society.</p><h5><em>All The King’s Men</em></h5><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 217</p><p>“Man is conceived in sin and born in corruption and he passeth from the stink of the didie to the stench of the shroud. There is always something.”</p></blockquote><p>Although it might seem from the quote above that Stark views people as being innately evil, this is not the case, as can be seen from his own life. Stark began as something who was very innocent, but gradually developed into the ruthless dictator that he later became. This seems to have been a result of his exposure to the true nature of the world of politics, which required people to get their hands dirty in order to get what they want.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a><ul><li>This is the prerequisite for holding the believes outlined here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately evil\">All humans are innately evil</a><ul><li>The opposite of this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Soft power is premised on hard power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Soft power is something that is largely built on top of hard power, i.e., a foundation of hard power is needed in order for soft power to succeed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Huntington argues that the soft power exhibited by the East Asian countries are built upon their hard power.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Soft power is based on the attractiveness of culture/ideology, and soft power is only seen as affective because it is rooted in the material success and influence of a region that exhibited the power. The success of the East Asian states make their soft power particular effective.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a><ul><li>The soft (and consequently, hard) power of East Asian is partly the reason for the decline of the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a><ul><li>Perhaps the weakened state of the Western military made it less able to exert soft power over the world.</li></ul></li></ul>","Some cell organelles are related to bacteria":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are some cell organelles which originated as bacteria cells in a luminal space. For example, the mitochondria.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains many odd features of these organelles, including how they have two cell membranes, have some chromosomes, and have internal ribosomes different from those extant in the cytoplasm.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some cell organelles are related to bacteria\">Some cell organelles are related to bacteria</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes\">Phospholipids are the main building block of cell membranes</a></li></ul>","Some people must deserve something":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A Moorean refutation of the idea that nobody deserves anything (as per luck egalitarianism) by claiming that, <em>surely</em>, <em>some</em> people deserve <em>some</em> things.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant argument against the claims made by luck egalitarianism and Rawls.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is specifically outlined in Zaitchik’s “On Deserving to Deserve.” The target is the regress principle \"If A deserves X in virtue of Y, then A must also deserve Y.\" He rejects it as a suppressed premise: one can deserve the outcome of one's effort without deserving the capacity to exert effort. If Zaitchik is right, that desert does not require ELC as a precondition, it requires only that the agent exercises agency in the relevant way.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Mankiw offers a second argument to the same conclusion, from the other end. A distribution that perfectly equalised utility would reward the more and the less productive alike, and almost nobody accepts that; the residue of that refusal is a desert claim, whatever the argument against desert says.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>A response to one of the claims that Rawls makes in the book.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences\">Basic desert holds that blame is warranted by the act alone and not by its consequences</a><ul><li>The same notion of desert as it appears in the free will debate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A desert claim requires both aboutness and fitness\">A desert claim requires both aboutness and fitness</a><ul><li>The conditions such a claim has to satisfy to be assessable.</li></ul></li></ul>","Some pleasures are greater than others":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Some pleasures are greater/better than others, which can be proven from the fact that, when faced with a choice between things that both bring pleasure, can actually make a choice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core of Mill’s tiered utilitarian system where some pleasures are inherently greater than others.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mill claims that the way in which you decide which pleasures are through collective consensus. If its the case that most or all of those who have experienced the pleasures being compared prefer one to the other (irrespective of any moral obligation to prefer one or the other), then the preferred one is the “greater” pleasure.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life\">Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life</a><ul><li>But you can still rank pleasure/freedom from pain, i.e., some or more impactful than others.</li></ul></li></ul>","Something that is pleasurable is not necessarily bad":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many arguments have been put forth for why pleasurable things are bad. Aristotle refutes these arguments.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This note presents some arguments for the moral quality of pleasure, something that is still fiercely debated.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ol><li>Since pleasure is a process by which we reach a desirable state, e.g., we gain pleasure from eating since we don’t want to be hungry, pleasure cannot be an end in and of itself. Therefore, pleasure is not a final good.<ol><li><em>While bodily pleasures involve filling a lack, intellectual pleasures (learning, seeing, smelling ,etc.) involve no pain beforehand. Pleasure is sometime therefore a complete experience in a moment, not a journey to somewhere.</em></li></ol></li><li>Pleasure is a distraction from actually being able to do stuff.<ol><li><em>While foreign pleasures (pleasures unrelated to the task at hand) may be distracting, pleasures can also arise from the activity itself. Such pleasure can actually be beneficial.</em></li></ol></li><li>Children and animals seek pleasure.<ol><li><em>We should not despise what nature dictates, just because nature dictates it.</em></li></ol></li><li>Some pleasures are distasteful, since they are pleasure that is the result of something evil.<ol><li><em>We should be focused on the pleasure experienced by the</em> virtuous <em>person, not the evil/morally corrupt person.</em></li></ol></li></ol><p>There are also some arguments for why pleasure is good:</p><ol><li>It is agreed that pain is (usually) bad, and since the opposite of pain is pleasure, pleasure ought to (usually) be good.</li><li>Since both humans and animals who are rational and irrational respectively seek pleasure, pleasure must be good. Although this does not prove that pleasure is the supreme good.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself\">Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself</a><ul><li>A temperance person has the right attitude towards pleasures. They accept the right pleasures when it comes to them, but does not actively seek out base pleasures.</li></ul></li></ul>","Spiritual continuity is the primary concern of history":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The purpose of having a historical consciousness is to be able to establish an active continuity with the past.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was an idea espoused by Burckhardt.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>First, the continuity mentioned here is not a merely passive sort of continuity, or as Burckhardt says, “accepting the cake of custom.” Rather, it means consciously remembering and renewing one’s heritage. Burckhardt argues that this is the only way in which historical consciousness is meaningful, since there is not a grand, overarching, teleology to history, nor is history deterministic in the way that the ancient Greeks thought.</p><ul><li>Although Burckhardt did believe that human nature did not change, in the same way that the Greek did.</li></ul><p>Rather, Burckhardt argues that the reason for the active preservation of our historical past is that such is the only proof of the “significance of the duration of our existence.”</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion\">Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion</a><ul><li>Related.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Perhaps the idea of continuity is related to the idea of traditionalism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Stable, successful political institutions have strong self-correcting mechanisms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>All stable and successful institutions in history have had robust self-correcting institutions such that it is able to correct itself without the need for external stimuli.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core, identifying factor of successful institutions, which may help us understand why existing institutions work and design better institutions for the future.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Grouping the examples by the mechanism actually doing the correcting.</p><p><strong>Electoral and franchise correction</strong></p><ul><li>British Reform Acts of 1832, 1867, and 1884. Acemoglu and Robinson read these as elites extending the franchise under threat of revolution, because a promise to redistribute while keeping power was not credible. The 1832 Act raised the electorate from roughly 478,000 out of 24 million to 813,000, and the decades after saw radical social reform and a falling Gini in England and Wales. Pressure converted into rule change rather than rupture.</li><li>Westminster removing sitting prime ministers mid-term (Thatcher 1990, Truss 2022) without a regime crisis.</li></ul><p><strong>Constitutional amendment and judicial reversal</strong></p><ul><li>US 17th Amendment (1913) responding to legislature-appointed Senate corruption; the 13th–15th after 1865.</li><li><em>Brown v. Board</em> (1954) overturning <em>Plessy</em> (1896). Note the lag: 58 years is a slow thermostat.</li></ul><p><strong>Post-crisis institutional redesign</strong></p><ul><li>German Basic Law (1949) explicitly engineered against Weimar's failure modes: constructive vote of no confidence, 5% threshold, Constitutional Court.</li><li>Post-Watergate: FEC, FOIA amendments, Church Committee.</li><li>Pecora hearings into Glass-Steagall (1933).</li></ul><p><strong>Monitoring and low-cost dispute resolution</strong></p><ul><li>Ostrom's design principles for long-enduring commons institutions include graduated sanctions, rapid access to low-cost local arenas for conflict resolution, and nested enterprises, with monitors who are accountable to users or are the users themselves. Japanese irrigation associations are the standard case.</li></ul><p><strong>Polycentric experimentation</strong></p><ul><li>Swiss cantons plus the referendum; US federalism. Errors are localized and cheap to reverse.</li></ul><p><strong>Recent live case</strong></p><ul><li>Chile 2022: the constitutional draft was rejected at referendum, the process restarted, the state held.</li></ul><p><strong>Where I would push back on the claim</strong></p><ul><li>It risks circularity. \"Stable and successful\" is often operationalized as \"survived shocks,\" and surviving shocks is what self-correction means. To make the claim non-trivial you need a measure of correction capacity that is observable <em>ex ante</em> and predicts survival, not read off the outcome.</li><li>Survivorship bias. Weimar had elections, PR, and judicial review, and corrected nothing. Correction mechanisms are necessary-ish, not sufficient.</li><li>Correction machinery is capturable. The Roman tribunate was a genuine correction device in the Conflict of the Orders and then a tool for Clodius and Caesar.</li><li>Rival explanation: some polities were stable for centuries with weak correction and strong repression (Qing, Ottoman). Acemoglu and Robinson themselves treat persistence as often coming from a vicious circle in which extractive institutions reinvigorate themselves, so persistence alone proves nothing about correction.</li></ul><p>The defensible version is narrower: institutions that survive <em>and</em> remain prosperous under changing conditions tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors.</p><h4>Homeostasis</h4><p>A contrast can be drawn between good self-corrections and the idea of homeostasis in biology. An instruction can be analogized to be a single person, where our internal system is able to self-correct only with the need for external stimuli. On the other hand, good institutions are ones that are able to self-correct without the need for external stimuli, mostly because such external stimuli is almost always negative and harmful for the overall stability of the institution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>This note is a specific example of the broader pattern identified in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>Good institutions don’t do this? Perhaps self-correction allows the flaw to be minimized.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>Important for the design of good institutions.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>Perhaps whether an institution has self-correcting mechanisms can affect the level of trust in that institution.</li></ul></li></ul>","States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For states that surround a rising hegemon, they can choose between two options:</p><ul><li>Bandwagon, or</li><li>Balance.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides a framework for thinking about how states surrounding rising powers can respond to the rising power.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>If states bandwagon, it means that they join with the rising power. This demands that surrounding states have trust in the rising power that it does not simply consume the surrounding states.</p><ul><li>This is more likely within civilizations, though the likelihood of this happening still depends on the specific civilization.</li></ul><p>If states balance, they will do so through the formation of alliances that oppose the rising power (primary balancers). Huntington argues that with the rise of China, states are likely to be the former and not the latter, while the norm has been the latter in European history.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>The reason that things will most likely be the former is due to China's historical cultural and economic and political influence on surrounding states.</li></ul></li></ul>","Status functions are created by declaration speech acts, which in turn, creates institutional facts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Declaration speech acts are statements that announce the creation of institutional fact, a linguistic representation of status function (a piece of paper can be representative of value). This status function then creates institutional facts (a piece of green paper with $20 written on it is representative of $20 of value).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the existence of institutional facts in the world, and connects to the underlying, “empirical” reality.</p><h4>Institutional Reality Constructed</h4><p>Status functions are first constructed through the formula “$X$ counts as $Y$ in context $C$ .” This process is facilitated with a declarative speech act, which is the linguistic act that serves as the superstructure of the status function. $Y$ then becomes an institutional fact (once socially accepted), a state of affairs that is socially accepted as being objectively true. Declarative speech acts do not describe reality, but rather create reality within a shared context.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deontic powers, which are desire-independent reasons for acting, are built upon institutional facts\">Deontic powers, which are desire-independent reasons for acting, are built upon institutional facts</a></li></ul>","Striving toward something greater dignifies a death that falls short of it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An aspiration can confer worth on a life even when it is never reached, so that dying in the attempt is not the same as dying without one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This separates the value of a pursuit from its success, which is what allows failure to be meaningful rather than merely unfortunate.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The figure Hemingway uses is the frozen leopard near the summit of Kilimanjaro, which nobody can explain being there. The counter-reading is that the leopard is simply lost, and the story does not settle which.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Struggles during youth are meaningful\">Struggles during youth are meaningful</a><ul><li>A related claim about the value of unsuccessful effort.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority\">Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority</a><ul><li>A stronger version, and one this claim does not require.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased\">Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased</a><ul><li>Why the ambiguity between the two readings is a feature.</li></ul></li></ul>","Strong induction sees electrons move throughout the entire object; local polarization effects only see the shifting of electron clouds":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Strong induction, which can only occur in a conductor, sees charge moving through the entirety of the object, such that entire areas of the object can shift in charge, creating a polarization effect on the scale of the entire object. On the other hand, local polarization, which can only occur in an insulator,</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>The conductor/insulator split is exactly what determines which effect occurs.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor\">Electrostatic induction polarizes a neutral conductor</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule\">The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule</a></li></ul>","Struggles during youth are meaningful":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that our struggles during our youth have some special meaning, something which disappears during our struggle in our mid and late life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an oft-referenced idea in literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In <em>Youth</em>, Marlow’s struggles during his youth are said by himself to have been meaningful, perhaps both for their aesthetic value and also the practical experience gained. </li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Successful rebellions need a constructive element":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Successful rebellions require a constructive element other than the merely destructive element in order to be successful.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be the case when it comes to successful historical revolutions.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The Taiping Rebellion, although it was ultimately brought down for unrelated reasons, was able to survive for a long time due to its ability to build a successful alternative to the Qing state in the regions that it conquered.</li><li>The American Revolution was able to build a successful alternative to the British one that came before it, offering representation and more freedoms to the states.</li><li>Spartacus’ rebellion failed since while he was a capable military commander, he did not have a clear goal, and failed to be constructive in that sense.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Suffering is a way for some people to feel a sense of moral superiority.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in various philosophies or literary works.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>, this manifests in:</p><ul><li>Father Ferapont, who lives as an aesthetic in the woods. In doing so, he claims that he is able to “see” demons, and he becomes revered by people after the death of Zossima, since he denounces Zossima after his death. This is likely because he experienced suffering while living in the woods.</li><li>Katerina Ivanova continues to express her own suffering as a result of being unable to love Ivan because he is engaged to Dimitri, although she is able to end her affair with Dimitri at any time she wants.</li></ul><p>This is the idea of “lacerations” that is present throughout the novel. Put another way, it is the ego, disguised as the victim.</p><ul><li>People can feed on a feeling of hopelessness are vindictiveness because it makes them feel special, or morally superior to others.</li><li>At the end of this, the only possible result of isolation. One is not able to relate to the pain of others, since we are so stuck in our own pain.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Suffering has metaphysical meaning":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The general idea that there is some meaning to the suffering that humans have to endure, either because suffering itself is intrinsically metaphysically valuable or because it is instrumentally valuable to help us develop certain virtuous traits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant idea within many schools of philosophy, including as a major part of theodicies.</p><h4>Examples</h4><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering is good, if done correctly\">Suffering is good, if done correctly</a> -</li></ul>","Suffering is good, if done correctly":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Dostoevsky argues that suffering can be good, and lead to a spiritual transformation, if it is done correctly. If done incorrectly, it can trap someone.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a major theme that is explicated in <em>TBK</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Dostoevsky distinguishes between two forms of suffering:</p><ul><li><strong>Lamentation</strong>: “true suffering” that has a genuine cause that originates in the world. If one has faith and openness to the “web of connectedness,” lamentations can serve to purify us. However, if it is treated badly and allowed to fester, it can become the other form of suffering.</li><li><strong>Lacerations:</strong> suffering to feel morally superior to others, or because one feels guilt tripped into continuing to suffer. Dostoevsky considers this to be the wrong kind of suffering, and since it is centered on the self and pride, it leads to isolation.</li></ul><p>In order for suffering to “absolve one of sin,” one must go through a process of <strong>expiation,</strong> which means accepting the suffering and not avoiding it, for instance, through confession. Dimitri is the example that is used in TBK to show that at the end of suffering, there is light and joy. One just has to suffering in the right way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority\">Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority</a><ul><li>This is the idea of “lacerations” mentioned here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that to be truly happy, one must not only be virtuous (embody “goodness”) but must also have proper material conditions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This portrays Aristotle’s philosophy as one that is eminently practical and realistic, rather than idealistic and aesthetic.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a></li></ul>","Suggestiveness, not articulateness, is the ideal of Chinese art":"<h4>Summary</h4><h4>Significance</h4><p>Suggestiveness is the fundamental feature of Chinese art, and something that differentiates Chinese art with Western art.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>This is a pattern that can be seen through various mediums and definitions of Chinese art:</p><ul><li>Most obviously, it manifests in paintings and other artworks of a visual nature.</li><li>It also manifests in Chinese literature, which is often phrased more ambiguously than comparative Western ones.</li><li>Specifically, it affects Chinese philosophy, whose writings are often very ambiguous and pithy.</li></ul><p>Fong Youlan suggests that this is because Chinese philosophy strives to communicate the maximum possible with the most minimal medium.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Zhuangzi</em></p><p>A fish-trap is for catching fish; once you've caught the fish, you can forget about the trap. A rabbit-snare is for catching rabbits; once you've caught the rabbit, you can forget about the snare. <strong>Words are for catching ideas; once you've caught the idea, you can forget about the words.</strong></p></blockquote><p>Additionally, the central purpose of Chinese philosophy is to seek the <em>dao</em>, though it is said that the <em>dao</em> cannot be told, but only suggested.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"荷花图轴\">荷花图轴</a><ul><li>This is an example of visual piece of Chinese art that displays the characteristics described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Eels\">Eels</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Systems analysis works best when the system is static":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that systems analysis is best applicable and most accurate when the system is not dynamic and unpredictable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This outlines when systems analysis ought not to be used.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Systems analysis does not work very well when the system is dynamic is changes drastically (and often unpredictably) with a slight change in input.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>When scientists (lead by Song Jian) attempted to use systems theory to model birth rates during the formation of the one-child policy, th ey failed, and China now suffers extremely low birth rates</li><li>When Jay Forrester attempted to model the entire world through the flow of capital and resources, he was not able to predict the future accurately either.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>It is this tendency that makes systems complex. They \"fight back.\"</li></ul></li></ul>","Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Most systems, when there are internal imbalances, will tend towards equilibrium due to some equalizing force within the system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to be an inductive rule regard the many systems that exist across many fields, and could be used as a conceptual framework to predict the outcomes of yet-to-exist systems.</p><h4>Explanation/Examples</h4><p>This is something that is observable throughout all different fields, namely the principle that when an external force attempts to impose some change on a system, the system will internally readjust such that the system returns to its original state. For instance, this is seen through:</p><ul><li>Le Chatelier's Principle</li><li>Political Realism attempts to create a balance of power that limits the power of an individual state to maintain equilibrium<ul><li>An example of this principle in practice in international relations.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Counterexamples</h4><ul><li>When silver was introduced en mass in the Spanish Empire in the 16th century, hyperinflation and economic collapse ensued.<ul><li>An example of how a system without limitations eventually collapsed.</li></ul></li><li>The one-child policy made irreversible changes to China's population dynamics.</li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>Perhaps the reason that some systems don't actually settle into a stable equilibrium is because not all systems have a stable equilibrium. Some systems may be have unstable equilibriums whereby once the system is disrupted, the system collapses to a completely different state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a><ul><li>An example of this from political science.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When two conductors touch, they both acquire the same charge such that the overall charge is preserved\">When two conductors touch, they both acquire the same charge such that the overall charge is preserved</a><ul><li>An example from eletromagnetism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Once the threshold potential is reached, voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, and depolarization begins\">Once the threshold potential is reached, voltage-gated sodium ion channels open, and depolarization begins</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","TIPS yields are not a good proxy for real interest rates":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Treasury Inflation Protected Securities are generally not an accurate proxy for the real interest rate.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a common misconception regarding TIPS.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The common reasoning is that since TIPS are adjusted for inflation, that would mean that it represents the true, real interest rate of the government. However, this logic is flawed because of two factors:</p><ol><li>Large institutional investors (e.g., pension funds) are often forced by the government to buy TIPS, which artificially drives down the yield.</li><li>Investors typically demand a liquidity premium on TIPS, since they are harder to transact than other bonds. </li></ol><p>These two factors combined means that, depending on which factor is stronger at a particular moment, they could radically misrepresent the true real interest rate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank\">Business cycles arise due to artificial manipulations of the interest rate by the central bank</a><ul><li>(1) in this note may be an example of government manipulation of the interest rates.</li></ul></li></ul>","Taxation may justly be more than proportional to income":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle that people should contribute in proportion to their ability does not settle the schedule, and the same principle has been read as requiring the rich to contribute more than in proportion to their revenue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Proportionality is often treated as the neutral baseline from which progressivity is a departure, and this shows the baseline is already contested within the tradition that is usually cited against progressivity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Adam Smith holds both positions in the same text, arguing for contribution in proportion to ability and also that it is not unreasonable for the rich to contribute something more than in that proportion. He also endorsed a tax on luxury carriages. The flat-tax reading of Smith is therefore a selection from him rather than a summary of him.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The invisible hand was advanced narrowly and with caveats\">The invisible hand was advanced narrowly and with caveats</a><ul><li>The same pattern of selective citation, on a more famous point.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system\">Any consumption tax can be made progressive with the right rebate system</a><ul><li>A modern route to the same schedule.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>The institutional form the question takes now.</li></ul></li></ul>","Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is a difference between the idea of technical goodness and moral goodness, the former meaning being good at some skill, and the latter referring to being a good person.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important distinction to draw, not because there is technically a concrete distinction between the two concepts, but because this is a point of tension in Aristotle’s philosophy, as he seems to conflate these two types of goodness in his Ethics.</p><h4>Aristotle’s Treatment</h4><p>Aristotle argues that a good person is something who fulfills their duties/goals as a person to a satisfactory degree. However, this seems to conflate the ideas of moral goodness (i.e., being a good person) and technical goodness (i.e., being a good blacksmith). A potential solution to this is simply to reframe Aristotle’s intentions. There is no explicit discussion in Aristotle’s text that he was pursuing “good” in the strictly moral sense, and indeed, he may have just sought to find human goodness in the strictly technical sense.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>He refers to moral goodness here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Temperance is a state whereby lack of pleasure does not pain oneself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Someone who is temperate is someone who actively seeks pleasure, but they do not pain when there is no pleasure. They take pleasure in the right things (they desire the right things), in the right amount, at the right time, and in the right way.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a part of Aristotle’s analysis of what is means to be various virtues.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>While the temperate man desires the right things, the continent man desires the wrong things (though he is able to overcome such a incorrect desire with rationality).</li></ul></li></ul>","That history entails progress is a modern conception":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Lowith argues that this is the standard modern conception of history; i.e., conceiving of history as a progressive force that gradually eliminates parts of the problem of evil until the problem is no more.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Lowith argues that this attitude is uniquely modern, and differs both from the Classical and Christian solutions to the problem of evil.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>During antiquity, people dealt with the problem of evil with rebellion, as can be seen through the myth of Prometheus. The other response is subservience and submission to the higher authority, as to Christ. The Greek believed that man has the resources to be able to meet every situation, and as thus, they were not as concerned with the meaning of history, but rather with the operation of the cosmos.</p><ul><li>They were humanists.</li><li>Since human nature does not change, events that have occurred in the past will occur again. In this way, history is deterministic in a very rough sense, thus explaining their lack of interest in the philosophy of history.</li></ul><p>For the Christians and Jews, however, history is primarily a history of salvation.</p><ul><li>Believed that knowledge of the future is something that is predetermined by God, though there is personal will involved. Either way, man is not subject to knowledge of the future, as the Greeks believed.</li><li>This is best exemplified by the thought of Augustine, who believed that history is defined by the two dispensations: the death of Christ and the second coming of Christ. Additionally, he believed that providence was decided by God and carried out by him.<ul><li>The only visible sign of divine providence is the death of Christ. If the signs were too obvious otherwise, then we would lose our ability to have faith in God.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Our modern conception of history is Christian in framework but un-Christian in substance: we believe in progress towards some future “outside of history,” while dropping the fact that the death of Christ is the beginning of the end and that his life and death are what imbues history with this meaning.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 202</p><p>Neither of these two interpretations, however, explains the curious mixture of <strong>our “Christian world,” which lives by the hope in a better world and yet sets its hope on material production and welfare.</strong></p></blockquote><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems that the idea of ancient Greece having an attitude of rebellion is a mistaken once, since their general attitude is likewise one of submission to the gods. This can be seen through the histories written by Thucydides and Herodotus and Polybius. This is in fact one of the common points between ancient Greek and Christian thought: they both submitted to fate, unlike today, where we look towards progress. Nonetheless, there is truth in the fact that they believed that since human nature remained constant, the future was in some way, predictable.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Vico’s views on history</p><p>In many ways, Vico’s view of history is rather classical instead of Christian. He adopts a theory that is similar to the cyclical theory of the ancients. This, however, puts him in conflict with the progressive theory of the Christians. This is illustrated by the following quote:</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 135</p><p>History as seen by Vico has a prehistoric beginning by no end and fulfilment, and yet it is ruled by providence for the sake of mankind.</p></blockquote></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a><ul><li>Specifically, this is the modern idea that morality advances through history.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority\">Christian morality entails passive obedience to authority</a><ul><li>This is the “submissiveness” that is referred to in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>It seems that the conception here of Classical views on the problem of evil may be too simple? Oedipus rebels against his fate, but he fails. Other classical works like the Odyssey also describe a process of rebellion against the gods but the ultimate failure of such attempts. </li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)\">The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus at Colonus\">Oedipus at Colonus</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes\">History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The “iron triangle” of government, consisting of congress, the bureaucracy, and the lobbyists, may often act to benefit each other (as the three exist in a symbiotic relationship) rather than for the good of the public.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The “iron triangle” is, as apparent in its name, difficult to break. This makes it a tough topic to address, to enact reforms to ensure that the government actually acts in the interest of the public.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><em>3 ways Trump can deliver an economic ‘golden age’ for America</em></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"20th-century American presidents have expanded presidential powers beyond their original constitutional intentions\">20th-century American presidents have expanded presidential powers beyond their original constitutional intentions</a><ul><li>If presidents become more powerful, it means that it could potentially strengthen the iron triangle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that one’s suffering is brought upon ultimately by oneself. A major theme in works such as Hamlet, and the Greek Tragedies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has been a common theme for many works of philosophy and literature throughout the ages and is a crucial aspect of the human condition as explored through works of literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>This is a prominent theme in <em>Hamlet</em>, as Hamlet is forced to suffer through the murder of his father, and his own indecision, being unable to take revenge.<ul><li>This indecision is likely due to several factors, including his unsureness about the nature of the ghost that informs him of the murder of his father, his intellectualism (he was a scholar at the University of Wittenburg) leading to over-analyzation and paralysis of action, and his psychological complexity, related to his potential Oedipus complex.</li></ul></li><li>This is likewise a prominent theme in the Sophoclean tragedies, for example, <em>Oedipus the King</em>, where Oedipus is forced advance his own down fall unknowingly, suffering all the way along.</li><li>This is also the case with both Victor and the creature throughout <em>Frankenstein</em>. <ul><li>Victor is forced to constantly suffer the result of his own creation, his creature, as the creature terrorizes him and murders the ones that he loves. </li><li>At the same time, the creature feels unloved due to his appearance, turning to violence as a means of attempting to achieve catharsis with his inner torment. Yet, this violence does not serve to mitigate his suffering; rather, it worsens his suffering tormenting him with remorse.<ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Blasted as thou wert, my agony was still superior to thine; for the bitter sting of remorse will not cease to rankle in my wounds until death shall close them for ever.</span></li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The AI misalignment problem may be self-fulfilling":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The AI misalignment problem may be self-fulfilling, since the web is filled with such much information on the potential for AI-misalignment that</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something that is hugely relevant for understanding and dealing with AI.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a><ul><li>Perhaps this moralization of AI could lead to AI moralizing itself?</li></ul></li></ul>","The Absurd man pursues merely aesthetic value":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For the Absurd man, since traditional values and meanings are no longer considered, the only value in the world exists in the form of aesthetic/sensory value.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the nature of the Absurd man in the face of the lack of moral values and guidance.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the Absurd man, life is simply a series of impressions. There is no value attached to the meanings constructed above this layer of sensory experience. Where a normal person could claim to “love” even when they are not thinking about the object of their “love,” and they would even be willing to suffer physically/mentally for such an abstract concept, the Absurd man finds this absurd.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">An Explication on the Stranger</p><p><strong>Since God does not exist and man dies, everything is permissible. One experience is as good as another; the important thing is simply to acquire as many as possible.</strong> “The ideal of the absurd man is the present and the succession of present moments before an ever-conscious spirit” (Sisyphus). Confronted with this “quantitative ethic” all values collapse; thrown into this world, the absurd man, rebellious and irresponsible, has “nothing to justify.” He is innocent, innocent as Somerset Maugham’s savages before the arrival of clergyman who teaches them Good and Evil, what is lawful and what is forbidden. For this man, everything is lawful. He is as innocent as Prince Mishkin, who “lives in an everlasting present, lightly tinged with smiles and indifference.” Innocent in every sense of the word, he, too, is, if you like, an “Idiot.”</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a><ul><li>To accept it means to seek things other than moral value. Thus, one turns to aesthetic/sensory value.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Ayodhya Dispute between Hindus and Muslims mirror’s India struggle with identity, secularism, and historical memory":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Ayodhya dispute centers on a plot of land in Ayodhya where both Hindus and Muslims claims is a religious site. Although the issue has largely been settled by 2020, it serves as an indicator of religious tensions in India before that.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The event highlights conflicts over religion in India, and disputes the claim that due to Hinduism’s tolerance for other belief systems, that there would not be any religious conflict.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India\">Hinduism’s tolerance of other belief systems limits religion conflicts in India</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li></ul>","The Belgian Revolution occurred because of an especially moving Opera performance":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Belgium Revolution of 1830, which saw the establishment of a United Kingdom of Belgium following its occurrence, was instigated by a particular moving performance of the Opera <em>La muette de Portic</em>.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the power of music to instill strong emotions in people, such that it can serve as the match that lights a pile of dry sticks on fire, eventually leading to a revolution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Music can influence your confidence\">Music can influence your confidence</a></li></ul>","The Bhakti Hindu movement overtook Buddhism in India around 1000AD":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Buddhism was overtaken by the <em>Bhakti</em> movement, a movement with roots in Hinduism, around the turn of the first milenium.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Spread through Indian between 600 - 1300 A.D., and overtook Buddhism.</li><li>Made Hindu practice more accessible to the common people, even people of low caste, since it focused on intense worship of deities, rather than philosophical speculation or sacrifices, often conducted by the Brahmins.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there\">Buddhism barely exists in India, despite originating there</a></li></ul>","The British political system has fundamentally changed since the 20th century":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The British political system is suffering from a number of problems that has fundamentally changed the way that it works compared to the government from three decades ago.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates a major change in the British political system that could potentially have lasting effects on the way that politics is conducted in the future in Britain.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>People are becoming less loyal to their party, meaning that new political parties are rising into the forefront. The traditional Labor and Conservative parties are both now trailing behind Reform UK.</li><li>The electorate is becoming more fractured and the majoritarian system means that leaders supported by only a small number of people come to power.</li><li>There are a rising number of independent political figures that become elected, who essentially run their own campaigns and also are often single-issued.<ul><li>This could leads to a Dutch-style pillarization, where parties become increasingly single-issued, or it may lead to a US style government where political outsiders are able to enter the political sphere by taking advantage of frustration.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Political attitudes is one&#x27;s opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much\">Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much</a><ul><li>British political attitudes are changing, which is leading to a period of political upset in Britain.</li></ul></li></ul><p>-</p>","The Brothers Karamazov":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Dostoevsky’s masterpiece that explores faith through the tale of the Karamazov brothers.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is considered one of the foremost works of literature in world history.</p><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Fyodor Pavlovich Karamazov</strong></p><ul><li>Immoral, irreverent, and careless. He is a materialist who is ignorant of all things spiritual or metaphysical.<ul><li>He is also a terrible father, since he neglects his children. <ul><li>He forgets that Sophia is Ivan’s as well as Alyosha’s mother. It’s almost like a “Fruedian slip” in that he likes Alyosha more than Ivan.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Atheistic because he simply doesn’t care. It seems like he has no internal moral sense, and thus, he is not drawn to religion. So, it seems like although he attempts to justify his Atheism with rationality, the reasoning is sound but trivial, and the underlying motivation is simply a lack of a desire to be moral.<ul><li>He criticizes the church for there perceived </li></ul></li><li>On the surface, he seems like a buffoon. In fact, this is how he often refers to himself.<ul><li>However, he is surprisingly rational, though in a petty way. He doesn’t use his rationality for virtuous means. Rather, he uses it to acquire wealth and spend it with little consideration.</li><li>He has this tendency to enjoy diminishing himself, talking down about himself.</li></ul></li><li>Represents the “unconscious”; letting lose repressed emotions in Freudian terms.<ul><li>Is not aware of the existence of the self (or is afraid of the contradictions apparent in his self), and tries to run away from attempting to define his own life through debauchery. </li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Alexandrovich Miusov</strong></p><ul><li>A “man of principle.”<ul><li>Having traveled to the West, he is influenced by Western European ideas of liberalism. In this way, he is also an atheist, except his atheism comes from almost a  feeling of superiority over the people who do believe in faith.</li><li>He does have “principle,” so he does not reject religion on the same basis that Fyodor does. Rather, he rejects it because he finds it superstitious and irrational. Fundamentally, this is grounded upon his liberal values.</li><li>As a result of his principles, he seems to be far more (at least on the surface), open to different ideas. This is seen when he is in the church, where he often expresses his doubts about the institution of religion with internal thoughts, but never expresses them to anyone else.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Ivan Fyodorovich Karamazov</strong></p><ul><li>Intellectual and brilliant. </li><li>In many ways represents a “Greek” way of life.<ul><li>In many ways, he is a Platonist, always looking at the “idea” of something rather than the physical manifestation of that thing.</li><li>Attempts to achieve <em>reinheit</em>, purity in the meaning of “washing oneself clean.” </li><li>Connects with this dislike of being dependent on others. In Ancient Grecian morality, it was viewed as servile and morally degrading to be dependent on another.</li><li>A world of forgiveness is morally repugnant to Ivan. He wants a world of vengeance, and rather the world have “unavenged suffering” than forgiveness. This is a result of his argument against the belief in God outline below.</li><li>Ivan’s rationality is what is preventing him from being able to be a good person. He always thinks to hard about something, which makes it difficult for him to actually do good things.</li></ul></li><li>Often acts as an observer, a bystander, seeking the catalogue and observe the occurrences of the world rather than participate in them.<ul><li>This is also reflected not just in his physical actions, but in the way that he approaches his thinking: for instance, he seems perfectly able to hold contradictory views on the nature of religion and belief, making arguments for each side, yet not believing in any.</li><li>He is able to build moral architecture (his argument for the takeover of the state by the church, which would end crime), despite not having any moral reservations himself. This torments him throughout the book.</li><li>Ivan’s jokes while he talks about the meaning of life and religion is his way of distancing himself (perhaps unconsciously) from these topics.</li><li>He finds it impossible to “love thy neighbor” because he himself is unable to get close to anyone else. He reasons that this is because the “cruelty and evilness of man” prevents him from loving his neighbors.</li></ul></li><li>Independent, and does not want to rely on anybody.<ul><li>He believes that he is above everybody else, so he argues for the takeover of the state by the church, since that would mean that the “weak masses” would have moral guidance. This view is what he espouses through the story of the Grand Inquisitor.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Ivan, “The Brothers Make Friends”</p><p>Very, I want to get to know you once for all, and I want you to know me. And then to say good-bye. <strong>I believe it's always best to get to know people just before leaving them.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Attempts to repress the “base” side of his nature and embrace the “noble” side. It, however, it seems that his base side still shows through at times. <ul><li>When he is describing the torture of children, he says how there is a part of all of us that desire the same sadistic feeling of being able to torture another. What this says is that this desire is also a part of him, and something notable enough that he uses it to make a general claim about the nature of humanity (which may or may not be true).</li><li>His argument for only living until thirty is again another performance of morality, the suppression of his base desire to lengthen his life like Fyodor.</li></ul></li><li>He makes an “anti-mystic” argument against belief in God.<ul><li>Ivan’s argument from “Rebellion”: The “eternal harmony” which is God’s kingdom/heaven is built upon the sum of human history, which includes human suffering. This includes the suffering of children, who have not “eaten the apple” and are thus not responsible for the suffer that is brought upon them. A moral agent cannot accept the suffering of an innocent even if it brings them happiness. To participate in God’s world is to accept this arrangement. Therefore, while God may exist, Ivan chooses to “return the ticket.”<ul><li>Reponses:<ul><li>You are not a spectator that can simply “give back the ticket.” You are already a part of the world, and you cannot simply leave, because you are not a pure, innocent bystander.</li><li>Returning the ticket is not the right response.</li><li>Ivan is a sadist. He is not the innocent bystander. He enjoys watching kids suffer. So he can’t give back the ticket.</li><li>You should try to make the world better, not leave the world.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>It’s not that “God’s motives are beyond human comprehension,” it is this very fact that he rejects. He believes in his human comprehension, and using it, he essentially finds God at fault for the suffering in the world.</li><li>He uses the argument from evil not to disprove the existence of God, but to prove why man ought not to believe in God, even if he does exist.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Alexei Fyodorovich Karamazov</strong></p><ul><li>Intelligent, but not intellectual.</li><li>Seeks purity and transcendence in life, although he attempts this in a vastly different way than Ivan. He is so invested in the spiritual that he seems to disregard the earthly (at least until father Zossima tells him to leave the monastery after his death).<ul><li>He does, however, have a sensual side (he remains a “Karamazov”), which is revealed when he meets Katerina Ivanovna, is and stunned by her beauty.<ul><li>This is again the case after Zossima’s death. As the miracle fails to appear with the body of Zossima stinking, Alyosha loses his faith for some time. </li><li>This is only ended with his realization that love is powerful, as when he showed Grushenka love, she responded by changing herself, and regaining her perspective on life. This leads Alyosha on its own journey, where he reclaims his own faith.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Represents a Christian way of life.<ul><li>Attempts to achieve <em>lauterkeit</em>, purity in the sense of transformation of transfiguration.</li><li>His philosophy of Christian (active) love runs into issue when he loves both side of a conflict, and thus there is no clear way to support one or the other.</li><li>His unconditional love for other is sometimes misinterpreted as being ulterior motives.<ul><li>His early interactions with Lise.</li><li>HIs early interactions with Ilyusha.</li></ul></li><li>Interestingly, he seems to be partially convinced by Ivan during his conversation with him, and abandons some of the principle that are a part of his “Christian love” perspective.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Alyosha in “The Confession of a 'Passionate Heart — in Verse”</p><p>Alyosha was certain that no one in the whole world ever would want to hurt him, and, what is more, he knew that no one could hurt him. This was for him an axiom, assumed once for all without question, and he went his way without hesitation, relying on it.</p></blockquote><ul><li>Dependent on others, but not such that others dislike him for his dependence. This is in line with his Christian morality, a morality that is much more about community and supporting one another.</li><li>He withdraws from the world in order to get away from his sensual side, which seems to afflict every other Karamazov. <ul><li>At the same time, he seems aware of his sensual side, of the potential that he has as a “Karamazov.”</li><li>He is able to provisionally subsume all of his impulses and the need to face up to the contradictions in the self through his love of Zossima. He often hides behind Zossima, in essence hiding behind faith.</li><li>His rejection of his base side is what ultimately leads him to a sense of isolation in the world and his implicit guilt in the murder of his father.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Dimitri Fyodorovich Karamazov</strong></p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Dimitri in “The Confession of a 'Passionate Heart — in Verse”</p><p>The awful thing is that beauty is mysterious as well as terrible. God and the devil are fighting there and the battlefield is the heart of man. But a man always talks of his own ache.</p></blockquote><ul><li>A person of contradictions, attempting to occupy the middle ground between Alexei and Ivan.<ul><li>He is the only character that attempts to occupy the middle, shifting between angel and insect.</li><li>He is the only one who is genuinely trying to reconcile these two sides of himself.</li><li>His contradictions appear in everything that he does, the things that he experiences. He always is able to perceive both the “base” and the “noble” side of anything. <ul><li>“The ice burned my forehead like fire…”</li><li>“Kill oneself from delight…”</li><li>How, despite his hatred of Katerina Ivanovna, he was a “hair-breadth” from “the maddest love.”</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He is satisfied, even pleased when he heard of Katerina Ivanovna’s humiliation by Grushenka.<ul><li>Shows how he is attracted to the raw sensuality of Grushenka, and not the far more upstanding Katerina.</li></ul></li><li>After he murders Fyodor, he seems to have a moment of realization that matches the moment of realization that occurred to Alyosha.<ul><li>He is no longer as petty as he was before, although he remains emotional. He seems to have gained a larger perspective on life.</li><li>He wins Grushenka back from the Polish officer.</li></ul></li><li>Dimitri suffers for everyone in the world, since he acts is the paragon of what to do when faced with suffering. He accepts it, and digests it, and is able t take control of it. In doing so, he emerges joyful.</li></ul><p><strong>Pavel Fyodorovich Smerdyakov</strong></p><ul><li>Epileptic &amp; intelligent.</li><li>Lacks the contradictions that are present within every other Karamazov (therefore, he is not even referred to as a Karamazov).<ul><li>This could have been snuffed out with the cat-killing incident?</li><li>He has no sense of self that the other members of the Karamazov family do. He doesn’t have a “Karamazov” in him, and thus he is never referred to in that way.</li></ul></li><li>He is like Ivan without a superficial sense of morality.<ul><li>He is a genuine sadist; everything he says is tinged with contempt.</li><li>In fact, Ivan is afraid of him. He scares Ivan during a conversation with him because he truly embodies what Ivan believes in. <ul><li>This exposes Ivan as a sort of Platonist intellectual, since he is afraid, on an instinctual/personal level, of the explication of his ideas.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>He has no personality, no eternal validity.<ul><li>He mirrors Ivan as a result, intellectually and physically.</li><li>When Ivan is not there to support him intellectually, he falls ill.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Lise Khokhlakov</strong></p><ul><li>Masochistic</li><li>She is seems to be pure in the Christian sense.<ul><li>She is an invalid (she suffers), and she is genuine with her emotions (e.g., her love letter to Alyosha).</li><li>This is part of the reason why Alyosha is in love with her.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Katerina Ivanovna Verkhovtseva</strong></p><ul><li>The counterpart of Ivan. </li><li>She invents a reason to say in the “Dimitri drama.”<ul><li>Although she loves Ivan, she plays up a sense of duty towards Dimitri. She enjoys the feeling of moral superiority over others through her self-laceration (it is a “self”-laceration since she choose herself to play this role of the suffering women who <em>must</em> protect her honor before her feelings).</li><li>The primary target of this “self”-laceration is Ivan, since Katerina is essentially inventing moral obligations in order to create moral barriers for him. In this way, she “lacerates” Ivan’s love for her and enjoys it.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Elder Zossima</strong></p><ul><li><s>The most enlightened character in the book.</s><ul><li>He receives this enlightenment stance from his brother, Markel.<ul><li>There is an idea of apostolic succession that is existentialized here, as Markel passes down his “sainthood” to Zossima, who passes it down to Alyosha. <ul><li>Each stage of this changes the form, bringing it closer to the truth.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>The incident where he beats up its subordinate reminds him of the suffering of Markel and the things that he said to him. This changes him and makes him into the person that he would eventually become.</li><li>He joins a monastery, since that is the place where his radical spirituality would be protected.<ul><li>Markel dies because his spirituality was not “protected,” and people just thought he was a crazy person.</li><li>But the retreat to the monastery still faces the problem of “Platonism.”<ul><li>Zossima is not actually in the world, doing something. He is merely relegated to a monastery. And he realizes this, which is why he calls for Alyosha to leave the monastery.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li><s>The only character who realizes that the two sides of the soul are equal to one another, that no one should trump another, and they should exist in harmony.</s><ul><li>Becoming a monk is the only means of shedding oneself of superfluous and unnecessary desires.<ul><li>But this does not mean having no desires. It means having the right amount of desire, which is rather Aristotelian.</li></ul></li><li>If this is the case, then why is he a monk? Why does he only seem to pursue the “noble” side of the soul?</li></ul></li><li>He wants to suffer for all of the sins of humanity. He thinks that the monks are the worst sort of people, and that is the reason that they have ended up in the monastery. They are the people who need the most saving.<ul><li>We must always be doing good, and to everyone, since not doing so means “planting a seed of evil” in someone else.</li><li>A moral “butterfly effect” whereby an action has extended consequences, and an evil action plants a seed of evil in someone else, and the infection spread throughout society.<ul><li>Therefore, one is responsible for everything. And, therefore, we must communally confess.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Represents the ideals of the solution presented in the antinomies of the Grand Inquisitor.<ul><li>And this is revealed after his experience of the duel and in his dealings with the mysterious visitor.</li><li>Represents the transformation of the individual through a sort of moral transformation, which is the solution to the contradictions from the Grand Inquisitor chapter.<ul><li>The problem with the Grand Inquisitor is that the people are not transformed like Zossima.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Hell is being unable to love. This refers to the actual Hell, though existentialized and demagicalized.<ul><li>Sin means isolation, and thus confession to no one or just one person (Catholic view) doesn’t help. It requires group confession/confessing to the world/</li><li>This is why he has such a different view of confession than the Catholic Church or the Protestants.</li><li>Confession allows one to release guilt and be able to love others again.</li><li>One is responsible for a lot more suffering than one may realize, even though it may not mean suffering for everyone on Earth.</li><li>He argues for the opposite of Ivan, where the state becomes the church through the process of active love. This is his doctrine of “Christian Socialism.”<ul><li>Moral individualism is the opposite of Christian Socialism; it brings about “destructive\" impotence.” This is exemplified by the onion story.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Mikhail Osipovich Rakitin</strong></p><ul><li>Is the devil incarnate?<ul><li>He tries to tempt Alyosha into after the death of Zossima, in a moment of weakness for him.</li></ul></li><li>Argues against Ivan that immortality is not needed for virtue. Rather, freedom, equality, and fraternity are needed.</li></ul><p><strong>Agrafena Alexandrovna Svetlov</strong></p><ul><li>The personification of sensuality and desire, becoming a point of contention between Dimitri and Fyodor.</li><li>Seems rather like the female double of Fyodor in her willingness to ignore social conventions and flaunt her senuality.</li><li>She is able to be “saved” by Alyosha after his discussion with her and Rakitin.<ul><li>Alyosha makes her understand her own self-worth. He suffers for her sins. He is able to address her respectfully even though she is a wicked women.</li><li>This is the “existentialized miracle”; transformation of a human soul through love.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Captain Nikolai Ilyich Snegiryov and Ilyusha</strong></p><ul><li>A “Greek” family.</li><li>Creates a family that serves as a contrast to the Karamazovs. They are moral, poor, and value honor above all else.<ul><li>Nikolai is ashamed of having folded so quickly in the face of the money that Alyosha offered him, so he rejects it. He is also ashamed of having befriended Alyosha so quickly.</li></ul></li></ul><p><strong>Kolya</strong></p><ul><li>He is at first someone who performs a sort of intellectualism, having been influenced by Ratkitin. <ul><li>He is plagued by blatant contradictions. For instance, though he says that he loves Illusha, he never visits him under the very end.</li></ul></li><li>He is reshaped by Alyosha into someone who is more genuine and loving.</li></ul><p><strong>The Mysterious Visitor</strong></p><ul><li>He wanted to kill Zossima, the only person who knew about his murder. Doing so would allow him to go back to his life as normal.<ul><li>But it would also seal him in hell, as he has not committed two murders, killing his only path out of hell.</li><li>The split second choice not to kill Zossima marked a changed in the person, an indication that they can finally be saved.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>The Grand Inquisitor</h4><ul><li>An antimony between the Roman Catholic Church and Protestantism.<ul><li>But eventually there is going to to be a third way offered.</li><li>Represents the conflict between the base (represented by the GI) and the noble (represented by Jesus).</li></ul></li><li>The Grand Inquisitor argues that what Jesus expects of people is impossible.<ul><li>Thinks that people in general are weak, rebellious, and slavish.</li><li>He gives people the freedom of choice, but does not understand that normal people are simply overwhelmed by this power.</li><li>It may work for the select few, like Jesus, but it won’t work for everybody else.</li><li>And this is where the Catholic Church steps in, as they serve as the earthly agent for the people, taking their freedom of choice away from them (taking on their burden) and making things easier for them.</li><li>The Catholic Church gives people happiness through their authority.</li></ul></li><li>Jesus believes that people, at least deep down, are good and will make the right choice. This is why he offers the choice to people.<ul><li>Thinks that all people are lofty and good.</li></ul></li><li>While Jesus offers total freedom and individuality, the Catholic Church offers total authority and control. Both views seem extreme.</li><li>The three antinomies:<ul><li>Materialism &amp; faith:<ul><li>Jesus argues that you don’t need any food to be moral.<ul><li>The person who exemplifies this is Father Ferapont. </li><li>Ferapont, however, is a completely crazy person. He is presented in a way that makes him arrogant, wild, and prone to hallucinations.</li><li>So this is an argument against Jesus’s position.</li></ul></li><li>The GI argues that you need food in order to be moral.<ul><li>This is the position that is supported in the next section, since in Zossima’s story, he talks about how little the priests survive off of.</li><li>In fact (through the cherry jam examples), it is ok to desire things beyond just the necessities. It becomes only bad when these things become a crutch or an addiction.</li><li>This is similar to the golden mean of Aristotle? You should enjoy things, but do so in a moderate degree.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Community: does faith require others/community?<ul><li>The Protestant view argues that this is not needed.<ul><li>Luther: “Every man is his own pope.”</li><li>A Kierkegaardian view.</li></ul></li><li>The Catholic view is that a central church is needed.<ul><li>There needs to be one universal church so that people will stop killing each other, since they won’t have any more personal religious differences</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Morality/confession:<ul><li>The Lutheran view is that people do not need moral guidance and are free.<ul><li>Suspending the ethical, the individual; can’t be confessed to others since they are uniquely individual.</li></ul></li><li>The Catholic view is that people do, and thus they have created institutions for such guidance.<ul><li>Every person gets moral guidelines by the church that has universal moral guidelines.</li></ul></li><li>The former view means crushing isolation, and the latter means the loss of moral agency.<ul><li>The example given of this is the mysterious stranger, who is totally isolated in his guilt. This guilt is only removed once he confessed to the whole village.</li><li>Hell is not fire and brimstone: it is an existential state of absolute isolation.</li><li>Confession is what allows us to exit this existential isolation, since it brings together the heavenly (the desire to be good) and the earthly (the reality of the crime). This represents the synthesis of the two aspects that have been introduced in the previous sections of the book as a contrast, i.e., the base and the noble, the insect and the Madonna.<ul><li>Confession is not suffering consequences for one’s actions. It is rather a process of alignment, bringing together one’s feels and the way the world is around them.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Miracles:<ul><li>The Lutheran view is that people don’t need miracles.<ul><li>But it is ok to perform miracles if it doesn’t coerce belief.</li></ul></li><li>The Catholic view is that miracles are needed to ensure that people continue to believe in the religion.</li></ul></li><li>Mystery?</li><li>Authority?</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">The Grand Inquisitor</p><p>The most painful secrets of their conscience, all, all they will bring to us, and we shall have an answer for all. And they will be glad to believe our answer, for it will save them from the great anxiety and terrible agony they endure at present in making a free decision for themselves.</p></blockquote><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Religion is compatible with science\">Religion is compatible with science</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments\">One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is the root cause of immorality\">Lying is the root cause of immorality</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely\">The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular\">It is easy to love humanity in general, but difficult to love people in particular</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sincerity means coming to terms with one’s baser side\">Sincerity means coming to terms with one’s baser side</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority\">Suffering can serve as a vehicle for moral superiority</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering is good, if done correctly\">Suffering is good, if done correctly</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moral mastery is achieved through choice\">Moral mastery is achieved through choice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love\">Intellect is a way to rationalize selfishness; impulsiveness is needed to love</a></li><li>Eternal validity: the moral struggles and the identity that makes someone who they are.<ul><li>Smerdyakov lacks this. </li></ul></li><li>Five levels of guilt:<ol><li>The wishing for death (Dimitri)</li><li>Allowing for it to happen, turning a blind eye (Ivan)</li><li>Setting it up, creating the logistical pathway (Ivan)</li><li>Physically telling someone to do it, paying them (Ivan?)</li><li>Committing the act (Serdyukov)</li></ol></li></ul><h4>Meta</h4><ul><li>To give an argument that Dostoevsky want to be convincing, he:<ul><li>Makes the people in the story acknowledge the truth of an argument.</li><li>He puts an argument in the enemy of that view.</li></ul></li><li>Dostoevsky embarks on a journey to demagicalize Christianity since a magical faith coerces belief.<ul><li>At the same time, he is not against miracles. He just thinks that they should be used out of love, not as a demonstration of power.</li></ul></li><li>Dostoevsky utilizes physical sickness as a demonstration of mental sickness:<ul><li>Grushenka gets sick because of her suffering under her spiritual transition with the help of Alyosha.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a><ul><li>This is similar to the point that is made by the Grand Inquisitor, and the first antimony.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>Ibid. (act in moderation)</li></ul></li></ul>","The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The main benefit of the Catholic church lies in the fact that it is able to ensure a separation of spiritual and political power such that the church is able to use its universal spiritual power to order the political world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Comte’s view of the Catholic church.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the Catholic church is supremely and universally involved in spiritual matters, its monopoly on this power allows for it to be able to mediate earthly affairs in accordance with its moral principles. In this way, it is both able to maintain order and also allow for progression in society by aligning the world more closely with Christian morality.</p><ul><li>By Comte’s time, however, the spiritual power of the church had diminished greatly, and no longer was able to act in the way specified here.</li><li>Specifically, also, what Comte is interested in is the <em>organization</em> of the church, not necessarily the beliefs of the church.</li></ul><p>In Comte’s philosophy, the role that the church serves is one of the organization of the three “levels” of human thought: theological, metaphysical, and positive. The organization of the Catholic church is preserved, but as a vessel for positivist ideas.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Protestantism represents a deviation this ideal. While there is “progress” in the sense that people are able to engage in free inquiry, such a system lacks social order, since social order is incompatible with the continual discussion of the very foundations of society by people who are not competent enough to be able to engage in such discussions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a><ul><li>It is precisely the Catholic church that enables this final stage to be a positive one.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since the content that they were testing for in the exams were largely outdated (based on Neo-Confucian teachings that were millennia-old at this point) and also that they were not an effective means of bettering the citizenry.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The abolishment of the civil service exams and the failure to erect an institution to replace it that served similar functions was cited as one of the reasons for the fall of the Qing.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The civil service exams were:</p><ul><li>largely still testing the Four Books and Five Classics, and were focused on classical scholarship, rather than modern technical material, and </li><li>focused merely on producing an elite, rather than primary education, which other successful countries at the time had implemented.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>The exams outlived the doctrine that justified them.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>Abolition removed the ladder that produced this class.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>This note argues that, specifically, near its end, it was not really, or not really effectively.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>Outlines the true purpose of the civil service exam system.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The ultimate purpose of the imperial examination system was not to find the best bureaucrats for the Chinese states, but to ensure that Chinese elites worked within the system, rather than against it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the ulterior reason for the imperial examination system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In essence, if a Chinese elite worked their lives in order to participate in the exams, they would not be able to counteract the state, since there is in fact a method by which the state accommodates them, though it is very difficult. That the system was not truly meritocratic can be seen through the fact that</p><ul><li>only the rich would have had the resources to sit for the exams, and </li><li>the exams often tested subjects that are not related to administrating.</li></ul><p>In fact, the end of the Chinese imperial state (1912) was closely proceeded by the abolishment of the imperial exams in 1905.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Who Was the Most Consequential Emperor in Chinese History?</p><p>Because all talent and ambition flowed into one state channel, <strong>little was left to build anything outside of the state</strong>. Yes, Keju raised China’s literacy, but <strong>no independent intelligentsia formed</strong>; nothing like a Chinese Royal Society or an independent Cambridge University came into being. <strong>Commerce was overshadowed, and rival ideas — Legalism, Daoism, Buddhism — were crowded out.</strong> Keju preempted alternative ideas and ideologies before they could organize.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>The stated justification for the exams, as against the political function argued here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>The class that integration actually produced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a><ul><li>This note argues against this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Argued against Fukuyama’s thesis of the “end of history” while presenting a culture-centric view of geopolitics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important work on foreign relations that continues to impact foreign policy of nations such as the US today.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition\">Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion\">The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Despite some civilizational convergence, there is no universality in culture\">Despite some civilizational convergence, there is no universality in culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Soft power is premised on hard power\">Soft power is premised on hard power</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The late 20th century is marked by a drastic increase in religiosity across the world\">The late 20th century is marked by a drastic increase in religiosity across the world</a> z</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture\">Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself\">A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While nuclear weapons served to unite the world under a common order in the past, today, it serves to fracture the global system\">While nuclear weapons served to unite the world under a common order in the past, today, it serves to fracture the global system</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mexican immigration to the US is unique compared to immigration to the US from other countries\">Mexican immigration to the US is unique compared to immigration to the US from other countries</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Conflicts arise when a rising power begins to overtake an existing hegemon\">Conflicts arise when a rising power begins to overtake an existing hegemon</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Conflict is sharpened by differences in culture\">Conflict is sharpened by differences in culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade brings people into contact, not agreement\">Trade brings people into contact, not agreement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon\">States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China will dominate in East Asia\">China will dominate in East Asia</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic states are far more likely to engage in violence than non-Islamic ones\">Islamic states are far more likely to engage in violence than non-Islamic ones</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete\">The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US should not blindly abide by its foreign policy commitments made during the Cold War\">The US should not blindly abide by its foreign policy commitments made during the Cold War</a></li></ul><h4>Three Rules for Foreign Policy in the Future</h4><ol><li><strong>Abstention rule:</strong> Core states must abstain from intervening in conflicts of other civilizations.</li><li><strong>Joint Mediation rule:</strong> Core states ought to negotiate to end fault line conflicts as much as possible.</li><li><strong>Commonalities rule:</strong> All civilizations ought to search for common values, institutions, and practices and emphasize them in international dealings with each other.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics (book)\">Politics (book)</a><ul><li>Another book that regards the study of politics.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If determinism is true, then acting otherwise at a moment of decision would require either the laws of nature or the prior state of the world to have been different, and since neither is within anyone’s power, no one is ever able to do otherwise.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the strongest incompatibilist argument, and it is what a categorical analysis of ability has to answer.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Lewis replies by distinguishing two theses. On the weak thesis, an agent is able to do something such that, were they to do it, a law would have been broken; on the strong thesis, the agent is able to do something that would constitute or cause a law being broken. Only the strong thesis is incredible, and the compatibilist needs only the weak one. Van Inwagen objects that the weak thesis is too weak to be worth having, since it lets one affirm both that an agent is able to act and that a necessary condition of that action fails to obtain.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>The conclusion this argument supports.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will\">The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will</a><ul><li>The weaker analysis this argument was designed to bypass.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Dutch Angle can be used to convey a sense of disorientation and confusion with the surrounding world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Dutch Angle can be used in files in order to show the disorientation of a viewpoint, or character, or world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a way to elicit feelings of discomfort in the viewer through the use of cinematographical techniques.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>The Dutch Angle is used extensively in <em>The Third Man</em>.  </p><h4>References</h4>","The EU provides a framework for Libertarian reforms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Many of the legal and economics frameworks setup in the European Union allow for it to be used in a Libertarian manner.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Currently, the framework is being used to push a left-wing agenda, even though it has the potential for a Libertarian system to be propagated.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>There are laws that ensure an open market and free trade between countries in the EU.</li><li>There are many laws in the EU that ensure individual protections.</li><li>There are many laws in the EU that allow for deregulation to occur.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved\">Free trade is generally beneficial for those involved</a><ul><li>This is the principle underlying the free trade agreements of countries in the EU.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>This is the principle underlying protections for individual rights.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This is the principle underlying the laws that enable deregulation.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Efficient Market Hypothesis cannot account for market anomalies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH) is not able to account for market anomalies such as the Momentum Effect or the January Effect.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates that there are moments when the market becomes inaccurate predictors of all information relating to an asset.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Market anomalies exist for three main reaons:</p><ol><li>Limits in risk models: if there are inaccuracies with the basic models of the EMH, then what may actually be risk hedging could appear as if they were anomalies.</li><li>Arbitrage is not always risk-free and frictionless.</li><li>Humans are not perfectly rational and they may make mistakes or suffer data issues.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a><ul><li>What markets do when they are well structured.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Financial momentum is predictive of the outcome of a prediction market\">Financial momentum is predictive of the outcome of a prediction market</a><ul><li>An anomaly that disobeys the EMH.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutional investors ensure that individual firm are correctly priced\">Institutional investors ensure that individual firm are correctly priced</a><ul><li>Helps provide price information in markets.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Frankfurt Cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Frankfurt cases argue against the Principle of Alternative Possibilities (PAP).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The PAP is a core tenet of libertarian schools of thought. If it is invalidated, then the libertarian school of thought will be damaged.</p><h4>Argument</h4><ul><li><strong>The Counterexample:</strong> Frankfurt describes a scenario where a neurosurgeon implants a chip in your brain. If you try to vote for Party A, the chip forces you to vote for Party B. If you choose to vote for Party B on your own, the chip does nothing.</li><li><strong>The Result:</strong> You voted for Party B on your own. You are responsible. Yet, because of the chip, you <em>could not have done otherwise</em>. This suggests freedom depends on the <em>source</em> of the action, not the options available.</li></ul><h4>Consequences</h4><p>Two conclusions follow if the cases work. The ability to do otherwise is not necessary for moral responsibility, and freedom is a property of the actual sequence rather than of the alternatives that were available, so what did not happen is irrelevant to whether the agent is responsible.</p><h4>The Dilemma Objection</h4><p>The standard reply is that the cases face a dilemma. If the agent’s decision is determined, the incompatibilist simply denies responsibility and the case begs the question. If it is undetermined, then the agent could have decided otherwise after all, and the alternative has not been removed. Either way the case fails to show what it claims.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility\">Disputes about free will are usually disputes about the nature of moral responsibility</a><ul><li>Why the cases target responsibility rather than freedom directly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past\">The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past</a><ul><li>The incompatibilist argument these cases are meant to sidestep.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Frankfurt School shaped postmodernism and modern progressive activism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that modern progressive activism was shaped by the Frankfurt School, though many of the core leftism tenants are perversions of the original thought of the Frankfurt School.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the ideological origins of the modern progressive activism movement.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The Frankfurt School practiced <em>negative dialectics</em>, which involved describing a concept by what it is not.</li><li>The Frankfurt School opposed political activism, since they opposed propaganda and the adulteration of their philosophy with propaganda.</li><li>They also invented the method of examining society not just through the economic substructure, but also, directly from the cultural superstructure.</li></ul><p>The modern progressivism has adopted the second of these and has weakened the central philosophical core of the first move through critique as merely critique, and nothing more. Herbert Marcuse was the person who took Frankfurt School philosophy out of the abstract and began advocating for political activism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)\">The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)</a><ul><li>This is part of the influence of the Frankfurt School.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Future of Freedom":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This book talks about democracy, freedom, and constitutional liberalism, and related concepts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This book presents an alternative view for that is common thought of in our view of democracy and freedom.</p><h4>Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy\">Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American democracy is too populist\">American democracy is too populist</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Glorious Revolution expedited the Industrial Revolution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Glorious Revolution gave the English parliament, and thereby, the English landowners much more power. This allowed them to pass legislation that directly benefitted them, and also the country as a whole.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains one of the reasons why industrialization occurred in English around this time, but no where else.’</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are several reasons pointed to as how the Glorious Revolution impact English industrialization:</p><ol><li>Fundamentally, the Glorious Revolution increased the legislative efficiency of Parliament, meaning that more legislation can be passed.</li><li>This went into the passage of enclosure acts, which secured/centralized land holdings for landowners, and this meant that there was far less land use inefficiency (this allowed for economies of scale).</li><li>Increased efficiency meant that Parliament was able to take a strategy of dealing in detail with cases of estate laws. Previously, in order to avoided the descends of a family from splitting off the estate (which may cause inefficiency, though this was not the main reason for the passage of these laws), many positive expected value investments were forgone. Now, with increased inefficiency, Parliament was able to engage with these cases more on an individual basis, and thus allow for productivity-improving requests to be granted.<ol><li>Sometimes, these improvements meant improvements to transportation, such as the construction of new ports or canals, and this decrease in transportation costs was also crucial.</li></ol></li><li>Specifically, turnpike trusts were established, which allowed for local landowners and merchants to form a non-profit in order to invest in their nearby roads. This greatly improved the ability for materials to be transferred across the nation, and thus lead to industrialization.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Property rights should not be too powerful\">Property rights should not be too powerful</a><ul><li>They were too powerful before the Parliamentarian intervention and evaluation of individual cases.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Since the Industrial Revolution, inventions have occurred more readily\">Since the Industrial Revolution, inventions have occurred more readily</a><ul><li>Perhaps the increase in Parliament’s efficiency also lead to more patents?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge\">Before the Industrial Revolution, there was a disconnect between propositional knowledge and prescriptive knowledge</a><ul><li>Related to the former?</li></ul></li></ul>","The Hojo clan fell due to resentment from other warrior families, the Mongol invasion, and resentment from the emperor":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Hojo fell from power due to resentment from the other warrior families, as the Hojo held de facto power in Japan as the regents for the Kamakura, the cost of the defense against the Mongols, and the allying of the emperor with enemies of the Hojo.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the fall of the Hojo clan, and the rise of the Ashikaga shogunate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li></ul>","The House of Representatives is a more populist institution compared to the Senate":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The House of Representative is a more populist institution since its members are more numerous and are elected from smaller districts. This means that they more directly represent the interests of their constituents. The Senate, on the other hand, represents larger populations, and lacks many of the rules that constrain the House, most notably the ability to end filibustering. This is since the Senate is considered a more deliberative body.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the difference between the two chambers of the US Congress.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The committee system in Congress enables specialization and increases efficiency\">The committee system in Congress enables specialization and increases efficiency</a><ul><li>This is a system that employed in Congress. Specifically, this connects to the Joint committee, which is where members from both houses meet together.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself\">Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself</a><ul><li>This is characterized by this note, since it describes the differences between the two branches.</li></ul></li></ul>","The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for promoting Western neocolonial interests":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The IMF and World Bank have been criticized for enacting “Western neocolonialism” because the loans require neoliberal reforms in the form of austerity measures.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the criticism of the IMF that is often levied by the global South.</p><h4>References</h4>","The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exam represents a meritocracy, as it allows people to achieve high ranking government positions through hard work, rather than simply through nepotism. This sets China apart from many other civilizations of the world in a similar time period.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the highly efficient Imperial Chinese state institutions, as the Civil Service Exams ensured that only the most qualified applicants are able to make it into government.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>Its meritocratic nature was more apparently early in its existence, since by the Qing, it was certainly the case that the examine was no longer meritocratic to the same extent that it was earlier.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>This could partly be sustained by the meritocracy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>They were formed through meritocracy.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>This prevents another perspective on the issue.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Imperial Chinese state’s monopoly on knowledge prevented a scientific revolution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The reason for the lack of a Chinese scientific revolution is the fact that China held a strict monopoly on scientific innovation which prevented the advancement of science that would have kicked-started such a revolution.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the reason for the lack of a scientific revolution in China despite the high levels of scientific knowledge that they had and should have been able to accrue.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The study of science was first and foremost largely reduced to a predictive endeavor, since the political nature of science meant that there was no reason for the state to adopt a causal-theoretic model. What’s more, the individuals that could afford to learn about science on their own were the scholar-officials with deep ties to the state, which means that they have a vested interest in not letting knowledge to the public which may damage the regime. Astronomy, for instance, was something that was banned to be studied by the general public.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>Once they are integrated, they do not want science to be able to de-stabilize the regime and lead to the loss of their power.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Japanese healthcare system is highly socialized":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In Japan, the healthcare system is highly socialized, as people are forced to purchase healthcare by the government. Additionally, the government set all prices for medical procedures. At the same time, around 50% of Japanese hospitals were in debt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Japan’s healthcare system was radically different from many different countries, as they managed to merge a public and private system. At the same time, many of their hospitals were hugely in debt.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence\">The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence</a></li></ul>","The Japanese rail system is successful because of its unique business model and limited government intervention":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Japanese model of railroads have been particularly successful not because of culture specifically, but because of the unique business model of the Japanese rail companies, and the restraint of the government when it comes to regulation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can serve as an example other nations would follow when it comes to creating a well-ordered rail system.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The success of rail in Japan doesn't have to do with culture.</li><li>On the national scale, there are six large private rail networks as a part of the Japanese Rail (JR) system.</li><li>They all have a unique business model where they don't just operate trains. They also have a bunch of side businesses that generate a lot of revenue. In fact, the railroad business (although it is almost always profitable) usually generates only a plurality or small majority of the revenue.<ul><li>They essentially serve as city builders, where they develop rural land with train lines and houses and stuff (that they construct), and thus (1) make the land valuable, (2) provide a guaranteed consumer base for their train, and (3) provide from real estate and other businesses that they then run.</li><li>To make this work they also have smart land use where a lot of the land was reserved for parks and forests and stuff, to increase living quality.</li></ul></li><li>Railroads generally work the best in areas of high urban density, since they have high throughput.</li><li>Overall, Japanese cities are not actually as dense as their European counterparts or cities in Korea.<ul><li>What makes them unique is their hyperdense centers.</li></ul></li><li>What allowed for the development of this is their liberal development control systems compared to the West.<ul><li>It is really easy to do land adjustment in Japan, since all it takes is that 2/3 of residents and landowners agree, and they can replan the land.</li></ul></li><li>There is not an implicit subsidy for car ownership in Japan like in the US.<ul><li>Japan has private parking (people can't park on public land), and someone who buys a car must first prove that they have private land where they can park their car.</li></ul></li><li>Vertical integration of the Japanese train companies ensures that companies are run efficiently.</li><li>The generous price cap on train fares ensure that companies actually make a profit, and thus actually have an incentive to maximize ridership.</li><li>They can also make their own decisions about timetables, service patterns, and day-to-day operations.<ul><li>In other countries, this is inefficiently done by government.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities\">Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities</a><ul><li>This is perhaps what is happening in Japan.</li></ul></li></ul>","The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The July Revolution of 1830 was a revolution against Charles X, due to his poor management. This was due in large to two changes carried out by Charles, namely the <a href=\"https://www.wikiwand.com/en/articles/Anti-Sacrilege_Act\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anti-Sacrilege Act</a> and the provision of indemnities for those whose properties were seized during the First French Revolution, in addition to overall poor administration by Charles.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This example showcases the important of ensure that adequate and correct reforms are passed, because otherwise, the people may just rebel.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Yang of Sui&#x27;s excessive spending, disregard for human life, and general poor management led to the collapse of the Sui\">Yang of Sui's excessive spending, disregard for human life, and general poor management led to the collapse of the Sui</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill\">Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill</a></li></ul>","The LSRL always goes through the mean x and y values":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The least-squares regression line always goes through the means of the x and y values ($\\bar{x},\\bar{y}$).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a property of the LSRL, which may be useful for an AP Stats test.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The lower the variability around the regression line, the higher the coefficient of determination\">The lower the variability around the regression line, the higher the coefficient of determination</a></li></ul>","The Labor Theory of Value is false":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Labor Theory of Value is false.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a theory that is argued for my Marx, and forms the basis of Marxist economics.</p><h4>Counterarguments</h4><ol><li>“<strong>Intrinsic Value” is non-Popperian</strong>: The idea of Socially Necessary Labor Time as something that is only able to be considered through price is unfalsifiable. It is entirely constructed based on the prices observed in the market ex-post.<ul><li>A good that sells at a low price has a low SNLT, and a good that sells at a high price has a high SNLT. Both these are only determined after the fact, and thus can be defined as anything someone wants.</li></ul></li><li><strong>The Transformation Problem</strong>: <ul><li>Marx states the Law of Value: since profit only comes from “surplus value,” or laborers who are paid below their marginal product, a labor-intensive venture should have higher profit margins than a capital-intensive venture.</li><li>He also states the Law of Equal Profit: capital moves around the market until profit equalizes across all industries.</li><li>The problem is that how can every capitalist have the same amount of profit even if they have different amounts of labor use?</li><li>To solve this, Marx introduces the transformation, which says that firms that are more labor intensive are contributing to firms that are more capital intensive. <ul><li>He does this by defining the Price of Production: $Constant \\ Capital + Variable \\ Capital + Average \\ Profit$. And Value: $Constant \\ Capital + Variable \\ Capital + Surplus \\ Value$</li><li>When you subtract the two, labor heavy firms lose value, while capital heavy industries gain the value lost by the capital heavy industries (assuming that capital does not create value).</li></ul></li><li>The problem here is that the price of constant capital changes due to the that surplus value adds to the price of goods needed as raw materials for production. This means that any moment, the total price in the economy is greater than the total value, meaning that capital must have created value, disproving the labor theory of value.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Joint Production</strong>: When multiple goods are produced at the same time, any attempt to quantify the labor value between the goods produced at the same time is arbitrary.</li><li><strong>Homogenous Labor is false</strong>: Labor is not homogenous. The labor of a doctor and the labor of janitor cannot be quantified the same way.</li><li><strong>Natural Resources and Scarcity Rents:</strong> Factors such as the local supply of something also determines the price. A bottle of really rare wine, although it might have taken the same amount of labor as many other wines, is nonetheless valued more because it is rare.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The effects of capital deepening are uncertain\">The effects of capital deepening are uncertain</a><ul><li>Typically, capital deepening is viewed as a way for economies to grow. Under the LVT, however, it seems actually constrict growth.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Late Roman Republican period was characterized by a series of increasingly powerful and independent military commanders":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Late Republican period was characterized by generals who essentially had armies that were independent of the control of the Senate, which allowed them to seize power more easily.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was part of the reason for the fall of the Republic.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The late Roman Republic saw a lot of generals take control of armies since they were personally paying them (as a result of the professionalizing reforms of Marius), which meant that their loyalty was to the particular general, not to the state. This meant that, when the time came, the generals could leverage their personal armies to directly challenge the state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians\">The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians</a><ul><li>Another reason for the fall of the Republic.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Legalist school taught the theory and method of organization and leadership, not jurisprudence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Legalist school of China was one focused on teaching people the theory and method of organization and how to wield power, not jurisprudence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common misconception about the Legalists.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While the Legalists emphasized the importance of adherence to law in administration, they did not specify what specific laws the ruler should implement. Rather, they argued that the laws should be designed to fit the specific circumstances of state, and that competent officials with such capabilities ought to be the ones designing such laws.</p><h4>References</h4>","The Luck Objection argues that indeterminism does not support Libertarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Luck Argument argues that indeterminism (i.e., that actions are not pre-determined) means that actions are the result of randomness. If that is the case, control over one’s actions is still nonexistent. Therefore, indeterminism does not actually support the case for Libertarianism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important critique of indeterminism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>The incompatibilist premise the objection targets. Granting incompatibilism, libertarianism still does not follow.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism\">Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism</a><ul><li>A stronger conclusion in the same direction, since it denies free will whether or not compatibilism holds.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Mongols took ideas from the peoples that they conquered":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Just like how The Romans learned from the people they conquered , the Mongols did the same thing, adopting the advanced siege machinery invented by other civilizations and tolerating their ideas regarding civic society and religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Their tolerance and adaptation is part of the reason that they were so successful in their conquests.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Romans learned from the people they conquered\">The Romans learned from the people they conquered</a></li></ul>","The Oedipus complex appears in many works of literature":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The pattern Freud named the Oedipus complex, in which a child’s attachment to one parent is coupled with hostility toward the other, recurs across literature written long before the theory existed, which is what makes psychoanalysis usable as an interpretive lens rather than merely as a clinical one.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If the pattern predates its description, then either it is a real regularity of family life that authors observed independently, or it is loose enough to be read into anything, and which of those holds determines whether psychoanalytic criticism has any evidential value.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Hamlet’s solicitude toward his mother and hatred of his uncle fit the pattern directly, and Ophelia’s relationship with her father is a second instance within the same play.</li><li>Read this way, Hamlet’s repression of his own thoughts explains his inaction, which is otherwise the hardest feature of the play to motivate.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed\">The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed</a><ul><li>A competing explanation of the same inaction, which does not require the psychoanalytic apparatus.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Old South was reluctant to change after the Civil War":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Antebellum South was reluctant to change and accept a new position in the world after their defeat in the Civil War, which manifested in ways both culturally and politically.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the persistence of many Antebellum features of the South well past the end of the Civil War.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This was explored through literary treatment of the topic, for instance through <em>TSTF</em>:</p><ul><li>One reading of the book is that it is 3 failed attempts to tell one story is metaphorical of the fact that the three brothers represent three ways the South should not deal with its trauma.<ul><li>Benji is stuck in the past, with no conception of the future. He is literally castrated.</li><li>Quentin is stuck mourning the past in the present, and this mourning drives him to commit suicide.</li><li>Jason is able to see the future, but his worldview is incredibly cynical and he sees the world as being cosmically unjust. He is unable to come to terms with past, present, or future.</li></ul></li><li>All three characters struggle with a loss of innocence/purity.<ul><li>Benji is unable to process this, and thus is the least affected.</li><li>Quentin kills himself because he realizes such a loss is permanent and there is nothing he can do.</li><li>Jason fights against the world and tries his best to preserve his purity/honor, but fails.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Partition of Jin ended the Spring and Autumn Period":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Spring and Autumn period was ended with the partition of the Jin, as the three dukes that ruled under the Jin gained enough power as to establish kingdoms for themselves.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This triggered the beginning of the Warring States period, during which there was large scale warfare, as states began to more openly fight against each other.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power\">The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained\">Decentralized systems develop better; centralized systems are more easily maintained</a></li></ul>","The Polish economic miracle is largely a result of EU benefits and a diversified economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Polish economic miracle occurred largely as a result of EU cohesion funds, which it effectively invested into infrastructure and agriculture; the EU single market, which allowed for the free flow of goods and capital across Europe; and a diversified economy, which helped it weather tough economic storms.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Poland’s economy is as strong as it is, given its background as a former Soviet state.</p><h4>References</h4>","The Practical Syllogism motivates us to perform some action":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Practical Syllogism consists of the major premise (regarding a universal), a minor premise (regarding the particular), and the concluding action.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the tool that Aristotle uses to diagnose action, and why even when people know that they ought to do something, they don’t do that thing.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><strong>Major premise:</strong> being late to school is bad.</li><li><strong>Minor premise:</strong> I’ve hit the snooze button on my alarm clock twice now, and it is a Monday morning.</li><li><strong>Action:</strong> \\[gets out of bed, dresses, and heads to school].</li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>The final conclusion of the syllogism isn’t some knowledge. It is the action itself, the action necessitated by the major and minor premises. Additionally, the syllogism can fail. There are three broad categories when this happens:</p><ol><li><strong>Potential:</strong> we are disabled from being able to our knowledge of the world due to external circumstances (e.g., we are asleep).</li><li><strong>Partial:</strong> we understand the universal principle, but fail to see the specific case that relates to the universal.</li><li><strong>Physiological:</strong> Our passions have physically altered our state (e.g., we are drunk), making us temporarily unable to graph reality.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires\">Continence is the ability to for one’s reason to overpower one’s desires</a><ul><li>The continent person is able to keep desire from hijacking his usage of the Practical Syllogism by desire.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control\">A variety of factors could cause us to be incontinent, or lack self-control</a><ul><li>The incontinent man has desire disable his perception of the particular in a circumstance, and thereby leads him down the wrong path.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily\">Licentiousness is acting wrongly voluntarily</a> <ul><li>The licentious man’s universal premise is corrupted, and thus, the action resulting from the syllogism is likewise corrupted.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Precipice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A wide-ranging argument that humanity faces an unprecedented existential risk in the 21st century, but also has a real chance to avert catastrophe if it acts wisely. Ord examines major threats such as nuclear war, engineered pandemics, unaligned artificial intelligence, and other “existential risks,” then explains why their combined danger is alarmingly high compared with past centuries. The book’s core message is both urgent and hopeful: we are living at a uniquely fragile moment in history, and by investing in risk reduction, global cooperation, and better long-term decision-making, we can protect the future of civilization and the vast potential of humanity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Ord argues that, if you do not take the problem of existential risks serious, we may face a permanent loss of our potential, which would be very bad.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time\">Humans are uniquely able to cooperate across time</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise\">Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should never halt technological innovation\">We should never halt technological innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem\">Protection from existential risks faces a tragedy of the commons problem</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity\">Climate change poses an existential risk for humanity</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Proposes the thesis that capitalism was able to flourish due to what Weber termed the “Protestant Work Ethic.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This book serves as one of the foundational texts of sociology, and proposes an influential thesis that is this impactful today.</p><h4>Ideas &amp; Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement\">The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth\">The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of predestination in Calvinism provides a methodical rationalization of moral life\">The idea of predestination in Calvinism provides a methodical rationalization of moral life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism\">The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement\">The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Reformation did not engender the spirit of capitalism\">The Reformation did not engender the spirit of capitalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul><h4>References'</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Protestant Work Ethics is a work that sought to refute the claims of Economic Determinists\">The Protestant Work Ethics is a work that sought to refute the claims of Economic Determinists</a></li></ul>","The Protestant Work Ethics is a work that sought to refute the claims of Economic Determinists":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Marx’s original claim of the economic substructure that underlaid the cultural superstructure is refuted (intentional or not) by Weber’s work in the Protestant Work Ethic.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This serves as a refutation by Weber of Marx’s strict conception of economic determinism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In essence, although many of Weber’s claims are spurious, he at least showed that a non-trivial amount of influence is exerted on the economic substructure by the cultural superstructure. This undermines Marx’s claims.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems that if Weber’s claims are even in the slightest true, then the entire chain of causation, either forwards or backwards between culture and economics, is broken. Therefore, it seems to be to invalidate conceptualizing society by simplifying its causal factors to these two.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Frankfurt School shaped postmodernism and modern progressive activism\">The Frankfurt School shaped postmodernism and modern progressive activism</a><ul><li>One of the major innovations of the Frankfurt school is their acknowledgment of the influence of culture on economics.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Rashidun conquest was made possible by geography and unique political economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The conquests of the Rashidun were made possible by the fact that the geography of the area that they conquered was similar to the geography of the area in which they originated. Additionally, implemented a unique tax system that incentivized people to become Islamic, which both served to raise tax revenues and gain converts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the rapid rise of the Rashidun state.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Some other factors that allowed for their rapid conquest include the weakness of the surrounding states, the Byzantines and Sassanids having recently fought the “Last Great War of Antiquity.”</p><h4>References</h4>","The Reformation did not engender the spirit of capitalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Reformation was not what initially created the spirit of capitalism. Rather, it only contributed to the “qualitative and quantitative” expansion of the spirit across the world. He claims that the Protestant Ethics is only one among many causes of the development of the modern view of capitalism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Weber stresses the idea that the Reformation did not create the spirit of capitalism, as it evident by the fact that capitalism existed, and even flourished, in many parts of the world before that point.</p><h4>Cultural Determinism</h4><p>Many today argue that Weber’s work is a work that espoused Cultural Determinism, the idea that certain cultures are destined to create certain institutions (in this case, that Protestants are somehow predisposed, as a result of their cultural practices, to create modern capitalism). This is not what Weber attempts to do in his book. He simply claims that Protestantism had an impact in the way that capitalism had been shaped, not that Protestantism is the root of modern capitalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The Revenue Equivalence Theorem states that any auction where the highest bidder wins will generate the same expected revenue for the seller":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that no matter the auction format, as long as the auction is one whereby the highest bidder wins, the expected revenue generated through the auction remains the same.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important law in Auction Theory and serves as the basis for understanding the type of auctions that exist and should be used in particular instances.</p><h4>Conditions</h4><p>There are four conditions for the Revenue Equivalence Theorem to hold true:</p><ol><li><strong>Risk Neutrality</strong>: Bidders are indifferent to risk, only caring about maximizing profit. This means that they will not bid aggressively to ensure that they win.</li><li><strong>Independent Private Values (IPV)</strong>: Each bidder’s valuation of an item is not influenced by the valuation of any other bidder.</li><li><strong>Symmetry</strong>: All bidders draw form the same probability distribution, so that there is no “strong” or “weak” bidder. Every bidder’s values essentially have to have the same range (same probably distribution), and all bidders have to have the same risk profile.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Polybius’ Histories is a 40-book work that explains how Rome rose from a regional power to dominate the Mediterranean in little more than a century, especially through the aftermath of the Second Punic War and the defeat of Macedon and Carthage. Polybius argues that this success was not accidental but came from Rome’s mixed constitution, military discipline, political institutions, and ability to absorb and adapt to change. He writes as a Greek statesman and hostage living in Rome, so the work also serves as a practical guide to understanding power, causation, and the interconnected nature of world events, with a strong emphasis on eyewitness inquiry, political realism, and lessons for future leaders.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main sources for historians with regards to the rise of the Roman Republic.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>Historical Narrative</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hannibal was an excellent tactician, but poor grand strategist\">Hannibal was an excellent tactician, but poor grand strategist</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Divided leadership compromises the effectiveness of an army\">Divided leadership compromises the effectiveness of an army</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cavalry were effective in ancient warfare because of the mobility asymmetry that they created\">Cavalry were effective in ancient warfare because of the mobility asymmetry that they created</a></li></ul><h5>Political &amp; Military Insights</h5><p>Polybius argues that political systems follow this cycle:</p><ol><li>People follow the strongest leader, and one leader eventually starts a <strong>kingship</strong> where they are virtuous. </li><li>The kingship deteriorates into a <strong>tyranny</strong> and the people overthrow it.</li><li>The people establish an <strong>aristocracy</strong>, which is likewise corrupted and becomes an <strong>oligarchy</strong>.</li><li>People overthrow the oligarchy and establish a <strong>democracy</strong>.</li><li>The democracy eventually deteriorates into anarchy, and the cycle repeats.</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction\">The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Romans learned from the people they conquered\">The Romans learned from the people they conquered</a></li></ol><h4>Tactics Compendium</h4><ul><li>The use both a contravallation and circumvallation in the Siege of Agrigentum by the Romans, a tactic that would later by employed by Caesar during the Gallic Wars (specifically the Siege of Alesia).</li><li>The use of the <em>corvus</em>, a long, hinged stick with a metal hook at the end, which would have been used against Carthaginian vessels to pull them closer to the Roman vessels and thus neutralize the power of their fleet.</li><li>Hannibal’s crossing of the Alps and the Arno Marshes, which allowed him to penetrate deep into Roman territory without encountering much resistance.</li><li>At the Battle of the Trebia, Hannibal ensures that when the time came for the armies to engage, while the Romans had to ford a river and were left without eating breakfast, the Carthaginians were well-prepared.</li><li>At the Battle of Cannae, the use of a convex line which drew the Romans in and eventually produced a concave shape that allowed for the envelopment of the Roman army.</li><li>At the Battle of Ager Falernus, although Hannibal was trapped in the Capuan valleys, he was able to use a herd of oxen with torches attached to their horns in order to distract the Romans and escape.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The structure of the government of the Roman Republic was designed in such a way as to balance the power of its various elements, so that the government is more structurally sound.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the efficiency and power of the Roman Republican government.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The three parts of the government: the Senate, the Consuls, and the people all balance each other, since they each hold powers which check the others’ power.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium\">Systems naturally tend toward equilibrium</a><ul><li>When any one branch of the three parts becomes too powerful, its power is checked by the other two branches.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Romans learned from the people they conquered":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the key features of the Romans were their readiness to adapt. They would adopt the most effective means of accomplishing something, even if it meant copying from their enemies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains their success in creating such a large empire and ruling it successfully.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The Romans adopted the Greek style of cavalry which provided better equipment for them and allowed the units to be more useful in combat.</li><li>They also copied the Gladius from Spanish tribes which they fought against.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Romans switched to a comprehensive defense strategy as centralized states developed along its borders":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>With the development of powerful tribal confederations along the Northern border, the arrival of central Asian nomadic groups, and powerful Eastern states, Rome had to utilize a comprehensive defensive strategy rather than the client state system as they had done before.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the evolution of Rome’s defense system with the passing of time, as the prior system of client systems stopped working with the development of centralized states along Rome’s borders.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following the Crisis of the Third Century, the Roman military became centralized around the emperor\">Following the Crisis of the Third Century, the Roman military became centralized around the emperor</a><ul><li>This explains the final evolution of Roman border defense, as the military became concentrated around the emperor, rather than being spread across the frontier.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Romans utilized a client state system during the Julio-Claudian era to maintain border to defense":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since the states on the borders were able to actually be turned into client states during the Julio-Claudian era, and the territories that the clients protected where the “un-Romanized” parts of the empire,  it didn’t matter much when the clients failed, allowing for this system to function.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explained success of the empire at defending its border during the Julio-Claudian era, and how even while defending vast borders, they were able to amount concentrated offensives against enemies.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the tribes that occupied the front were largely unorganized and able to be brought in as client states, this system was viable. Additionally, this represented a positive feedback loop whereby more client states meant stronger border defense/greater offensive ability, which meant the ability to accumulate even more client states.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Romans switched to a comprehensive defense strategy as centralized states developed along its borders\">The Romans switched to a comprehensive defense strategy as centralized states developed along its borders</a><ul><li>This is the result of later developments that made the defense strategy explicated in this note impossible.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Savings and Loan Crisis was caused by interest rate shocks, risky investments, and regulatory failure":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Savings and Loans Crisis was caused by interest shocks, risky investments by the thrifts, and the slowness to act of regulatory institutions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The failure of the Savings and Loan Crisis can teach us something about how to ensure that a market does not fail do to external pressure, and how to regulate markets well.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The surge in inflation during the late 1970s led the Fed to raise rates significantly, which meant that thrift had to pay much higher rates, driving their profit margins into the ground. Deregulation during the period also led to the thrifts making much more risky and speculative investments, which meant that they were at a higher chance of failure when their investments failed. The regulatory institutions also failed in their attempt to ensure the stability of the market, as they were underfunded and bureaucratic, slow to update regulation to adapt to changing circumstances.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation\">Markets help allocate resources efficiently, mobilizes savings, fund innovation</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li></ul>","The Sound and the Fury":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Tells the story of the Compsons as they descend from prosperity to despair in the American South.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the great American works.</p><h4>Themes</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People often obsess over abstractions\">People often obsess over abstractions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Old South was reluctant to change after the Civil War\">The Old South was reluctant to change after the Civil War</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to focus on the present and not remain bogged down in the past\">One ought to focus on the present and not remain bogged down in the past</a></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Benjamin Compson</strong></p><ul><li>Severely mentally disabled, only able to moan, scream, and cry. Unable to speak.</li><li>Views the world through associative memory rather than linearly through time.</li><li>Views the world of the Compsons as “good” and the outside world as “bad.”<ul><li>Is only able to see the outside world through Caddy.</li></ul></li><li>He interprets engagement with the outside world as “muddying,” and is unable to accept it when it occurs.</li><li>Benjamin is unable to understand the adult world, specifically the idea of cultivating one’s personal appearance and understanding one’s place in the world among others.<ul><li>He doesn’t like it when Caddy smells of perfume (something that covers up her natural smell) and instead prefers that she smell like trees.</li><li>He constantly moans in the presence of others, despite people’s dislike of such behavior.</li></ul></li><li>Benjy is unrealiable because he<ul><li>Has an inventive to portray the people that he likes in a better light that they actually might be</li><li>And he is unable to mentally comprehend everything that is going on.</li></ul></li><li>\"Idiot\" is a formal developmental term that categorizes someone as being stuck at the mental development of age 2.</li><li>The sense of smell is important in the book, because while Freud said that as civilization developed, smell has been replaced by sight, Faulkner disagrees.<ul><li>Through the character of Benji Faulkner portrays the importance of smell in our understanding of the world, even if at a base level.</li></ul></li><li>Benji demands that Caddy stay with him, physically and mentally (developmentally).</li><li>But this is an impossible demand. <ul><li>Caddy starts wearing perfume after she reaches adolescence.</li><li>Caddy is caught kissing a boy.</li></ul></li><li>Benji is essentially holding Caddy's development hostage.</li><li>Caddy losing her virginity is the breaking point for Benji, since that is not something that Caddy can wash away. It is permanent.</li><li>Benji demands that time stands still. He wants the world the freeze with him. He represents the past.</li></ul><p><strong>Candance Compson</strong></p><ul><li>Caring and represents the point of intersection between the Compson world and the outside world.</li><li>She is the conduit whereby the Compson boys are able to view the world beyond just their home (through the contrast between her innocence and her “spoiling”).</li></ul><p><strong>Quentin Compson</strong></p><ul><li>The fact that he focuses on his shadow is because he wants to focus on the passage of time, since that is something that is important to him.</li><li>A martyr for the cause of purity.<ul><li>Not a hero</li></ul></li><li>Quentin kills himself in order to freeze his grief (at the loss of innocence of Caddy) in time.<ul><li>He breaks his watch.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><ul><li>Each of the women represents something:<ul><li>Ms compson: the past</li><li>Miss Quentin &amp; Caddy: the present</li><li>Dilsey: the future</li></ul></li><li>In this way, each of the female characters has a match with the male characters.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>Both works deal with themes of fate and time.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Soviet Union left an heritage of institutional corruption and state control":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The collapse of the USSR left a lasting mark on the institutions of Russia, since elements of statism and corruption continued in the country. This is continuing to have an adverse effect on the country, politically and economically, today.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This continues to effect the functioning of the Russian state, and explains, perhaps partly, why the USSR fell back to into autocracy soon after leaving it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a><ul><li>Russia was not successful in this third wave of democratization perhaps due to their institutional heritage.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This is the case in Russia.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a><ul><li>But sometimes they can coincide/influence/change each other?</li></ul></li></ul><p>-</p>","The Stranger":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Meursault, a French Algerian clerk, learns that his mother has died and attends her funeral without showing much emotion. He starts a relationship with Marie, befriends his neighbor Raymond, and becomes involved in Raymond’s conflict with some Arabs. One day at the beach, Meursault later goes back alone, meets one of the Arabs, and kills him with a gun after being overwhelmed by the heat and sunlight. He is arrested, put on trial, and sentenced to death, with the court focusing as much on his behavior and lack of feeling as on the murder itself.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the most important novels which explores the philosophical position of Absurdism.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it\">To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome\">Absurdity manifests through cleavages which are impossible to overcome</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Absurd man pursues merely aesthetic value\">The Absurd man pursues merely aesthetic value</a></li></ul><h4>Philosophy</h4><p>In a sense, what this book is trying to say is that if the conventional view of the world is as being in two dimensions of “value” and “aesthetics,” the most sensible way to approach the world is to collapse the dimension of “value.” The apparent meaninglessness of the universe provides no evidence of the objectiveness of values and thus, gives us no reason to maintain a belief in such a socially constructed facade. Although doing so would mean isolating oneself from the community, who continue to hold such values, such isolation ought to be of no concern. Although the community will condemn the person who is Absurd that their philosophy is fundamentally destabilizing and corrupting, it seems to capture the truth in a more accurate manner. To care about the condemn nation of others is to concede that there is at least one value which exists. In realizing that other people are likewise simply corpses waiting to be buried is comfort. The understanding of the fact that the universe itself is meaningless already provides a sense of fraternity. To hold no values and yet enjoy aesthetics is a life that is possible to live, and indeed, it seems rational to live as such.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Absurdity can be revealed either by estranging the mundane or by inventing the impossible\">Absurdity can be revealed either by estranging the mundane or by inventing the impossible</a><ul><li>A characteristic of Camus’ writing, contrasted with Kafka.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Taiping Rebellion failed due to internal instability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The main reason for the failure of the Taiping Rebellion was the Tianjing Incident whereby most of the effective Taiping leadership was either killed, or fled. This doomed the state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A competing claim could be made that the Taiping rebellion was destroyed due to pressure from the Qing, though this note argues that the larger factor was an internal one.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Due to the growing power of Yang Xiuqing, the East King, Hong decided to order Wei Changhui, the North King to come in and kill Yang and his supporters. But then, Wei also attempted to seize power, though Shi was able to counter him. In the end, this episode merely weakened the Taiping leadership.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Successful rebellions need a constructive element\">Successful rebellions need a constructive element</a><ul><li>This note described what ultimately lead to the downfall of the Taiping, not their lack of a constructive element.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Thin Theory of the Good serves as a standard framework for agents to consider in the original position":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Thin Theory of the Good is the framework that is adopted in the original position in order to judge the outcomes of particular arrangements of society.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the framework that is used in the original position in the bargaining.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Agents in the original position can’t know about that particular circumstances of the society that they are, though they need some way of predicting approximately the good that will be considered by the people operating in the society afterward, and therefore, they adopt the most conservative conception of that good, which is the Thin Theory. The Thin Theory of the Good would be in line with the principles of justice since:</p><ol><li>In a well-ordered society, there would be social pressure to do so.</li><li>In such a society, we cannot pinpoint an attack on anybody, meaning that one is disincentivized to act rashly and aggressively towards others, and thereby abide by the principles of justice.</li><li>According to the Aristotelian Principle, people are complementary for each other such that the total capability of people together outweighs people’s individual capabilities. However, in order to maintain the well-ordered society in which this occurs, the principles of justice need to be maintained.</li><li>The Kantian Interpretation states that the desire to act justly and the desire to express our nature as free and independent persons in one in the same. Both principles would be chosen in the original position, and best allow us to express ourselves as rational and equal persons.<ul><li>We can differ in our expression of our nature as free and rational persons, but we cannot weigh the sense of justice against other ends.</li></ul></li></ol><p>The indeterminacy of the Thin Theory is acceptable given its fundamental requirement to be coherent with justice, and given that, any other variations are innocuous.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>In short, this is very similar to Rawls’ matter on the issue as well.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Third Front allowed for the Reform and Opening Up to occur":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Third Front is a period of massive infrastructure development which spread human and physical capital around the nation, ultimately making the Reform and Opening Up possible.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The Reform and Opening Up, an important moment in Chinese history, was only possible due to the preexisting infrastructure that is constructed during this period of time.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a><ul><li>The reason that China was able to succeed in this project is due to their strong centralized state.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Third Man":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Holly Martins, an American writer who arrives in war-torn, divided Vienna after being invited by his old friend Harry Lime—only to discover that Lime has supposedly died in a traffic accident. Suspicious of contradictory accounts surrounding the death, Holly investigates and becomes entangled with Lime’s lover, Anna, and British officer Major Calloway. His search eventually reveals that Lime is alive and involved in a ruthless black-market scheme selling diluted penicillin, forcing Holly to confront the moral corruption of someone he once admired. Set amid Vienna’s ruins and shadowy sewers, the film explores friendship, betrayal, love, and the uneasy boundary between innocence and complicity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This film explores themes of existentialism and tragedy in an entertaining and profound manner.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a><ul><li>While <em>The Third Man</em> is not exactly a tragedy, it could be argued that in his pursuit of Harry’s killers, Holly alienated himself from his love interest, Anna.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>Finding out about the truth regarding the condition of his friend Harry meant that Holly now had to consider a dilemma between his loyalty to his friend and his moral duty.</li><li>For Anna, the truth does not matter. She is unceasingly loyal, and thus, the dilemma does not occur. Holly’s pursuit of the truth, in fact, is what drives her  away from him, as Anna views Holly as disloyal and a traitor.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects\">A strict deference to moral values may have intended negative side effects</a><ul><li>This is perhaps both true for Holly and Anna?<ul><li>Holly’s determination to find out about Harry and turn him in is what prevents him from getting together with Anna.</li><li>Anna’s obstinate desire for Harry obscures the morally ignoble things that Harry has actually done.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><ul><li><strong>Harry</strong> <ul><li>Loves others, but he is willing to sacrifice others in order to save himself.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Holly</strong> <ul><li>Loves others, and he is willing to sacrifice a lower ethics (betraying his friend) in order to achieve a higher ethics (catching a murderer). </li><li>He is dangerous because he is so innocent, he cannot understand the motives of all of the evil people out there.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Anna</strong> <ul><li>Loves others, and is unchangeable in her love. </li><li>She does not change her love for Harry even after she learns that he was a murderer. </li><li>She is not ethical. She is viewing things through the individual perspective, not the universal perspective. </li><li>Her principle of non-betrayal is beyond the ethical. It is a categorical rule outside of the ethics.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Calloway</strong><ul><li>Cannot understand anything outside the ethical either. He trumps unconditional commitments with his professional work. </li><li>He is not callous; he is professional. </li><li>He is sober and professional, where as Holly is cowboy and professional.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>This work explores similar themes with regard to tragedy and truth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Frankenstein (book)\">Frankenstein (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Three Represents marked a major ideological shift for the CCP":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Three Represents (三个代表) marked a shift of party ideology from class struggle to economic development, helping the party align with the rapid economic development that has occurred in China in the past decade.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important chances to party ideology, since it marked a major shift from the ideology of previous Chinese leaders.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>By allowing the wealthy elite into the party, the CCP successfully integrated the economic elite into the party system by arguing that entrepreneurs were essential to “advanced productive forces.” This was done partly to ensure the survival of the party, by essentially co-opting the emerging economic elites and intellectuals, ensuring that these powerful people were on their side.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China&#x27;s basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens\">China's basic social contract is that the government gets autocratic powers, in exchange for positive rights for citizens</a><ul><li>Part of the positive rights that the social contract guaranteed was economic growth, and the party needed to ensure that the economic elite were on their side in order to ensure that the social contract could be maintained.</li></ul></li></ul>","The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US has a marginal tax system, meaning that for each additional dollar of income earned, that dollar is taxed at a higher rate. In essence, this means that the US has a progressive taxation policy. The US does not have a flat tax system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important to understand, as many people misunderstand how the US tax system works, believing it to be a flat tax rate. This is important to understand when analyzing the US tax system.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a></li></ul>","The US should embrace Western culture and reject multiculturalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US is made weaker by embracing multiculturalism, which undermines the core Western civilization that underlies the US. Ultimately, this makes the US directionless and weak.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents an argument against multiculturalism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The US should instead embrace its civilizational roots and reconnect with Europe through things like free trade agreements in order to revitalize Western civilization. Specifically, Huntington states that the US must:</p><ul><li>Integrate militarily, politically, and economically with other states of the West.</li><li>Incorporate into NATO &amp; EU more Western (culturally speaking) states in Europe.</li><li>Encourage alignment of Latin American states with the West.</li><li>Restrict the military development of Islamic and Sinic states.</li><li>Slow the drift of Japan away from the West.</li><li>Accept Russia as the core state of Orthodoxy.</li><li>Maintain Western technological and military superiority.</li></ul><p>Most importantly, the US must <strong>recognize that Western intervention in the affairs of other civilizations is counterproductive and almost always detrimental to US interests in the long run.</strong></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a><ul><li>Huntington argues that this gap needs to be closed to ensure civilizational stability.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a><ul><li>Due to the inherent differences in culture with respect to the way that different cultures view politics, US intervention in places across the globe that have different cultures then is necessarily counterproductive.</li></ul></li></ul>","The US should not blindly abide by its foreign policy commitments made during the Cold War":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US should reevaluate the foreign policy engagement that it made during the Cold War era to see if they are still valuable today, now that the borders of conflict have shifted to civilizational ones, and not ideological ones.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This note is calling for a re-evaluation of US foreign policy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Many of the foreign policy commitments made by the US during the Cold War were shaped by the ideological conflict during the Cold War, and thus do not reflect the new world order that is civilization-based. Examples of such involvements include:</p><ul><li>American alliance with Japan.</li><li>American alliance with Pakistan.</li><li>The West's alignment with Turkey.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a><ul><li>This is why the US cannot continue with his ideology-driven commitments made during the Cold War.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a><ul><li>With these military changes, there should be commensurate foreign policy changes.</li></ul></li></ul>","The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The US betrayed its civilizational allies through the Bosnian War due to American feelings of idealism, moralism, humanitarianism, naivete, and ignorance towards the Balkans.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the US betrayed its civilizational allies during the Bosnian War.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Due to the ability of the Bosnian Muslims to present themselves as victims, they were supported by the US, despite their commitment of war crimes to a similar degree as Bosnian Croats and Serbs.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"NATO militarily intervened in Bosnia in 1995 due to the Srebrenica massacre\">NATO militarily intervened in Bosnia in 1995 due to the Srebrenica massacre</a><ul><li>The proximate cause this note downplays. Srebrenica is the strongest case that the intervention was a response to events rather than to ideology.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Upanishads teaches that all things are one in Brahman":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A collection of sacred texts composed by anonymous thinkers from around 800 to 400 B.C.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most significant texts for Hinduism.</p><h4>Contents</h4><p>Discusses the idea of the Brahman, the World Soul, and the final and ultimate reality, which is the primal unitary energy that infuses all things. Essentially, the idea that \"All Things Are One.\"</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman\">The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman</a></li></ul>","The Variability Hypothesis states that males exhibit a wider spread in many traits":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Variability Hypothesis essentially states that males have a greater range in terms of their trait exhibition (ex. IQ, physical traits, personality, spatial reasoning) compared to females. This may be due to how makes are the heterogametic sex ($XY$ chromosomes).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has been used to explain many differences between the two sexes, though the theory is disputed.</p><h4>References</h4>","The West is in decline":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The West is current in a state of decline, which, although it could take a long time and experience fluctuations, will ultimately progress.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a crucial aspect of Huntington’s thesis in <em>Clash of Civilizations</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The West is lacking in several crucial power resource:</p><ul><li>Territory and population.</li><li>Economic production.</li><li>Military capacity.</li></ul><p>Huntington characterizes this as a relative decline, whereby the rise of the rest is outpacing the growth in power of the West, not directly as a weakening of Western power.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>An example of the decline of the West is there inability to enforce Western morality upon other countries. For instance, in Asia, the West has not been able to successfully enforce a universal application of human rights. This is due to</p><ul><li>The rising influence of Asian economic power, which Western businesses desperately want to work with.</li><li>The fact that Asian nations work together in order oppose what they see as Western encroachment on their sovereignty.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a><ul><li>This is part of the reason.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion\">The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is the reason why it is so difficult for them to enforce their ideology on others now that the technological differential has disspated?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>The rejection of Westernization is the result of the decline of the West.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a><ul><li>A consequence of the decline of the West.</li></ul></li></ul>","The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The dominance of the world in the past by the West was the result of superior technology, and not the result of superior values or technology, as some have claimed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes the idea that the West's culture is superior compared to the culture of other civilizations.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>But there is no evidence that the reason that the West had superior technology is not because their value/religion promoted the development of such things?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies\">Chinese society is generally more receptive to new technologies</a><ul><li>If this note is the case, it could mean that China (due to their more positive view of new technology) could come to dominate the world in the future.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>If it was actually superior values, it seems that people would not then reject these values.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Yan'an Rectification Movement solidifies Maoism within the CCP":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Yan'an Rectification Movement, carried out during the Second Sino-Japanese war, solidified Mao’s position in the CCP, and maintained ideological unity in the party. It marked a split with Soviet Marxist thought at the time, and established a cult of personality for Mao.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is when Mao’s cult of personality was established, and when the CCP officially had an ideological split with the USSR.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Maoism advocates for a &#x27;bottom-up&#x27; revolution, while Leninism argues for a &#x27;top-down&#x27; revolution\">Maoism advocates for a 'bottom-up' revolution, while Leninism argues for a 'top-down' revolution</a><ul><li>This is a crucial difference between Maoism and traditional Marxist-Leninism practiced in the USSR.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Arguably, the main reason for the collapse of the Han was the weak central state and the increasing power of warlords across the nation. Due to the central government’s inability to quell the Yellow Turbans, they had to call for help from regional warlords, greatly strengthening their power, eventually allowing then to challenge imperial power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a main reason for the collapse of the Han dynasty, which threw China into the Three Kingdoms Period.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society\">China is characterized by a strong state, while India is characterized by a strong society</a></li></ul>","The alignment tax on AI makes the models too altruistic in economic distribution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The alignment tax on AI makes models more altruistic than they ought to be. This means that they often sacrifice economic efficiency for the sake of equity or altruism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that, straight out of the box, AI models are not best able to ensure the most efficiency distribution of resources.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The AI misalignment problem may be self-fulfilling\">The AI misalignment problem may be self-fulfilling</a><ul><li>Perhaps if we make AI less misaligned, then we could reduce the AI alignment tax and thus make models generally more geared towards rational economic thinking.</li></ul></li></ul>","The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the existence of evil in the world makes it unlikely that a perfectly loving God actually exists.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the powerful arguments against the existence of God.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h4>In Literature</h4><p>This argument is espoused by Ivan Karamazov from <em>The Brothers Karamazov</em>.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The around 60% of China’s total trade goes through the Malacca Strait":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Around 60% of China’s total trade and 80% of its crude oil is imported through the Malacca Strait. This means that for China to have strategic access to the strait is extremely important to ensure that they would not be easily isolated from trade if a rival controls the strait.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for understanding China’s actions in the South China Sea and how they are diversifying the source of their imports.</p><h4>References</h4>","The basic structure is not responsible for inequalities in associations which one enters voluntarily":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because an association which one enters into voluntarily is not a contingency of any kind (rather, it was a choice made by a rational agent), the basic structure does not provide protections against inequalities in such situations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains Rawls’ views on voluntary associations.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it\">An act is voluntary when an agent chooses to engage in it</a><ul><li>A definition of voluntary, per Aristotle.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly\">One cannot be treated unjustly voluntarily; one also cannot treat oneself unjustly</a><ul><li>Again, Rawls shares this similarity with Aristotle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The basic unit for nomadic peoples was the tribe":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The basic unit of many nomadic groups was the tribe. This meant that power almost always fell to the strongest, since the tribe’s survival was dependent on the leadership of the leader, and weak leaders would be abandoned.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This was the context in which mongol society developed, which speaks to the way that they approach life and warfare.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies\">Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies</a><ul><li>What Genghis Khan had to overcome: the tribe was the default unit, and unification meant superseding it.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere\">Because the Mongols originated from harsh weather, they were able to fight everywhere</a><ul><li>The environment that made the tribe, rather than the settlement, the viable unit.</li></ul></li></ul>","The benefactor typically loves his beneficiary more than the other way around":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that benefactors love their beneficiary more than the other way around.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is used to explain why parents love their children more than the other way around, and lay down a rule about the relationship between benefactor and beneficiary.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is because the maker typically loves his work more than his work loves him, since bringing into existence is a choice.</p><ul><li>Additionally, a maker of something is defined by the thing that he makes. I.e., we exist through our activity.</li><li>Craftsman and poets love their creation. In the same way, the benefactor loves the beneficiary.</li><li>Giving is noble, whereas receiving is merely advantageous.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A friendship that is based on mutual virtue is both long lasting and fruitful.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle considers this form of friendship to the best of all of the types of friendships.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Friendships based on mutual virtue are perfect.</p><ul><li>They last for a long time, since virtue is a disposition and dispositions are not easily changed.</li><li>They take time to develop, since two people need to prove to each other that they are deserving of love.<ul><li>A person ought to be loved by another both universally (generally, absolutely, based on general goodness/pleasantness) and contingently (good/pleasant for themself).</li></ul></li><li>They encompass both the friendship of utility and of pleasure, since good people are both useful and pleasant to other good people.</li><li>This is the only type of friendship where there is true selflessness, where you wish good for the friend for the friend’s sake, not for personal gain.<ul><li>This connects back to the principle Aristotle states, which is that it is more important to love than to be loved in a friendship.</li><li>When one wishes well for a friend, it is out of a moral state (disposition), not just a passing feeling.</li></ul></li><li>These types of friendships are rare.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved\">Friendship based on mutual utility or pleasure are easily dissolved</a><ul><li>Friendships of virtue can be contrasted with friendships of utility or pleasure.</li></ul></li></ul>","The bias of researchers manifest clearly through their research (typically left)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The left-wing biases of researchers manifest through their research, as was proven through a study whereby teams of scientists were given the same data on immigration, yet around 2/3 of the researchers found positive outcomes for immigration. Additionally, the personal biases of the researchers correlate positively with the outcome of their research.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that much of the science that comes out of researchers, especially in social science and humanities fields could potentially be biased.</p><h4>Notes</h4><ul><li>This does not regard a particular orientation, since while left-leaning researchers tend to find conclusions that support their political belief, right-leaning researches tend to find conclusions that support their political belief.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"American white males are underrepresented in academia and media in lower-level positions, and overrepresented in upper level positions\">American white males are underrepresented in academia and media in lower-level positions, and overrepresented in upper level positions</a><ul><li>In fact, this is the case even in hiring.</li></ul></li></ul>","The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Traditionalism, or a lack of desire to accumulate capital, is capitalist spirit’s greatest enemy. It is in essence a rejection of the entrepreneurial spirit and in general a rejection of the desire to improve. It is also a rejection of the divine calling that Weber attributes for the genesis of the capitalist spirit.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This concept is used to contrast the capitalist spirt with the “status quo\" at the time, which Weber analyzed as being traditionalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>Traditionalism is a rejection of exactly this calling, which is why the two are opposed.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a><ul><li>If the religious motive drops away, it is unclear what remains to hold traditionalism off.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While many point to the problem of incentives as the biggest problem facing systems like communism, these problems can usually be solved with coercion and violence. The bigger problem that is unsolvable in a non-market system is the problem of economic calculation, as since state-run economies lack market signals, their capital allocation is extremely inefficient.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the greatest problem that faces a state-run economy, important for when analyzing such economies.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The common explanation for why socialist economies don’t work is that they have an incentive problem, that without proper compensation, people lack the incentives to do stuff like in a capitalist economy. While this is certainly part of the problem, the bigger problem is that of economic calculation. Without freedom to allocate capital freely as individuals, capital will not be allocated to their most productive uses, meaning that the system will not be efficient.</p><h4>Capital Markets</h4><p>A solution that is sometimes proposed is to introduce a market mechanism for consumer goods such that people are able to bid on those goods, but nationalize the means of production. This does not fundamentally solve the economic calculation problem, however, since here is no way to determine the allocatively efficient use of capital when capital is centralized. Without prices, the marginal value of heterogeneous capital cannot be found, and thus, economic calculation cannot be performed.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy\">Private property is a prerequisite for an allocatively efficient economy</a><ul><li>This is the fundamental claim behind the Economics Calculation problem.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism has a knowledge problem\">Socialism has a knowledge problem</a><ul><li>Hayek makes a less absolute claim than the conceptual problem of economic calculation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Socialism cannot deal with change\">Socialism cannot deal with change</a><ul><li>A secondary problem with socialism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The blind are able to see what the sighted are not":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Being able to “see” something could often times mean more than simply being able to observe something concrete. It can often be the case that in works of literature, those who are blind are able to see the “truth” in a more direct manner than those who can physically see.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a type of symbolism that is common in many works of art and literature, especially of the blind prophet who can see much clearer than some other figure of authority who is able to see.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">How terrible—to see the truth when the truth is only pain to him who sees!</span><ul><li>The blind seer is the only one that is able to see the truth.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Let me tell you this.. You with your precious eyes, you're blind to the corruption of your life, to the house you live in, those you live with— who are your parents? Do you know?</span><ul><li>Although Oedipus is able to see, he is blind to the truth, the life that he actually lives.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">OEDIPUS: Riddles—all you can say are riddles, murk and darkness. TIRESIAS: Ah, but aren't you the best man alive at solving riddles?</span><ul><li>Not only is this line really funny, it again connects back to the idea that although Oedipus is able to see, he is unable to see the truth, only able to see “riddles, murk, and darkness.”</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Go in and reflect on that, solve that. And if you find I've lied from this day onward call the prophet blind.</span><ul><li>While again, the prophet mocks Oedipus’ ability to solve riddles, he again states his ability to see the truth, despite being physically blind.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">A prophet? Well then, free yourself of every charge!</span><ul><li>While Jocasta seems to disbelieve the nature of prophecies, after saying this, she tells a prophecy that is where Oedipus is first beginning to actually be concern about who he is.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">I have a terrible fear the blind seer can see.</span><ul><li>This is the moment when Oedipus realizes Tiresias’ power.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Brave words, and you'd persuade me if mother weren't alive. But mother lives, so for all your reassurances I live in fear, I must.</span><ul><li>It seems that Oedipus has entirely been convinced by the power of the prophecies, even after Polybus’ death, which seemed to invalidate the premise of the prophecy according to Jocasta.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">You, you'll see no more the pain I suffered, all the pain I caused! Too,long you looked on the ones you never should have seen, blind to the ones you longed to see, to know! Blind from this hour on! Blind in the darkness—blind!</span><ul><li>The moment Oedipus realizes the truth, he blinds himself. </li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Who will marry you then? Not a man on earth. Your doom is clear: you'll wither away to nothing, single, without a child.</span><ul><li>Here, after Oedipus has been blinded, he is able to correctly predict the future for his children, at least Antigone.</li></ul></li><li>During the entirety of the play when Oedipus was sighted, he was entirely unable to even see the true nature of his family. When he finds about the truth coincides with then he blinds himself. </li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a><ul><li>The blind are usually able to see true fate.</li></ul></li></ul>","The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Unlike what might seem logical at first, the capitalist spirit was developed to a far greater extent in the heavily protestant North of the US, rather than the South, many of which were founded with the sole purpose of generating income.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explain the importance of religion (specifically Protestantism) when it comes to the development of the capitalist spirit.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a><ul><li>The Calvinist doctrine that the North carried and the South did not.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Explains the puzzle: the commercial South was more traditionalist despite being more commercial.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism\">The Protestant Work Ethic and the Spirit of Capitalism</a></li></ul>","The city is the fundamental political unit":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The very fundamental political unit is the city, and it comes before considerations regarding things like the household, since the household is like an appendage of the city, and without the body (the city), the appendages cannot survive.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explores the importance of the city, since it is the most fundamental political unit.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Things like justice, which require political awareness, are only possible in a city.</p><h4>References</h4>","The committee system in Congress enables specialization and increases efficiency":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The committee system in Congress enables specialization, since individual congress people are able to work in the area that they are most familiar with. At the same time, it increases efficiency since the people most familiar with a topic are able to work on that topic with a small group of other people proficient in the topic, and many of these groups can work at the same time.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the benefits of a key institution in Congress.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself\">Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself</a><ul><li>A related characteristic of Congress.</li></ul></li></ul>","The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fully realization of the concept of justice arises when it manifests in a city, between free and equal people under the law. Justice may exist elsewhere, but only in a weaker form or in analogy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s unique ontology of justice, something that shapes his ethics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice in its most realized sense is political, and regards free and equal persons who share a common life directed towards self-sufficiency under the rule of law. There are two types of political justice:</p><ul><li>Legal justice: arises out of the particular circumstances of the time, which produced unique laws which one must obey.</li><li>Natural justice: arises naturally; the intuitive morality for justice in each individual.</li></ul><p>Domestic/household justice is similar to political justice, since it also specifically regards a community of people. However, the household is hierarchical, with a basis of authority and necessity, not law and reason. Therefore, it is not relevant in a discussion of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Aristotle's Taxonomy of Justice</span><ul><li>Defines political justice contrastively with other forms of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","The concept of ‘good institutions’ is either tautological or unsupported":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the thesis that “good institutions” lead to economic growth is false because the concept of “good institutions” is fundamentally mistaken.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This rejects the thesis that there is such a thing as “good institutions,” or that it does not prove a causation that “good institutions lead to economic growth.”</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since it is the case that rich countries have many of the things that we associate with “good institutions,” it doesn’t mean that those things are necessarily the cause of the countries being rich. There is the risk of missing confounding factors and also the possibility of reverse causation. Overall, the concept of “institutions” is too vague. A better method of study is to look at specific institutions instead.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>The counterpart note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The claim that an agent could have done otherwise if they had chosen otherwise analyses the freedom of the action, not the freedom of the choice, so it leaves the disputed question untouched.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the standard objection that forces compatibilists off the simple conditional and toward a categorical account of ability.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There is a second and more damaging problem. Consider someone with a severe phobia who would never choose to go outside. The conditional is satisfied, since were they to choose to go out they would go out, yet we do not want to say they are able to choose otherwise. Satisfying the analysis is therefore not sufficient for the ability it is meant to capture.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism\">Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism</a><ul><li>The position this analysis was built to defend.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past\">The Consequence Argument holds that acting otherwise would require altering the laws of nature or the past</a><ul><li>The problem that the categorical replacement then inherits.</li></ul></li></ul>","The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The congruence problem is easier solved with contract theories than utilitarian ones since the utilitarian view is too demanding.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the argument for a contract view and against utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two ways in which utilitarianism is too demanding:</p><ol><li>It requires too much sacrifice in order to maximize aggregate gains. It means that one could have to sacrifice, theoretically, nearly all of their ability to accomplish their plan of life in order to maximize the overall utility.</li><li>Utilitarianism makes no guarantee of the basic level of rights that an individual should have, meaning that if it maximizes utility to remove some people of their rights, it would be justifiable to do so. It seems that a rational person would hardly agree to something like this.<ul><li>Having rights and abiding by the principles of justice is fundamentally regulative under contract theories.</li></ul></li></ol><p>Additionally, utilitarianism cannot account for things like love, which may cause individuals to act in non-utility-maximizing ways.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Another branch of Rawls’ attack against utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The consumer chooses to consume the amount that makes the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods equal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is known as the Equimarginal Principle. Due to the law of diminishing marginal utility, when choosing between two goods, we optimize utility by choosing to consume the amount where the marginal utility per dollar of the two goods is the same.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This law allows us to understand how to maximize utility.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This intuitively makes sense because if one of the goods has a higher marginal utility per dollar, than we would shift our consumption from the other good to that good in order to maximize utility. The mathematical formulation of this principle is as follows: $$ \\frac{MU_{x}}{P_{x}}=\\frac{MU_{y}}{P_{y}} $$ This model can actually account for many different things since we can consider one of the goods to be a composite good, i.e., money. Then the mathematical formulation would be as such: $$ \\frac{MU_{x}}{P_{x}}=\\lambda $$ Where $\\lambda$ represents the marginal utility of income.</p><h4>References</h4>","The decline in the labor share of capital is related to a decline in construction productivity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The decline in the labor share of income can be partly attributed to the decline inn construction productivity and the rise in real housing prices (the two are complements).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This worsens inequality and stunts economics growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Due to the stagnant productivity in the real estate market, the real price of buildings have risen. Since workers have to be housed in buildings, this means that the labor share of income would fall.</li><li>The Baumol effect is also at play, since while manufacturing productivity has skyrocketed, construction productivity has not. </li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Baumol&#x27;s Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries\">Baumol's Cost Disease slows economic growth due to increasing input costs in low productivity industries</a><ul><li>This effect is at play in the case of this problem described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This principle is given evidence by this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The development of viable political institutions lags behind social and economic change":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The development of viable political institutions often comes after economic and social changes, since it is these changes that eventually lead to the creation of new political institutions. This means that, in the period before the new political institutions are created, there is naturally a period of instability.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the relationship between the development of political institutions and economic and social change.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When economic and social changes occur, they make drastic changes to the way that people live. Therefore, people demand political changes in order to accomodate for these changes, and often, this fuels political radicalism. An example of this is during the late 1800s and the early 1900s, during the second industrial revolution, when this lead to a wave of new radicalism in the form of Communism and Facism.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>But it seems that the rise of liberalism could also have been explained by this, specifically, by the industrial revolution? So the “political radicalism” is not necessarily bad, and thus this argument essentially still rests on the assumption of the supremacy of liberalism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location\">Institutions designed to correct a flaw tend to reproduce that flaw in a new location</a><ul><li>But do institutions actually work to solve problems?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Good institutions are the source of economic growth\">Good institutions are the source of economic growth</a><ul><li>This note seems to be in line with the linked note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end\">Government institutions, once established, are difficult to end</a><ul><li>Here is another problem with instituting new institutions.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle embodies the idea that natural contingencies are commonly shared between members of a society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle is fundamentally an expression of the idea that the natural talents that we are born with (natural contingencies) ought to be shared in society, such that they are used to achieve the greatest common good, which means aiding the worst-off.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This highlights a difference between the difference principle and the principle of redress. While the latter focuses on desert, the former focuses more on achieving the optimizing the expectations of the least advantaged.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>See another note that talks about the nature of the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle expresses fraternity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle expresses a sense of fraternity among the people who participate in it, which strengthens the social glue that holds everybody together.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the advantage that the difference principle offers.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the principle expresses a certain sense of concern for the less well off, similar to the way the concern is expressed in the family (where fraternity is most present), it can be said to generalize fraternity to the societal level.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses reciprocity\">The difference principle expresses reciprocity</a><ul><li>Another argument for the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle expresses reciprocity":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle expresses a social condition that is similar to reciprocity, i.e., a principle of mutual benefit.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This ensures that the difference principle is publicly justifiable to everyone.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Given that there are two individuals, $A$ and $B$, where $A$ is better-off than $B$,</p><ol><li>$B$ accepts being worse-off than $A$ since the difference principle ensures that $A$ being better-off helps him the most.</li><li>$A$ accepts helping $B$ since <ol><li>A’s being better-off is a result of the social institutions which exist, which $B$ is a part of, and </li><li>the idea that $A$ deserves his position presupposes a cooperative scheme rather than just justify it. One cannot appeal to desert to resist the difference principle, because desert only operates after the basic structure is already justified.</li></ol></li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle expresses fraternity\">The difference principle expresses fraternity</a><ul><li>Another argument for the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are a few reasons why the difference principle is preferred by Rawls over utilitarian principles:</p><ul><li>Utilitarian principles require that we make comparisons of cardinal values (utilities) across individuals, which is difficult to do.</li><li>Utilitarian principles compare subjective “utilities,” while the difference principle compares more objective and observable primary goods.<ul><li>People may have different utility functions.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Rawls uses the difference principle over something like utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For these reasons, Rawls thinks that it is better to embed the moral ideas directly into the principles, such as through the difference principle. Additionally, it seems difficult if not impossible to do an accurate utility calculation from behind the veil of ignorance, since you don’t know the utility function of the actual people in the society.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>Another means of satisfying such needs is through a more utilitarian framework, which is defeated here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle makes false assumptions about people's rationality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The difference principle lies at the heart of Rawls’ justice as fairness. However, it seems that there are some incorrect assumptions made by Rawls in outlining this principle.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes a core component of Rawls’ justice as fairness.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It seems that, behind the veil of ignorance, individuals would not choose the difference principle. Rather, with probabilistic reasoning, they would choose a basic structure which maximizes the average benefit.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a><ul><li>This note is a critique of the difference principle.</li></ul></li></ul>","The difference principle requires that expectations be close-knit and chain-connected":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls makes the assumption that there is</p><ul><li>chain-connected expectations, and </li><li>close-knit expectations.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>These assumptions help reinforce Rawls’ theories, through they need not actually be the case in order for justice as fairness to be possible.</p><h4>References</h4>","The division of labor degrades the mind, which independently justifies universal public education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Specialisation confines workers to repetitive tasks that erode the very capacities that made them capable, so a state may owe them education as a remedy for what economic organisation does to them, quite apart from any gain in output.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The argument grounds public education in a harm caused by the market rather than in the returns to human capital, which makes it available to someone who rejects the usual economic case for schooling.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Smith argues in this form, which is notable given how often he is enlisted against public provision. His economic policy has social and moral objectives and not merely the maximisation of national income.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a><ul><li>Accepts the obligation and disputes the monopoly.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should be entirely in charge of education\">The state should be entirely in charge of education</a><ul><li>The stronger position this argument is sometimes taken to support.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>The same conclusion from a virtue-based rather than a remedial premise.</li></ul></li></ul>","The effects of capital deepening are uncertain":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Capital deepening is an increase in the ratio of capital to labor (more capital for the same amount of labor), or an increase in labor productivity. This can also be referred to as an increase in capital intensity. It is often considered a necessary but not sufficient factor in economic growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Capital deepening is important in studying economic growth, as capital deepening is usually seen as important to economic growth.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Depending on the growth model, capital deepening can indefinitely lead to economic growth, or lose it potency due to diminishing returns.</p><ul><li>In the Solow model, the effect of capital deepening will gradually become more muted due to diminishing returns and depreciation.</li><li>In the AK model or some endogenous models, capital deepening can lead to indefinite growth, even without technological progress.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The starkest disparity between Calvinism and Catholicism is Calvinism’s rejection of salvation through the Church, .</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This feeds into Weber’s thesis regarding the capitalist spirit, as the idea of a divine calling, to which he attributes the capitalist spirit, is individualized, and thus, cannot appear in a Catholic setting.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>Removing sacramental salvation is what forces the calling to be worked out in worldly labour instead.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a><ul><li>The geographic evidence for this doctrinal difference producing economic consequences.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Happiness is the final goal of a human life, so one’s efforts should be directed towards achieving happiness in life. And to achieve happiness as Aristotle defines it, we must live a complete life of virtuous action (goodness), and live with comfortable material means.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the final goal of Aristotle’s ethical philosophy is to promote human happiness. This is what it attempts to achieve in his <em>Ethics</em>.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are three crucial components to living a good life:</p><ol><li>Sufficient material conditions.</li><li>Virtuous action of the soul (being a good human), enabled by (1).</li><li>A complete life (maintaining virtuous action through the entirety of one’s life).</li></ol><p>Only with these three components, is one able to be happy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>Aristotle’s method to achieving happiness is through long goodness.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Contemplation is the highest form of happiness\">Contemplation is the highest form of happiness</a><ul><li>The greatest form of happiness, the topic of this note, is a contemplative one.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation\">Happiness is identified with pleasure, with honor, or with contemplation</a><ul><li>Aristotle comes up with multiple different definitions for happiness, though the one he finds the most satisfying is above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness\">Sufficient material conditions are required for happiness</a><ul><li>Happiness not only requires virtue, but also comfortable living conditions. An elaboration of a concept form this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Another elaboration of a concept discussed herein.</li></ul></li></ul>","The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The end of the cold war produced many different effect, a major one of which is the decline of the military power of the West and associated problems.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This leaves room for other powers, specifically in East Asia and the Middle East, to rise to regional prominence.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, Huntington outlines five observations:</p><ol><li>The collapse of Russian military capabilities.</li><li>Decline of Western military power in general (decreased spending, etc.)</li><li>Increase in East Asian spending on military.</li><li>Military capabilities are becoming more eventually distributed across borders.</li><li>Security is increasingly being regionalized, such that future conflicts will be largely regional, not directly between superpowers.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development\">Liberal democracy may be the endpoint of ideological development</a><ul><li>Here is some evidence that could be used to undermine this claim.</li></ul></li></ul>","The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The erosion of Christianity means that the cultural foundations of many of the threads that holds the West together flay and thus crumble. Many of the institutions built upon Christian foundations are left without their original foundations, which weakens them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a factor in the weakening of the West.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>Even if this is correct, there are other reasons that Christianity should be kept around.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>A closely-related idea.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US should embrace Western culture and reject multiculturalism\">The US should embrace Western culture and reject multiculturalism</a><ul><li>Related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","The exercise of moral virtue lies in how we attain a goal":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that moral virtue is not about having the right end goal, it is about how one chooses to attain that goal, the actions that they undertake.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This ties into Aristotle’s larger philosophy regarding what is considered virtuous and how we attain virtue.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In this way, deliberation and choice are required in order for someone to be virtuous, since it is the job of deliberation to decide on the means, and it is choice which allows us to enact the means that we decide upon.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>When we exercise a moral virtue, it ought to be me in the mean between two excesses.</li></ul></li></ul>","The expectations of representative men are defined by the index of primary goods available to them":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The expectations of some representative person is determined by the primary goods available to them. This is derived from the definition of the good as one’s achievement of one’s long-term rational plan, which requires primary goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This allows us to pinpoint down what someone’s “expectations” means.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>A few things to consider when constructing the index which makes the construction easier:</p><ol><li>Equal basic liberties account for equality of rights and liberties.</li><li>Income and power are typically correlated.</li><li>Only the least advantaged group needs to be indexed at any time, as per the difference principle.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances\">What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances</a><ul><li>This is the definition referred to in this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The fact that Japan was not subjugated by the Mongols, while China was, led to their rejection of outside influence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fact that the Japanese were not conquered by the Mongols following two invasion attempts ended the prevalence of outside influence in Japan, as they began to believe that they were superior.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains in part the origins of Japanese isolationism, and their ideology of superiority that would play a part in WWII.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Hojo clan fell due to resentment from other warrior families, the Mongol invasion, and resentment from the emperor\">The Hojo clan fell due to resentment from other warrior families, the Mongol invasion, and resentment from the emperor</a><ul><li>Connected to the Mongol invasion.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take\">Geography can influence the way the manner in which states form and the actions they take</a><ul><li>The Japanese were able to stave off the Mongols because of their unique island geography.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Washington’s Farewell Address inspired American isolationism\">Washington’s Farewell Address inspired American isolationism</a><ul><li>Another case of isolationism, although developing in very different circumstances.</li></ul></li></ul>","The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One potential cause for the fall of the Roman Republic was that it failed to appropriately account for the interests of the Plebians, which led to the Plebian empowerment of rouge demagogues, which eventually lead to the downfall of the Roman Republic.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one potential cause of the fall of the Republic.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>I think that the direction of causation of unclear, i.e., whether it is the demagogues which enflamed the passions of the masses, or whether they merely took advantage of the circumstances and, in the name of the good of the masses, engaged in their reckless behavior.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction\">The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction</a><ul><li>It seems that Polybius’s prediction were actually right, as it pertains to the interpretation of the fall of the Republic here, since towards the tail end of the Republic, the people gained more and more power and eventually there was mass chaos, until the reinstitution of a dictator in the form of Augustus.</li></ul></li></ul>","The final stage of history is a positive one":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>That the final stage of human history is a world in which positive thinking dominants and becomes the absolute principle of human reasoning.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the three stages argument put forth by Comte.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Positivism was formulated by Comte and it forms the final stage in his three stage progression:</p><ol><li>Theological/fictitious: knowledge is formed through superstition/supernatural agents, and seeks first and final causes.</li><li>Metaphysical: replaces the supernatural entities with abstract agents.</li><li>Positive: abandons the search for first and final causes, and replaces most knowledge acquisition tools with a priori and a posterori ones.</li></ol><p>The prior stages are necessary: the theological stage is that which first stimulates the human mind such that people are actually thinking about the relevant problems, and the metaphysical stage is necessary to smooth the process of the abstraction of our logical tools. The positive stage began with Descartes, Bacon, and other thinkers of the scientific revolution. Comte argues that eventually, such thinking would permeate the social sciences/humanities as well as the natural sciences.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>In order for positivism to triumph over the other schools of thought, Comte imagines using the structure of the Catholic church, with its separation of the spiritual (or in Comte’s case, the “spiritual” philosophy of positivism) and the political serving as the basic social order of society. A few problems with such a conception:</p><ul><li>It seems very unlikely that we are able to stripe the Catholic church merely of its spiritual elements and replace with positivism.</li><li>He critiqued the ideals of the church as being arbitrary and vague, though his own ideas can also be ascribed such labels.</li><li>His own ideas display a form of providence (namely in achieving a positivist world), which is intricately tied to Christianity.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action\">The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action</a><ul><li>The Catholic church is required in order for this final stage of history to be able to be executed.</li></ul></li></ul>","The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If the first derivative of a function is equal to zero, test for the values around the point at which it is equal to zero. If the values left are positive and the right, negative, then the function has a relative max at that point. If the opposite, there is a relative min at that value.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The first derivative test is one of the fundamental test in calculus that is used to determine the behavior of a function at a specified location.</p><h4>Mathematical Representation</h4><p>Given that $f'(c)=0$ and the first derivative of $f$ exists on an open interval containing $c$.</p><ol><li>If $\\lim_{ x \\to c^- }f'(x)&gt;0$ and $\\lim_{ x \\to c^+ }f'(x)&lt;0$,, then $f(c)$ is a relative maximum.</li><li>If $\\lim_{ x \\to c^- }f'(x)&lt;0$ and $\\lim_{ x \\to c^+ }f'(x)&gt;0$ then $f(c)$ is a relative minimum.</li><li>Otherwise, there is no local maxima or minima.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second derivative test allows for the classification of critical points\">The second derivative test allows for the classification of critical points</a></li></ul>","The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The first wave of democracy began around the 1820, and ended around the 1920s.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the longest and arguably, most impactful wave of democratization. It is also the one that had the most conspicuous reversal, with the rise of Fascism and Communism at its end.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The first wave of democracy began around the 1820s, when a majority of white males in the US were able to vote. Over the next decades, countries around the world began to democratize. This wave ended around 1922, when Benito Mussolini overturned the democratic government in Italy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals\">The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding\">Many argue that democracy today is, again, backsliding</a><ul><li>Some draw a similarity to today.</li></ul></li></ul>","The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fiscal limit can be found using the standard debt dynamics equation ($\\Delta d \\approx (r - g)d - s$), which can be rearranged to produce $Fiscal \\ Limit = \\frac{s_{max}}{r-g}$.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to calculate the amount of acceptable debt that a government can take on.</p><h4>Derivation</h4><p>The most accurate version of this equation is actually$$ \\Delta d_{t} = (\\frac{1+r}{1+g})d_{t-1}-s $$ This comes from the debt and GDP equations, i.e., the following: $$ d_{t}=(1+r)d_{t-1}-s $$ $$ y_{t}=(1+g)<em>{t-1} $$ To get the change in the debt-to-GDP ratio in the following year then, we have to find $\\frac{d</em>{t}}{y_{t}}$ , which produces $$ \\Delta d_{t} = (\\frac{1+r}{1+g})d_{t-1}-s $$</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>A contributor to the debt, something that is discussed in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Predicted fiscal surplus lowers the risk of inflation\">Predicted fiscal surplus lowers the risk of inflation</a><ul><li>There are benefits to lower fiscal deficits (beyond what is covered in this note, )</li></ul></li></ul>","The fragmentation of military power within a state weakens central power":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the military power of a state is split among different agents who are beyond the control of the central authority, the state is showing a sign of weakness, and often imminent collapse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a pattern that has been observed across time and societies, and can be used as a an indicator for the health of a state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Late Roman Republican period was characterized by a series of increasingly powerful and independent military commanders\">The Late Roman Republican period was characterized by a series of increasingly powerful and independent military commanders</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power\">The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power</a></li><li>[[]]</li></ul>","The goal of Hinduism is to achieve Brahman":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The overall goal of Hinduism is for its adherent to achieve Brahman, or the ultimate state of being.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the overall goal of Hinduism, one of the world’s major religions.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Moksha could be under taken in many different ways, depending on the person and their caste.</li><li>The chief goal of humankind is to achieve union with Brahman, the fundamental reality that transcends the duality of existence and non-existence, through a process known as <em>moksha</em>.</li><li>The process to achieving union with the Brahman is undertaken through multiple lifetimes, through a process known as <em>samara</em>. This is an example of how Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems\">Reincarnation is an important aspect of many belief systems</a></li></ul>","The goal of the city is to foster virtue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The teleology of the city is one directed towards the cultivation of virtue. All parts of the city function imbued with virtue, and producing virtue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fundamental character of cities for Aristotle.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>If a city does not have virtue as its common goal, then it is no longer a city. And by the “city,” that references the leaders of the city and all its citizens, who must all live according to political virtue and personal virtue, which, in a virtuous city, become one and the same. <strong>Most importantly, for a city to be virtuous is for it to serve the common interest.</strong></p><ul><li>When cities deviate from the virtuous path, they justify the deviance via a particular criterion of desert, such as wealth (oligarchy) or freedom/equality (democracy).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends\">Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends</a><ul><li>One important function of the city is that of educating the populace and making them virtuous.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a><ul><li>Only the leaders need the virtue of prudence in a city.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law\">Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law</a><ul><li>Justice is important for the city too.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved\">A contract theory must be justified, not merely proved</a><ul><li>This would be a critique that Aristotle levels at Contractarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The goal of thought is to generate belief":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The goal of thinking is in order to form beliefs, which are regulations for action.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the very fundamentals of thinking and what it means to think.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the goal of thinking is in order to generate belief, and belief is a specific course of action that we should take in specific circumstances, it means that differences in meaning which motivate no differences in action are not different beliefs, and thus we should not waste our time in thinking about them.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act\">A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act</a><ul><li>Draws on this definition of beliefs.</li></ul></li></ul>","The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often claimed that the high taxes of the 1950s was what enabled the explosive growth during the period. However, this is in fact not true. While on paper, the top marginal tax rate was around 91%, the actual effective tax rate was much higher.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This dispels a common misconception that there were incredibly high tax rates during the 1950s.</p><h4>Rent-seeking behavior</h4><ul><li>People began to pay for luxury goods with company funds, since it was a fully deductible expense for the company and tax-free for the individual. This also created a culture whereby business decisions were influenced by their tax-deductibility rather than their economic merit.</li><li>Executives began to be paid with stock options, since the capital gains tax rate is far lower than the income tax rate.</li><li>Corporations were created simply as a way of manipulating the tax system, so that people pay the lower capital gains tax, rather than the far higher income tax rate.</li><li>Lobbying commenced in an attempt to reduce the tax rate of specific groups, most infamously, the Oil Depletion Allowance, where oil men were allowed to deduct 27.5% of their gross income from taxes.</li></ul><h4>Effective Tax Rate</h4><ul><li>The top 0.1% of individuals paid, on average during that period, an effective federal, state, and local tax rate of ~52%.</li><li>The top 1% of individuals paid, on average during that period, an effective federal, state, and local tax rate of ~52%.</li></ul><p> </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system\">The US has a marginal tax system, not a flat tax system</a><ul><li>Something that is needed to understand the US tax system and this note specifically.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes\">An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes</a><ul><li>Related to the idea of tax rates, which is extensively discussed by this note. It could be the case that if an unrealized gains tax were to be implemented, there would be even more rent-seeking behavior.</li></ul></li></ul>","The history of monasticism is a continual struggle with the problem of secularizing the influence of wealth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Weber makes the claims that the entire history of monasticism, that is, the ascetic practice, can be frame as strings of attempts to secularize the influence of wealth and detach it from religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Weber uses this to contrast the Protestant attitude: Protestants essentially began viewing wealth through the lens of religion, in a sense incorporating it into their belief system. This can be seen through their belief that making money is a sign of God’s blessing (when the money is not wasted).</p><h4>Quote</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">John Wesley</p><p>I fear, wherever riches have increased, the essence of religion has decreased in the same proportion… For religion must necessarily produce both industry and frugality… But as riches increase, so will pride, anger, and love of the world…</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that divine calls are a concept coherent to Judeo-Christianity, and as especially prevalent with some branches of Judeo-Christianity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This support’s Weber’s thesis regarding the origins of the capitalist spirit from Protestantism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a><ul><li>The doctrinal move that gives the calling its economic force.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment\">Economic compulsion is detached from religious calling beginning during the Enlightenment</a><ul><li>Where this idea eventually goes: the calling is discarded but the compulsion remains.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a></li></ul>","The idea of predestination in Calvinism provides a methodical rationalization of moral life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of predestination, which is a major aspect of Calvinism, provides a structure for the rationalization of moral life.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Unlike the idea of “good works” present in other branches of Christianity, the idea of predestination entails the creation of a regimented life of asceticism (instead of just \"good works\"), as acting consistently with discipline represented the characteristics of someone who would go to heaven.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism\">The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism</a></li></ul>","The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits in the economy is one that is inherently utilitarian, as any deontological system would prohibit acting in an self-interested way. Only a utilitarian conception of the world would allow for self-interest to take place in an economic system.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This crucial because it seems to reveal an ideological inconsistency in the right. Typically, right wing thinkers are the most supportive of capitalist theory, and yet often believe in structured moral theory. This seems to be a contradiction.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism\">The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a></li></ul>","The ills usually blamed on private property come from vice, not from ownership":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Having private property does not necessarily make people greedy or corrupt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Leftists often claim that becoming private property corrupts people, and this note refutes that claim.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle argues that corruption is not a necessary result of having private property, corruption if most often simply a result of vice. On the other hand, having a lot of wealth (unlimited acquisition) can be corrupting influence that make people stray from virtue.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a><ul><li>Perhaps this provides some empirical evidence in support of this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","The imprecision of moral intuitions is the reason for most moral conflicts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It seems that most of the time, moral conflicts arise because of a conflict between different individual’s moral intuitions, specifically when it comes to weighing different factors of moral importance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to suggest that we need some moral system beyond merely intuition, since our intuitions are extremely unpredictable and indeterministic.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Human moral intuitions, even within the individual, can often conflict with each other. This seems to suggest that perhaps some moral intuitions are innate, while others are imposed upon us by outside influences, and therein lies the conflict.</p><ul><li>Perhaps it is like this: we (all sane people) each have same very similar set of fundamental moral axioms, upon which we do our own moral deduction and thus produce our own set of moral principles. However, this deduction has multiple equilibria, and our milieu includes people who arrived at different moral solutions, which, in our education, is then imposed upon us. The result is that each of us grow up with different sets of moral equilibria which are superimposed over each other.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>Perhaps the correct strategy then is really a Taoist/Neo-Confucianist one; that is, to discard moral knowledge that is not genuinely one’s own.</li></ul></li></ul>","The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The individualistic institutions that underlie much of the Baptist sects and the Quackers (exemplified by their rejection of large scale church institutions, which even the Calvinists adopted), allowed for a development of a sense of individualism, leading to the spirit of capitalism.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows that part of the development of the spirit of capitalism could be traced to the Baptist sects and the Quackers. Also, this could serve as the explanation for why the spirit of capitalism is so intense in the US.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The intolerant must mostly be tolerated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the intolerant must mostly be tolerated, except in cases where the intolerance threatens free institutions and basic liberties.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This presents one of the main positions on the topic of whether to allow intolerant people in society.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Unless the intolerant people are overtly and actively threatening the free institutions of a society or the basic liberties of the citizens in that society, their rights ought to be protected just like any other person. Additionally, the intolerant have nothing to complain about, since they do not acknowledge the very principles of liberty that would protect them. The intolerant still have moral personalities (i.e., they are still potentially capable of acting in a moral manner) and therefore deserve rights. This view was held both my Rawls and Karl Popper, though Popper holds a somewhat more intolerant perspective than Rawls on the issue, i.e., his bar for what qualifies as “intolerance” is lower.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Freedom is the only non-contradictory political idea</span><ul><li>A thesis that seems to undermine the claims made here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>Mills would reject any form of intolerance, even towards the intolerant.</li></ul></li></ul>","The introduction of bureaucracy into the housing construction with section 701 led to an increase in single family homes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that introducing bureaucracy, especially bureaucracy that places the ability to regulate housing construction on the local level promotes the construction of single family homes (for example, section 701 of the Urban Planning Assistance Program), since that is in the interests of the existing homeowners.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the ineffectiveness of past regulations that initially were established to ensure that housing would be more plentiful.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Section 701 of the Urban Planning Assistance Program introduced bureaucracy into the process of new housing construction. This meant a shift towards the new construction of less dense, single family homes, rather than higher density apartments. In this way, the government introduced the planning capacity to the local cities that allowed them to take charge of development, meaning that the existing homeowners were able to capitalize on their preferences and ensure that future construction would also be single family homes.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The introduction of bureaucracy into the housing construction with section 701 led to an increase in single family homes\">The introduction of bureaucracy into the housing construction with section 701 led to an increase in single family homes</a><ul><li>Policies like the one mentioned in this note could be the reason that people don’t believe in “supply-side solutions,” since this one failed.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is a good example.</li></ul></li></ul>","The invention of paper money by the Song allowed for the development of modern economics":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The invention of paper money, which replaced much heavier coin money, allowed for the easier transport of value across vast distances, making trade easier. Additionally, it no longer meant that the production of units of value was tied to the rarity of the intrinsic material.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The invention of paper money is one of the most important developments in economics, and allowed for the development of advanced economics in the future.</p><h4>References</h4>","The invisible hand was advanced narrowly and with caveats":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The metaphor appears once in the founding text of the discipline, is applied to a narrow case, and is hedged more heavily than its author’s usual practice, which makes the modern doctrine attributed to it an extrapolation rather than an inheritance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A large part of the case for laissez-faire is conducted by appeal to authority, and the authority turns out not to have held the position in the strong form in which it is cited.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Smith’s own worry was that if merchants pursue their interest by seeking regulation and privilege rather than by producing, the mechanism does not operate at all. Rothschild goes further and reads the metaphor as ironic, which is speculative; the narrow claim about frequency, scope and hedging does not depend on that reading.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Threats to economic freedom come from guilds, corporations and local bodies as much as from the central state\">Threats to economic freedom come from guilds, corporations and local bodies as much as from the central state</a><ul><li>The worry that the caveats express.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one\">The idea that self-interest can produce overall benefits is an inherently consequentialist one</a><ul><li>What the doctrine amounts to once stated carefully.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Encompassing interests mean that an actor&#x27;s private optimum resembles the social optimum; narrow interests mean that an actor&#x27;s gains most from rent-seeking\">Encompassing interests mean that an actor's private optimum resembles the social optimum; narrow interests mean that an actor's gains most from rent-seeking</a><ul><li>The modern statement of the condition under which the mechanism works.</li></ul></li></ul>","The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The irrational part of the soul that Aristotle defines is composed of the vegetative (which humans share with animals) and the appetitive.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This part of the soul is the domain of the moral virtues.</p><h4>Vegetative vs. Appetitive</h4><ul><li><strong>Vegetative:</strong> controls the basic/unconscious elements of an individual, i.e., biological growth, nutrition, sleep, etc. It functions even when the human is unconscious. Does not play a part in human excellence.</li><li><strong>Appetitive:</strong> controls the emotions, desires, and appetites of the individual. It is irrational, but is able to be tamed with reason. Moral training aligns this part of the soul with the rational part, producing the moral virtues.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative\">The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative</a><ul><li>The complementary part of the soul.</li></ul></li></ul>","The labor market is not perfectly competitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There has been a misconception that the labor market is perfectly or near perfectly competitive. However, this does not seem to be the case.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Many policies are based on the assumption that the labor market is near-perfectly competitive, so understanding that it is not can have policy implications.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Many of the effects of monopoly are present in the labor market, which seems to be a monopsony applied to the worker, not the consumer.</li><li>Firm mergers should be judged on whether they actually increase productivity and raise wages, rather than just transfer wealth through firing or wage suppression.</li><li>Downward Wage Pression ($DWP$) can be measured with $Markdown \\times Diversion \\ Ratio$, where if it rises about 1-2%, then it indicates a significant incentive to suppress wages.</li><li>There is labor market power because usually there are only a few employers in a location that require specific skills and there are search frictions for jobs.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Non-compete clauses are bad for the worker and the consumer\">Non-compete clauses are bad for the worker and the consumer</a><ul><li>A concrete instance of the imperfection, and of the harm it does.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The decline in the labor share of capital is related to a decline in construction productivity\">The decline in the labor share of capital is related to a decline in construction productivity</a><ul><li>The assumption of the competitiveness of labor led to policies that, since labor is actually not perfectly competitive, actually harm laborers.</li></ul></li></ul>","The lack of support for pro-supply solutions to the housing crisis is due to people's misunderstanding of what causes the issue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While people have good economic intuitions about the nature of market forces in many areas of life, one areas where this intuition seems to be missing is when it comes to housing. This leads to people blaming the wrong demographic for high housing prices.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows how misconceptions about the way that the economy works can have tangible and notable effects on the actual function of the economy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>While many people think that developers and landlords are the problem behind high housing prices/high rent, the root of the problem is ultimately that lack of housing restricts supply, and ultimately leads to higher prices.</p><h4>References</h4><p>-</p>","The land value tax (LVT) is the best form of taxation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Proponents of Georgism argue that thee land value tax is the best form of tax since it only taxes what is not the result of human investment and labor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core component of schools of thought like Georgism or Geolibertarianism, who argue that all other forms of tax are misdirected and unequal.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Proponents argue that taxing land is effective since it is not the product of human labor, and merely a part of nature, which they consider to be owned by no one/all of us. Additionally, since a tax would ensure that land does not go to waste, as people simply sit on their land doing nothing as the value of land slowly increases. It incentivizes people to make the best use of their land and make sure that it is productive enough to offset the taxes on the land itself. Otherwise, it will be sold to someone who can better make use of the land. However, this is premised on the idea that land is somehow communally owned, or that it cannot be the result of private property. Such a view emphasizes the fact that people should be not subject to any taxation, including land, since it violates the property rights of the landowner (this is mostly an Austrian view). </p><ul><li>The LVT is a tax that does not have any dead weight loss, since supply is perfectly inelastic.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>With a LVT, this type of behavior would be avoided, since it be very difficult to avoid a tax on land by engaging in rent-seeking behavior.</li></ul></li></ul>","The late 20th century is marked by a drastic increase in religiosity across the world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The late 20th century saw an uptick in the amount of religious belief and the strength of such belief around the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant to Huntington’s thesis that conflict will more and more be driven by differences in religion/culture.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The increase in religiosity around the world is the result of urbanization/modernization making previously established belief systems inadequate and at the same time, people are not willing to accept moral relativism. Additionally, the end of competing ideologies at the end of the Cold War likewise left people directionless.</p><h4>References</h4>","The laws and regulations of the EU often end up spreading outside its borders":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Known as the “Brussels Effect,” due to the geographic influence of the EU, its regulations and laws often spread outside its border. A prominent example of this is the GDPR, as many nations outside the EU have adopted its regulations in order to operate within the EU.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows the influence that European regulation can have, but also, that the negative effect that come from European regulation could spread around the world.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions\">A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a></li></ul>","The liberal nation state is doomed to fail since it gives people nothing to preserve":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The liberal state, since it is ahistorical and bureaucratic, is weak and doomed to fail, especially in conflict against a civilizational state.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Liberal states don’t have the same stakes that civilizational states have, and therefore, they are weaker when fighting against civilizational states.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The liberal nation-state has nothing to defend, since by its very nature, it serves only as a bureaucratic supplier of goods and services, which almost never gives its clients value for money. On the other hand, it demands that citizens sacrifice their lives for the state. </li><li>This can be analogized to “dying for the telephone company.”</li><li>Liberalism does not provide a hierarchy of goods as a civilization does, and it is supremely ahistorical.</li><li>A civilizational state seeks defense, but not the conquest of other territories. On the other hand, the liberal state has an inherently imperialistic impulse as it seeks to level the hierarchy of goods which civilizational state possess and replace it with a bureaucracy.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order\">The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order</a><ul><li>This book offers a similar perspective.</li></ul></li></ul>","The lower the variability around the regression line, the higher the coefficient of determination":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The greater the variability around the least-squares regression line, the lower the $R^2$, meaning that the better the x variable is able to explain the variability in y.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant for understanding the concept of the coefficient of determination, and this value is also important for other fields, such as investing.</p><h4>Graphical Depiction</h4><p> The graph on the left has a higher $R^2$ as there is less variability in the points relative to the right. This also means that the x variable is able to explain more of the variation in the location of the y values.</p><h4>References</h4>","The match between a task and whoever performs it best is intuitively primitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The judgment that the person best able to do a job should be the one who does it appears to be basic rather than derived, arising in the way an aesthetic judgment does, without an argument standing behind it.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If the intuition is genuinely primitive, then arguments against meritocracy have to explain the intuition away rather than merely outweigh it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The example usually given is deliberately trivial. When the goal is to produce bagels, it seems immediately true that the job should go to whoever makes the best bagels, and no further premise is doing work. The obvious reply is that the intuition tracks the consequentialist point about output and is not about desert at all.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics\">A good conception of justice is one that aligns with our intuitions and also structures more controversial moral topics</a><ul><li>The methodological principle that gives such intuitions evidential weight.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge\">Meritocracy can be justified either consequentially or categorically, and the two justifications diverge</a><ul><li>The ambiguity this intuition trades on.</li></ul></li></ul>","The military-industrial complex's may cause the government not in act in the interest of the public":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The close ties between the military and the defense industry manufacturers means that the defense industry manufacturers are able to shape policy, perhaps in such as way as to turn against the interest of the public.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The military-industrial complex may have a substantial impact on public policy due to the extensive influence of the defense industry on the government and the close ties between the two.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>There is a fragmented monopsony when it comes to the military-industrial complex, as internal divisions allow for defense firms to lobby and influence procurement, which may create demand beyond pure strategic need.</li><li>It also persists due to rent-seeking and regional job incentives, which makes then economically sticky.</li><li>There are long-term contracts, which create a lock-in effect that make it difficult for the government to switch suppliers.</li><li>There is a revolving door, as former military officials and policy makers often join defense firms as consultants or executives.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;iron triangle&#x27; of government can lead to regulatory capture\">The 'iron triangle' of government can lead to regulatory capture</a></li></ul>","The minimum wage can reduce unemployment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While traditional economic logic suggests that having a minimum wage reduces unemployment because some people whose productivity are below the minimum won’t be hired, theoretically, the claim could be made that minimum wage in fact increase employment.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the view of the traditional economist and traditional economic logic, which suggests that the minimum wage actually raises employment rather than reduce it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Say there are two people, John and Jack, the former ready to accept a wage of $10 and the latter $15. Assuming that the firm can hire only at a standard wage, and the revenue per worker is $16.</p><ol><li>In a case without minimum wage, the firm would only hire John, since they make a profit of $6 at a wage for John of $10. </li><li>In a case where the minimum wage is $15, the firm would rationally hire both John and Jack, since at that point, adding an additional worker only increases their revenue, not decrease it. I.e., hiring just John means that the firm would make $1 of profit, while hiring both John and Jack means that the firm would make $2 of profit.</li></ol><p>Therefore, there is a situation whereby having a minimum wage actually increases employment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy\">Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy</a><ul><li>This is a potential refutation against the idea that deregulation is always beneficial for the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","The minorities in a nation tend to focus on economic advancement":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The minorities in a nation, due to limited access to political influence, are “driven with peculiar force into economic activity.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This helps establish the reason for the Protestants to focus on economic activity, thus finally leading to the development of capitalism.</p><h4>Connection with the Protestant Work Ethic</h4><p>Since the Protestants were the minorities, they focused their efforts mainly on economic advancement. This lead to their development of capitalism.</p><h4>Merchants in China</h4><p>An an interesting parallel of this idea is that merchants were the lowest social class of people in Imperial China. Compared to the peasants, which made up around 90% of the population, they were definitely the minority, who just so happened to also work in a very lucrative profession.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China\">Merchants were the lowest social class in Imperial China</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a><ul><li>Perhaps minorities are less susceptible to “traditionalism” since the only way that they are able to achieve influence in a nation where they are the minority is through economic advancement.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism\">The individualistic nature of Baptism and Quakerism allowed for the development of the spirit of capitalism</a><ul><li>Baptists and Quakers are minorities.</li></ul></li></ul>","The more electronegative an atom, the more negatively charged it is":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The more that an atom is electronegative, the more negatively charged the atom is. This maybe a partial charge ($\\delta^-$) or a full negative charge.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is hugely relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4>","The ontological argument is not convincing":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>I think that there are many valid critiques against Anselm’s Ontological argument. There are some well-known ones that are not included, here, as I simply offer my own perspective in the Zettel.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Anselm’s Ontological Argument is a commonly brought up argument for the existence of God. Here, I offer my own critique of it.</p><h4>My Critique</h4><p>Anselm seems to create artificial ontological stratums between things that “exist in imagination” and “things that ‘exist’.” I think this is a little questionable. Anselm’s argument that the “greatest” possible being that we can conceive, ceteris paribus, must exist not only in the imagination, but also actually <em>exist.</em> However, this seems a little questionable. For example, take an idea such as “culture.” It is simply a type of concept that could not make sense to exist in a physical sense. Ceteris paribus, would a “culture” that exist be better than a “culture” that exists only in the imagination? While this does not disprove Anselm’s argument, it makes it more likely that Anselm is wrong.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology\">Radical doubt can be used to clear the ground for a reconstructed epistemology</a></li></ul>","The overwhelming power of labor unions in the post-war consensus harmed the UK economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>As the environment of the post-war consensus as highly conducive to the development of labor unions, as labor unions grew more numerous and larger, they began to have a chokehold over the economy and slow down its productivity growth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the UK shifted to such radical neoliberalism in the 1980s, since this socialist approach to management simple had not produced results.</p><h4>Effect of Labor Unions</h4><p>There was very little productivity growth compared to the other European nations and low levels of investment in the economy. The UK became termed the “Sick Man of Europe,” This reached a nadir from 1973-1979, when a combination of supply-side shocks, inflation, and labor union strikes wiped out British economics growth. During the Winter of Discontent (late 1978-early 1979), the situation was at its worse.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Mark Kesselman, <em>Introduction to Comparative Politics</em></p><p>It seemed as if everyone was on strike. Strikes by truckers disrupted fuel supplies. Strikes by train drivers disrupted intercity commerce and visits to Granny. Some ambulance drivers refused to respond to emergency calls. Gravediggers refused to bury the dead.</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4>","The paramount contribution of Western philosophy to Chinese philosophy is that of positive logical analysis":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The most important contribution that the Western made to Chinese philosophy was the introduction of methods of positive logical analysis.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Chinese philosophy was largely focused on negative analysis (what something is not), rather than positive analysis (what something is). Therefore, the West made a crucial contribution to Chinese philosophy in this way, but introducing this method.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Ultimately, Fong Youlan argues that the future of Chinese philosophy (specifically metaphysics) lies in a combination of the use of the negative and the positive methods.</p><ul><li>He argues that a perfect philosophical method should start with the positive method and end with the negative method in order to achieve the best results.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation\">Chinese philosophy isn’t concerned with gaining positive knowledge, but rather, spiritual elevation</a><ul><li>In this way, Chinese philosophy also has something to contribute to the philosophy of the future, both with its focus on the negative method and also its focus on spiritual elevation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic\">Imperial Chinese astronomy was predictive, rather than causal-theoretic</a><ul><li>One part of this means a greater focus on the idea of finding out the causes, rather than just doing what works.</li></ul></li></ul>","The permanent self does not exist":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea of a permanent self is fallacious, since every aspect of what can be considered the self is constantly in flux, and therefore, there cannot be a permanent, “true” self.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core concept in Buddhism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The Buddhist cannon identifies five aspects of experience: <em>Rūpa</em> (form), <em>Vedanā</em> (feeling), <em>Saṃjñā</em> (perception), <em>Saṃskāra</em> (mental formations), <em>Vijñāna</em> (consciousness). Each moment, each of the five elements change for a given person. Therefore, one cannot point to the existence of a self.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This view is weakened on a synchronic reading, since it seems like a bundle of experiences could define some sort of self in that way. However, a synchronic reading would have to solve the problem of defining what it is that ties the individual experiences together.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Animalism regarding identity states that humans are merely biological animals\">Animalism regarding identity states that humans are merely biological animals</a><ul><li>Another view on identity.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our identity is defined by our nature as agents\">Our identity is defined by our nature as agents</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self\">Reductivism regarding personal identity rejects the existence of an egoist self</a><ul><li>Perhaps this view is related to the Buddhist view?</li></ul></li></ul>","The philosophy of history is essentially an elaborate theodicy for all of human existence":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The philosophy of history is essentially the project of understanding the reason for the existence of evil in  world history, and reconciling it with the existence of God.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Hegel’s view of the philosophy of history.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The mechanism that Hegel introduces for this theodicy is that of the cunning of reason.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems however, that this argument is fundamentally unfalsifiable, and the argument proves too much. There is no occurrence that cannot be justified with the cunning of reason. Additionally, the existence of the cunning of reason basically supplies what faith was supposed to do without. It has realized all of the reason behind faith, and thus, there is no use for faith.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Chapter 3, <em>Meaning in History</em>, p. 58</p><p>More decisive than the material limitations of Hegel's vision is the inherent weakness of his principle that the Christian religion is realized by reason in the history of the secular world <strong>as if the Christian faith could ever be \"realized\" at all and yet remain a faith in things unseen!</strong></p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will\">Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will</a><ul><li>This is the argument for the cunning of reason.</li></ul></li></ul>","The polarization caused by electrostatic induction is akin to a polar molecule":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The polarization that is caused by electrostatic induction is very much similar to a polar molecule described in chemistry.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Since both objects polarized by electrostatic induction and polar objects in chemistry are <em>polar,</em> they share similar traits. For example, they are both overall neutral, but the polarized regions may experience a electromagnetic force pulling it towards the opposite charge.</p>","The princes of Russia attempted to ward the Mongol threat off alone, and were defeated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Because the princes of Russia refused to cooperate each other, they were individually easily defeated by the invading Mongols.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This illustrates one of the major advantages that the Mongols had over their enemies. The Mongols were an united, overwhelming force, while their enemies were often weak and divided.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies\">Genghis Khan united the Mongol tribes so their combined strengths could be directed at external enemies</a></li></ul>","The principle of efficiency does not mitigate natural contingencies":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of efficiency is not able to mitigate natural contingencies, which means that interpretations of the second principle of justice must appeal to some other conception of “everyone’s advantage.”</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The principle of efficiency is not able to account for things such as natural-born intelligence or creativity, which Rawls deems unacceptable. He thus prefers the difference principle.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies\">Career open to talents does not mitigate social nor natural contingencies</a><ul><li>The parallel objection. Careers open to talents fails on both social and natural contingencies; efficiency fails on the natural ones.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Meritocracy\">Meritocracy</a><ul><li>This is also part of the reason why meritocracy is argued against, is that it is able to cope with the existence of natural contingencies, though some formulations of meritocracy are able to do so.</li></ul></li></ul>","The principle of insufficient reason is false":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The principle of insufficient reason is false since when you have no information, you simply cannot make any judgements about the nature of the probability distribution. Probabilities must be based in particular believes.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Rawls uses this as a part of his argument to reject average utilitarianism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Still, Rawls rejects average utilitarianism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>This is part of the reason why.</li></ul></li></ul>","The priority problem has several solutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The priority problem can be solved with the bargaining in the original position, lexical priority, the adoption of singular principles, and viewing the problem from the prudential interests of the worst-off person.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The various solutions to the priority problem is needed, since it is a pressing concern with the development of any conception of justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Some solutions that Rawls proposes for the priority problem include:</p><ol><li>Using the reason that a principle was derived in the original position to support giving the principle certain weights when it comes to the priority of the moral principles.</li><li>Use lexical priority, and justify the lexical ordering.</li><li>Adopt a single principle (e.g., utilitarianism, though Rawls rejects uilitarianism).</li><li>Replacing this broad ethical question with prudentially consider the moral implications of conflicting moral principles from the view of the worst-off in society, and make a decision based on that.</li></ol><p>The difference between 1 and 2 lies in the fact that when weighing, the principles can be applied to different degrees with the resolution of some particular conflict depending on how much of each value is at stake. Lexical ordering, on the other hand, demands that one “exhaust” the application of one principle before moving onto the next. Additionally, solution 4 is intended to reduce our reliance on intuitions when it comes to making moral decisions, which is a problem with the other three approaches.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns\">Justice has lexical priority over other societal concerns</a><ul><li>Justification for lexical priority.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle\">Fair equality of opportunity is lexically before the difference principle</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>It solves the priority problem, or at least is a possible solution for it.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Part of Rawls’ argument against utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The problem of evil can be answered by appeal to the limits of human understanding":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One response to the existence of evil is to deny that we are positioned to judge: the apparent gratuitousness of suffering is a fact about the limits of human comprehension rather than about the world, so no inference from evil to the non-existence of God goes through.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the most common defence against the problem of evil, and it is also the least falsifiable, which is both its strength and the standard objection to it.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The Book of Job gives the argument its canonical form. God’s answer from the whirlwind is not a justification of Job’s suffering but a demonstration of the gulf between what Job can grasp and what is going on.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely\">The amount of evil in the world makes the existence of a perfectly loving God unlikely</a><ul><li>The argument this is a reply to.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments\">One should not attempt to analyze faith with logical arguments</a><ul><li>The more general position this defence tends to collapse into.</li></ul></li></ul>","The process of history follows naturalistic laws that can be determined by humans":"<h4>Summary</h4><h4>Significance</h4><h4>References</h4>","The profit motive sharpens incentives":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The profit motive ensures that there are no perverse incentives, keep incentives sharp, to the point, and strong.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The profit motive is the key underlying lynchpin of capitalism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Friedman states the underlying principle in its general form: nobody spends money that is not their own as carefully as they spend their own. The profit motive works because it makes the spender and the residual claimant the same person, and it fails wherever that identity is broken.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying\">Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying</a><ul><li>The failure case, where the spender bears none of the cost.</li></ul></li></ul>","The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to ensure the stable growth of the economy, maintain low unemployment, and minimize inflation. If possible, it should also attempt to procure a positive trade balance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Macroeconomics is the study of macroeconomic policy, a branch of economics that has the stated objective. It is important to understand these objects in designing policy.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run\">Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs\">Economists tend to overestimate the detrimental effect of tariffs</a></li></ul>","The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An agent who treats certainty as a precondition for acting will keep gathering evidence past the point at which the evidence was already sufficient, and the delay itself produces the outcome the agent was trying to avoid.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This gives a failure mode of epistemic conscientiousness, which is otherwise a virtue, and it explains why the most careful agent in a situation is often the one who is destroyed by it.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Hamlet is driven throughout the play by a relentless pursuit of the truth about his father’s death. The clues accumulate to the point of being overwhelming, yet he does not act on them, choosing instead to gather more, and it is that delay rather than any error of judgment that produces the catastrophe.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>A different cost of the same pursuit.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a><ul><li>The faculty that is being over-exercised here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The rational soul is bifurcated into the scientific and calculative":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that the rational soul is composed of two parts: the scientific (contemplative) and calculative (deliberative).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The two different parts of the soul serve different purposes when it comes to intellectual virtues.</p><h4>Scientific vs. Calculative</h4><ul><li><strong>Scientific:</strong> the study of “necessary/eternal truths,” i.e., mathematics, first principles, etc.</li><li><strong>Calculative:</strong> the study of “contingent/variable truths,” i.e., what to do in a particular circumstance, how to act in a particular circumstance, etc.</li></ul><p>It is the goal of both parts of this rational soul to search for truth. What each part of the rational soul does well is their virtue, and these virtues are what allows each part of the soul to search for the truth.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive\">The irrational part of the soul is bifurcated into the vegetative and appetitive</a><ul><li>The complementary part of the soul.</li></ul></li></ul>","The real-world applicability of the Pigou effect is contested":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Pigou effect, which states that a general decrease in prices leads to an increase in real wealth, leading to increased aggregate consumption, seems to hold theoretical plausibility, though its real-world applicability is contested.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The lack of the inclusion of the Pigou effect is one of the critiques of Keynes’s <em>General Theory</em>. The theory also seems to lack support from empirical evidence.</p><h4>Why?</h4><ul><li>The Pigou effect’s claim that deflation leads to increased real wealth is undermined by the fact that such deflation also increase the value of debts, which can counteract its effect in increasing wealth.</li><li>In Japan, its sustained deflationary crisis was not solved through the Pigou effect, as during the liquidity crisis, people hoarded their money instead of spending it, as the increased real wealth suggested by the Pigou effect would imply that they would have.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect\">A change in price level produces the Income Effect; a change in net worth produces the Real Wealth Effect</a><ul><li>Defines the mechanism at issue. The Pigou effect is the Real Wealth Effect described there.</li></ul></li></ul>","The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The rejection of Western culture in East Asia has led to various beliefs that have been integrated into the way that Asians think about Asian culture.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the rise in tensions across cultural boundaries.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The US's power has declined in Asia due to a combination of the following factors:</p><ul><li>The US does not have the will to pressure Asian societies.</li><li>The US thinks concessions will be reciprocal, but Asians just see them as victories.</li><li>Asian countries have banded together in order to oppose the dominance of the US in the region.</li></ul><p>There are four such “affirmations”:</p><ol><li>Asia will have sustained economic growth that will overtake the West.</li><li>Asian culture leads to economic growth, and is thus superior to the West (specifically, the influence of Confucianism is prominent).</li><li>Relative homogeneity of Asian culture, which is again a result of influence from Confucianism.</li><li>Asian culture should be adopted by the West.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a><ul><li>With the decline of the West comes the rise of Asia.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>This is perhaps part of the culture of Asian, the thing that lead to their economic growth.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations\">Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations</a><ul><li>Another thing to potentially explain the economic prosperity of Asian countries.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a><ul><li>The pattern in play here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The right is not the same as the good":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The right is not the same thing as the good.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the argument that Rawls uses to refute utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are three reasons Rawls identifies why the right is not the same thing as the good:</p><ol><li>The principles of justice (the right) are chosen in the original position, while what an individual considers to be good is not chosen at all, but merely arbitrary.</li><li>It is generally desirable for conceptions of the good to differ, but this is not the case for conceptions of justice.</li><li>The explication of the principles of justice must be conducted from behind the Veil of Ignorance, while goodness requires the full knowledge of particulars in the world.</li></ol><p>All these principles imply a <strong>priority of right</strong> such that what is right overrides claims of what is good. This means that perverse utilitarian outcomes, such as when a majority suppresses the rights of a minority with order to satisfy their own good, cannot occur.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a><ul><li>If they are not, the priority of rights is required, and thus utilitarianism is disqualified as a valid system of justice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism\">Average utilitarianism is more justifiable for rational agents than classical utilitarianism</a></li></ul>","The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>During the dynastic period of China, highly influenced by Confucianism, many government officials in China were also scholars.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Their existence was a testament to the Meritocracy that was present in Chinese society at the time, though the Imperial Examination System. It also strengthened the influence of Confucianism in Chinese governmental institutions.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>The doctrinal source of the scholar-official ideal.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>The mechanism that recruited and bound this class to the state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient\">The Chinese imperial civil service exams were abolished since they were outdated and inefficient</a><ul><li>What happened to the institution that sustained this class.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a></li></ul>","The second derivative test allows for the classification of critical points":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>If the second derivative at a critical point is greater than zero, then the graph of the function is concave up, indicating a local minimum. If the second derivative at the critical point is less than zero, the graph is concave down, or it is a local maximum.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The second derivative test is one of the fundamental test in calculus that is used to determine the behavior of a function at a specified location.</p><h4>Mathematical Representation</h4><p>To find the local extrema of $f(x)$:</p><ol><li>Find $f'(x)$, set it to zero, and solve for $x$ to find the critical values.</li><li>Find $f''(x)$.</li><li>Plug each of the critical values found into $f''(x)$. If the resulting output is positive, then the critical value location is concave up or a relative min. If the output is negative, then the critical value location is concave down, or a relative max.<ol><li>If the value found after plugging in is 0, the second derivative test failed. You must use the first derivative test to find the local extrema in that case.</li></ol></li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points\">The first derivative test allows for the classification of critical points</a></li></ul>","The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The second wave of democracy began around 1945 and ended around the early 1960s.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This period saw many major democracies established as a result of American efforts to rebuild the West, and British decolonization.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The second wave of democracy began after the end of WWII, when much of Western Europe was re-democratized and decolonization began, leading to the formation of new democracies. This came to halt by the 1960s, as democratic norms began to dissolve, partly due to the rivalry created by the Cold War leading to both sides prioritizing strategic alliances over democratic ideals.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini&#x27;s Rise\">The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR\">The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR</a></li></ul>","The social institution of families play a large role in liberalism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The institution of the family is crucial for the prospering of liberal ideals in a society.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>While many argue that the institution of the family is incompatible with the principles of liberalism, this note says otherwise.</p><h4>Explanations</h4><ul><li>Family is where humans first learn about rules and rule-making. They also learn about decreasing their time preference, which is a crucial component in being able to participate in the free market.</li><li>Family members have genuine information about the state/nature of people in their family, and are thus knowledge-process institutions which give far more granularity then if the state were to attempt such a thing.</li><li>Families can train their children to have the liberal ethos, which produce people who act in the same way in society.</li><li>The principle of subsidiarity finds its most natural representation in the family. </li><li>Cultural liberalism likewise originates from the family, and attempting to enforce a single model of the family, whether left or right wing, threatens the diversity which undergirds liberal societies.</li><li>Family teaches us epistemic humility, since we are forced to be with different people that we did not choose.</li><li>Importantly, defending the concept of family is not the same as defending a particular conception of the family.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism</a><ul><li>What allows for the cultural that allows constitutional liberalism to exist, may in part be due to the family.</li></ul></li></ul>","The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Capitalism was enhanced out a sense of a divine calling, even if the resulting work is not optimal from the standpoint of pure self-interest, as the divine calling advocates for a somewhat aesthetic lifestyle. This connects the origins of capitalism with religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of Weber’s core ideas, that the spirit of capitalism was furthered out of sense of a religious calling. He uses this has a jumping off point to dive deeper and elucidate the precise religious origins of this concept.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Money is considered a great danger. Therefore, it is morally suspect and the individual must always act rationally and frugally, living an ascetic lifestyle.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">James William Barclay, according to Max Weber</p><p>The campaign against the temptations of the flesh, and the dependence on external things, was, as besides the Puritans the great Quaker apologist Barclay expressly says, not a struggle against the rational acquisition, but against the irrational use of wealth.</p></blockquote><ul><li>When acquisition occurs as a means to an end, it is morally reprehensible. When it occurs as the fruit of labor in a calling, it was a sign of God’s blessing.<ul><li>When this did occur, the money was saved, not spent → this created capital.</li></ul></li><li>Unwillingness to work is considered a sign of a lack of divine grace.</li><li>Laboring honestly was considered to be acting “in God’s will.”<ul><li>Even if it is at low wages, it is considered “hugely pleasing to God.”</li></ul></li><li><strong>Capitalism is the best/most effective way to act in line with God’s will.</strong><ul><li>This is partly due to its system of the division of labor, which increases productivity ability.</li></ul></li><li>Inequality was represented as “Divine Providence,” that those who were wealthier were more favored by God. This helped justify inequality.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself\">In a capitalist system, economic acquisition is not merely for satisfying material needs, but is also an end in and of itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism\">The biggest opponent of the capitalist spirit is that of traditionalism</a></li></ul>","The standard CPI overestimates inflation, since it does not take into account the substitution effect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The standard CPI measurement does not take into account the fact that consumers are able to substitute more expensive goods for cheaper ones (substitution effect), meaning that it overstates the effect of inflation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows a downside to using the standard CPI as a measurement for the effects of inflation, a problem that the Chained CPI measure helps prevent.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When inflation occurs, prices become higher and consumers are able to purchase less of the same goods as before. However, after a certain point, they may switch to purchasing a different set of goods that result in a higher total utility given that they are a lower price. Where the consumption bundle of the individual was originally, and $q_{2}$ represents a composite good. Let us define this budget constraint as $X$:  $$q_{1}p_{1}+q_{2}\\leq m$$ Due to inflation, $p_{1}$ shifts to $p'<em>{1}$, such that $p'</em>{1} &gt; p_{1}$, meaning that either $q_{1}$ or $q_{2}$ or both have to shift down by some amount. For purposes of simplification, let us reduce $q_{2}$ to $q'<em>{2}$. Thus, the new budget constraint, $X</em>{2}$ is $$q_{1}p'<em>{1}+q'</em>{2}\\leq m$$ Say there is some third good $q_{3}$ that has the following budget constraint, $X_{3}$ if there consumer were to consume it: $$ q_{3}p_{3}+q_{2}\\leq m $$ If, say $X \\succ X_{3} \\succ X_{2}$, if inflation were to occur, the consumer would rationally decide to consume $X_{3}$ rather than $X_{2}$, thereby illustrating the effect of the substitution effect and the tendency for the CPI to overstate inflation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A change in some exogenous variable may affect both demand and supply\">A change in some exogenous variable may affect both demand and supply</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability\">The purpose of macroeconomic policy is to encourage stable growth, maintain low unemployment, and ensure price stability</a></li></ul>","The state is legitimized when it acts as a moral institution, and its head, a moral leader":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Confucianists, especially after Mencius explicated their theory of state in greater detail, saw the state as being a sole moral institution that grows naturally out of the hierarchies espoused by Confucian thought.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the way in which the state is justified under Confucian thought.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the leader of the state must be someone who a moral leader, only a sage can be a real king. Mencius traces this back to the sage Yao, and his descendants, who continue to be sages. Mencius in fact claims that if the ruler lacks the ethical qualities that make a good leader, the people have the moral right to revolution.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">People have a moral right to revolution when their rights are violated by the state</span><ul><li>This idea that gained most prominence during the Enlightenment in John Locke can be seen here.</li></ul></li></ul>","The state ought to prevent childbirth in cases where the parents would not be able to support the child":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The state has the authority to prevent the birth of children to parents who don’t have the ability to take care of the child.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that Mill advocates for.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Although this might prevent the birth of some children, who would otherwise not exist, this policy would, under any widely accepted conception of liberalism, violate core liberal principles of non-intervention and personal liberties. That being said, Mill probably justified this in that this policy protects the rights/liberties of the child, which cannot protect its own rights/liberties before it is born.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth\">Persons who are born with low but non-negative utility cannot be said to have been ‘harmed’ in their birth</a><ul><li>This is a potential counterargument to this claim.</li></ul></li></ul>","The state should be entirely in charge of education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The state should entirely be in charge of education both because it is important for the city to be able to cultivate virtue (and people must have the common goal and an identical vision of virtue).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the constitution of the ideal city, since in the ideal city, everyone cultivate virtue and they all have the same goal of virtue.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Aristotle views education as a crucial part to creating a good city, and thus, in order to facilitate the best education, the state must be thus in charge of the education and educate all citizens such that they would be able to cultivate virtue. For Aristotle, this education, very roughly, includes:</p><ul><li>Light physical education up until puberty.</li><li>During the three years after puberty, they engage more intensely to other subjects of learning (language, art, etc.)</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>It seems that in this way, Aristotle is very similar.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education\">While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education</a><ul><li>Mill would disagree with this, for the fear that it causes citizens to be too similar, and thus, the society loses out on the benefits of greater individuality.</li></ul></li></ul>","The state should do as little as possible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The claim that the state, for whatever reason, should do nothing at all, or act very minimally.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an idea that is present throughout many different strands of thought, including Taoism and Libertarianism.</p><h4>Laozi</h4><p>Since Laozi believes that knowledge is bad and people should live according to the Tao, and the state represents a means by which the natural course of things is disrupted, that the state should do as little as possible. When the Taoist sage comes to power, he should first dismantle the preexisting rules and regulations, and then govern with “non-action.”</p><ul><li>The ruler should do nothing and let the people do what they can themselves.</li></ul><p>Zhuangzi makes a similar argument, though for him, he highlights that the very artificial nature of the state is something which goes against the Tao, and thus should be reduced/destroyed.</p><ul><li>He also talk about how the governance of the state is not able to take into account the diverse interests of the people, and thus, any attempt at governing merely increases human misery.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is similar to what Zhuangzi emphasizes.</li></ul></li></ul>","The theory of justice is in large part the theory of rational choice":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice is largely determined through the rational choice of individuals (from the perspective of contractarian frameworks), and thus, the study of justice is a study of rational choice fundamentally.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is helpful for understanding the foundation for many schools of thought in the contractarian camp.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is derived from the fact that a theory of justice has to be:</p><ul><li>Logical and rationally justified.</li><li>Able to be agreed upon by agents.</li></ul><p>And a theory arrived upon via rational choice fits both these criteria.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons\">Justice as fairness would be accepted by free, rational, and prudential persons</a><ul><li>Fundamentally, justice for fairness is a study of how agents rationally arrive upon the conclusion of justice as fairness.</li></ul></li></ul>","The things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Typically, the things that are more contrary to the mean are the things that we have a stronger tendency to do, since they are usually more pleasurable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle makes an important point that the things that we most want to do are often things that bring us farther away from the mean.</p><h4>Aristotle’s Guide</h4><p>Aristotle says that to find the mean:</p><ol><li>Keep away from the side (in the spectrum between the two extremes of some action) that is more contrary to the mean.</li><li>Notice our own errors and do the opposite.</li><li>Guard against pleasure.</li></ol><p>But he says that getting to the mean is ultimately up to the individual, and that there is no general philosophical principle that can guide someone to the mean in their life. It just our own intuition that has to do the work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>We must constantly and often do things that are against what we might otherwise want to do.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>To act in a virtuous state means, in practice, to act in the manner described herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>To achieve happiness, then, means to act in a way that gets us as far away from the side that is more opposed to the mean as possible.</li></ul></li></ul>","The third wave of democratization (1974-1990s) ended a few years following the collapse of the USSR":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The third wave of democracy began around 1974 and ended around 1991.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This defines the end of the Cold War and saw many of the formerly Communist nations democratize.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The third wave began with the Carnation Revolution in Portugal, and spread to many areas around the world, including the Four Asian Tigers and Eastern Europe following the Collapse of the Soviet Union.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini&#x27;s Rise\">The first wave of democratization (1820s-1922) ended with Mussolini's Rise</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals\">The second wave of democratization (1945-1962) ended with 1960s authoritarian reversals</a></li></ul>","The traditional left-right split is turning into a split between the well and the poorly informed":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The traditional dichotomy between the left and the right is slowly becoming a split between the people who are well informed and those who are ill-informed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a unique diagnosis of the current trend of polarization between the left and the right.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Only Democrats engaged with serious publications in substantial numbers, such as the New York Times and the Washington Post, while Republicans mainly rely on watching Fox News for information. The people who are more poorly informed (Republicans) are drifting towards authoritarianism.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets\">AI has the potential to eliminate information asymmetry in markets</a><ul><li>Does AI have the power to relieve the information asymmetry in general, and make people generally better informed?</li></ul></li></ul>","The universality of history is a product of Christian history":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The concept of universality in history is something that originates in Christianity, can thus cannot be applied in a context that is outside of Christianity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The idea of universality is something that is unique to Christianity.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Universal salvation and other such concepts which present Christianity as something that is universal impacts all of humankind is something that is unique to Christianity and something that does not exist naturally in the world or in many other religions. Therefore, attempts to create “universal” theories of history outside of the Christian framework is impossible.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">A post-Christian world cannot be sustained</span><ul><li>Related to this, especially is a post-Christian world continues to maintain many of the aspects of universalism introduced first by Christianity.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","The virtue of art is concerned with making, where the end goal is the product, not the action itself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The virtue of art (<em>techne</em>) is a productive state that is “truly reasoned.” it focuses on the production, i.e., the end goal, rather than the action, i.e., the process by which one reaches the end goal. It deals with the variable, not the necessary/eternal. It involves reason, not just instinct.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>The virtue that allows people to create things that don’t necessarily have to exist.</p><h4>References</h4>","The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Intellect or intuition (<em>nous</em>) deals with the ability to grasp first principles, or the “extremes” that deductive reasoning cannot reach. It operates in two distinct directions:</p><ul><li>Theoretical <em>nous</em> grasps unchangeable first principles (axioms) through induction. </li><li>Practical <em>nous</em> grasps the immediate, variable facts of a specific situation.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>Intellect also constitute a part of the virtue of wisdom. It is also the foundation upon which scientific knowledge is able to build.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a><ul><li>The complementary intellectual virtue. Intellect grasps the starting points; scientific knowledge reasons from them.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect\">Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect</a><ul><li>Intellect constitute the second half of wisdom.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a><ul><li>Intellect is required in order to under the particulars that eventually develop prudence in someone.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>Practical <em>nous</em> requires being morally virtuous.</li></ul></li></ul>","The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Prudence (<em>phronesis</em>) is the ability to deliberate rightly about what is good and advantageous for oneself (not what is good for something in particular, but what is good to the good life in general). Focuses on the action, not the production. It deals with the contingent/variable. Experience with particulars (through the virtue of intellect, <em>nous</em>) is required to become more prudent.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most important intellectual virtues, since it is something that is practical and used everyday in order to rationally deliberate on what is needed to achieve the good life.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence\">Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence</a><ul><li>These minor intellectual virtues assist in making someone prudent.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>The “acting with a quality of virtuousness” mentioned here is essentially acting with prudence.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences\">Politics is the fullest realization of prudence, and it is divided into the legislative and political sciences</a><ul><li>Politics is the exercise of prudence in a polis.</li></ul></li></ul>","The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Scientific knowledge (<em>episteme</em>) deals with necessary/eternal truths and uses logic (deductive reasoning, syllogisms, etc.) in order to get from the first principles to the conclusion. This virtue is part of the scientific soul.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important virtue that makes up one of the parts of wisdom.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation\">The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation</a><ul><li>Supplies the first principles that this virtue reasons from. Deduction cannot reach its own premises.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect\">Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect</a><ul><li>Scientific knowledge forms a part of wisdom.</li></ul></li></ul>","The virtuous man needs friends":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The virtuous man possesses all that is good, and since it is better to have friends than not to have friends, the virtuous man needs friends.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Makes an argument for why the virtuous person needs friends, since, as some say, the virtuous person is already doing well by themselves.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Virtuousness is an activity, and it is easier to be virtuous when you keep up the continuous activity with friends.</li><li>Because virtuous friends is like our second self, when we see others do good things and live well, we, in a meaningful way, see ourselves being the same in our friends.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue\">The best form of friendship is one based on mutual virtue</a><ul><li>This is the type of friendship referenced in this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Practicing virtue with friends makes this easier.</li></ul></li></ul>","The waterbed effect is false":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The waterbed effect is commonly cited as a reason for instituting antitrust regulation, since it supposedly hurts consumers. However, there are some issues with the commonly espoused theory.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important since it undermines a commonly cited theory.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two things which together undermines this theory:</p><ol><li>The firms must be able to take the markup, or otherwise they will break-off the agreement and switch to using a different supplier.</li><li>It must be the case that the weight average price for the consumer is higher after such a negotiation (since the lowering of input costs for the larger firm could leader to lower prices for consumers).</li></ol><p>However, it might not be the case that both assumptions are true for all circumstances, and also, in some circumstances, accepting one of the assumptions prevents one from being able to accept the other assumption.</p><h4>References</h4>","The world is unfair and amoral":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The world does not care about what is fair. One’s luck is entirely independent from their moral status/virtuousness.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common theme in many philosophies and or works of literature.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The figure of Oedipus, all that he has to suffer when he finds out about his true nature, was not his fault. In fact, he saved the city of Thebes from the Sphinx. Yet, he is not recompensed with his service, rather arbitrarily punished despite his good deeds.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li></ul>","Theoretically, the side on which a tax is imposed is irrelevant; the outcome is the same":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It the statutory incidence of a tax does not matter, since the ultimate market outcome will be the same regardless of if it is tax on the consumers vs. the producers.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that the demand of whether tax should be on consumers or producers is irrelevant in many circumstances.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>There are circumstances in the real world, including with the feasibility of implementation, ease of tax loop holes, etc., such that this law does not always hold.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic tax incidence is based on relative price elasticity\">Economic tax incidence is based on relative price elasticity</a><ul><li>On whom the tax burden falls is dependent on the price elasticity more than the statutory incidence.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior\">The high tax rates of the 1950s were not actually paid, and resulted in rent-seeking behavior</a><ul><li>This is an example of when this principle could potentially be violated, since there is rent-seeking behavior and an inability to actually enforce the tax.</li></ul></li></ul>","There are many possible ways to hurt someone in a transaction":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are many ways of hurting someone through a transaction, some methods being just, and others not.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is how Aristotle’s ethics would decide whether someone is unjust in some way.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The two types of harm-causing acts are not unjust.</p><ul><li><strong>Misadventure</strong>: causing a harm though the harm occurring was “contrary to reasonable expectations.”</li><li><strong>Mistake</strong>: causing a harm that is not “contrary to reasonable expectations,” though there was no viciousness intended in the harm.</li></ul><p>These two types of harm-causing acts can be unjust.</p><ul><li><strong>Injury</strong>: causing a harm with prior knowledge and expectation, but no deliberation. </li><li><strong>Harming from choice</strong>: causing harm with prior knowledge, deliberation, and expectation of doing harm.</li></ul><p>In short, for an act to not constitute being unjust, the act has to have to been done out of <em>ignorance</em>, rather than “unnatural and subhuman reactions” or deliberate intention.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals\">Rectificatory justice concerns undoing a transaction between individuals</a><ul><li>This is where the contents of this note are useful.</li></ul></li></ul>","There are several benefits to having big, dense cities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are many benefits that come from having big and dense cities, improving the productivity, increasing rates of innovation, and promoting equality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents an argument for why we should have big and dense cities.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ol><li>People are more productive in big cities. <ol><li>This can be seen through the fact that there is a noticeable wage premium when people come to work in big cities.</li><li>This effect is especially apparent when the city has an intangible economy, where there is a high concentration of intellectual value.</li></ol></li><li>Innovation increases when people congregate in big cities.<ol><li>Especially, there is an increase in innovation which required the combination of atypical combinations of knowledge.</li><li>Inventors who move from smaller clusters to bigger clusters tend to see large increases in their patenting productivity.</li></ol></li><li>By restricting housing, we are promoting inequality.<ol><li>Rising wages means that the poorer residents are priced out of their homes, and this leads to gentrification.</li><li>By allowing, for instance, local residents to vote on rezoning to increase density, we can solve this problem and ensure that the local residents capture the benefit of development instead of having that development displace them.</li><li>There is also regional inequality.<ol><li>Since it may be the case that for less well-paying jobs, it wouldn't be worth it for people to move to big cities where the rents are much higher compared to their wage increases, this means that people are just trapped in their original cities/states, increasing the disparity between states.</li></ol></li><li>High housing prices also reduce fertility.<ol><li>Women have less children than they say that they actually want. They often don't have children because it requires getting a bigger house, which they cannot afford.</li></ol></li></ol></li></ol><h4>Example</h4><p>One of the potential problems with African development is their lack of density. Their cities are currently not dense enough to produce the economic value generation that cities typically produce.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities\">Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities</a><ul><li>Dense cities also have dense rail networks, which also generate positive economic value.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Japanese rail system is successful because of its unique business model and limited government intervention\">The Japanese rail system is successful because of its unique business model and limited government intervention</a><ul><li>Part of their success id due to the dense Japanese cities.</li></ul></li></ul>","There are two considerations relevant to social positions, which are the existence of equal citizenship, and where someone is in the distribution of wealth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There are only two considerations which are relevant when we consider how to measure people’s social positions:</p><ol><li>whether there is equal citizenship, and </li><li>where someone is in the distribution of wealth &amp; income.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>Here, Rawls specifies what exactly he is measuring when he measures the “expectations” that a representative might have.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Here, (1) is satisfied by equal basic liberties and (2) is satisfied by the second principle of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others\">Each person has equal basic rights most extensively compatible with similar liberties for others</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity\">Social and economic inequalities are to satisfy the Difference Principle and Fair Equality of Opportunity</a></li></ul>","There exist eternal and perfect metaphysical frameworks of physical things":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>This is a common idea in idealistic schools of philosophy, such as the Platonic school or the Neo-Confucianists.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This often represents a crucial aspect of the metaphysics of the aforementioned schools.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><h5>Neo-Confucianism (School of Principle)</h5><p><em>Li</em> (理) is the thing which exists within all things in the world, and is what makes that thing <em>it</em>, as opposed to something else. It can be conceived of as being the form of the object. There is no thing without <em>li</em>. In the physical world, objects manifest as the combination of <em>qi</em> (气) and <em>li</em>. Things come into existence through the <em>qi</em> condensing in accordance with the <em>li</em> and therefore makes the object part of a category of objects as a whole, and disappear through the <em>qi</em> decondensing. <em>Li</em> is conceived of as being ontologically/explanatorily prior to the <em>qi</em>. <em>Li</em> in the most universal/comprehensive level manifests as the highest (ultimate) <em>li</em>, is termed the Supreme Ultimate (太极). The Supreme Ultimate is the ultimate pattern according to which the dynamic processes of <em>qi</em> (e.g., ying-yang, activity-stillness) become intelligible. <strong>Comparison with Aristotle</strong> A comparison can be drawn here with Aristotle’s four causes:</p><ul><li><em>Material</em>: is akin to the <em>qi</em>.</li><li><em>Formal</em>: is akin to the <em>li</em>.</li><li><em>Efficient</em>: is very roughly equivalent to the transformations/interactions of the <em>qi</em>.</li><li><em>Final</em>: no clean equivalent in the Neo-Confucianist framework, though some aspects of the <em>li</em> can embody such a normative/teleological sense.</li></ul><h4>Note</h4><p>It is important to note here that there is scholarly debate regarding the precise nature of Zhu Xi’s metaphysics, with some calling him a type of dualist, and others disputing that label. It is difficult to say how exactly the <em>li</em> is separable from the <em>qi</em>.</p><h4>References</h4>","There is a finite amount of compute per token in an LLM":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a token undergoes inference, there is a limited amount of compute that can be applied to each input token.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that reasoning should be spread across various tokens (and thus compute) in order to generate the best response.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For example, asking an LLM to generate the response to a math question and asking it to do by only providing the answer and not the steps crams all of the calculations into one token, thus lowering the potential quality of the response.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To elicit high quality responses from an LLM based on some preexisting content, provide the content in the context window\">To elicit high quality responses from an LLM based on some preexisting content, provide the content in the context window</a><ul><li>A practical consequence of this constraint.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"LLM hallucinations are &#x27;shameless guesses,&#x27; not &#x27;hallucinations&#x27;\">LLM hallucinations are 'shameless guesses,' not 'hallucinations'</a><ul><li>When LLMs make mistakes due to the fact that they were provided too little tokens for a response, this could be the justification.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning, whereas most models use SFT\">Reasoning LLMs use reinforcement learning, whereas most models use SFT</a><ul><li>Reasoning LLMs could operate in a different manner, where this is less of a problem?</li></ul></li></ul>","There is a much higher concentration of potassium inside a cell compared to sodium":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to $Na+/P^+-ATPase$, there is a much higher concentration of potassium inside a cell compared to outside of it, and a much lower concentration of sodium compared to outside.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>It is this process which generate membrane potential and a concentration gradient in a cell.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein generating membrane potential in animal cells\">A sodium-potassium pump is the most important protein generating membrane potential in animal cells</a></li></ul>","There is an inherent ontological hierarchy in nature":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is an natural order to things in the world, in that “lower” things are meant to serve “higher” things.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fundamental pillar of Aristotle’s metaphysical world view. Also, this metaphysical view continued to be held into the middle ages in the form of the Great Chain of Being.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This concept is what ultimately justifies things like:</p><ul><li>Natural slavery,</li><li>The subordination of women,</li><li>The merely instrumental value of animals and plants,</li><li>The ranking of lives and activites, e.g. The contemplative life outweighs the political.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>There is an ordering, for example, of justice where political justice is at the top.</li></ul></li></ul>","There is debate as to whether inequality is rising in the US":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is debate as to whether inequality is actually rising in the US, since different economists have used data differently to estimate inequality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This undermines the common statement that inequality is rapidly rising in the US.</p><h4>Different Measures</h4><p> Auden and Splinter have, for example, done research into inequality that contradicts the research that Piketty, Saez, and Zucman (PSZ) have done. One of the reasons for this difference is that the PSZ team assigns reported income proportionally, where the rich are assumed to have proportionately greater unreported income than the less-rich. Auden-Splinter, on the other hand, uses data from the IRS’s unreported income, which tracks mostly middle and lower income people’s unreported income (e.g., being paid in cash for a service), and uses that. Both methods have bias.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality has dropped globally, though increased within countries\">Inequality has dropped globally, though increased within countries</a><ul><li>For instance, it has increased in the US, though the degree to which it has increased is debatable.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>A potential cause of this inequality in the US, since there is so much innovation in the US?</li></ul></li></ul>","Third-party payment explains why a health system can be both expensive and unsatisfying":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When neither the patient nor the provider is spending the patient’s money, neither party has an interest in the price, so costs rise without any corresponding rise in output or satisfaction.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This locates the defect in the payment structure rather than in any participant’s conduct, which makes it a structural rather than a moral diagnosis of health care costs.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Friedman states the underlying principle bluntly: nobody spends money that is not theirs as carefully as they spend their own. The same structure predicts the growth of administrative cost, since once a large pool of third-party money exists, regulating access to it becomes worthwhile and the regulation is itself an expense.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Insurance companies are not the main reason for the high costs in the US health care system\">Insurance companies are not the main reason for the high costs in the US health care system</a><ul><li>Agrees that the insurer is the wrong target, and locates the cost in providers rather than in the payment structure.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The profit motive sharpens incentives\">The profit motive sharpens incentives</a><ul><li>The general principle of which this is the failure case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide\">Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide</a><ul><li>A second incentive defect operating on the provider side.</li></ul></li></ul>","Threats to economic freedom come from guilds, corporations and local bodies as much as from the central state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The classical worry about interference with economic activity was directed at parish councils, church wardens, guilds and chartered companies, all of which were part of eighteenth-century government, and not at a central bureaucracy of the modern kind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Reading that worry as an argument against the national state specifically imports a distinction between public and private power that the original argument does not make.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>A contemporary instance of private restriction functioning as regulation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society\">Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society</a><ul><li>The guild structure in its modern form.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>The same pattern at the level of international rule-making.</li></ul></li></ul>","Through synthesis of the heretical and orthodox opinions, we arrive closer to the real truth":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it is often assumed that either the heretics or the orthodoxy is right, a far more common occurrence is that both are right to some degree, and that a synthesis of the two viewpoints produces the best approximation of the underlying truth.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains another situation in which it is beneficial to allow for freedom of expression.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>This note explains another possibility beyond the two covered in the linked note.</li></ul></li></ul>","To achieve anything, one must begin with the opposite":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To be able to achieve anything and preserve that thing, we must acknowledge and understand the opposite of that thing and always keep some aspect of the opposite in the thing that we are trying to accomplish.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental principle by which Taoism, according to Laozi, operates.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For example, if one wants to be strong, then one must start with a feeling of one being weak, and once we become strong, we must continue to understand what made us weak in the first place and counteract that. Another manifestation of this principle comes in way that arrogance manifests, as Laozi states that arrogance is the sign that one’s advancement has reached its extreme limit, since in arrogance one has forgot the opposite of what one was attempting to achieve.</p><ul><li>Humility is therefore the sign that one’s limit is far from reached.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","To be courageous is not to be afraid of death":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle claims that someone who is truly courageous is someone who is not afraid of death. Such a person will only fear “what is natural for men to fear” but that they will face such a fear in the “right way” and with principle.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is important, since courage is a main moral characteristic that Aristotle analyzes.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Courage implies the presence of pain, and thus should be praised since it is more difficult to endure pain than abstain from pleasure.</p><h4>References</h4>","To be just, one must have a disposition towards just action and be able to make deliberate choices":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In order for one to be just, one has to continually practice adopting a just disposition, and one must be in control of their actions. The opposite is also true for injustice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle explaining the conditions that must be met in order for someone to be considered just.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>It is important to note that just doing an unjust thing once does not make someone unjust. The unjust actions have to be both sustained and deliberate by the person in order for the person to be unjust, and vice versa.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>This is related to the idea that virtue must be repeatedly practiced.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>The agent not only needs to be able to perform just actions, they must be able in a just disposition in order for them to be considered just.</li></ul></li></ul>","To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean, which means not acting with deficiency nor with excess. Additionally, to be virtuous means to be in the condition of being in the mean, so the mean cannot simply be a passing impulse.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of Aristotle’s most significant moral ideas, and the central pillar of his moral philosophy.</p><h4>Aristotelianism</h4><p>The mean is something that is circumstantial and not absolute. In other words, it is ordinal, not cardinal. It varies based on the time, location, people involved, etc. In some things, the mean is the “extreme.” this has two meanings:</p><ol><li>The very fact that some action is virtuous means that it is extreme on the “virtuous-immoral” spectrum, given that is the most virtuous things that you can do.</li><li>Additionally, there are some things that are inherently bad, and there is no “mean” to reach for those things, including spite, envy, adultery, theft, murder, etc.</li></ol><h4>Chinese Philosophy</h4><p>An analogous idea is found in Chinese philosophy. As both stated in the <em>Book of Changes</em> (易经) and by Laozi, when things develop to one extreme, they are then brought to the other extreme. Therefore, one must seek the mean, the balance between the two extremes.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>Aristotle stresses, however, that virtue must be repeatedly practiced, and not simply the result of a passing impulse.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>The quality of virtuousness referenced in this note is referring to its adherence to the principle of the golden mean.</li></ul></li></ul>","To come to terms with the meaninglessness of the universe is to accept it":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the meaninglessness of the universe is best dealt with through acceptance, through the realization of the fact that one is not alone since the universe itself is meaningless and absurd.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the central concept in Absurdism.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Often times, people can have a passive acceptance of the absurd, but it is an active embrace of the absurd which Camus is arguing for here, something that is more profound.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering has metaphysical meaning\">Suffering has metaphysical meaning</a><ul><li>The Absurdist would argue that such meaning does not actually exist.</li></ul></li></ul>","To elicit high quality responses from an LLM based on some preexisting content, provide the content in the context window":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When you are attempting to elicit a response from a LLM that is based off some source material (e.g., you are asking it to summarize Shakespeare’s <em>Hamlet</em>), the model can produce a much higher quality response is the text is given in the context window.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This helps one understand how to use an LLM in the most effective way.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"There is a finite amount of compute per token in an LLM\">There is a finite amount of compute per token in an LLM</a><ul><li>Gives the reason this works. Supplying the text spends compute on processing rather than on recalling it.</li></ul></li></ul>","To reach enlightenment, things must be done sincerely, understanding, and intentionality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In order for one to be able to reach enlightenment, it is actually quite simple. One simply needs to act in the way required of them (仁，孝，礼， etc.), and also do so with sincerity (诚).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the Confucian path to enlightenment.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>For the average person, this means beginning with education, and learning through practice, and eventually coming to be able to understand the “realness” of the concepts (achieve sincerity).</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>Zhongyong</em> 21</p><p>自诚明,谓之性;自明诚,谓之教。诚则明矣,明则诚矣。</p></blockquote><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Confucianism has a prominent focus on education\">Confucianism has a prominent focus on education</a><ul><li>This is the reason that they have such a strong focus on education.</li></ul></li></ul>","Trade brings people into contact, not agreement":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Trade brings people into contract, but it does not necessarily mean that people will be brought into agreement.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This challenges the narrative presented by the Doux Commerce thesis.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>For instance, there is a persistent cultural divide between Australia and the rest of Asian (which is its biggest trading partner). Although they have close trading ties, their diplomatic relations remain tense, and there is no acceptance of Australia as an \"Asian country\" among the actual \"Asian\" countries.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture\">Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture</a><ul><li>This holds true given the contents of this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability\">Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability</a><ul><li>These notes are contradictory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest\">Islam spread to Southeast Asia through trade, rather than through military conquest</a><ul><li>Perhaps this is a counterexample to the ideas presented in this note?</li></ul></li></ul>","Trade civilizes people and encourages lower time preference, cooperation, and predictability":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that trade makes people more civilized, self-controlled (lower time preference), more willing to cooperate, and more predictable. People rely more on trade and bargaining and contracts than violence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents the Doux Commerce theory, which has been highly influential.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Studies have shown that trade flows increase the cost of conflict, which has the effect of reducing the level of conflict. Additionally, through looking at the initial days of wide-body aircraft which allowed for more goods to be transported, researches found that more trade resulted in a significant decrease in conflict. All of this evidence points to the fact that trade does seem to mitigate conflict.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy\">As societies become more modern and wealthy, they shift towards liberal democracy</a><ul><li>This is related.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation\">Capitalism erodes morality, creating greed, inequality, consumerism, and alienation</a><ul><li>The opposite idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In the future, trade in increasingly be influence by the culture of the nations engaged in trade.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that specific trade blocs may emerge that are culturally specific.</p><h4>Japan</h4><p>Because Japan is its own civilization, this means that they may have difficulty trading with others in the future.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>This note is a refutation of the idea presented in the linked note here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that, though it may be unintuitive at first, comedy is a medium that can be far more pessimistic and negative than tragedy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important interpretation of the forms of comedy and tragedy, reinterpreting them in a way that shifts and entirely changes their stated purposes.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In tragedy, we take the noble aspiration of man seriously, in order for the tragic events to even have an impact. In comedy, however, we merely “laugh our heads off” when faced with the despairing reality of the world, and view the events of the world as simply petty and inconsequential. We withdraw from sympathy (as in tragedy) and engage in a more desperate and cynical act of laughing at the misfortune of others.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>The story of Oedipus is used as an example of a tragedy when supporting the idea put forth herein.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus at Colonus\">Oedipus at Colonus</a><ul><li>Same as above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Third Man\">The Third Man</a><ul><li>This is a counterexample to the idea presented in this note, since this “tragedy” is not in fact optimistic.</li></ul></li></ul>","Train networks can produce economic value in certain cities":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Three factors allow for train networks to have positive economic value:</p><ol><li>High urban density,</li><li>High rail density (contributing to network effects),</li><li>Limited car reliance.</li></ol><h4>Significance</h4><p>This demonstrates the principles that can make a rail network successful, and can to applied in analyzing different rail networks.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Bamba calibrates an urban commuting model on Tokyo and finds household welfare gains exceeding construction, operating and maintenance costs together. Three results sharpen the three conditions above.</p><ol><li>The returns are concentrated in the dense core. Subway segments return several times what radial extensions do, and the marginal return on further radial expansion is diminishing and in places negative.</li><li>The gains are a network property rather than a sum of line-level gains. Removing the transfer connections into subway stations eliminates roughly $43\\%$ of them.</li><li>The system is sensitive to both density and the car margin. It remains beneficial at New York densities and becomes unprofitable at Los Angeles densities, and raising car speeds to American levels cuts the gains by about $30\\%$ while pushing activity out of the centre.</li></ol><h4>Note</h4><p>The third result is the one with transferable content: rail value depends on the absence of a competitive alternative as much as on the presence of demand, so the same network can be worth building in one city and not in another with identical construction costs.</p><h4>References</h4>","Trust measurement for the quality of institutions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The amount of trust in a society is a proxy measure which one can use to determine the effectiveness of the society’s institutions. The lower the trust, the lower the quality of institutions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the important of institutions in a society, since they are the foundations for the establishment of trust, and important factor in the way that societies function.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>It seems to me a little of hard to have a causality measure in this case. Is it low trust that creates bad institutions, or is it bad institutions that lead to low trust? Or is it both at the same time?</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cultures and institutions are different\">Cultures and institutions are different</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors\">Institutions that survive and remain prosperous tend to have low-cost, non-catastrophic channels for reversing elite errors</a></li></ul>","Truths which are important to society ought to be the ones exempt from skepticism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something which people often, if not explicitly, then implicitly uphold, is the fact that we should not be able to have discussion/skepticism towards facts which hold societal importance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a point of view which Mill addresses and rebutes.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specifically, the argument is referring to beliefs which are indispensable to the well-being of society, such as a fundamental moral value that underlies the legal system of a society, or some other lynchpin of the societal cultural/moral zeitgeist.</p><ul><li>As Mill says, it is often thought that only “bad men” would seek to overturn these values fundamental to society.</li><li>This argument is fundamentally a pragmatist one; what is useful for society is what is “true.” People who support this view reject a search for the real, metaphysical “truth.”<ul><li>But it is the case that the very claim that some idea is “useful” to society is itself a personal judgment that could be wrong.</li></ul></li><li>To know for sure that some idea is so sacred to the fabric of society that we must restrict attempts to discuss alternatives is to assume infallibility.</li></ul><p>This is the means by which Socrates was condemned to die.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>Because people might deem some idea to be so central to society that one should not be able to challenge it, that leads to this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Uncertainty is detrimental to growth in the short run, but indeterminate in the long run":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Uncertainty in the economy leads to slowed economic growth, in terms of diminished consumption, hiring, and investment. It also weakens the effect of monetary and fiscal policy. However, its effects in the long run are indeterminate, as uncertainty may in fact encourage R&amp;D and innovation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Today, uncertainty, especially under the Trump administration is especially prominent. It is important to understand the significance of such uncertainty, especially for the economy in the short and long term.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Uncertainty in countercyclical, meaning that it rises during period of economic downturn. This also means that it can worsen recessions, as it can lead to poorer quality of information, heightened policy experimentation, and generally amplify volatility. However, it has ambiguous long term consequences since it seems to have beneficial effects on innovation.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment\">High inflation raises interest rates and increases uncertainty, which depresses investment</a><ul><li>Also regarding the effect of uncertainty on the economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Under a state of barbarism, typical legal & execution of moral norms no longer apply as they would under a sovereign state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Under a state of barbarism, whereby there is power in the absence of freedom and law, no one is bound to typical legal norms as they would be under a sovereign state. Additionally, they are no longer bound by the typical execution of moral norms, meaning that although moral norms still apply, a state of barbarism may force immoral action in resistance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why Kant would argue that you could lie to a Nazi officer if he came to look for hidden Jews. Since under the Nazi state, you would not be in a state of sovereignty, you are no longer bound by the typical execution of moral norms, meaning that you can lie justifiably.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Lying is a &#x27;formal wrongdoing&#x27; and not a material wrong\">Lying is a 'formal wrongdoing' and not a material wrong</a></li></ul>","Under certain conditions, a wealth tax leads to greater investment in human capital and other intangible assets":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A wealth tax may create an incentive to shelter wealth in the firm, leading to greater intangible asset and human capital investments, though this does not apply to physical capital investments.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a potential argument in favor of establishing a wealth tax, given the right kind of barriers and regulations.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Obviously, this tax would only be effective if place upon business owners that have the ability to readily investment their capital. This effect is only present when the tax represents less than 5% of liquid assets of the taxpayer. If the tax is increased to more than 20%, then the effect becomes negative as owners begin to extract capital from their business in order to pay the tax. For the average firm owner in Norway, the portfolio reallocation effect (i.e., investing more into the firm) is stronger than the liquidity constraint (i.e., taking capital out of the business). The wealth tax also means that owners will work more, since they fell like they are relatively poorer.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Out-migration taxes are a method of preventing tax flight\">Out-migration taxes are a method of preventing tax flight</a><ul><li>This is a related idea of taxing the wealthy/capital owners, and both this and that note identify potentially overall beneficial ways of conducting such a tax.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes\">An unrealized gains tax may force owners to liquidate illiquid assets just to pay taxes</a><ul><li>A related idea, which seems to only be true when the tax is greater than 20% of the individual liquid assets, at least in the case of business owners and through the results of this study.</li></ul></li></ul>","Under standard Medicare, doctors are compensated for the quantity, not quality, of services they provide":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Standard Medicare has a fee-for-service system, meaning that physicians are compensated for the quantity of service that they provide, not necessarily the quality of the service that they provide.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that the incentives of doctors can be distorted, causing them to provide services that are not necessarily needed, and thus, drive up the cost of Medicare. This may in part explain my American healthcare is so expensive.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Social Security contributes to the federal debt\">Social Security contributes to the federal debt</a><ul><li>The effect of the contents of this note on the federal debt are noted here.</li></ul></li></ul>","Understanding, good deliberation, and judgment are needed for the virtue of prudence":"<h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Understanding (<em>synesis</em>) is the ability to judge the statements or decisions of others critically.</li><li>Good Deliberation (<em>euboulia</em>) is the ability to reason well and to a good goal.</li><li>Judgement (<em>gnome</em>) is the ability to see with “fairness,” when particular things in the world are unjust. </li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>These minor intellectual virtues are required in order for someone to be prudent.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Cleverness, or resourcefulness (<em>deinotes</em>) is a neutral faculty that describes the ability of an individual to reason well. It then depends on if they reason to some good (or bad) end that this becomes good deliberation.</p><ul><li>If cleverness is used to do bad, it becomes unscrupulousness.</li><li>If cleverness is used to do good, it becomes good deliberation (and thus prudence).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue\">The virtue of prudence is concerned with how best to act in order to achieve virtue</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced\">Older people are more prudent because they are more experienced</a><ul><li>Naturally, it seems that older people then are better versed in these minor virtues than younger people.</li></ul></li></ul>","Utilitarianism (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A defense of the moral theory that actions are right in proportion as they promote happiness and wrong as they produce the reverse of happiness. In the book, Mill explains that happiness means pleasure and the absence of pain, but he also argues that pleasures differ in quality, not just quantity, so intellectual and moral pleasures are superior to purely bodily ones. He responds to common objections by showing that utilitarianism is not a crude doctrine of selfish indulgence, and he defends it as a foundation for morality, justice, and social improvement, emphasizing that the best moral rules are those that tend to maximize overall human well-being.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>One of the foundational works which explicates the theory of utilitarianism.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Issues involving justice involve high levels of utility\">Issues involving justice involve high levels of utility</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological\">All ethical theories are fundamentally teleological</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"What is good does not need to be ‘proved’\">What is good does not need to be ‘proved’</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life\">Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Some pleasures are greater than others\">Some pleasures are greater than others</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While its easier for less advanced animals to be satisfied, the more advanced can make the imperfection bearable\">While its easier for less advanced animals to be satisfied, the more advanced can make the imperfection bearable</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We shouldn’t calculate utility before each action, but rather construct rules for generally beneficial actions\">We shouldn’t calculate utility before each action, but rather construct rules for generally beneficial actions</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"All humans are innately good\">All humans are innately good</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Law is not constitutive of justice\">Law is not constitutive of justice</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Justice is an individual’s moral right\">Justice is an individual’s moral right</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having\">A right is something society ought to defend individuals in having</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>This book attacks utilitarianism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a><ul><li>Presents an alternative theory to utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","Utilitarianism fails":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Utilitarianism as an ethical theory fails due to a variety of reasons.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Utilitarianism is one of the foremost ethical theories.</p><h4>Arguments</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism\">A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism</a></li></ul><h5>Rawlsian Objections</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Classical utilitarianism is too demanding\">Classical utilitarianism is too demanding</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones\">The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A Theory of Justice\">A Theory of Justice</a><ul><li>Rawls’ book is a major counterargument to utilitarianism.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"On Liberty\">On Liberty</a><ul><li>Mill is a defender of utilitarianism, which is implicitly used in this book.</li></ul></li></ul>","Utility cannot be compared across individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There is no common scale on which one person’s gain in welfare can be measured against another’s loss, which means the utilitarian instruction to maximise aggregate welfare has no determinate content.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the technical objection that removes the foundation of welfare-maximising redistribution, independently of any moral objection to it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pareto improvement does not take into account the relative strength of utilities or disutilities\">Pareto improvement does not take into account the relative strength of utilities or disutilities</a><ul><li>The same limitation, expressed as a defect in the standard efficiency criterion.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Utilitarianism fails\">Utilitarianism fails</a><ul><li>The broader conclusion this supports.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>An alternative criterion that avoids the comparison.</li></ul></li></ul>","Value is different from price":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that value is something that is different from price. Interestingly, this is both an idea in Austrian Economics, and in Marxist Economics, through the definition of value is different in both cases.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a core concept in both Austrian and Marxist economics.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In Austrian Economics, value is subject and ordinal. Price is merely a reflection of the subjective value in terms of a currency. In Marxist Economics, value is objective and defined as the Socially Necessary Labor Time, which is the “average labor required under normal conditions to produce something.” This value is only observable through price, the chaotic signal on the market that fluctuates around the true value (SNLT).</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Labor Theory of Value is false\">The Labor Theory of Value is false</a><ul><li>A key part of the LTV is the idea of the Socially Necessary Labor Time, which is their definition of value.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy\">Price level is driven by the amount of money in the economy</a><ul><li>Perhaps a reason that price is not representative of value is that the amount of money in the economy can differ, meaning that the price can change while the underlying value of something might not.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus\">Price level is driven by the ratio of government debt to expected future surplus</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking\">Price controls cause supply shortage, misallocation of resources, and rent-seeking</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Virtue divides into the intellectual, which is taught, and the moral, which is habituated":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle claims that there are two different types of virtue: intellectual and moral. Intellectual virtues are ones which are taught, and can be forgotten if not used. Moral virtues come from habit (ethos), which means that you learn them through repeated practice, not studying theory. Thus, they become a permanent way that one engages with the world once established.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is crucial for Aristotle’s elucidation of, specifically moral virtue, in Book II.</p><h4>Moral Virtue</h4><p>Moral virtues are established through repeated practice, not just abstract study. The integration of physical practice into moral virtue is its big separator from intellectual virtue. We are constituted by nature to have a propensity for moral virtue, but their full development still requires repeated practice until it becomes a habit.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>Virtues are the key to happiness, which Aristotle says is the goal of life.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean\">To be morally virtuous is to act in accordance with the golden mean and exist in the condition of being in the golden mean</a><ul><li>This explains what Aristotle means when he talks about being morally virtuous.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself\">Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself</a><ul><li>An elaboration of the idea present in this note that virtue must be practiced repeatedly in order for it to manifest in one’s “disposition.”</li></ul></li></ul>","Virtue must be repeatedly practiced to be manifest in oneself":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that virtue must be repeatedly practiced in order for it to be manifest in a person, that an action is not merely virtuous if it is the result of some fleeting impulse and not fundamentally a part of someone’s character.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s most important point regarding the nature of virtue, that it must be constantly practiced.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>When it talks about “\\[acting] in a virtuous state” here, it is specifically referring to acting consistently, over a fixed period of time, in a virtuous state.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>The condition for a happy life is sustained virtue.</li></ul></li></ul>","Virtue should be shaped by law and public education if possible; otherwise, it should be shaped by family or friends":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle claims that the best case is that the state, since its laws have compulsory force, should shape the virtues of individual citizens. But if this is not possible, then family or friends should attempt to shape virtue.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the nature of how virtue ought to be instilled in the individual.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>Creation of virtue by family or friends could actually be good in a certain respect, in that they are more circumstantial/individual to the person that it is directed to, which could have a better effect than the blanket law of a state.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one\">Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one</a><ul><li>This is related to Aristotle’s idea here, placing politics as something that is moral in nature.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself\">Natural virtue is a disposition towards virtue, but it is not virtue itself</a><ul><li>Natural virtue, plus the state or the family or friends, can create true virtue.</li></ul></li></ul>","Washington’s Farewell Address inspired American isolationism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Washington’s Farewell Address outline a foreign policy stance that was strictly isolationist. He argued against the US getting embroiled in future conflicts in distant places and instead focus more directly on the American continent/domestic issues.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>In a broad sense, this imperative held until the US intervention in the First World War, meaning that it significantly shaped the US view of foreign policy in the periods leading up to that point.</p><h4>References</h4>","We find something funny because it grants us relief":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We often find things funny because they allow us to release some kind of pent up stress.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could serve as a theory to explain why we laugh.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A soldier facing execution makes a joke about how terrible their last meal is, releasing the pent up tension that exists at the moment.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority\">We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority</a><ul><li>Another theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur\">We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur</a><ul><li>Yet another theory.</li></ul></li></ul>","We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The mainstream accepted theory of humor, which states that we find something funny because what actually occurs represents an incongruence with our perceptions of what ought to occur, or simply what we thought would occur.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents the most widely accepted theory of humor today.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>A comedian tells a joke that includes a pun that changes the entire meaning of the sentence to something that is dark or otherwise unexpected.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it grants us relief\">We find something funny because it grants us relief</a><ul><li>A different theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority\">We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority</a><ul><li>Another theory.</li></ul></li></ul>","We find something funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>One of the foremost theories of humor states that we find something to be funny because we feel a temporary sense of superiority over the humorous thing.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to explain why we find something funny.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>When a comedian makes a joke about how disable people are not able to ambulate, the audience feels a temporary sense of superiority which makes them laugh.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur\">We find something funny because it is a logical non-sequitur</a><ul><li>Another theory.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We find something funny because it grants us relief\">We find something funny because it grants us relief</a><ul><li>Yet another theory.</li></ul></li></ul>","We have power over nothing except our will":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the only thing we can control is ourselves. This usually implies that we should not be distressed by the things that are out of our control.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a significant stoic idea that has influenced not just followers of the Stoics, but also many other philosophers.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One has to confront their problems head on\">One has to confront their problems head on</a></li></ul>","We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Since it is the case that we tolerate people having different aesthetic choices and the such, it seems that, we must also tolerate people with different lifestyle choices, i.e., the eccentric.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This must be the case since otherwise, we miss out on the geniuses in society and the people who are truly of strong character.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>Suppressing individuality is bad.</li></ul></li></ul>","We often mistake our own unclearness of thought for some inherent mystery in the subject of our thought":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often the case that our own unclearness of thought regarding a subject is projected unto our understanding of the subject itself, such that we belief it to be an inherently mysterious subject.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This diagnoses a significant problem in our everyday epistemology.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When it comes to discussing complex topics such as the nature of morality or the meaning of life, we often come to the conclusion that there is ultimately just no inherent solution or some equivalent conclusion. However, we must never discount the possibility that it is merely that we have not thought long enough or have not clarified our thoughts enough.</p><h4>References</h4>","We ought not to interference with the personal liberties of other individuals":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We should not interfere with the personal liberties of others, for a variety of reasons.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core axiom of liberalism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are many explanations which can be given as to why this is ought to be followed.</p><ul><li>We don’t necessarily know what is best for others.</li><li>It could be that people who act unorthodoxly are actually right, and by suppressing their behavior, we are in fact moving further away from the truth.</li></ul><p>We cannot justify repression of individual via the fact that:</p><ul><li><em>The person is harming themselves in the absence of outside intervention.</em> <ul><li>But the wider society rarely knows about what is good or bad for the person, and may, in an attempt to prevent “bad” behavior, wipe out a potentially good thing.</li><li>For something like not taking care of one self’s appearance, though this may affect the general interest in some minute way, we must accept this in order to preserve general person liberties.</li></ul></li><li><em>The person is setting a bad example for others.</em><ul><li>If its the case that the person is setting a bad example for others, there would naturally be bad consequences for others to see, and thus, others would avoid that person’s behavior.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>Notes</h4><p>There are some personal injuries which must be tolerated, since they arise out of people considering the protection of their own rights and liberties.</p><ul><li>For instance, someone who is short tempered may have very few friends, though they cannot claim that this is an injustice done to them, since people are merely protecting their own interests.</li><li>Likewise, societal institutions such as competition cannot cause harm, since there has not been an unwarranted invasion of one’s rights which is contrary to the general interest.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality\">‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality</a><ul><li>This is one instance where the rule laid out in this note can be vioalted.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>Another case.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society\">We must tolerate, even celebrate eccentric people in society</a><ul><li>Another related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even though the original position is a hypothetical decision scenario, it still makes sense for us to believe in its methodological power.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Here, Rawls justifies the use of the original position.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Although it is hypothetical, it is made up of a collection of axioms which we would independently accept (this is by definition, since our own description of the original position utilizes our own axioms; in this way, the heavily epistemic work is done when Rawls attempts to convince us of the truth of his particular set of axioms).</p><ul><li>These principles outline the limits of what we would accept as fair terms of social cooperation.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","We should desire less":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The elimination of superfluous desire is something that Laozi repeatedly stresses.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is something Laozi believes is more according to the Tao, and that too much desire overwhelms the individual and causes suffering.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>苏辙《老子解》</em></p><p>五色令人目盲，五音令人耳聋，五味令人口爽，驰骋田猎令人心发狂，难得之货令人行妨。是以圣人为腹不为目，故去彼取此。</p></blockquote><p>The idea that one should not constantly strive for more stimulation, since that is liable to corrupt us. Instead, we should just be satiated (like the belly, which gets full) at some point and stop desiring more (like the eyes, which can continue to receive stimuli indefinitely).</p><h4>References</h4>","We should never halt technological innovation":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>We should never actively halt technological innovation, since that would in fact be an existential catastrophe, since it entails a destruction of humanity’s future potential.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides a unique take on the argument that humanity should never halt technological development, despite any temporary downsides that it may have.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise\">Humans have grown smart before they have grown wise</a><ul><li>This is a potential problem with this claim, since new technologies can be abused or misused to hurt humans.</li></ul></li></ul>","We should not have more friends than we can get intimate with":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that the perfect number of friends allows us to get intimate with each friend.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle sets a rule for the number of friends we ought to have, because otherwise, since it seems that having friends is good, we ought to have as many as possible.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>If we have more friends than we could get intimate with, it would be difficult to sympathize or get devoted to the friend.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtuous man needs friends\">The virtuous man needs friends</a><ul><li>This is the principle that expands upon the concept that the virtuous man needs friends.</li></ul></li></ul>","We should pursue autonomous AI governance rather than AI containment":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A better strategy for managing agents is not containment, which fails as computational abilities skyrocket, but the creation of institutional regulations allow for more effective management of agents.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This argues for a significantly different strategy towards governing AI.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>In this framework, the humans are the ultimate constitutional authority and give the essence of the law for the AI agents, who build an administration that specifies and enforces the rules. There is no axiomatic argument to prove that agents will tend to comply with the rules, but observation from existing tests and also human societies in general argue that this would be the case.</p><h4>Note</h4><p>It seems to me that this anthropomorphizes agents too much, since they do not seem to hold many of the same moral values that humans do. The current success of this system may be a result of the fact that AIs act in imitation of humans, but this might vary with future models.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation\">Agentic AI can radically improve the rate of scientific research and innovation</a><ul><li>Perhaps in specific circumstances, such as high risk scientific research, agents would have the best incentive to work with a bounded framework?</li><li>But at the same time, maybe not, since that would constrain their ability to make breakthroughs.</li></ul></li></ul>","We shouldn’t calculate utility before each action, but rather construct rules for generally beneficial actions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The fundamental idea of rule utilitarianism, where utility is not calculate before each action, but rather a set of generally utility improving actions are drawn up, and people merely act in accordance with them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main branches of utilitarianism.</p><h4>References</h4>","Weak and Kafkaesque institutions created a large informal economy in Latin America":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The economies of Latin American often have large informal sectors due to the fact that they have weak institutions and extremely bureaucratic legal systems.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the large informal sector that exists in the economies of Latin America.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Latin Americans create informal businesses because of the high cost of legalization.<ul><li>But they do pay taxes, since most of the tax is in the form of excise and consumption taxes.</li><li>They also pay for this by holding large cash balances, whose value is constantly decreasing due to the deficit spending by the government and the inflation that comes with it.</li><li>In fact, they have their own informal set of system, which, although is not as strong as the government enforced rules, respects property rights, insurance systems, and torts.</li></ul></li><li>Both homeowners do not have titles, which means that it is difficult for them to use it as a collateral for loans.</li><li>Since the laws are weak, there is no limited liability.</li><li>There is no enforcement of contracts, which means that it is difficult to pool resources effectively, since people can just betray that people that they are working with whenever they want.<ul><li>This means that overall production will be less efficient.</li></ul></li><li>Bad laws are created (and a lot of them) because most of it originated form the executive.<ul><li>When laws are not just created by the executive, it is not monopolized by one person/office.</li><li>There is no way for the people who create the laws to be accountable in Latin America.</li><li>There is no public access to information/public accountability.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit\">A bad system or institution is one where only the incumbents benefit</a><ul><li>This is the case in Latin America, leading to the development of a large informal sector.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>There is no trust in the institutions that exist in many Latin American countries.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society\">Culture is not determinant of the political institutions that can exist within a society</a><ul><li>The information in this note could both be used to argue against and for this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","Western government is defined by constitutional liberalism, not democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that Western government is better envisioned as being built upon Constitutional Liberalism, and not Democracy (mass plebiscite).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This offers an unique view on the nature of Western governments, deriving from the distinction between Democracy and Constitutional Liberalism.</p><h4>Source</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Fareed Zakaria</p><p>The “Western model of government” is best symbolized not by the mass plebiscite but the impartial judge.</p></blockquote><h4>Evidence</h4><ul><li>Ever since Roman times, there has been a strong traditional in the Western world of preserving certain individual rights and enforcing the rule of law.</li><li>\"Democracy\" in the sense of a mass plebiscite did not really originate in any meaningful capacity until the 19th century, in the US and the UK.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Democracy does not entail constitutional liberalism\">Democracy and Constitutional Liberalism</a></li></ul>","What is good does not need to be ‘proved’":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Something that is good does not need to be proved that it is good, since it is intuitively obvious that thing is good. Reductively, something that is good is a means to something good.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This lies at the core of Mill’s utilitarian philosophy.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This seems rather weak to me. Additionally, it faces the Open Question Argument objection, which, although it has its own flaws, is regardless a notable problem for such a conception of the good.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A good person is someone who has an above average moral character\">A good person is someone who has an above average moral character</a><ul><li>Related discussion of the good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated\">Technical goodness and moral goodness should be differentiated</a><ul><li>It is largely (only?) referring to moral goodness here.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Suffering is good, if done correctly\">Suffering is good, if done correctly</a><ul><li>Is this the case?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good\">Goodness is heterogeneous, with no single characteristic common to everything called good</a><ul><li>So goodness can be debated, which means that to some level, goodness as intuition differs between people. It seems crazy to say that goodness is entirely without a need for proof.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself\">One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself</a><ul><li>It seems more difficult for to account for this differentiation under this defintion.</li></ul></li></ul>","What is good for a person is a rational long-term plan of life given reasonably favorable circumstances":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls deliberately give a thin and non-controversial definition of the good (since people in the original position do not know what they would consider good). Importantly, his definition of good incorporates rationality, and also are necessarily based on/need the primary goods, since the execution of a rational plan needs primary goods.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This theory of the good is what underlies all of Rawls’ other claims about what justice would be.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Long term goodness is the means to a happy life\">Long term goodness is the means to a happy life</a><ul><li>Aristotle could perhaps argue, based off of Rawls’ definition, that to be good means you will be happy? But this also makes sense, given that Rawls’ definition is an evolution of Aristotle’s.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>Rawls would argue that pleasure is in no way the good.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself\">One ought to choose what is good for them, not what is good in itself</a><ul><li>Rawls would agree.</li></ul></li></ul>","What is pleasurable to the good man is truly pleasurable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle allows that pleasures vary with people’s character, but he holds that the virtuous person is the measure of what is pleasant without qualification, so the pleasures that accompany virtuous activities are genuinely pleasant and therefore choice worthy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains that not all pleasures are good/desirable.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>There are certain pleasures, such as bodily ones, which may appeal to certain individuals.</li><li>However, what is truly worthy striving for are the things that bring about pleasure based on the character of the virtuous person.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Acting virtuously is not painful\">Acting virtuously is not painful</a> <ul><li>In fact, the virtuous man enjoys many pleasures, and these pleasures that they enjoy are the ones that are genuinely pleasant.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>In acting in a virtuous state, the agent gains pleasure, which is the highest pleasure possible.</li></ul></li></ul>","When a country hits its fiscal limit, it can suffer a range of negative consequences":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a country hits its fiscal limit, it can suffer a range of negative consequences, including reduced market access, higher borrowing costs, currency depreciation, capital flight, or even economic contraction or default.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows why nations really do not want to hit the fiscal limit.</p><h4>Consequences</h4><p>The consequences for hitting the fiscal limit is high. It could be any of the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Reduced market access</strong>: investors buy less government debt or demand shorter term debt. It reduces the ability for the government to borrow/spend.</li><li><strong>Higher borrowing costs</strong>: markets demand greater interest on government debt since there is a higher perceived risk of default or inflation.</li><li><strong>Currency depreciation</strong>: the national currency is less demanded due to the high risk of government default. This raises the cost of foreign debt and imported goods.</li><li><strong>Capital flight</strong>: investors want to pull out their assets due to the high perceived risk of government default.</li></ul><p>All these could further contribute to economics contraction, or even government default.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation\">The fiscal limit of a government is determined by the standard debt dynamics equation</a></li></ul>","When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When the government is unwilling to enact reform that would appease the people, the people may simply take the matter into their own hands and revolt.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This has been the cause of many revolution in history, and could be an important framework through which to analyze political situations that currently exist and will exist.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms\">The July Revolution was a result of the French government suppressing needed reforms</a><ul><li>An example of the principle described in this note in action.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill\">Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill</a><ul><li>An example of the principle described in this note in action.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians\">The fall of the Roman Republic could be partly attributed to the fact that the Senate failed to adequately represent the Plebeians</a><ul><li>An example of the principle described in this note in action.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power\">The Yellow Turbans were only suppressed with the aid of local warlords, which strengthened their power</a><ul><li>An elaboration of when people do rise up in revolt, how the government deals with the revolt is also important.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions\">Following 1848, government shifted from repression to reform to avoid further revolutions</a></li></ul>","When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure, since the original phenomenon that the measure was intending to record may be distorted by attempts to increase the observed measurement.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a general adage that applies to many areas of study, but especially when it comes to economics, since the measurements are the only way to determine the success of a policy. Furthermore, since all systems are, at their core, meant to be optimizers, then it seems that the best heuristic when it comes to choosing between systems is choosing the system which minimizes loss/overfitting.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This can fundamentally be explained by the fact that all optimization is subject to some amount of overfitting. This means that while a system seems to have been trained in order to optimize for some goal, this goal for which it is optimizing is actually not representative of the overall nature of the the goal at hand. Over a long enough time scale, the optimization will show its cracks and will no longer track the original intention of the optimization.</p><ul><li>Practically, this seems to be the result of informational frictions, which arise through the conversion of information from one form to another, or the failure for information to be expressed in a succinct yet accurate and precise way.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trust measurement for the quality of institutions\">Trust measurement for the quality of institutions</a><ul><li>A potential measurement that could become a bad measure, if it is pursued incessantly?</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">AI alignment suffers from two interconnected problems of overfitting</span><ul><li>I.e., how well the mesa objective of the AI matches with the overall goal, and how well the AI is able to optimize for the mesa goal.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should pursue autonomous AI governance rather than AI containment\">We should pursue autonomous AI governance rather than AI containment</a><ul><li>Perhaps this strategy allows for a smaller loss function/overfitting?</li></ul></li></ul>","When the polarity of an amino acid is discussed, it is specifically referring to the polarity of the R group":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When discussing the polarity of an amino acid, what is in fact being discussed is the polarity of the R group, not of the backbone. The backbone is always polar.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for AP Bio.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li>The more polar a molecule, the more soluble it is in water<ul><li>This explains further the implication of the property of polarity that is described in this note.</li></ul></li><li>Hydrogen bonds form between polar molecules (or charged molecules)<ul><li>A type of bond that is formed between the subject of this note, polar molecules.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein\">Nonpolar r-groups are likely to be found in the interior of a protein</a><ul><li>A related note that describes the nature of non-polar notes.</li></ul></li></ul>","When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When considering the case of innovation, being an incumbent in a market may actually be a disadvantage.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This turns common antitrust logic on its head.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When there is a new technology being introduced that has the capability to replace an existing technology, having large sunk costs can actually be a barrier to change rather than a barrier to entry. For instance, the new model of LLM search has the capability to replace web search. This means that if Google were to invest in LLM search, they would essentially be cannibalizing a crucial part of their current services in order to engage with this new innovation. On the other hand, established players in a field, by innovating, reduce the market share of the incumbent, so it benefits them to innovate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries\">IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries</a><ul><li>A concrete case of the incumbent disadvantage.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs\">Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs</a><ul><li>Suggests the disadvantage can be structural, not merely a matter of incumbent complacency.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>Having interlocking patents then, would be a way for incumbents in a market to retain their dominance in the market.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a><ul><li>Same as above.</li></ul></li></ul>","When two conductors touch, they both acquire the same charge such that the overall charge is preserved":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When two conductors touch, their combined charge remains the same. However, the charges are individually evenly distributed across the two conductors.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This represents the law of charge conservation in action, and is relevant for AP Physics C E&amp;M.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>Given two conductors, one with $Q$ and the other with $2Q$ charge, when the two conductors touch, they both end up with $1.5Q$.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li>Charges always refers to either electrons or ions<ul><li>Relevant for understanding the content of this note.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not\">A conductor allows charges to flow freely; an insulator does not</a><ul><li>Describes the nature of conductors, which is also relevant for understanding this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","When two genes are farther apart, it is more likely that recombination will occur":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When two genes are farther apart on a chromosome, it is more likely that they are going to recombination with the paired sister chromatids.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why the recombination frequency increases as the distances between the genes increases.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Think of a chromosome like a long piece of rope. If you close your eyes and cut the rope at a random spot, you are much more likely to cut between two knots if they are at opposite ends of the rope than if they are right next to each other.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"During Prophase I, homologous chromosomes meet in a process called synapsis\">During Prophase I, homologous chromosomes meet in a process called synapsis</a><ul><li>The precondition. Recombination requires the tetrad that synapsis forms.</li></ul></li></ul>","Whether a currency has exorbitant privilege is primarily determined by the existence of a viable alternative currency":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Whether is currency can continue to have exorbitant privilege is determined by whether there is a real alternative to that currency extant in the world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to understand if a currency dominant currency with exorbitant privilege is able to continue having that privilege.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is something that has been observed in past examples of currencies which have enjoyed exorbitant privilege.</p><ul><li>When the Dutch gilder enjoyed the exorbitant privilege, it was the only nation in Europe that had a modern banking system that allowed it to operate in the way that it did and keep the Netherlands wealthy. <ul><li>This ended when the Bank of London rose in power and the pound was able to overtake the gilder, partially due to the involvement of the Dutch in continental conflicts.</li></ul></li><li>When Britain's pound enjoyed the exorbitant privilege, there was no real alternative to it. <ul><li>It was not until after WWII when the US financial system became globalized and US industrial power become something that was felt all around the world that the dollar became the premier currency.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Soft power is premised on hard power\">Soft power is premised on hard power</a><ul><li>The power of exorbitant privilege rests on the country being able militarily and able to maintain its financial system.</li></ul></li></ul>","While its easier for less advanced animals to be satisfied, the more advanced can make the imperfection bearable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While it can be easier for lower animals to satisfy themselves, animals with greater intelligence can make the fact that they will never be able to satisfy all of their desires bearable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains something fundamental about human existence, namely that though we may not be able to achieve complete satisfaction since we are such advanced and creatures, that very fact means that we can allow ourselves to make this incompleteness bearable.</p><h4>References</h4>","While most people share a common set of fundamental beliefs regarding justice, more broadly, conceptions differ":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Most people share the belief that</p><ul><li>There should be no unfounded/irrational distinctions between people in assigning rights and duties.</li><li>There should be a proper/fair distribution of social advantages.</li></ul><p>However, they would disagree as to how those principles are arrived upon.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Here, Rawls is making clear the distinction between the concept of justice and conceptions of justice. In the rest of the book, it is explaining his conception of Justice and why is the best one.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This is meant to be extremely deflationary, since Rawls will fill in the structure step up here with his own conception of justice.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city\">The concept of justice is most fully realized only when applied in a city</a><ul><li>Aristotle's take on the object of justice.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit\">Distributive Justice means that each individual receives the amount of something commensurate to their merit</a><ul><li>Aristotle distinguishes distributive justice as a particular form of justice, offering his conception of justice.</li></ul></li></ul>","While nuclear weapons served to unite the world under a common order in the past, today, it serves to fracture the global system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While there was a nuclear monopoly/oligopoly in the world, the leading powers were able to use the influence of said nukes to ensure stability in the global system. However, with nuclear proliferation, the global system is likely to fragment.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that nuclear proliferation would be something that the current holders of nukes desperately do not want to occur.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>China helped Iran and Pakistan develop nukes. Part of the motivation for this would be to challenge the dominance of the US</li><li>Today, there is not a race to build more/better nukes. Rather, it is race to prevent nations that do not yet have nukes from building any.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","While the state is justified in requiring education, it cannot be the only provider of education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While the state has a right to require that parents give their children education, the state cannot be the only provider of education.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This composes a part of Mill’s thoughts on education.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The state must require education since Mill claims that life would be exceedingly dull without basic education. The state, however, cannot be the sole provider of education, since then everybody would be educated in the same way, and we want people to be able to be educated in different ways to produce different types of people. The state ca provide education in cases where the parent’s can’t afford it otherwise, etc.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should do as little as possible\">The state should do as little as possible</a><ul><li>The Taoists would not agree with this.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The state should be entirely in charge of education\">The state should be entirely in charge of education</a><ul><li>Aristotle would argue that the state should entirely be in charge of education, for the cultivation of virtue.</li></ul></li></ul>","Windows 11 in 2025":"<p>In the 18 years following the publication of <em>Bad Samaritans</em>, many of economist Ha-Joon Chang’s hypotheses have been put to the test, including his views on patent and intellectual property rights (IPRs) regulation. I will seek to elucidate the veracity of Chang’s claims, made in chapter 6 of his seminal work, <em>Bad Samaritans</em>, “Windows 98 in 1997.” Since 2007, evidence has largely vindicated Chang: patents can spur R&amp;D in high–fixed-cost sectors, but rigid IPR regulation via diluted patent content and interlocking patents raises entry barriers, slows diffusion, and entrenches incumbents.</p><p>In the past 18 years, one thing has remained constant: patents and IPR still exist. And with them remains the fundamental justification for their existence, namely that IPRs incentivize innovation.</p><p>Now, why would IPRs incentivize innovation? It seems that the very nature of IPRs (especially their exclusive, monopolistic nature) would <em>prohibit</em>, not encourage, innovation. To understand this, we must understand the concept of a “natural monopoly.” <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A natural monopoly is produced when fixed costs are high and variable costs are low in an industry\">A natural monopoly arises when the fixed costs are high in an industry, but the marginal costs are low. This means that once production, which may be initially expensive, starts, the firm can quickly achieve an economy of scale.</a> The key component to consider is the high initial costs of production, which necessitate that few are able to meet the high capital requirement. Those who are able to afford such capital requirements thereby take on immense risk, which demands that the reward be adequately commensurate. Here is where patents and IPRs come in. As monopolies provide their holders with disproportionate surplus, they can be utilized as a form of reward for the risk-takers, without whom innovations in science and technology would not have occurred. If there had been no legal protection of the result of such a high capital investment in the form of protections for IPRs, competitors could create imitations, thereby bringing the price—and thus profit—down for the original capital owner.</p><p>Herein lies the nucleus of Chang’s contention: the government controls the length of a patent and, thereby, the size of the carrot which leads people to take substantial risks.</p><p>But how big, <em>exactly</em>, ought the carrot be?</p><p>Chang argued that the world’s governments have provided too generous a benefit for capital owners, which, paradoxically, leads to the originally unparadoxical conclusion: that such patents and IPRs create a barrier to innovation and benefit only incumbents in the market. But is this the case? Let us examine his argument:</p><p>Returning to the discussion regarding the fundamental justification for patents and natural monopolies, Chang puts forth a challenge, arguing that <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas\">material incentives are not the only thing that motivates people to produce new ideas</a>. He points to the amount of research conducted by non-profit and government organizations, which seems to be testament to the idea that research need not be conducted by profit-seeking private actors.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p. 124-125] However, there are a few inaccuracies in such an argument. First, from 1996–2016, 67 to 97 percent of drug development was conducted by the private sector.[@chakravarthyPublicPrivateSectorContributions2016, p. 2] This seems to undermine the claim that non-material incentives play a substantial role in funding investment in new research. Yes, perhaps some research into pharmaceuticals was conducted via government funding or through private charities, but the contribution of funding through such sources pales in comparison with private, for-profit funding. In addition, much of the government research focuses on “basic science,” i.e., research that, although crucial, does not directly contribute to the development of pharmaceuticals. Rather, it is primarily private institutions that carry out the expensive, risky, and highly specialized process of creating actual medicines.[@chakravarthyPublicPrivateSectorContributions2016, p. 5, 10]</p><p>Chang also makes the claim that patents are not needed in most industries, as often new inventions are sufficiently difficult to imitate to provide the innovator with a substantive first-mover advantage.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.125-126] Regarding this, Chang seems to be mistaken: the pharmaceutical industry is an example of an industry where it is extremely difficult to make imitations of competitors’ products.[@kirchhoffBiosimilarsKeyRegulatory2017] Yet, around 65 percent of commercial inventions in the pharmaceutical industry would not have existed had patents not been granted.[@cockburnImportancePatentsInnovation2015, p.740] That being said, Chang’s claim is indeed applicable in some industries; for example, the fashion industry has achieved what some term a “low-IP equilibrium,” where rampant copying actually drives innovation.[@raustialaPiracyParadoxInnovation2006, p. 1732-1734] In these industries, the existence of patents would only impede progress.</p><p>Industries that require patents and IPRs face a further problem, however: new patents are increasingly trivial, even potentially malicious, which Chang attributes to <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inventors have an incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs\">inventors’ incentive to lower the bar of originality for patents and IPRs</a>. First, he points out, there is theft of traditional knowledge from developing nations; knowledge that, despite being widely known in its area of origin, has been patented by other countries.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.137] Second, he identifies the phenomenon of “<a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">interlocking patents</a>”: patents have tended to be increasingly fragmented and minute, meaning that any new major innovation would have to navigate a landscape of dozens, perhaps hundreds, of other relevant patents [@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.139-140]. Regarding his former concern, systems instituted since the publication of his work have now placed much stricter limits on the ability of nations to patent traditional knowledge. For example, the 2025 WIPO Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic Resources and Associated Traditional Knowledge (GRATK) will “enhance the efficacy, transparency and quality of the patent system with regard to genetic resources and traditional knowledge…, and prevent patents from being granted erroneously for inventions that are not novel…”[@alvesWIPOsNewTreaty2024] In addition, many countries have implemented laws or compiled traditional knowledge databases to further prevent theft of traditional knowledge.[@gutierrezHowLatinAmerica2020; @canadianintellectualpropertyofficePatentsBasedGenetic2025] Regarding the latter concern, while it is very much extant, there are no “easy” potential solutions to implement. “Patent pools,” where all members of an industry cross-license all relevant patents to one another, are difficult to coordinate, especially without government intervention. Yet, excessive government intervention in promoting patent pools could, once more, stifle innovation. The problem of interlocking patents identified by Chang is a complex one and will require nuanced regulation and institutional reform in order to mitigate it, being beyond the scope of this short essay.</p><p>Ultimately, Chang claims that a strict and overregulated patent and IPR regime (in the manners previously described) hurts developing countries.[@changBadSamaritansMyth2008, p.141] The high cost of running a patent office, especially when having to comply with variegated international regulations, acts as <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">a drag on the economies of developing, less technologically advanced nations</a>. International regulations represent a misallocation of resources that prevent less affluent countries from investing in crucial aspects of government functions, such as education, health care, or law enforcement. Moreover, they prevent developing nations from being able to acquire new knowledge in the long term, thereby deterring these nations from further economic development. Recent evidence bears this out. Implementing international IPR regulation has indeed imposed measurable administrative and fiscal burdens on low-income states in the form of fixed outlays to stand up offices, recurrent enforcement costs, and royalty payments.[@maskusIntellectualPropertyChallenges2001, p. 466-470] Moreover, comparative reviews find that without strong local absorptive capacity, regulation chiefly advantages incumbents, raising prices and constraining economic growth rather than benefiting developing nations as a whole.[@hassanIntellectualPropertyDeveloping, p.27-30]</p><p>Chang’s conclusion that IPR should be designed to help developing countries be more productive and attain new technology is a cogent one. Thus, while the “carrot” of monopoly remains defended as necessary for innovation, and some empirical data remain contrary to Chang’s claims, the overall evidence leans in favor of Chang’s conclusion: as global inequality increases, it is up to our international organizations to recognize the faults in their systems, rectify them, and promote a more equitable and inclusive global community.</p><h4>Bibliography</h4><p>Alves, Ayla Do Vale. WIPO’s New Treaty on Intellectual Property, Genetic Resources and Traditional Knowledge – A Turning Point for Indigenous Heritage? May 24, 2024. https://esil-sedi.eu/esil-reflection-wipos-new-trea ty-on-intellectual-property-genetic-resources-and-traditional-knowledge-aturning-point-for-indigenous-heritage/.</p><p>Canadian Intellectual Property Office. “Patents based on genetic resources and associated traditional knowledge.” Innovation, Science and Economic Development Canada, June 16, 2025. https://ised-isde.canada.ca/site/canadianintellectual-property-office/en/patents-based-genetic-resources-andassociated-traditional-knowledge.</p><p>Chakravarthy, Ranjana, Kristina Cotter, Joseph DiMasi, Christopher-Paul Milne, and Nils Wendel. “Public- and Private-Sector Contributions to the Research and Development of the Most Transformational Drugs in the Past 25 Years: From Theory to Therapy.” Therapeutic Innovation &amp; Regulatory Science 50, no. 6 (November 2016): 759–68. https://doi.org/10.1177/2168479016648730.</p><p>Chang, Ha-Joon. Bad samaritans: the myth of free trade and the secret history of capitalism. New York: Bloomsbury, 2008.</p><p>Cockburn, Iain, and Genia Long. “The Importance of Patents to Innovation: Updated Cross-Industry Comparisons with Biopharmaceuticals.” Expert Opinion on Therapeutic Patents 25, no. 7 (July 2015): 739–42. https: //doi.org/10.1517/13543776.2015.1040762.</p><p>Gutiérrez, Nicolás. “How Latin America Countries Protect Their Traditional Knowledge Through IP - European Commission.” IP Helpdesk, January 16, 2020. https://intellectual-property-helpdesk.ec.europa.eu/news-events /news/how-latin-america-countries-protect-their-traditional-knowledgethrough-ip-2020-01-16\\_en.</p><p>Hassan, Emmanuel, Ohid Yaqub, and Stephanie Diepeveen. Intellectual Property and Developing Countries: A Review of the Literature. n.d.</p><p>Kirchhoff, Carol F., Xiao-Zhuo Michelle Wang, Hugh D. Conlon, Scott Anderson, Anne M. Ryan, and Arindam Bose. “Biosimilars: Key Regulatory Considerations and Similarity Assessment Tools.” Biotechnology and Bioengineering 114, no. 12 (2017): 2696–705. https://doi.org/10.1002/bit.26438.</p><p>Maskus, Keith E. “Intellectual Property Challenges for Developing Countries: An Economic Perspective.” University of Illinois Law Review, April 16, 2001. https://www.researchgate.net/publication/294496476\\_Intellectual\\_proper ty\\_challenges\\_for\\_developing\\_countries\\_An\\_economic\\_perspective.</p><p>Raustiala, Kal, and Christopher Sprigman. “The Piracy Paradox: Innovation and Intellectual Property in Fashion Design - Virginia Law Review.” Virginia Law Review 92, no. 8 (December 2006). https://virginialawreview.org/arti cles/piracy-paradox-innovation-and-intellectual-property-fashion-design/.</p>","Wisdom is the virtue of scientific knowledge plus intellect":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Wisdom (<em>sophia</em>) is the combination of the virtues of scientific knowledge and intellect. A person with wisdom both understands the first principles, and the necessary truths that follow from those first principles. It is directed at the most divine and superior objects in the universe (e.g., the gods, the cosmos, etc.), rather than human affairs. It allows the individual to search for truth, and it not aimed at increasing utility for the individual.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Wisdom is the highest of all of the intellectual virtues (even above prudence), and thus, it is the most desirable.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation\">The virtue of intellect deals with the ability to grasp first principles or the circumstantial facts of a situation</a><ul><li>One of the two components. Intellect supplies the first principles.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths\">The virtue of scientific knowledge deals with logic and reasoning upon necessary truths</a><ul><li>The other component. Scientific knowledge reasons deductively from those first principles.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>While wisdom is not needed in order to achieve happiness, it <em>is</em> needed to achieve the <em>highest</em> happiness (a contemplative life).</li></ul></li></ul>","Xi Jinping seeks to establish a revolutionary spirit to maintain ideological purity and prevent institutional decay":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Xi seeks to enact a “self-revolution” (自我革命), essentially an attempt to redefine the party with a revolutionary zeal and to ensure that it does not become stagnant and corrupt. This is the reason for his anti-corruption campaigns.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the fundamental ideological justification for Xi’s anti-corruption campaigns and lays out his grand vision for the party.</p><h4>References</h4>","Yang of Sui's excessive spending, disregard for human life, and general poor management led to the collapse of the Sui":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Emperor' Yang’s poor management, including his reckless invasions of Goguryeo and Champa, as well as his efforts to complete the Grand Canal and Great Wall led to his downfall and the end of the Sui.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows how overspending and poor management can lead to the downfall of an empire, even when before, the country is stable and prosperous.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion\">When a government is unable (or unwilling) to reform, the people may rise up in rebellion</a><ul><li>This is the principle which this case serves the demonstrate.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill\">Great Britain narrowly avoided civil war in 1832 as a result of the Reform Bill</a><ul><li>Another similar case study, except revolution and overthrow was avoided.</li></ul></li></ul>","Yi Yi":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows three generations of the Jian family as they navigate love, regret, illness, work, adolescence, and personal crises in contemporary Taipei. Framed by a wedding and a funeral, the film interweaves the father NJ’s encounter with a former love and his struggles to preserve his business, the mother Min-Min’s spiritual exhaustion, teenage Ting-Ting’s guilt and first romance, and young Yang-Yang’s attempts to understand the world through photography.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>My favorite film, and a film that touches on questions regarding the nature of human existence and the meaning of human growth and change.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, one has nothing to regret\">In life, one has nothing to regret</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments\">In life, the little things often matter more than the big moments</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Life is too short for regrets\">Life is too short for regrets</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions\">The end goal of human life is happiness, which requires sustained virtuous action and comfortable material conditions</a><ul><li>The philosophy of the film embodies a little of what Aristotle thought.</li></ul></li></ul>","glossary":"<h3>Fabula</h3><p><em>Fabulas</em></p><p>The chronological sequence of events within the world of a narrative.</p><hr><h3>Syuzhet</h3><p><em>Syuzhets</em></p><p>The sequence of events as they are presented to the audience, even if out of order compared to the fabula.</p><hr><h3>Exorbitant Privilege</h3><p><em>Exorbitant Privileges</em></p><p>Describes a condition whereby a country are able to borrow at lower rates (and thus more) than other countries, especially during crisis. Additionally, it means that the country is able to sell its debt at a higher price than other countries.</p><hr><h3>Nigeria</h3><p><em>Nigerias</em></p><ul><li>1960 - 1967: First Republic<ul><li>Adopted the British model of government</li><li>Westminster model<ul><li>Had a president</li><li>Prime minister and bicameral legislature</li><li><strong>Northernization:</strong> There was an allocation of resources to the North to develop it, since it was underdeveloped at the time.<ul><li>Made ethnic groups in the South nervous.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Four major ethnic groups:<ul><li>Fulani-Hausa: Muslim</li><li>Igbo: Christian</li><li>Yoruba: Christian, Muslim, Animist</li></ul></li><li>Oil was discovered in SE Nigeria (Biafra), which made the Igbo want to keep the oil for themselves.</li><li>A third of the population does not belong to any of the four groups.</li></ul></li><li>1967-1970: Biafra Civil War<ul><li>An attempt by the Igbo to break free of Nigeria and establish their independence. Biafra loses the war.</li><li>Televised war.</li></ul></li><li>1970-1979: Military Junta<ul><li><strong>Yakabu Gowon</strong> (r. 1970-1975):<ul><li>Led the Federal Military Government during the war.</li><li>Instituted the 3R policy: reconciliation, rehabilitation, and reconstruction.<ul><li>But this was more a slogan than actually implementation of policy.</li><li>They gave everybody 20 pounds as compensation. About $86 USD today (according to Claude)</li></ul></li><li>He marginalized the Igbo through the Decree No.46 of 1970. This prevented the Igbo from being fully reintegrated into the federal and state public services.</li></ul></li><li>Following the 1973 Arab-Isreali crisis, oil prices quadrupled, and government revenue changed from 166 million to 3.7 billion.</li><li>Joined OPEC in 1971. Sold 2 million barrels per day in 1974.</li><li>This destroyed the rest of the economy, and Nigeria became a rentier state (see Dutch disease).<ul><li>But Nigeria was able to purchase a lot of capital.</li></ul></li><li>There was a centralization of oil revenue, which lead to rapid growth in inequality and corruption in the government.<ul><li>This institutional legacy persists today.</li></ul></li><li>He was overthrown by Mohammad due to<ul><li>Reneging on his promise of civilian rule;</li><li>Corruption among governors;</li><li>Cement Armada scandal, which became a symbol of inefficiency, poor management, and waste;</li><li>Poor control of military.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>1979-1983: Second Republic<ul><li>American system.</li><li><strong>Shehu Shagari</strong><ul><li>There was a drop in oil prices, which made his governing difficult.</li><li>There was also increase in corruption.</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>1983-1993<ul><li>Buhari</li><li>Babangida</li><li>Abacha<ul><li>Massive corruption and complete disregard for human rights.</li><li>Turned Nigeria into a Pariah state.</li><li>Stole 6 billion from the state.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><hr><h3>Dutch Disease</h3><p><em>Dutch Diseases</em></p><p>When a resource boom in one sector of an economy causes a real exchange rate appreciation which erodes the competitiveness of the rest of the economy (typically manufacturing and agriculture), leading to deindustrialization.</p><hr><h3>Civilization</h3><p><em>Civilizations, civilizational, civilizationals</em></p><ol><li>(Huntington) The broadest level of cultural identification, especially with regards to things like religion and history, distinguished from other civilizations by fundamental differences in values, institutions, and social organizations. Can be multi-state and multi-ethnic.</li></ol><hr><h3>Political Attitude</h3><p><em>Political Attitudes</em></p><p>People's opinions on the speed, extent, and direction of political change.</p><hr><h3>Political Ideology</h3><p><em>Political Ideologies, ideology, ideologies, ideological, ideologicals</em></p><p>People's opinion of the nature (content) of political changes/beliefs.</p><hr><h3>Subformula</h3><p><em>Subformulas</em></p><ol><li>(Logic) Something that appears as a node in the parse tree of a formula.</li></ol><hr><h3>Structuralism</h3><p><em>Structuralisms</em></p><p>Things derive their meaning by way of their relations with others things in the system.</p><hr><h3>Essentialism</h3><p><em>Essentialisms</em></p><ol><li>(Philosophy/Identity) The idea that identity comes from \"inside something\" and thus has no correlation with the nature of other things in the system.</li></ol><hr><h3>Context Window</h3><p><em>Context Windows</em></p><p>The information that is in the working memory of the AI model that is model is able to access in a highly accurate manner.</p><hr><h3>Mandate of Heaven</h3><p><em>Mandate of Heavens, 天命</em></p><p>A political doctrine that is used to justify the overthrow of an unpopular Chinese dynasty. Whether due to the fault of the ruler, or simply adverse environmental conditions, dynasties and rulers can \"lose\" the Mandate of Heaven.</p><hr><h3>Irredentism</h3><p><em>Irredentisms</em></p><p>A state's desire to annex the territory of another state, driven by ethic/cultural reasons.</p><hr><h3>Revanchism</h3><p><em>Revanchisms</em></p><p>A state's desire to annex the territory of another state, driven by nationalistic/political reasons.</p><hr><h3>Civic Nationalism</h3><p><em>Civic Nationalisms, civic-nationalism, civic-nationalisms</em></p><p>Nationalism driven by common values, civic institutions, laws, etc.</p><hr><h3>Ethnic Nationalism</h3><p><em>Ethnic Nationalisms, ethnic-nationalism, ethnic-nationalisms</em></p><p>Nationalism driven by a common language, culture, or religion. Thus, the nation precedes the state.</p><hr><h3>Empirical Abstraction</h3><p><em>Empirical Abstractions</em></p><p>The process by which individuals extracts knowledge of a property inherent in some external object while ignoring other properties of that object.</p><p>Ex. Looking at a small, red, rubber ball and learning of the concept of redness from that ball.</p><hr><h3>Reflective Abstraction</h3><p><em>Reflective Abstractions</em></p><p>The process by which an individual learns of some property which governs the relation of external objects through the manipulation of those objects. It proceeds through two stages:</p><ol><li><em>Réfléchissement</em>: the process by which a lower-level operation or structure is projected upward onto a higher cognitive level, such that what was a procedure executed upon objects become itself an object available for examination and thought.</li><li><em>Réflexion</em>: the process by which projected content is reorganized at a higher cognitive level, such that what was an ad hoc relation between specific objects becomes a generalizable and cognizable principle.</li></ol><hr><h3>Jevons Paradox</h3><p><em>Jevons Paradoxes, Jevons effect, Jevons effects</em></p><p>Technological improvements that improve the efficiency of the use of a resource increases the total use of that resource rather than decrease it.</p><hr><h3>Well-ordered Society</h3><p><em>Well-ordered Societies, well ordered society, well ordered societies, society that is well-ordered, society that is well-ordereds</em></p><p>(Rawls) A society where</p><ul><li>there is a generally accepted conception of Justice, and</li><li>the social institutions generally reflect the agreed upon conception of justice.</li></ul><hr><h3>Concept of Justice</h3><p><em>Concept of Justices</em></p><p>(Rawls) The formal, shared understanding of what justice (as the underlying moral reality) is for. Specifically, the fundamental and shared belief in how</p><ul><li>there should be no unfounded/irrational distinctions between people in assigning rights and duties, and</li><li>there should be a proper/fair distribution of social advantages.</li></ul><p>May be interpreted different based on the particular conception of justice employed.</p><hr><h3>Conception of Justice</h3><p><em>Conception of Justices</em></p><p>(Rawls) A unique interpretation of the concept of justice. It is the means by which the distributive aspects of the basic structure are assessed.</p><p>Ex. utilitarianism, Libertarianism, etc.</p><hr><h3>Lexical Priority</h3><p><em>Lexical Priorities, lexical ordering, lexical orderings</em></p><p>Something that must be prioritized and satisfied to the fullest extent before other principles are considered.</p><hr><h3>Basic Structure</h3><p><em>Basic Structures</em></p><p>(Rawls) The way in which major social institutions fit into a singular scheme through which fundamental rights, duties, and advantages are determined.</p><hr><h3>Original Position</h3><p><em>Original Positions</em></p><p>(Rawls) The hypothetical decisions situation which is constructed of various axioms, which, through their various permutations, generate unique conceptions of justice. People are aware of the following in the original position:</p><ol><li>The circumstances of justice, i.e., people are mutually disinterested and under moderate constraints of scarcity.</li><li>Each individual tires to advance their own long-term plan without prior moral ties to anyone else.</li><li>Each individual considers the interests of their descendants.</li></ol><p>Additionally, people in the veil of ignorance are not subject to distorting emotions such as envy.</p><hr><h3>Veil of Ignorance</h3><p><em>Veil of Ignorances</em></p><p>The epistemic constraint whereby one does not know their particular abilities, moralities, status, or any other specific circumstances in the original position. They do, however, how about the general laws and theories present in the world. Additionally, though they are mutually disinterested, since they don’t know who they would be, they retain some concern over the rights and claims of others.</p><hr><h3>Egoistic</h3><p><em>Egoistics, egoism, egoist, egoisms, egoists</em></p><ol><li>(Rawls) Someone who actively seeks domination over others and who measure their success relative to others.</li></ol><hr><h3>Mutual Disinterest</h3><p><em>Mutual Disinterests, mutually disinterested, mutual-disinterest, mutually-disinterested, mutually disinteresteds, mutual-disinterests, mutually-disinteresteds</em></p><p>(Rawls) Someone who is unaffected by the activities of others, focusing on their prudential interests only.</p><hr><h3>Reflective Equilibrium</h3><p><em>Reflective Equilibriums</em></p><p>A state of coherence between the principles of justice (derived from the description of the original position) and agent moral intuitions. This allows:</p><ul><li>The dislocation of the moral system from self-evident axioms, and</li><li>For the the moral system to stand independently of pure moral intuition. See <span class=\"wl-off\">Rawls' Reflective Equilibrium</span> for more information.</li></ul><hr><h3>Publicity Condition</h3><p><em>Publicity Conditions</em></p><p>A condition that makes up the original position which states that the principles chosen must be ones that parties can accept knowing they are publicly known and applied. This must be the case be the resultant principles are to be accepted as a public charter to facilitate cooperation between parties.</p><hr><h3>Nation-state</h3><p><em>Nation-states, nationstate, nationstates</em></p><ol><li>(Weber) The institution which holds a monopoly on violence. Herein lies also the root of the state's legitimacy.</li></ol><hr><h3>Realism</h3><p><em>Realisms, political realism, political realists, classical realism, classical realists, realists, political realisms, classical realisms</em></p><p>A theoretical framework which views the world as an enduring competition among self-interested states struggling for power. It has three main axoims:</p><ul><li>men are fundamentally self-interested,</li><li>ideals are counterproductive in geopolitics,</li><li>man's natural state is lawless anarchy.</li></ul><hr><h3>Subsidiarity</h3><p><em>Subsidiarities, principle of subsidiarity, subsidiarity principle, principle of subsidiarities, subsidiarity principles</em></p><p>The idea that social and political issues should be dealt with at the most immediate/local level that is consistent with the resolution.</p><hr><h3>Le Chatelier's Principle</h3><p><em>Le Chatelier's Principles</em></p><p>If a system in equilibrium is disturbed by a change in the concentration, temperature, or pressure, the system shifts to a new equilibrium by partially counteracting that change.</p><hr><h3>English Auction</h3><p><em>English Auctions</em></p><p>An open-outcry auction whereby the auctioneer accepts increasingly higher bids from the floor and the highest bidder is announced after a set time period of no other bids. The highest bidder pays the price they bid.</p><hr><h3>Dutch Auction</h3><p><em>Dutch Auctions</em></p><p>The auctioneer begins with a high price and gradually lowers it until someone accepts the price or it reaches a set reserve price.</p><hr><h3>Vickrey Auction</h3><p><em>Vickrey Auctions</em></p><p>A seal-bid auction whereby the highest bidder wins but pays the price of the second highest bidder. This type of auction gives bidders an incentive bid their true value.</p><hr><h3>Comprehensive Doctrine</h3><p><em>Comprehensive Doctrines</em></p><p>(Rawls) A broad philosophical, religious, or moral worldview that covers many aspects of life.</p><p>Ex. Kantianism, Confucianism, Catholicism, etc.</p><hr><h3>Condorcet's Jury Theorem</h3><p><em>Condorcet's Jury Theorems</em></p><p>When a group wishes to reach a decision by majority vote, and one of the two decisions is correct, and each voter has an independent probability pp p of choosing the correct decision, whether the individuals' pp p are greater than or less than 0.5 determines of the right choice is chosen.</p><hr><h3>Pork Barrel Politics</h3><p><em>pork barrel, pork barrels</em></p><p>Allocating government funds to localized projects for the representative's district/constituencies.</p><hr><h3>Marchetti's Constant</h3><p><em>Marchetti's Constants</em></p><p>The idea that people have a constant daily travel time budget of around one hour for a round trip.</p><hr><h3>Circumstances of Justice</h3><p><em>Circumstances of Justices</em></p><p>Distributive ideas only arises because of (1) scarcity (demand out stripping supply), (2) limited human altruism, and (3) no one human is powerful enough to dominate all others.</p><hr><h3>Essentiality of Origin</h3><p><em>Essentiality of Origins, essentialism of origin, original essentialism, essentialism of origins, original essentialisms</em></p><p>A person's biological origin is not a contingent, but a metaphysically necessary property. I.e., in any possible world where he exists, he has that origin. The essential properties is not simply a matter of luck.</p><hr><h3>Great Gatsby Curve</h3><p><em>Great Gatsby Curves</em></p><p>Countries with higher income inequality have lower intergenerational mobility.</p><hr><h3>Fishkin's Trilemma</h3><p><em>Fishkin's Trilemmas, trilemma of Fishkin, trilemma of Fishkins</em></p><p>The idea that three underpinnings of a liberal society: the principle of equal opportunity, equality of life chances, and autonomy of families, are fundamentally incompatible, where accepting two pillars naturally undermines the third.</p><hr><h3>Olley-Pakes</h3><p><em>Olley-Pakes decomposition, Olley-Pakes decompositions</em></p><p>Splits the measurement of productivity down to two components:</p><ul><li>the average efficiency of firms weighted by market share, and</li><li>the unweighted average efficiency of all firms. If the difference between these values is positive, it means that consumers are buying more from efficient firms, making them bigger. If not, it means that firms are being artificially prevented from expanding.</li></ul><hr><h3>Luck Egalitarianism</h3><p><em>Luck Egalitarianisms, egalitarian regarding luck, egalitarian with regards to luck, egalitarian regarding lucks, egalitarian with regards to lucks</em></p><p>A family of theories which suggests that it is unjust for some to be worse off than others due to factors outside of individual control (i.e., luck).</p><hr><h3>Institution</h3><p><em>Institutions</em></p><p>(Rawls) A public system of rules which defines offices and positions with their powers and duties. Rules relating to institutions are bifurcated into:</p><ul><li>Constitutive rules which establish the institution's rights and duties, and</li><li>The strategies to take shape the institution in some particular way or to make it act in a particular way.</li></ul><hr><h3>Sum-threshold Criteria</h3><p><em>sum-threshold attack, sum-threshold action, sum-threshold attacks, sum-threshold actions</em></p><p>When the total impact of some action is large enough to achieve a goal, but each discrete component of that larger action is small, or remains beneath the threshold of being noticed.</p><hr><h3>Formal Justice</h3><p><em>Formal Justices, justice as regularity, justice as regularities</em></p><p>The equal and impartial application of laws/rules in all cases.</p><hr><h3>Primary Goods</h3><p>(Rawls) Rights and liberties, power and opportunities, and income and wealth.</p><hr><h3>General Conception of Justice</h3><p><em>General Conception of Justices</em></p><p>(Rawls) All social primary goods are to be distributed equally unless an unequal distribution of any of these goods is to the advantage of the least favored. In other words, it is the difference principle applied to all primary social goods. By following in accordance to the general conception, we can eventually eliminate natural and limitations and historical injustices and ensure that less than equal liberty would no longer by acceptable.</p><hr><h3>Desert-based Meritocracy</h3><p><em>Desert-based Meritocracies, deontological meritocracy, deontological meritocracies, desert-based merit, desert-based merits</em></p><p>Allocation of social goods is intrinsically justified because individuals deserve outcomes proportional to their merits.</p><hr><h3>Instrumental Meritocracy</h3><p><em>Instrumental Meritocracies, consequentialist meritocracy, consequentialist meritocracies, instrumental merit, instrumental merits</em></p><p>Allocation of social goods by merit is only justifiable because it tends to produce the most optimal outcome.</p><hr><h3>Supranational Organization</h3><p><em>Supranational Organizations</em></p><p>Have legal authority over their constituent nations; constituents' sovereignty reduced.</p><hr><h3>Race to the Bottom</h3><p><em>Races to the Bottom, Race to the Bottoms, Races to the Bottoms</em></p><p>When countries, to become more competitive on the international market, \"race\" to lower their regulatory standards to main an edge.</p><hr><h3>Pareto Efficiency</h3><p><em>Pareto Efficiencies, principle of efficiency, principle of efficiencies</em></p><p>A situation whereby no one can be made better off without making everyone else worse off.</p><hr><h3>System of Natural Liberty</h3><p><em>System of Natural Liberties</em></p><p>Composed of the following interpretations of Rawl's second principle of justice:</p><ul><li>the principle of efficiency, and</li><li>equality as careers open to talents.</li></ul><hr><h3>Careers Open to Talents</h3><p><em>Career Open to Talents</em></p><p>Requires that positions in society be open to all who are able to fulfill the required functions, with no formal legal barriers to exclude anyone on irrelevant characteristics.</p><hr><h3>Fair Equality of Opportunity</h3><p><em>Equality of Opportunities, fair equality of opportunities</em></p><p>Those with similar skills and abilities should have a similar chance of attaining some position/reward in society.</p><hr><h3>Social Contingency</h3><p><em>social contingencies</em></p><p>Things that one cannot control which is decided by the social circumstances that one is born into. Ex. family wealth, class background, quality of schooling, etc.</p><hr><h3>Natural Contingency</h3><p><em>natural contingencies</em></p><p>Things that one cannot control which is decided by the genetics/random chance. Ex. intelligence, physical strength and health, imagination and creativity, etc. One unique example that Rawls gives is that of temporal position, i.e., which generation you are born into.</p><hr><h3>System of Liberal Equality</h3><p><em>System of Liberal Equalities</em></p><p>Composed of the following interpretations of Rawl's second principle of justice:</p><ul><li>the principle of efficiency, and</li><li>equality of opportunity.</li></ul><hr><h3>Difference Principle</h3><p><em>Difference Principles, lexical difference principle, lexical difference principles</em></p><p>The well-off in society cannot be made more well-off unless it is to the advantage of the least well-off. There is a more precise formulation known as the <em>lexical difference principle</em>, whereby for a representative man, first maximize the worst-off, than the new worst-off, and so on, until lastly regard the best-off.</p><hr><h3>Chain-connected Expectations</h3><p>(Rawls) Improving the expectations of the worst-off improves all expectations for those less worse-off.</p><hr><h3>Close-knit Expectations</h3><p>To lower or improve the expectations of any representative agent is necessarily to lower or improve the expectations of every other representative agent. In other words, it is impossible for any representative's expectations to be changed without at least one other representative's expectations changed.</p><hr><h3>Perfect Procedural Justice</h3><p><em>perfect procedural justices</em></p><p>There exists an independent criterion for a just outcome, and a procedure that is guaranteed to produce it. The results are thus predictable. Ex. one person cuts the cake, and the other gets to pick which slice they want.</p><hr><h3>Imperfect Procedural Justice</h3><p><em>imperfect procedural justices</em></p><p>There exists an independent criterion for a just outcome, but not a procedure that is guaranteed to produce it. The results are thus unpredictable. Ex. the criminal justice system (some innocents are sentenced, while some criminals go free).</p><hr><h3>Pure Procedural Justice</h3><p><em>Pure Procedural Justices</em></p><p>There does not exist an independent criterion for a just outcome. In this case, justice is entirely a product of the proper execution of the procedure, not of the outcome. Importantly, the procedure must be carried out. Ex. gambling.</p><hr><h3>Principle of Redress</h3><p><em>Principle of Redresses</em></p><p>The categorical principle that underserved inequalities must be redressed directly. Society must provide more to those with fewer native assets and those born into the less favorable social positions.</p><hr><h3>Waterbed Effect</h3><p><em>Waterbed Effects</em></p><p>(Price theory) The theory that large firms can negotiate with suppliers for lower supplier prices, which translates to higher input costs for smaller firms. See <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The waterbed effect is false\">The waterbed effect is false</a> for a refutation of this theory.</p><hr><h3>Mexico</h3><p><em>United Mexican States, Mexicos</em></p><ul><li>1982: Debt crisis due to reaching the point where foreign debt outmatched the earnings power of their incomes, which meant that they were not able to repay their debts.<ul><li>Abandon protectionism, since they had to get loans from the IMF.</li><li>Resulted in the <em>lost decade</em>.<ul><li>Meant that spending on social programs decreased during this period.</li></ul></li><li>Led to a period of privatization of parastatals, more market oriented integration.</li></ul></li></ul><p>The PRI was weakened due to the weakened, so the National Action Party was able to take power in 2000.</p><ul><li>1986: Joined the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT).<ul><li>This was the precursor to the WTO.</li></ul></li><li>1994: Salinas signed NAFTA.<ul><li>This meant more environmental regulations that the country had to follow. This is most likely justified via a race to the bottom situation.</li><li>Led to the Zapatista Rebellion.</li></ul></li><li>1994: the Peso Crisis.</li></ul><p>Enrique Peña Nieto</p><ul><li>2013: Liberalized the oil industry, by allowing foreign and private companies to invest in the oil sector (e.g., PEMEX).<ul><li>Attracted greater investment to the industry after a lot of underinvestment.</li><li>This was somewhat reverse in recent decades.</li></ul></li></ul><p>Mexico city is one of the worst polluted cities in the world both due to the geography and the poor management by the government.</p><ul><li>1996: beginning of women representation in the government in Mexico.</li><li>50/50 parity reform (2019): Instituted a quota whereby 50% of every government position from local to national of the government positions need to be reserved for women.</li></ul><hr><h3>Non-identity Problem</h3><p><em>nonidentity problem, paradox of future individuals, Non-identity Problems, nonidentity problems</em></p><p>The paradoxical conclusion that a person cannot have been considered to have been harmed given that they were brought into existence, and that existence is worth having overall. This is because without that harm, the person brought into existence would not have been them, and a low quality existence is better than non-existence.</p><hr><h3>Monotonic Preferences</h3><p><em>weakly monotonic, strongly monotonic, weakly monotonics, strongly monotonics</em></p><ul><li>Weak: If the agent prefers all consumption bundles that have more of all goods.</li><li>Strong: If the agent prefers all consumption bundles that have more of at least one good.</li></ul><p>These are both usually too strong of an assumption in microeconomic consumer theory.</p><hr><h3>Local Nonsatiation</h3><p><em>Local Nonsatiations</em></p><p>For any bundle of foods, there is always another bundle of goods arbitrarily close that is strictly preferred to it.</p><hr><h3>Longitudinal Data</h3><p>Data collection from the same source repeatedly over time.</p><hr><h3>Bryd Rule</h3><p><em>Bryd Rules</em></p><p>Passed in 1985 to ensures that reconciliation bills are used for budget purposes, and cannot include extraneous purposes.</p><ul><li>Also, reconciliation bills cannot increase spending or reduce revenues in the period after budget resolution.</li><li>Needs 60 votes to sustain provisions that violate the rule.</li></ul><hr><h3>Reconciliation Bill</h3><p><em>reconciliation bills</em></p><p>A special type of congressional budget legislation which bypasses the usual filibuster rules. Can only concern the following three topics:</p><ul><li>changes to the statutory debt limit</li><li>changes in revenues</li><li>changes in mandatory spending</li></ul><p>It overrides the cloture rule by allowing the passage of a bill with a simply majority (rather than the 2/3 required to end filibuster normally).</p><hr><h3>Parliamentarian</h3><p><em>parliamentarians</em></p><p>(Congress) A non-partisan expert on legislative procedure.</p><hr><h3>Entitlements</h3><p>Federal programs that guarantee benefits to anyone who meets legally defined eligibility criteria, regardless of the total cost.</p><hr><h3>Disturbance Theory</h3><p><em>disturbance theories</em></p><p>(Interest groups) A theory of interest groups which states that even the currently unorganized groups are not ignored, because they have the potential to become organized interest groups.</p><hr><h3>Collective Action Problem</h3><p><em>Collective Action Problems</em></p><p>A group of people who share a common interest and who all benefit from the interest being achieved, even those who did not contribute to achieving the interest.</p><hr><h3>Duverger's Law</h3><p><em>Duverger's Laws</em></p><p>First-past-the-post (SMP) systems tend to produce two-party systems, whereas proportional representation systems tend produce a multiparty system.</p><hr><h3>Arguendo</h3><p><em>Arguendos</em></p><p>\"For the sake of argument\"; accepting some premise to examine their conclusions without admitting that the premises are true.</p><hr><h3>Gresham's Law</h3><p><em>Gresham's Laws</em></p><p>\"Good money drives out bad money\"; the idea that, when the two currencies are accepted as having similar face values by law, the currency with less intrinsic worth tends to be used more than the other currency in exchanges and thus “drive out” the other currency.</p><hr><h3>Industrial Policy</h3><p><em>Industrial Policies</em></p><p>Government led efforts to encourage the development of all parts of the economy, especially in the lack of sufficient investment from the private sector.</p><hr><h3>Priority Problem</h3><p><em>Priority Problems</em></p><p>(Rawls) How a society deals with competing moral claims.</p><hr><h3>Principle of Insufficient Reason</h3><p><em>Principle of Insufficient Reasons</em></p><p>(Rawls) When you have no information (e.g., in the original position) about a circumstance, assign equal probabilities to all possible outcomes.</p><hr><h3>Ideal Theory</h3><p><em>Ideal Theories</em></p><p>(Rawls) A theory which deals with ideal circumstances, such as strict compliance with the rules of the theory, having a well-ordered society.</p><hr><h3>Non-ideal Theory</h3><p><em>Non-ideal Theories</em></p><p>(Rawls) Concerns how to adapt a theory for the non-ideal situations in the real world, a big part of which are the principles for meeting injustice.</p><hr><h3>Categorical Imperative</h3><p><em>Categorical Imperatives</em></p><p>That the validity of a principle is not contingent upon some particular desire or aim, i.e., it is universal.</p><hr><h3>Thin Theory of the Good</h3><p><em>thin theory, Thin Theory of the Goods, thin theories</em></p><p>(Rawls) That the good is the rational long-term plan given relatively favorable circumstances. This is the main reason for people's want of primary goods, since it helps them satisfy this definition of the good.</p><hr><h3>Economic Calculation</h3><p><em>Economic Calculations</em></p><p>Capitalist economies, via price, offer subjective information about the relative value of different things and different uses of things. This allows for such an economy to efficiently allocate resources. Socialist economies, lacking a price system, fail to achieve this.</p><hr><h3>Last Great War of Antiquity</h3><p><em>Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628, Last Great War of Antiquities, Byzantine–Sasanian War of 602–628s</em></p><p>The final conflict between the Byzantine Empire and the Sassanid empire, which greatly weakened both sides as to have allowed the rise of the Rashidun.</p><hr><h3>Teleological</h3><p><em>teleology, Teleologicals, teleologies</em></p><p>(General) Exploring causality through ends, goals, or purposes.</p><p>(Rawls) Defining good irrespective of what is right, then defining right as being whatever produces the most of that good. Ex. Hedonism, Utilitarianism, Eudaimonism, etc.</p><hr><h3>Dominant-end</h3><p><em>dominant-end theory, dominant-ends theory, dominant-ends, value monism, eonistic practical deliberation, summum bonum, maximizing conception of rationality, dominant-end theories, dominant-ends theories, value monisms, eonistic practical deliberations, summum bonums, maximizing conception of rationalities</em></p><p>(Rawls) Proposes that rational choice requires identifying a single overriding end to which all other ends are subordinate, and then treating all decisions as questions of how best to advance that dominant end.</p><hr><h3>Congruence Problem</h3><p><em>Congruence Problems</em></p><p>(Rawls) Whether it makes sense for a rational person to regard the principles of justice as regulative of their plan of life.</p><hr><h3>Hyperobject</h3><p><em>hyper-object, Hyperobjects, hyper-objects</em></p><p>(Morton; Ontology) A metaphysical structure so massive that a single human struggles to understand it. Ex. the banking system, the government, etc.</p><hr><h3>Circumvallation</h3><p><em>circumvallations</em></p><p>A defense system built by besiegers facing the besieged.</p><hr><h3>Contravallation</h3><p><em>Contravallations</em></p><p>A defense system built by besiegers facing away from the besieged to protect themselves from forces allied to the besieged.</p><hr><h3>Classical Theory of Interest</h3><p><em>Classical Theory of Interests</em></p><p>Bases interest rates on real factors in the economy.</p><hr><h3>Liquidity Preference Theory</h3><p><em>Liquidity Preference Theories</em></p><p>Bases interests rates on the supply of money in the economy.</p><hr><h3>Perfect Duty</h3><p><em>perfect duties</em></p><p>Things that we are obligated to do, always. Has a correlated right.</p><hr><h3>Imperfect Duty</h3><p><em>Imperfect Duties</em></p><p>Something obligatory, though the particular occasion for doing, we can choose. Ex. charity. There is no correlated right.</p><hr><h3>Sanctions Overcompliance</h3><p><em>Sanctions Overcompliances</em></p><p>When market participants are over-strict and risk-averse with their compliance to regulations that it leads to under-investment.</p><hr><h3>Unilateralist's Curse</h3><p><em>Unilateralist's Curses</em></p><p>A phenomenon whereby even when all actors want what is best for the group, given a large enough number of actors, some action which is net negative utility will ultimately still occur.</p><hr><h3>Existential Catastrophe</h3><p><em>existential catastrophes</em></p><p>The destruction of humanity's long term potential. This either takes the form of extinction (or dropping below the minimum viable population), or failed continuation, which can either take the form of an unrecoverable collapse, or some unrecoverable dystopia. Crucially, this is specifically the destruction of our potential for recovery, since often, just a destruction of our capacities in the moment does not actually spell an end for humanity.</p><hr><h3>Existential risk</h3><p><em>Existential risks</em></p><p>Risk which leads to scenarios of existential catastrophe.</p><hr><h3>Ecosystem Services</h3><p>Benefits provided by the environment which would be highly costly/inefficiency for humanity to do. Ex. pollination, carbon storage, soil formation, etc.</p><hr><h3>Availability Heuristic</h3><p><em>availability heuristics</em></p><p>The tendency for people to estimate the likelihood of events based on their ability to recall examples. Therefore, people tend to incorrectly estimate the likelihood of very unlikely/unobserved probabilities.</p><hr><h3>Scope Neglect</h3><p><em>Scope Neglects</em></p><p>When we fail to scale our level of concern with the scale of a benefit or harm. Ex. killing 50 birds or 100 birds might cause the same level of distress for humans, despite their varying amount of moral weight.</p><hr><h3>Citizen</h3><p><em>Aristotelian citizen, Citizens, Aristotelian citizens, citizenship, Aristotelian citizenship, citizenships, Aristotelian citizenships</em></p><p>(Aristotle) A member of a city who shares (or has the capacity to take part) in the deliberative/judicial functions of the city. They ought to be given a role in such activities since they are the benefactors/victims of these functions, and therefore, are allowed to judge them.</p><hr><h3>Super-moral Values</h3><p><em>supermoral values, supermoral, super-moral, supermorals, super-morals</em></p><p>(Fung) Values understood within the context of one's position within the cosmos, rather than as within a potentially localized moral landscape.</p><hr><h3>Concept by Intuition</h3><p><em>concepts by intuition, concept by intuitions, concepts by intuitions</em></p><p>(Chinese Philosophy) A concept which is immediately apprehended. E.g. the experience of the color blue.</p><hr><h3>Concept by Postulation</h3><p><em>concepts by postulation, concept by postulations, concepts by postulations</em></p><p>(Chinese Philosophy) A concept whose complete meaning is the result of the deductive reasoning that occurs. E.g., an understanding of the color blue via understanding the physics behind it.</p><hr><h3>Differentiated Aesthetic Continuum</h3><p><em>Differentiated Aesthetic Continuums</em></p><p>The entire scene with all of the sensory stimuli and the associated concepts in one's mind.</p><hr><h3>Undifferentiated Aesthetic Continuum</h3><p><em>undifferentiated aesthetic continuums</em></p><p>The entire sensory experience of a scene, except all identifiable related concepts in the mind are removed, such that all that remains is bare consciousness, a mass of sensory experience.</p><hr><h3>Concepts of Differentiation</h3><p><em>concepts of differentiations</em></p><p>Unique mental concepts that are projected over the undifferentiated aesthetic continuum.</p><hr><h3>Rectification of Names</h3><p>Role-names and evaluative terms carry normative content, and when a mismatch between names and actions occurs, one or the other is rectified such that the names and actions are once again unified.</p><hr><h3>Moravec's Paradox</h3><p><em>Moravec's Paradoxes</em></p><p>Tasks that is simple for humans (such as perception or manipulation our appendages) require more computation for computers than tasks demanding for humans (such as doing high-level math).</p><hr><h3>Dharma</h3><p><em>dhamma, dharmas, dhammas</em></p><p>The Buddhist teaching, truth, law, or the nature of reality. Also, it refers in general to phenomena.</p><hr><h3>Sangha</h3><p><em>sanghas</em></p><p>The community (especially the monastic community), or the noble community of realized beings.</p><hr><h3>Vinaya</h3><p><em>vinayas</em></p><p>Monastic discipline and rules.</p><hr><h3>Abhidharma</h3><p><em>abhidhamma, abhidharmas, abhidhammas</em></p><p>A systematic philosophical and psychological analysis of experience.</p><hr><h3>Samsara</h3><p><em>samsaras</em></p><p>The round of birth, death, and rebirth driven by craving and ignorance.</p><hr><h3>Buddha-nature</h3><p><em>universal mind, buddha-natures, universal minds</em></p><p>The innate capacity for awakening in all sentient beings; not a soul, but the luminous potential of mind.</p><hr><h3>Anicca</h3><p><em>aniccas</em></p><p>Impermanence, or the idea that everything conditioned changes, so clinging on to and desiring things ultimately fails.</p><hr><h3>Anatta</h3><p><em>not-self, anattas, not-selves</em></p><p>No independent self can be found in experience.</p><hr><h3>Dukkha</h3><p><em>Dukkhas</em></p><p>Unsatisfactoriness, suffering, stress. This is the basic problem Buddhism addresses.</p><hr><h3>Supreme Ultimate</h3><p><em>太极, Supreme Ultimates</em></p><p>A \"first mover\" that is the ultimate li. In this way, it embodies the \"rule for movement\" which then \"cause\" the movement of the qi.</p><hr><h3>Heart-mind</h3><p><em>Heart-minds</em></p><p>The integrated center of perceiving, thinking, feeling, desiring, judging, and acting. Analogous to Aristotle's psyche.</p><hr><h3>Cunning of Reason</h3><p><em>Cunning of Reasons</em></p><p>That the great men of history do not know why they do what they do, since the final outcome of their private passions are divinely preordained and the result of a divine will.</p><hr><h3>Positivism</h3><p><em>Positivisms</em></p><p>That all truths are either true by definition or they are a posteriori facts which are true by logical deduction from sensory experience. Rejected other means of acquiring knowledge, e.g., via intuition, religious faith, introspection, etc.</p><h3>Signal Sequence</h3><p>A tag created at the start of a peptide chain that directs the ribosome to a translocator on the rough endoplasmic reticulum, determining where the finished protein is transported.</p><hr><h3>Apoptosis</h3><p><em>Apoptoses</em></p><p>Controlled cell death, by which mutated or otherwise defective cells are destroyed, keeping the surviving population of cells of high quality.</p><hr><h3>Cellular Respiration</h3><p><em>Cellular respirations</em></p><p>The three-stage process of glycolysis, the Krebs cycle and the electron transport chain, producing roughly 36 ATP per glucose, of which the electron transport chain contributes around 34.</p><hr><h3>Cell Cycle Checkpoint</h3><p><em>Cell cycle checkpoints</em></p><p>A stage in cell division at which the process halts unless a requirement, commonly undamaged DNA, is met.</p><hr><h3>Cilia</h3><p><em>Cilias</em></p><p>Microtubule-based organelles protruding from the cell membrane, either ambulatory, moving material across the cell surface or moving the cell itself, or sensory, detecting stimuli.</p><hr><h3>Denaturation</h3><p><em>Denaturations</em></p><p>The unfolding of a protein to a lower level of structure, usually irreversible.</p><hr><h3>Depolarization</h3><p><em>Depolarizations</em></p><p>The process by which the interior of a cell becomes less negative as positively charged ions enter it.</p><hr><h3>Enzyme</h3><p><em>Enzymes</em></p><p>A protein, or occasionally an RNA, that speeds a chemical reaction by lowering its activation energy without being consumed.</p><hr><h3>Facilitated Diffusion</h3><p><em>Facilitated diffusions</em></p><p>Diffusion that requires a transport protein.</p><hr><h3>Simple Diffusion</h3><p><em>Simple diffusions</em></p><p>Diffusion requiring no energy input, relying only on ambient thermal energy.</p><hr><h3>Feedback Inhibitor</h3><p><em>Feedback inhibitors</em></p><p>A molecule that inhibits an enzyme earlier in the pathway that produced it, creating a negative feedback loop.</p><hr><h3>Fermentation</h3><p><em>Fermentations</em></p><p>Anaerobic respiration, requiring no oxygen but yielding about 2 net ATP per glucose against roughly 36 for aerobic respiration.</p><hr><h3>Gibbs Free Energy</h3><p><em>Gibbs free energies</em></p><p>The energy in a system available to do work at constant temperature and pressure. A negative change is favorable to doing work, a positive change unfavorable.</p><hr><h3>Glycolysis</h3><p><em>Glycolyses</em></p><p>The conversion of one glucose into two pyruvate, yielding two net ATP and, more importantly, two NADH.</p><hr><h3>Pyruvate Oxidation</h3><p><em>Pyruvate oxidations</em></p><p>The oxidation of pyruvate inside the mitochondrion, producing further NADH.</p><hr><h3>Heterotroph</h3><p><em>Heterotrophs</em></p><p>An organism that consumes its food rather than producing it.</p><hr><h3>Autotroph</h3><p><em>Autotrophs</em></p><p>An organism that produces its own food.</p><hr><h3>Hormone</h3><p><em>Hormones</em></p><p>A chemical messenger that travels through the body and triggers changes in cell behavior in order to maintain homeostasis.</p><hr><h3>Kinase Cascade</h3><p><em>Kinase cascades</em></p><p>A sequence in which each kinase phosphorylates the next, relaying and amplifying a signal across distance.</p><hr><h3>Ligand</h3><p><em>Ligands</em></p><p>A molecule that binds to a receptor and triggers a change in cell behavior. Steroids, neurotransmitters and ions are examples.</p><hr><h3>Light-dependent Reactions</h3><p>The stage of photosynthesis carried out by Photosystem II, the electron transport chain and Photosystem I, generating ATP and NADPH.</p><hr><h3>Lysosome</h3><p><em>Lysosomes</em></p><p>A membrane-enclosed sac carrying hydrolytic enzymes, which digests macromolecules and dead cell parts.</p><hr><h3>Membrane Potential</h3><p><em>Membrane potentials</em></p><p>The voltage difference between the inside of a cell, roughly $-60$ to $-90$ mV, and the outside, roughly $0$ mV.</p><hr><h3>Monosaccharide</h3><p><em>Monosaccharides</em></p><p>A simple sugar, the building block of carbohydrates.</p><hr><h3>Disaccharide</h3><p><em>Disaccharides</em></p><p>Two monosaccharides joined together.</p><hr><h3>Polysaccharide</h3><p><em>Polysaccharides</em></p><p>A long chain of monosaccharides, either a starch or a fiber.</p><hr><h3>Osmosis</h3><p><em>Osmoses</em></p><p>The movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from high water potential, meaning low solute concentration, to low water potential. Water follows solute.</p><hr><h3>Polymerase Chain Reaction</h3><p><em>Polymerase chain reactions</em></p><p>A process by which a strand of DNA is copied millions or billions of times.</p><hr><h3>Endoplasmic Reticulum</h3><p><em>Endoplasmic reticulums</em></p><p>The organelle at which proteins destined for transport are synthesized, then packaged into vesicles formed from its own membrane and sent to the Golgi apparatus.</p><hr><h3>Pump (Membrane)</h3><p><em>Pump (membrane)s</em></p><p>A membrane protein that actively moves molecules across a membrane, typically against a concentration gradient.</p><hr><h3>Channel (Membrane)</h3><p><em>Channel (membrane)s</em></p><p>A membrane protein that facilitates passive diffusion driven by thermal energy, a concentration gradient or a charge difference.</p><hr><h3>Quorum Sensing</h3><p><em>Quorum sensings</em></p><p>Coordination in which individual cells release particles and act in synchrony once the concentration of those particles crosses a threshold.</p><hr><h3>Receptor</h3><p><em>Receptors</em></p><p>A protein inside or on the surface of a cell that binds a ligand and thereby triggers a change in cell behavior.</p><hr><h3>Semipermeable Membrane</h3><p><em>Semipermeable membranes</em></p><p>A membrane that admits anything meeting a size or charge requirement.</p><hr><h3>Selectively Permeable Membrane</h3><p><em>Selectively permeable membranes</em></p><p>A membrane that regulates passage by molecular identity rather than by size or charge alone.</p><hr><h3>Starch</h3><p><em>Starches</em></p><p>A polysaccharide used for storage.</p><hr><h3>Fiber (Polysaccharide)</h3><p><em>Fiber (polysaccharide)s</em></p><p>A polysaccharide used for structure.</p><hr><h3>Steroid</h3><p><em>Steroids</em></p><p>A lipid that acts as a hormone, influencing gene expression, metabolism, membrane fluidity, cell signaling and structural integrity.</p><hr><h3>Calvin Cycle</h3><p><em>Calvin cycles</em></p><p>The stage of photosynthesis that takes carbon dioxide from the air and combines it with existing G3P to produce an additional G3P, from which other plant molecules are built.</p><hr><h3>Golgi Apparatus</h3><p><em>Golgi apparatuses</em></p><p>The organelle that receives proteins from the rough endoplasmic reticulum, packages like with like, and dispatches them by vesicle to their destination.</p><hr><h3>Krebs Cycle</h3><p><em>Krebs cycles</em></p><p>The stage of respiration that consumes the two acetyl-CoA produced per glucose, yielding NADH, FADH2, a small amount of ATP, and CO2 as a byproduct.</p><hr><h3>Cytoskeleton</h3><p><em>Cytoskeletons</em></p><p>Protein structures providing internal structure to a cell and a route along which vesicles travel.</p><hr><h3>Extracellular Matrix</h3><p><em>Extracellular matrices</em></p><p>The structure providing overall tissue structure between cells.</p><hr><h3>Triglyceride</h3><p><em>Triglycerides</em></p><p>A lipid with a head and three fatty acid chains, saturated or unsaturated. Fats and oils are triglycerides.</p><hr><h3>Turgor Pressure</h3><p><em>Turgor pressures</em></p><p>The outward pressure produced when plant vacuoles bulge under osmosis, keeping the plant upright and aiding growth.</p><hr><h3>Water Potential</h3><p><em>Water potentials</em></p><p>The potential energy of water in a system relative to pure water, written $\\Psi$ and composed of solute potential $\\Psi_s$ and pressure potential $\\Psi_p$. Water always flows from high to low potential.</p><hr><h3>Electric Dipole</h3><p><em>Electric dipoles</em></p><p>A pair of equal and opposite charges.</p><hr><h3>Electric Field</h3><p><em>Electric fields</em></p><p>The region around a charged object within which another charged object would experience a force.</p><hr><h3>Insulator</h3><p><em>Insulators</em></p><p>A material that is absolutely non-conductive. A nonconductor covers both poor conductors and insulators proper.</p><hr><h3>Grounding</h3><p><em>Groundings</em></p><p>The movement of excess charge along a conductor between an object and the Earth. Electrons flow from the Earth to a positively charged object and to the Earth from a negatively charged one.</p><hr><h3>Z-score</h3><p><em>Z-scores</em></p><p>The number of standard deviations a value lies above the mean, $z=\\frac{x_1-\\bar{x}}{s}$.</p><hr><h3>Gradient Descent</h3><p><em>Gradient descents</em></p><p>The procedure by which a local minimum of a loss function is found, minimizing error in the model's outputs.</p><hr><h3>Hyperplane</h3><p><em>Hyperplanes</em></p><p>A subspace of an n dimensional space whose dimension is n-1.</p><hr><h3>Wage-price Spiral</h3><p>Occurs when expectations for inflation become embedded in workers' decisions such that they preemptively demand higher wages, thus generating inflation as a result, leading to more demands for higher wages, etc.</p>","pH is determined by the concentration of H+ atoms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>How basic (alkaline) or acidic a solution is determined by the concentration of $H^+$ atoms in the solution. Higher concentration indicates alkalinity, and lower concentration indicates acidity.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is relevant for AP Bio, as Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds.</p><h4>Formula</h4><p>$$pH = -\\log[H^+]$$</p><h4>The pH Scale</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds\">Change in pH or salinity can break ionic bonds and hydrogen bonds</a></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Pasted image 20250826093301.png</span></li></ul>","quote bank":"<ul><li><em>But even with a broken bone, old Ahab is untouched; and I account no living bone of mine one jot more me, than this dead one that's lost. Ahab is for ever Ahab, man. This whole act's immutably decreed. 'Twas rehearsed by thee and me a billion years before this ocean rolled. Fool! I am the Fates' lieutenant; I act under orders. Look thou, underling! 'Tis Ahab-his body's part; but Ahab's soul's a centipede, that moves upon a hundred legs.</em><ul><li>Captain Ahab, from <em>Moby-Dick</em></li></ul></li><li><em>Come \\[in reference to God/the Heavens/the cosmos] in thy lowest form of love, and I will kneel and kiss thee; but at thy highest, come as mere supernal power; and though thou launchest navies of full-freighted worlds, there’s that in here that still remains indifferent.</em><ul><li>Captain Ahab, from <em>Moby-Dick</em></li></ul></li><li><em>There is no steady unretracing progress in this life; we do not advance through fixed gradations, and at the last one pause:-through infancy's unconscious spell, boyhood's thoughtless faith, adolescence' doubt (the common doom), then scepticism, then disbelief, resting at last in manhood's pondering repose of If. But once gone through, we trace the round again; and are infants, boys, and men, and Ifs eternally. Where lies the final harbor, whence we unmoor no more? In what rapt ether sails the world, of which the weariest will never weary? Where is the foundling's father hidden? Our souls are like those orphans whose unwedded mothers die in bearing them: the secret of our paternity lies in their grave, and we must there to learn it.</em><ul><li>Ishmael, from <em>Moby-Dick</em></li></ul></li><li><em>\\[A] man's virtue may be but the defect of his desire, as his crime may be but a function of his virtue.</em><ul><li>Jack Burden, from <em>All the King’s Men</em> by Robert Penn Warren</li></ul></li><li><em>The creation of man whom God in His foreknowledge knew doomed to sin was the awful index of God's omnipotence. For it would have been a thing of trifling and contemptible ease for Perfection to create mere perfection. To do so would, to speak truth, be not creation but extension. Separateness is identity and the only way for God to create, truly create, man was to make him separate from God Himself, and to be separate from God is to be sinful. The creation of evil is therefore the index of God's glory and His power. That had to be so that the creation of good might be the index of man's glory and power.</em><ul><li>Jack Burden’s false father, from <em>All the King’s Men</em> by Robert Penn Warren</li></ul></li><li><em>History is blind, man is not.</em><ul><li>Hugh Miller, as recounted by Jack Burden, from <em>All the King’s Men</em> by Robert Penn Warren</li></ul></li><li><em>Trifles make the sum of life.</em><ul><li>David Copperfield, from <em>David Copperfield</em> by Charles Dickens </li></ul></li><li><em>Now, welcome poverty!… Welcome misery, welcome houselessness, welcome hunger, rags, tempest, and beggary! Mutual confidence will sustain us to the end!</em><ul><li>Wilkins Micawber, from <em>David Copperfield</em> by Charles Dickens</li></ul></li><li><em>We must meet reverses boldly, and not suffer them to frighten us, my dear. We must learn to act the play out. We must live misfortune down,…!</em><ul><li>Betsey Trotwood, from <em>David Copperfield</em> by Charles Dickens</li></ul></li><li><em>By all that’s wonderful, it is the sea, I believe, the sea itself—or is it youth alone? Who can tell? But you here—you all had something out of life: money, love— whatever one gets on shore—and, tell me, wasn’t that the best time, that time when we were young at sea; young and had nothing, on the sea that gives nothing, except hard knocks—and sometimes a chance to feel your strength—that only—what you all regret?</em><ul><li>Marlow, from <em>Youth</em> by Joseph Conrad</li></ul></li><li><em>I remember the drawn faces, the dejected figures of my two men, and I remember my youth and the feeling that will never come back any more—the feeling that I could last for ever, outlast the sea, the earth, and all men; the deceitful feeling that lures us on to joys, to perils, to love, to vain effort—to death; the triumphant conviction of strength, the heat of life in the handful of dust, the glow in the heart that with every year grows dim, grows cold, grows small, and expires—and expires, too soon— before life itself.</em><ul><li>Marlow, from <em>Youth</em> by Joseph Conrad</li></ul></li><li>The real voyage of discovery consists not in seeking new landscapes, but in having new eyes.<ul><li>Marcel Proust</li></ul></li><li><em><span class=\"wl-off\">Did you not call this a glorious expedition? And wherefore was it glorious? Not because the way was smooth and placid as a southern sea, but because it was full of dangers and terror; because, at every new incident, your fortitude was to be called forth, and your courage exhibited; because danger and death surrounded it, and these you were to brave and overcome.</span></em><ul><li>Victor Frankenstein to the crew of Robert Walton from <em>Frankenstein</em>, Chapter 7 Vol. 3 </li></ul></li><li><em><span class=\"wl-off\">Oh! be men, or be more than men. Be steady to your purposes, and firm as a rock. This ice is not made of such stuff as your hearts may be; it is mutable, and cannot withstand you, if you say that it shall not.</span></em><ul><li>Victor Frankenstein to the crew of Robert Walton from <em>Frankenstein</em>, Chapter 7 Vol. 3 </li></ul></li><li><em>But it is nice for a mediocre man to know that greatness must be the loneliest state in the world.</em><ul><li>Samuel Hamilton to Lee and Adam from <em>East of Eden</em>, page 264</li></ul></li><li><em>I am a wretched man but my heart is true to you still.</em> <ul><li>Michael Henchard from <em>Mayor of Casterbridge, Chapter 40</em></li></ul></li><li><em>Dear me, I think I’m becoming a god.</em><ul><li>Vespasian on his deathbed, 79 AD.</li></ul></li><li><em>Men are bad in countless ways, but good in only one.</em><ul><li>Aristotle on the golden mean, from his  <em><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nicomachean Ethics\">Nicomachean Ethics</a></em>.</li></ul></li><li><em>Be it so. This burning of widows is your custom; prepare the funeral pile. But my nation has also a custom. When men burn women alive we hang them, and confiscate all their property. My carpenters shall therefore erect gibbets on which to hang all concerned when the widow is consumed. Let us all act according to national customs.</em><ul><li>General Sir Charles James Napier, in response to the Indian practice of Sati.</li></ul></li></ul><h4><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a></h4><ul><li><em>Doubt thou the stars are fire,</em> <em>Doubt that the sun doth move,</em> <em>Doubt truth to be a liar,</em> <em>But never doubt I love.</em><ul><li>Hamlet from <em>Hamlet, (2.2.124-127)</em></li></ul></li><li>*This above all: to thine own self be true, And it must follow, as the night the day, Thou canst not then be false to any man.*<ul><li>Hamlet from <em>Hamlet, (1.3.82-84)</em></li></ul></li><li>*For who would bear the whips and scorns of time, Th' oppressor's wrong the proud man's contumely, The pangs of despised love, the law's delay, The insolence of office, and the spurns That patient merit of th' unworthy takes, When he himself might his quietus make With a bare bodkin? (3.1.79-85)*<ul><li>What is the purpose of living, if living is suffering? Answers to this questions can be found the various religions of the word (see Buddhism, for example), and in many philosophical schools of thought.</li></ul></li><li>*Which dreams, indeed, are ambition, For the very substance of the ambitious is merely The shadow of a dream. (2.2.276-278)*<ul><li>This is an interesting phrase that seems could have multiple definitions. Ambitions here could be interpreted as a more concrete version of one's goals. This is because shadows seem to represent a more \"grounded\" (literal) version of a dream, as it touches the ground, while dreams are typically light and floating, rather ethereal in composition. </li></ul></li></ul><p><em>Purpose is but the slave to memory, Of violent birth, but poor validity, Which now, like fruit unripe, sticks on the tree, But fall, unshaken, when they mellow be. (3.2.186-189)</em></p><p>What we intend to to can be forgotten over the course of time, so that although we may be very passionate about something in the present, by some time in the future, the passion could have completely faded.</p><p><em>But, orderly to end where I begun, Our wills and fates do so contrary run That our devices still are overthrown. (3.2.208-210)</em></p><p>The idea that nothing is Perfect, especially the execution of a plan. Though we may hope for something, what actually may occur is something else entirely.</p><p><em>A certain convocation of politic worms are e’en at him. Your worm is your only emperor for diet. We fat all creatures else to fat us, and we fat ourselves for maggots. Your fat kind and your lean beggar is but variable service—two dishes, but to one table. That’s the end. (4.3.22-27)</em></p><p>Hamlet describes again a sort of existentialism, questioning the purpose of existence. We do things for our own benefit, but ultimately, they are of no consequence. We will die one day, and all that we have done will be erased.</p><p><em>O, that that earth which kept the world in awe/ Should patch a wall t’ expel the winter’s flaw! (5.1.222-223)</em></p><p>This again references the fact that, no matter who you are, one day, you are doing to die and return to the earth, and be treated just the same as everyone else.</p><p><em>What a piece of work is a man, How noble in reason, how infinite in faculty, In form and moving how express and admirable, In action how like an Angel, In apprehension how like a god, The beauty of the world, The paragon of animals. And yet to me, what is this quintessence of dust? (2.2.327-332)</em> <em>His virtues else, be they as pure as grace, As infinite as man may undergo, Shall in the general censure take corruption From that particular fault. (1.4.. 35-38)</em></p><p>This seems to echo the idea that a chain is only as strong as its weakest link. Here, Hamlet talks about the habit of his people to party, and he puts this as something that other nations critique them for. Thus, he concludes, despite their successes in other areas, this one fault ultimately corrupts their other virtues.</p><p><em>For in the fatness of these pursy times Virtue itself of vice must pardon beg, Yea, curb and woo for leave to do him good. (3.4.171-173)</em></p><p>Hamlet’s desperation and emotionality really shows through here, as he begs his mother to listen to him and repent for her actions.</p><p><em>I must be cruel only to be kind. Thus bad begins and worse remains behind. (3.4.197-199)</em></p><p>Masterful use of juxtaposition. Hamlet justifies his actions though a utilitarian framework, arguing that what he has done is only to prevent a further evil, though in the following sentence, he again contrasts that and indicates that regardless, worst is to come. While there is a certain sense of truth to this situation, I think that this also shows Hamlet’s true madness showing through.</p><p><em>Why, let the stricken deer go weep, The hart ungallèd play. For some must watch while some must sleep. So runs the world away.</em></p><p>This is simply a fabulous quote, describing how there are some in the world that face the truth head on, while others (Claudius, in this case), escape, unable to face the truth.</p><p><em>But such officers do the king best service in the end. He keeps them, like an ape, in the corner of his jaw, first mouthed to be last swallowed. When he needs what you have gleaned, it is but squeezing you and, sponge, you shall be dry again. (4.2.17-20)</em></p><p>Hamlet compares Rosencrantz and Guildenstern to mere puppets of the king, something that makes sense given that every time they have appeared to Hamlet, they had been under the instruction of the king or were acting in his interest. Hamlet is justifiably angry about this.</p><h4><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Essays (book)\">Essays (book)</a></h4><ul><li>*I shall see to it, if I can, that my death makes no statement that my life has not made already.<ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book I, Chapter 7</em></li></ul></li><li>*There are a thousand ways of missing the bull’s eye, only one way of hitting it.<ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book I, Chapter 9</em></li></ul></li><li>*We sweat, we tremble, we turn pale, we flush, beneath our imagination’s impact; deep in our feather-beds, we feel our bodies shaken by its onslaughts, sometimes almost to the point of death; and fervent youth grows so heated in its sleep that it satisfies its amorous desires even in dreams.<ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book I, Chapter 21</em></li></ul></li><li><em>Only fools are certain and immovable.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book I, Chapter 26</em></li></ul></li><li>*We have one foot in the grave, and our appetites and pursuits are only just born.<ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book II, Chapter 28</em></li></ul></li><li><em>Everyone thinks himself the master pattern of human nature; and by this, as on a touchstone, he tests all others.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book II, Chapter 32</em></li></ul></li><li>*I am less ambitious; I cast myself upon the world, solely for my present advantage. When I leave it—that is that!<ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book III, Chapter 2</em></li></ul></li><li>*Moreover, discipline is a dull and dismal virtue. To storm a breach, conduct an embassy, govern a people, those are brilliant actions. To scold, laugh, sell, pay, love, hate, and deal gently or contradict oneself: that is something rarer, more difficult and less noticed by the world. <ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book III, Chapter 2</em></li></ul></li><li>*The worth of a soul does not consist in soaring to a height, but in a steady movement.<ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book III, Chapter 2</em></li></ul></li><li><em>I do not much value my own opinions, but I value those of others no more.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from <em>Essays, Book III, Chapter 2</em></li></ul></li><li><em>If I had my life to live again, I should live as I have lived; I neither deplore the past, nor fear the future.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 2 </li></ul></li><li><em>Our conscience must amend itself by the strengthening of our reason, not by the weakening of our appetites.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 2 </li></ul></li><li><em>But come what may, I am glad that the world will know the height from which I shall have fallen.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 2</li></ul></li><li><em>To be tied and bound of necessity to one single way is not to live but to exist.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 3</li></ul></li><li><em>It is very foolish to stifle one’s own brilliance in order to shine with a borrowed light.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 3</li></ul></li><li><em>I am afraid that our knowledge is weak in every direction; we do not see far ahead or far behind us. It embraces little, and its life is short both in extent of time and extent of matter.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 6</li></ul></li><li><em>I feel much prouder of the victory I win over myself when, in the very heats of the battle, I make myself bow to the strength of my adversary’s argument, than I feel gratified by a victory gained over him through his weakness.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 8</li></ul></li><li><em>It is the historian’s task to set down the common beliefs, not to censor them.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 8</li></ul></li><li><em>Collect yourself, and you will find within you nature’s true arguments against death, which are the fittest to serve you in your need; it is they that make a peasant, and entire nations, die with the same constancy as a philosopher.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 12</li></ul></li><li><em>Everyone turns elsewhere and to the future, since no one has discovered himself.</em><ul><li>Montaigne from *Essays, Book III, Chapter 12</li></ul></li></ul>","writing ideas":"<h4>Inspo</h4><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Astral Codex Ten</p><p>Contents</p></blockquote><h4>Ideas</h4><ul><li>Boao Hope City: China’s Libertarian city<ul><li>How it works, why it came into existence, how it is doing, and its future?</li></ul></li><li>Complaints in the airport noise.<ul><li>Much of the complaints that the airport receives come from very few households.</li><li>This is like NIMBY but for airports.</li><li>Prevents efficiency gains, shift to supersonic aircraft, and prevents the plotting of aircraft routes that are the most efficient.</li></ul></li><li>The similarities between East of Eden and The Brothers Karamazov</li><li>Why Frida Khalo is a bad painter.</li><li>The death of a toad poem and Ikiru: is it that suffering is always grand, or is it that suffering is always meaningless? Or both? <ul><li>What does this say about the art that portrays these ideas? How are they able to convey seemingly contradictory ideas on different readings?</li></ul></li><li>Argument for objective aesthetics.</li><li>Separate the art from the artist.</li><li>Saint Saens third symphony</li><li>The art of Ensor</li><li>In defense of SparkNotes (for the students)<ul><li>A lot of books that we read in high schools are not very good, and it really isn't worth it for the student to have to read through a bunch of text that is bad and is boring to them.</li><li>They could use the time instead to read books that actually interest them.</li></ul></li><li>The incoherence of \"rights\"<ul><li>Rights are often contradictory and meaningless.</li></ul></li><li>The US's development is going to be held back/is being held back by people's reluctance of engaging with new technology. <ul><li>In the past the West has been at the forefront of innovation, and people accepted such change.</li><li>Perhaps a reason people think that such change is bad now is that they increase inequality<ul><li>Also <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it\">Because people moralize AI, they are unreasonably afraid of it</a></li></ul></li><li>This anti-technological viewpoint is bad.</li><li>China does not have this problem.</li></ul></li><li>Death of the renaissance man and by hyperspecialization in the age of ai</li><li>Freedom is categorically internally consistent</li><li>All art that is not purely aesthetics is political, and thus, propaganda</li><li>There was infanticide in many ancient societies. Many children who were disabled were simply left to die.<ul><li>Utilitarian, before utilitarianism was a thing?</li></ul></li><li>In defense of X<ul><li>I like the community notes system.</li></ul></li><li>In defense of prediction markets</li><li>In defense of ai art</li><li>Music is the best form of art, since there are the most amount of surprises in music.</li><li>Optimal restaurant trying strategy.</li><li>The unparadoxicalness of the non-identity problem.<ul><li>It seems like if you were to look at a life linearly, through time, you would find that it is not determinate whether to a person at any point in their life, that they would have an above average utility.</li></ul></li></ul>","‘Tyranny of the majority’ or cancel culture suppresses individuality":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even beyond straight up despotism, the tyranny of the majority, manifested societally/culturally in modern times as “cancel culture,” is able to suppress individuality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This shows why tyranny of the majority is bad.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is bad on an individual level, since people’s unique individualities, something which is at least on some level a good in itself, is destroyed through censorship, which has the effect of making all the same. Also, this prevents the existence of geniuses who might think in unorthodox ways but end up hugely benefitting society.</p><h4>Notes</h4><p>This does not mean that we have to be entirely embracing of all people. Ultimately, we must also consider our own interest, though sometimes it may cause disutility for others. For instance, someone who is a compulsive liar might have few friends, though that no one wants to be friends with him is not a harm towards him (a suppression of their individuality), but merely a protection of our own interests.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Censorship is bad for everyone\">Censorship is bad for everyone</a><ul><li>This is an elaboration of the concept from this note.</li></ul></li></ul>","荷花图轴":"<h4>Notes</h4><h4>References</h4>"};
